      "explanation": "I can see how worried you are, Maria, especially given what happened to your brother. That must have been devastating for your family, and it's completely understandable that you're frightened now. Let me explain what I think is happening with Elena. Based on her symptoms - the high fever, the cough, runny nose, red watery eyes, and now this rash that started on her face - and the fact that she hasn't had any of her vaccines, particularly the MMR vaccine, I'm concerned that Elena may have measles. Measles is a viral infection that spreads very easily from person to person. The white spots you saw in her mouth yesterday are called Koplik's spots, and they're a very specific sign of measles. The good news - and I want you to hear this clearly - is that most children with measles do recover completely at home with supportive care. Medicine has advanced significantly since your brother was ill, and we understand much better now how to monitor children and when they need hospital treatment. While measles can be serious, and we need to watch Elena carefully, the majority of children get through it without major complications. I need to report this to our Health Protection Team - this isn't because you've done anything wrong, but because it's a legal requirement that helps us protect other vulnerable people in the community. We'll arrange testing to confirm the diagnosis, and I'll give you very clear advice about caring for Elena at home and exactly what warning signs to watch for.",
