    "management_plan": {
      "explanation": "Thank you so much for coming in today, Sarah, and for being so honest about how you're feeling - I know that takes real courage, especially when you're exhausted and feeling overwhelmed. What you're describing sounds like postnatal depression, which is a genuine medical condition that affects around 1 in 10 new mothers - and it's even more common in mothers of twins because of the additional physical and emotional demands. This is not about being weak, ungrateful, or a bad mother. Your brain chemistry has been through enormous changes with pregnancy, birth, and now the demands of caring for two babies, and sometimes the brain needs support to rebalance. Think of it like diabetes - it's a medical condition where the body needs help to function properly, not a character flaw. The fact that you're here seeking help shows you're being a responsible, caring mother who wants to be well for your babies. The really important thing to know is that postnatal depression is very treatable, and with the right support - which might include talking therapies, practical help, and possibly medication that's safe during breastfeeding - the vast majority of women recover fully. I want to work with you to create a plan that addresses both your emotional wellbeing and your practical concerns, and I can assure you that seeking help is the right thing to do - it won't result in anyone thinking you're unfit as a mother.",
