      "explanation": "Marcus, I really appreciate you coming in today and I can hear how much this is affecting every part of your life - your work, your relationships, and your ability to do the things you enjoy. I want you to know that I'm taking your symptoms very seriously, and the fact that your blood tests are normal is actually helpful information, not a sign that we're missing something serious. From what you've described - the widespread pain, the extreme fatigue, the poor sleep quality, the difficulty concentrating, and the way exercise makes things worse for days afterwards - this fits with a condition called fibromyalgia. Think of fibromyalgia as your pain processing system being turned up too high, like a volume dial that's stuck. Your nervous system is amplifying normal sensations and interpreting them as pain. It's not 'all in your head' - it's a real physical condition that we now understand much better. The good news is that while we can't 'cure' fibromyalgia with a single treatment, there are multiple effective strategies that, when combined, can significantly improve your symptoms and quality of life. This includes specific medications that work on the nervous system, structured exercise programs that are designed for fibromyalgia, sleep improvement strategies, and stress management techniques. Many people with fibromyalgia do get substantially better and are able to return to activities they enjoy. I'd like to work with you on a comprehensive management plan, and I want to reassure you that the tingling you're experiencing and the other symptoms don't indicate MS or arthritis - they're part of the fibromyalgia picture. Let me explain the approach we'll take together.",
