      "safety_netting": "Seek urgent medical attention by calling 999 if you develop: severe chest pain, sudden difficulty breathing, sudden weakness or numbness on one side of your body, sudden severe headache different from your usual headaches, or loss of consciousness. Contact the GP surgery within 24-48 hours if you experience: significant worsening of pain that's different in character from your usual pain, new joint swelling or redness, unexplained fever or night sweats, unintentional weight loss, new bladder or bowel dysfunction, progressive weakness or numbness, or intolerable side effects from medications. Book a routine appointment if: you're not seeing any improvement after 6-8 weeks of treatment, you have questions about your management plan, you need adjustments to your medications, or you're struggling to cope emotionally with your symptoms. Regarding amitriptyline specifically, contact us if you experience: severe drowsiness affecting daily function, rapid heartbeat or palpitations, difficulty passing urine, or any concerning side effects. If you develop thoughts of self-harm or suicide, contact the surgery immediately, call 111, or go to A&E. Out of hours: call NHS 111 for advice if you're concerned about any symptoms and can't wait for the surgery to open. Remember that fibromyalgia symptoms can fluctuate - having a flare-up doesn't mean the treatment isn't working, but if flare-ups are severe or very frequent, we should review your management plan. Keep your symptom diary and bring it to your follow-up appointment so we can track your progress together.",
