    "management_plan": {
      "explanation": "Dan, I can see this lump has been really worrying for you, especially with how noticeable it is on site, and it’s completely understandable to be concerned about what it might mean.\nI’ve examined your eyelid carefully, and the findings fit best with something called a chalazion. That’s a small cyst that forms when one of the tiny oil glands in the eyelid becomes blocked. A simple way to picture it is like a blocked gutter — the oil can’t drain properly, so it backs up and forms a firm, pea-like lump.\nThe reassuring thing is that this is harmless, it’s very rarely linked to cancer, and in most cases it settles on its own over a few weeks to a few months.\nThere are also several features that are reassuring — it isn’t red, painful or ulcerated, and your eyelashes are intact. Those signs make infection or anything more serious very unlikely.\nIn terms of treatment, the first step is warm compresses and gentle lid massage, which helps open the blocked gland and allows it to drain naturally. Most chalazia improve with this alone.",
      "additional_notes": "Patient keen to self-manage; ensure written instructions provided. May need referral to community ophthalmology if persistent.",
      "safety_netting": "Seek urgent help via GP same day or 111 if: swelling becomes rapidly painful, red or hot, vision blurs, you notice bleeding, lash loss, change in colour or shape, or the lump returns in exactly the same spot after treatment. Call 999 if sudden severe visual loss or eye pain. Otherwise, if no improvement at all after 8–12 weeks despite compresses, book sooner follow-up for possible procedure.",
      "non_pharmacological_treatment": [
        "Warm compresses: clean flannel soaked in comfortably hot water, applied to closed lids for 10 minutes, 3–4 times daily",
        "Lid massage after compress: gentle circular motion toward lash line to express oil",
        "Lid hygiene: diluted baby shampoo or proprietary lid-cleanser twice daily for four weeks",
        "Workplace adaptation: encourage wearing wrap-around safety glasses rather than tight goggles until lump subsides",
        "Lifestyle: stop-smoking referral (improves ocular surface health), review screen breaks and hydration"
      ],
      "ice": [
        "I can see why you might be worried about something being stuck in your eye, and it’s completely understandable to be concerned. Let me reassure you: there is no foreign body present. What you’re seeing is a chalazion, which happens when one of the small oil glands in your eyelid gets blocked. It isn’t caused by grit or anything stuck in your eye, and it’s not dangerous. We can monitor it together, and if needed, there are simple treatments to help it settle, so you don’t have to worry.",
        "I understand it’s natural to worry about cancer, so let me reassure you: chalazia are almost always benign, and the signs that would suggest something more serious are very uncommon. That said, if any red-flag symptoms appear, we can arrange a rapid review straight away. It’s also helpful to set expectations: these usually take 6–12 weeks to resolve. In the meantime, there are simple things you can do at home to help, and if it doesn’t settle or causes persistent problems, surgical options can be considered."
      ],
