    "management_plan": {
      "explanation": "I really appreciate you coming in to talk to me about this, Jayden. I can see you're worried, and it takes courage to be honest about what happened. Let me explain what's likely going on. When someone takes MDMA or similar substances, it affects chemicals in the brain called neurotransmitters - particularly serotonin. Think of it like your brain's chemical balance being temporarily disrupted, similar to how turning up the volume too loud on speakers can cause distortion. The palpitations, anxiety, and sleep problems you're experiencing are common after-effects as your brain readjusts its chemical balance. This is actually your body's way of recovering and returning to normal. The good news is that for most young people who use MDMA once, these symptoms improve significantly over the next few days to weeks as the brain rebalances itself. While I understand your fear about permanent damage, single use typically doesn't cause lasting heart or brain damage in otherwise healthy teenagers, though I do want to do some checks to reassure both of us. What's really important now is supporting your recovery, managing your anxiety symptoms, and making sure you're safe going forward. I'd like to work with you on a plan that addresses your physical symptoms, helps with the anxiety, and ensures you have the information and support you need to make safer choices in future.",
