      "ice": [
        "Address idea about 'laced' drugs: 'You mentioned worrying the drug was laced with something. That's a really valid concern - street drugs are completely unregulated, so you never truly know what you're taking or how strong it is. What you're experiencing could be from MDMA itself, or from other substances mixed in, or from the combination with alcohol. This uncertainty is exactly why these substances are so risky. The symptoms you're describing are consistent with MDMA after-effects, but without testing the actual substance, we can't know for certain what you took. What's most important now is managing your current symptoms and recovery.'",
        "Address concern about heart damage: 'I can hear how frightened you are about your heart. Let me reassure you - while MDMA does temporarily increase heart rate and blood pressure, single use in a young, healthy person typically doesn't cause permanent heart damage. However, I do want to examine you properly and check your heart rate, blood pressure, and do an ECG to give you that reassurance. We'll also check your heart rhythm is normal. If everything looks okay today, and your symptoms are improving, that's very reassuring.'",
        "Address concern about brain damage: 'Your worry about brain damage is understandable. MDMA does affect brain chemistry, particularly serotonin levels, which is why you're experiencing anxiety and sleep problems. However, the brain is remarkably resilient, especially in young people. Single use typically doesn't cause permanent structural brain damage. The symptoms you're experiencing are more about temporary chemical imbalance that improves with time. You don't need a brain scan based on what you've told me - scans are for when someone has had a head injury, seizure, or ongoing neurological symptoms, which you haven't.'",
        "Address concern about mum finding out: 'I've explained the confidentiality situation. What I'd add is that your mum clearly cares about you, and while I understand you're worried about disappointing her, she would probably want to support you through this. Many parents surprise their teenagers with how understanding they can be when their child comes to them honestly. That said, this is your decision about when and how to tell her, if at all. If you did want to tell her, I could help you plan that conversation or even be present for it if that would help.'",
        "Address expectation for tests: 'You mentioned wanting tests, which is completely reasonable. Here's what I think we should do: I'll examine you thoroughly including checking your heart and blood pressure. I'll do an ECG to check your heart rhythm. I'll also do some basic blood tests to check your kidney function, electrolytes, and general health markers. These tests will help reassure both of us that there's no ongoing physical harm. However, I want to be clear that the anxiety and sleep symptoms may take a bit longer to resolve, and those won't show up on tests - they're about brain chemistry rebalancing, which happens with time and support.'",
        "Address expectation for confidentiality: 'I've explained how confidentiality works. I want to document this consultation in your medical records because it's important for your ongoing care, but I'll be mindful about how I word things. Your medical records are confidential - colleges and universities don't have access to them. The only people who can see your records are healthcare professionals involved in your care, and they're bound by the same confidentiality rules.'"
