    "interview_questions": [
      {
        "answer": "My immediate concern is **acute compartment syndrome** of the lower leg, which is a surgical emergency. The clinical presentation raises significant red flags:\n\n- Pain out of proportion to the injury despite adequate analgesia\n- Recent trauma to the lower limb (likely tibial fracture)\n- Progressive worsening of symptoms\n\nCompartment syndrome occurs when increased pressure within a closed fascial space compromises tissue perfusion. The tibia is the most common site for compartment syndrome, particularly following fractures. The condition can lead to irreversible muscle and nerve damage if not treated promptly, with the critical ischemia time being approximately 6 hours.\n\nThe diagnosis is primarily clinical, and a high index of suspicion is essential as delayed diagnosis can lead to devastating consequences including permanent disability, contractures, infection, and even amputation in severe cases.",
        "question": "What is your immediate concern in this patient and why?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Assessment for compartment syndrome involves a structured approach:\n\n**Clinical Assessment (Primary Diagnostic Method):**\n1. **Pain:** Assess for disproportionate pain, especially pain unresponsive to appropriate analgesia\n2. **Passive Stretch Test:** Pain on passive stretching of muscles in the affected compartment (e.g., passive toe extension for deep posterior compartment)\n3. **Palpation:** Tense, firm compartments on examination\n4. **Paresthesia/Sensory Changes:** Assess for altered sensation in nerve distributions:\n   - Deep peroneal nerve (first web space)\n   - Superficial peroneal nerve (dorsum of foot)\n   - Tibial nerve (plantar aspect)\n5. **Motor Function:** Test for weakness in:\n   - Ankle dorsiflexion (anterior compartment)\n   - Great toe extension (anterior compartment)\n   - Toe flexion (deep posterior compartment)\n   - Foot eversion (lateral compartment)\n6. **Pulses:** Assess distal pulses, though importantly these are often preserved in compartment syndrome (absence is a very late sign)\n\n**NB: Pain out of proportion is the only reliable sign in the early stages. When the other signs become clinically apparent, it may well be too late to prevent complications so be on high alert!**\n**Compartment Pressure Measurement:**\n- Indicated when clinical assessment is difficult (unconscious patient, unreliable examination)\n- Absolute pressure >30 mmHg OR\n- Delta pressure (diastolic BP minus compartment pressure) <30 mmHg is diagnostic\n- Measurements should be taken in all suspected compartments\n\n**Documentation:**\nCareful documentation of all findings with times is essential for both clinical and medicolegal purposes.",
