        "answer": "The lower leg has four main compartments, each containing specific muscles, nerves and vessels:\n\n1. **Anterior Compartment:**\n   - **Muscles:** Tibialis anterior, extensor hallucis longus, extensor digitorum longus, peroneus tertius\n   - **Nerve:** Deep peroneal nerve\n   - **Vessels:** Anterior tibial artery and veins\n   - **Function:** Ankle dorsiflexion and toe extension\n\n2. **Lateral Compartment:**\n   - **Muscles:** Peroneus longus and peroneus brevis\n   - **Nerve:** Superficial peroneal nerve\n   - **Vessels:** No major vessels\n   - **Function:** Foot eversion and weak ankle plantarflexion\n\n3. **Deep Posterior Compartment:**\n   - **Muscles:** Tibialis posterior, flexor digitorum longus, flexor hallucis longus\n   - **Nerve:** Tibial nerve\n   - **Vessels:** Posterior tibial artery and veins, peroneal artery\n   - **Function:** Ankle plantarflexion, toe flexion, foot inversion\n\n4. **Superficial Posterior Compartment:**\n   - **Muscles:** Gastrocnemius, soleus, plantaris\n   - **Nerve:** Sural nerve (branch of tibial nerve)\n   - **Vessels:** Small sural vessels\n   - **Function:** Powerful ankle plantarflexion\n\nUnderstanding the contents of each compartment is crucial for recognizing the clinical manifestations of compartment syndrome and for performing effective fasciotomies.",
