      {
        "answer": "Management of suspected compartment syndrome follows a time-critical pathway:\n\n**Immediate Actions:**\n1. **Recognize the emergency** - compartment syndrome is a surgical emergency requiring urgent intervention\n2. **Remove all constrictive dressings/casts** to eliminate external pressure\n3. **Position the limb at heart level** (not elevated) to optimize perfusion\n4. **Optimize analgesia** while maintaining clinical assessment capability\n5. **Maintain normotension** - avoid hypotension which could worsen tissue perfusion\n\n**Resuscitation:**\n- Establish IV access with two large-bore cannulae\n- Administer IV fluids as required\n- Provide adequate analgesia (typically IV opiates)\n\n**Definitive Management:**\n- **Emergency four-compartment fasciotomy** - ideally within 1 hour of decision and within 6 hours of onset of symptoms. \n- This requires two incisions:\n  1. **Lateral incision** (accessing lateral and anterior compartments)\n  2. **Medial incision** (accessing superficial and deep posterior compartments)\n- **Fracture stabilization** - either temporary (external fixation) or definitive fixation depending on soft tissue status\n- **Wound management** with planned second look at 24-48 hours\n- Consider **negative pressure wound therapy** between staged procedures\n\n**Surgical Planning:**\n- Obtain informed consent (explaining risks including infection, need for further surgeries, potential functional deficits)\n- Mark patient and operative site\n- Organize appropriate surgical team and equipment\n- Ensure blood products available if needed\n\n**Post-operative Care:**\n- Consider HDU/ICU monitoring for high-risk patients\n- Regular wound reviews\n- Plan for delayed primary closure or skin grafting (typically 3-7 days)\n- Early physiotherapy and rehabilitation\n- Monitor for renal complications (myoglobinuria, acute kidney injury)\n\n**Documentation:**\n- Detailed notes including timing of decisions and interventions\n- Neurovascular status pre- and post-operatively\n\nThis management follows the BOAST guidelines for compartment syndrome, emphasizing the time-critical nature of intervention to prevent permanent tissue damage.",
