        "answer": "Surgical fasciotomy of the lower leg requires decompression of all four compartments, which can be achieved through two main approaches:\n\n**1. Two-Incision Technique (Most Common):**\n\n**Lateral Incision:**\n- Approximately 2-3cm lateral to the anterior border of the tibia\n- Extends from proximal tibia to ankle\n- Provides access to the anterior and lateral compartments\n- Technique:\n  - Incise skin and subcutaneous tissue\n  - Identify the intermuscular septum between anterior and lateral compartments\n  - Release anterior compartment by incising fascia along length of compartment\n  - Release lateral compartment by incising fascia along length of compartment\n\n**Medial Incision:**\n- 2cm posterior to the medial border of the tibia\n- Extends from proximal tibia to ankle\n- Provides access to superficial and deep posterior compartments\n- Technique:\n  - Incise skin and subcutaneous tissue\n  - Identify and protect the saphenous vein and nerve\n  - Release superficial posterior compartment\n  - Retract gastrocnemius/soleus to access deep posterior compartment\n  - Release deep posterior compartment, taking care to protect neurovascular structures\n\n**2. Single-Incision Technique (Fibulectomy Approach):**\n- Less commonly used\n- Involves a lateral incision with removal of a segment of fibula\n- Allows access to all four compartments through one incision\n- Generally reserved for specific situations (e.g., combined with fibular fracture fixation)\n\n**Key Surgical Principles:**\n- Incisions must be long enough to decompress the entire length of each compartment\n- Complete fascial release is essential - partial release is inadequate\n- Protect neurovascular structures during dissection\n- Leave wounds open initially\n- Plan for delayed closure or skin grafting\n- Consider negative pressure wound therapy between procedures\n\n**Post-Fasciotomy Management:**\n- Reassess neurovascular status\n- Plan for second-look procedure at 24-48 hours\n- 