        "answer": "**Complications of Untreated/Delayed Compartment Syndrome:**\n\n1. **Muscle Necrosis and Contractures:**\n   - Irreversible muscle damage leading to fibrosis and contracture\n   - Volkmann's ischemic contracture\n   - Permanent functional disability\n\n2. **Neurological Deficits:**\n   - Permanent sensory loss\n   - Motor weakness or paralysis\n   - Chronic neuropathic pain\n\n3. **Systemic Complications:**\n   - Rhabdomyolysis with myoglobinuria\n   - Acute kidney injury/renal failure\n   - Metabolic acidosis\n   - Hyperkalemia\n\n4. **Infection:**\n   - Increased risk of infection in necrotic tissue\n   - Osteomyelitis\n   - Necrotizing fasciitis in severe cases\n\n5. **Amputation:**\n   - May be necessary in severe cases with extensive tissue necrosis\n\n**Complications of Fasciotomy:**\n\n1. **Wound-Related:**\n   - Infection (reported in up to 25% of cases)\n   - Delayed wound healing\n   - Need for skin grafting (up to 50% of cases)\n   - Cosmetic concerns (scarring)\n\n2. **Neurovascular:**\n   - Iatrogenic nerve injury during fasciotomy\n   - Vascular injury\n   - Incomplete decompression requiring revision\n\n3. **Functional:**\n   - Muscle herniation through fascial defects\n   - Chronic pain\n   - Altered sensation around scars\n   - Reduced muscle strength\n\n4. **Long-term:**\n   - Chronic venous insufficiency\n   - Chronic edema\n   - Reduced exercise tolerance in affected limb\n   - Psychological impact (body image concerns)\n\n5. **Fracture-Related:**\n   - Delayed union or non-union of associated fractures\n   - Increased risk of infection at fracture sites\n\nThe literature suggests that while fasciotomy has significant associated morbidity, the consequences of untreated compartment syndrome are far more severe. This underscores the importance of early diagnosis and prompt surgical intervention.",
