      {
        "answer": "The evidence regarding timing of fasciotomy in compartment syndrome strongly supports early intervention:\n\n**Key Evidence on Timing:**\n\n1. **Critical Ischemia Time:**\n   - Muscle and nerve tissue can tolerate ischemia for approximately 4-6 hours before irreversible damage occurs\n   - Multiple animal and clinical studies demonstrate that fasciotomy performed within 6 hours of onset results in significantly better outcomes\n   - Sheridan and Matsen's classic study showed near-complete recovery when fasciotomy was performed within 6 hours, but only 8% good outcomes when delayed beyond 12 hours\n\n2. **Progressive Tissue Damage:**\n   - Rorabeck's research demonstrated that the extent of functional deficit correlates directly with the duration of increased compartment pressure\n   - Tissue damage follows a predictable sequence: nerve function is affected first (1-2 hours), followed by muscle (4-6 hours), with irreversible necrosis occurring after 6+ hours\n\n3. **Outcome Studies:**\n   - McQueen et al. showed that delayed fasciotomy (>8 hours) was associated with significantly higher rates of infection, longer hospital stays, and worse functional outcomes\n   - Finkelstein et al. demonstrated that fasciotomy after 12 hours resulted in 14× higher risk of infection and 11× higher risk of amputation compared to early fasciotomy\n\n4. **Systematic Reviews:**\n   - A systematic review by Mubarak and Hargens concluded that the \"6-hour rule\" remains the most evidence-based guideline for timing of fasciotomy\n   - More recent reviews continue to support this timeframe, though emphasizing that earlier intervention whenever possible is preferable\n\n**Clinical Guidelines:**\n\n- BOAST guidelines emphasize that compartment syndrome is a surgical emergency requiring immediate fasciotomy\n- The British Orthopaedic Association recommends fasciotomy as soon as the diagnosis is made, ideally within 6 hours of onset\n- The American Academy of Orthopaedic Surgeons similarly recommends emergency fasciotomy upon diagnosis\n\n**Practical Implications:**\n\n- The evidence supports a \"time is tissue\" approach to compartment syndrome\n- Diagnostic delays should be minimized through education and protocols\n- When in doubt, earlier fasciotomy is preferred over watchful waiting\n- Documentation of timing (symptom onset, diagnosis, intervention) is critical both clinically and medicolegally",
