        "answer": "Management of the tibial fracture in this patient with compartment syndrome requires a staged approach with careful consideration of soft tissue status:\n\n**Initial Management:**\n\n1. **Prioritize Compartment Syndrome:**\n   - Emergency fasciotomy takes precedence over definitive fracture fixation\n   - The fasciotomy incisions can sometimes be utilized or extended for fracture fixation\n\n2. **Temporary Stabilization Options:**\n   - External fixation is often the initial treatment of choice\n   - Provides stability while allowing wound management\n   - Maintains length and alignment\n   - Minimizes additional soft tissue trauma\n\n**Definitive Fracture Management:**\n\nThe definitive management depends on fracture pattern, location, soft tissue status, and patient factors:\n\n1. **Intramedullary Nailing:**\n   - Gold standard for diaphyseal tibial fractures\n   - Can be performed acutely if soft tissues permit or delayed after fasciotomy wounds improve\n   - Advantages: load-sharing device, minimally invasive, high union rates\n   - Considerations: increased compartment pressures during reaming, infection risk with open fasciotomy wounds\n\n2. **Plate Fixation:**\n   - Consider for metaphyseal/articular fractures\n   - Can utilize fasciotomy incisions for approach\n   - Minimally invasive plate osteosynthesis (MIPO) techniques when possible\n   - Higher risk of wound complications with extensive soft tissue dissection\n\n3. **Definitive External Fixation:**\n   - Consider in cases with significant contamination or soft tissue injury\n   - May be converted to internal fixation once soft tissues have healed\n   - Higher rates of malunion, pin site infection, and patient inconvenience\n\n**Timing of Definitive Fixation:**\n\n- **Early Total Care:** Definitive fixation at time of fasciotomy if patient stable and fracture amenable\n- **Damage Control Orthopaedics:** Temporary external fixation followed by delayed definitive fixation (typically 5-10 days) once soft tissue swelling subsides\n\n**Evidence-Based Considerations:**\n\n- SPRINT trial showed similar outcomes between IM nailing and plate fixation for closed tibial shaft fractures\n- Meta-analyses suggest IM nailing has lower infection rates and higher union rates compared to plating for diaphyseal fractures\n- BOAST guidelines recommend considering soft tissue status as a primary factor in decision-making\n\n**Adjunctive Treatments:**\n\n- Consider bone grafting for comminuted fractures with bone loss\n- Low-intensity pulsed ultrasound or bone stimulators for high-risk fractures\n- Early functional rehabilitation once soft tissues permit",
