      {
        "answer": "My primary concern is a prosthetic joint infection (PJI) in this diabetic patient with a recent TKR. The presentation of increasing pain, swelling, warmth, erythema, serous discharge, and low-grade fever at 8 weeks post-operation is highly suspicious for an early PJI.\n\nMy diagnostic approach would include:\n\n1. **Thorough history and examination** focusing on onset of symptoms, risk factors, and wound assessment\n\n2. **Blood investigations** including inflammatory markers (CRP, ESR), FBC, U&Es, and blood cultures if febrile\n\n3. **Joint aspiration** under strict aseptic technique for cell count, crystal analysis, Gram stain, and culture. A synovial WCC >1,500 cells/μL or >65% neutrophils is suggestive of PJI. \n\n4. **Radiological assessment** with plain radiographs to assess implant position and look for signs of loosening. Advanced imaging might include CT, MRI with metal artifact reduction protocols, or nuclear medicine studies if the diagnosis remains uncertain.\n\nI would apply the Musculoskeletal Infection Society (MSIS) criteria or the 2018 International Consensus Meeting (ICM) criteria for diagnosing PJI, which incorporate major criteria (sinus tract or two positive cultures) and minor criteria (elevated serum markers, synovial fluid findings, and histological evidence).",
