        "answer": "Prosthetic joint infections are typically classified based on timing after implantation:\n\n1. **Early infections** (<3 months post-surgery):\n   - Usually caused by virulent organisms (S. aureus, gram-negative bacilli)\n   - Often result from perioperative contamination\n   - May present with acute symptoms and wound problems\n   - Management: DAIR (Debridement, Antibiotics, Implant Retention) if implant is stable\n\n2. **Delayed/indolent infections** (3-24 months):\n   - Often caused by less virulent organisms (coagulase-negative staphylococci, P. acnes)\n   - May result from perioperative contamination with indolent organisms\n   - Present with gradual onset of pain, often without systemic symptoms\n   - Management: Usually requires single or two-stage revision\n\n3. **Late/hematogenous infections** (>24 months):\n   - Result from hematogenous seeding from distant infection sites\n   - Often acute onset after period of well-functioning implant\n   - Management: DAIR if acute presentation <4 weeks, otherwise revision\n\n",
