      {
        "answer": "**DAIR** stands for **Debridement, Antibiotics, and Implant Retention**. It's a surgical strategy aimed at treating prosthetic joint infections while preserving the original implant.\n\n**The procedure involves:**\n1. Thorough debridement of all infected and necrotic tissue\n2. Copious irrigation with antiseptic solutions\n3. Exchange of modular components (e.g., polyethylene liner, femoral head in hip replacements)\n4. Retention of fixed components if well-fixed\n5. Multiple tissue samples for microbiological analysis\n6. Followed by targeted antibiotic therapy\n\n**DAIR would be appropriate for this patient if:**\n- The infection is early (within 4 weeks of implantation or symptom onset <4 weeks in late hematogenous infection)\n- The implant is well-fixed with no evidence of loosening on imaging\n- The patient has a functional implant with good soft tissue coverage\n- There is no evidence of a sinus tract communicating with the joint\n- The causative organism is not a difficult-to-treat pathogen (e.g., MRSA, fungi)\n\nIn this specific case (8 weeks post-op with 10 days of symptoms), DAIR could be considered because:\n- The presentation is relatively early (within 3 months of implantation)\n- The symptoms have been present for a short duration (<4 weeks)\n- If preoperative imaging shows no evidence of implant loosening\n\nThe success rate of DAIR decreases significantly if:\n- Symptoms have been present for >4 weeks\n- The patient has had multiple previous revision surgeries\n- The infection is caused by resistant organisms\n- The patient has significant comorbidities affecting immune function (though diabetes is a concern here)\n\nIf DAIR is performed, it should be followed by an appropriate antibiotic regimen, typically 6-12 weeks of therapy, with initial IV antibiotics followed by oral therapy based on culture sensitivities.",
