      {
        "answer": "If DAIR is not appropriate due to factors such as symptom duration >4 weeks, implant loosening, presence of a sinus tract, or resistant organisms, the main alternative surgical options are:\n\n1. **Single-stage revision**:\n   - Complete removal of all components and cement\n   - Thorough debridement and lavage\n   - Immediate reimplantation of new prosthesis\n   - **Indications**:\n     - Known, sensitive organism\n     - Good soft tissue coverage\n     - Minimal bone loss\n     - No evidence of systemic sepsis\n     - Immunocompetent host\n   - **Advantages**: Single operation, faster recovery, lower cost\n   - **Success rate**: 75-90% when properly indicated\n\n2. **Two-stage revision**:\n   - First stage: Removal of all components, thorough debridement, placement of antibiotic-loaded cement spacer\n   - Interval: 6-12 weeks of targeted antibiotic therapy\n   - Second stage: Removal of spacer, further debridement, reimplantation of new prosthesis\n   - **Indications**:\n     - Unknown organism\n     - Resistant organisms (MRSA, fungi)\n     - Significant bone loss requiring reconstruction\n     - Compromised host (diabetes is relevant here)\n   - **Advantages**: Higher eradication rate for difficult infections\n   - **Success rate**: 80-95%\n   - **Spacer options**: Static vs. articulating (preserves motion and function between stages)\n\n3. **Salvage procedures** (if revision not feasible):\n   - Resection arthroplasty (Girdlestone for hip)\n   - Arthrodesis/fusion\n   - Above-knee amputation (rare, but may be necessary in life-threatening infections or failed multiple revisions)\n\nFor this 59-year-old diabetic patient, if DAIR is not appropriate, I would likely recommend a two-stage revision given:\n- The presence of diabetes as a comorbidity\n- The potential for more difficult-to-treat organisms in a diabetic patient\n- The need to ensure complete eradication of infection before reimplantation\n\nThe decision would be made in conjunction with microbiology/infectious disease specialists, considering the specific organism isolated, its antibiotic sensitivities, the patient's overall health status, and functional requirements.",
