        "answer": "Several diagnostic criteria exist for prosthetic joint infection (PJI). The most widely used are the Musculoskeletal Infection Society (MSIS) criteria, which were updated in the 2018 International Consensus Meeting (ICM).\n\n**2018 ICM Criteria for PJI:**\n\n**Major Criteria** (any one confirms PJI):\n1. Two positive cultures of the same organism\n2. Presence of a sinus tract communicating with the joint\n\n**Minor Criteria** with associated scores:\n\n**Preoperative:**\n- Elevated serum CRP (>10 mg/L) OR D-Dimer (>860 ng/mL): 2 points\n- Elevated serum ESR (>30 mm/hr): 1 point\n- Elevated synovial WBC count OR LE (++): 3 points\n- Positive synovial alpha-defensin: 3 points\n- Elevated synovial PMN% (>70%): 2 points\n- Elevated synovial CRP (>6.9 mg/L): 1 point\n\n**Intraoperative:**\n- Positive histology: 3 points\n- Positive purulence: 3 points\n- Single positive culture: 2 points\n\n**Score interpretation:**\n- ≥6: Infected\n- 2-5: Possibly infected\n- 0-1: Not infected\n\n**Applying to this case:**\n\nBased on the clinical presentation (pain, swelling, warmth, erythema, discharge, and fever), this patient likely has several points already:\n- If CRP >10 mg/L: 2 points\n- If ESR >30 mm/hr: 1 point\n- If joint aspiration shows elevated WBC or positive LE: 3 points\n- If synovial PMN% >70%: 2 points\n\nEven before culture results, the patient could easily score in the \"possibly infected\" or \"infected\" range based on clinical and laboratory findings.\n\n**Additional diagnostic considerations:**\n\n1. **Synovial fluid analysis thresholds for PJI:**\n   - WBC count >1,500-3,000 cells/μL (lower threshold for knee)\n   - PMN% >65-70%\n\n2. **Leukocyte esterase test** (using urine dipstick on synovial fluid):\n   - ++ result is highly suggestive of infection\n\n3. **Alpha-defensin test:**\n   - High sensitivity and specificity for PJI\n   - Less affected by prior antibiotic use than cultures\n\n4. **Sonication of removed implants:**\n   - Increases culture yield, especially in patients on antibiotics\n\n5. **PCR and next-generation sequencing:**\n   - May identify organisms in culture-negative cases\n\nIn this case, with the clinical presentation at 8 weeks post-op with 10 days of symptoms, I would proceed with urgent diagnostic workup including joint aspiration, blood tests, and imaging. Given the high clinical suspicion, I would prepare for surgical intervention (likely DAIR if criteria are met) while awaiting final culture results.",
