        "answer": "**Risk Factors for Prosthetic Joint Infection:**\n\n1. **Patient-related factors:**\n   - **Diabetes mellitus** (particularly if poorly controlled)\n   - Obesity (BMI >35)\n   - Rheumatoid arthritis and immunosuppressive medications\n   - Malnutrition (albumin <3.5 g/dL)\n   - Chronic kidney disease\n   - Liver cirrhosis\n   - Smoking\n   - Previous joint surgery or infection\n   - Malignancy\n   - Advanced age\n   - ASA score >2\n\n2. **Surgery-related factors:**\n   - Prolonged operative time (>2.5 hours)\n   - Simultaneous bilateral procedures\n   - Blood transfusion\n   - Wound complications (hematoma, prolonged drainage)\n   - Urinary tract infection\n\n3. **Postoperative factors:**\n   - Wound dehiscence\n   - Superficial surgical site infection\n   - Hematogenous seeding from distant infections\n\n**Management Modifications for a Diabetic Patient:**\n\n1. **Preoperative optimization** (if planning revision surgery):\n   - Strict glycemic control (target HbA1c <7% if possible)\n   - Nutritional assessment and optimization\n   - Screening for and treating distant infections\n\n2. **Perioperative considerations:**\n   - Meticulous glycemic control (target 140-180 mg/dL)\n   - Consider infectious disease consultation early\n   - More aggressive antibiotic prophylaxis (consider weight-based dosing)\n   - Careful soft tissue handling during surgery\n\n3. **Surgical approach:**\n   - Lower threshold for two-stage revision rather than DAIR or single-stage\n   - More extensive debridement of potentially compromised tissue\n   - Consider antibiotic-loaded cement with higher antibiotic concentrations\n   - Careful assessment of wound closure and consideration of plastic surgery input if soft tissues compromised\n\n4. **Antibiotic management:**\n   - Longer duration of IV antibiotics (4-6 weeks rather than 2-4)\n   - More frequent monitoring of renal function (diabetes + nephrotoxic antibiotics)\n   - Careful drug selection considering potential interactions with diabetes medications\n   - Lower threshold for suppressive antibiotics after acute treatment\n\n5. **Postoperative care:**\n   - More frequent follow-up\n   - Extended monitoring of inflammatory markers\n   - Aggressive management of wound complications\n   - Multidisciplinary approach involving endocrinology\n   - Careful foot care and prevention of other infections\n\n6. **Patient education:**\n   - Emphasis on glycemic control\n   - Vigilance for signs of recurrent infection\n   - Prophylactic antibiotics for invasive procedures\n\nIn this 59-year-old diabetic patient, I would have a lower threshold for two-stage revision if there are any concerning features (symptoms >4 weeks, implant loosening, difficult-to-treat organisms). I would involve infectious disease specialists early, consider extended antibiotic therapy, and ensure close monitoring of both the infection and diabetes control throughout treatment.",
