        "answer": "**Common Organisms in PJI:**\n\n1. **Gram-positive cocci (60-80% of cases):**\n   - **Staphylococcus aureus** (MSSA and MRSA): 20-30%\n   - **Coagulase-negative staphylococci** (especially S. epidermidis): 20-40%\n   - **Streptococcus** species: 10-15%\n   - **Enterococcus** species: 5-10%\n\n2. **Gram-negative bacilli (10-20%):**\n   - **Escherichia coli**\n   - **Pseudomonas aeruginosa**\n   - **Klebsiella** species\n   - **Proteus** species\n\n3. **Anaerobes (5-10%):**\n   - **Cutibacterium acnes** (formerly Propionibacterium acnes) - especially in shoulder arthroplasty\n   - **Peptostreptococcus** species\n   - **Bacteroides** species\n\n4. **Polymicrobial infections (10-15%)**\n\n5. **Fungi (<1%):**\n   - **Candida** species\n\n6. **Mycobacteria (rare):**\n   - **Mycobacterium tuberculosis**\n   - **Atypical mycobacteria**\n\n7. **Culture-negative infections (7-15%)**\n\n**Influence on Empiric Antibiotic Choice:**\n\n1. **Early post-operative infections** (like this case at 8 weeks):\n   - More likely to be caused by virulent organisms like S. aureus or gram-negative bacilli\n   - Higher risk of MRSA in healthcare-associated infections\n   - **Empiric coverage should include:**\n     - MRSA coverage (vancomycin, daptomycin, or linezolid)\n     - PLUS gram-negative coverage (piperacillin-tazobactam, cefepime, or a carbapenem)\n\n2. **Delayed/chronic infections:**\n   - More likely to involve less virulent organisms like coagulase-negative staphylococci or C. acnes\n   - **Empiric coverage should include:**\n     - Glycopeptide (vancomycin) or lipopeptide (daptomycin)\n     - Consider adding rifampicin for biofilm penetration once cultures confirm susceptible staphylococci\n\n3. **Special considerations for this diabetic patient:**\n   - Higher risk of gram-negative and polymicrobial infections\n   - Potentially more resistant organisms if previous antibiotic exposure\n   - **Empiric coverage should be broad:**\n     - Vancomycin (for MRSA and 