      {
        "answer": "**Key Components of the Multidisciplinary Approach to PJI:**\n\n1. **Core MDT Members:**\n   - **Orthopaedic surgeons:** Surgical planning and execution, overall coordination of care\n   - **Infectious disease specialists:** Antibiotic selection, duration, monitoring\n   - **Clinical microbiologists:** Culture interpretation, antibiotic susceptibility testing, biofilm considerations\n   - **Pharmacists:** Antibiotic stewardship, drug interactions, therapeutic drug monitoring\n\n2. **Extended MDT Members:**\n   - **Plastic surgeons:** For complex soft tissue coverage\n   - **Vascular surgeons:** For patients with peripheral vascular disease\n   - **Endocrinologists:** For diabetic patients (relevant to this case)\n   - **Geriatricians:** For elderly patients with multiple comorbidities\n   - **Physiotherapists and occupational therapists:** Rehabilitation planning\n   - **Pain specialists:** For complex pain management\n   - **Psychologists:** For patients with chronic pain and psychological impact\n\n3. **Diagnostic Collaboration:**\n   - **Radiology input:** Interpretation of complex imaging\n   - **Nuclear medicine:** For specialized scans (labeled leukocyte studies, FDG-PET)\n   - **Pathology:** Histological assessment of tissue samples\n   - **Specialized microbiology techniques:** Sonication, extended cultures, molecular methods\n\n4. **Treatment Planning:**\n   - **Regular MDT meetings** to discuss complex cases\n   - **Shared decision-making** regarding surgical approach (DAIR vs. revision)\n   - **Antibiotic planning:** Choice, duration, route, monitoring\n   - **Individualized risk assessment** based on patient factors\n\n5. **Perioperative Management:**\n   - **Anaesthesia team:** Risk assessment, perioperative management\n   - **Critical care:** For high-risk patients or those with sepsis\n   - **Wound care specialists:** For complex wound management\n\n6. **Long-term Follow-up:**\n   - **Coordinated monitoring** between orthopaedics and infectious diseases\n   - **Rehabilitation team:** Physiotherapy, occupational therapy\n   - **Primary care involvement:** For ongoing monitoring and support\n\n7. **For This Diabetic Patient:**\n   - **Endocrinology involvement** for glycemic control optimization\n   - **Renal team** if diabetic nephropathy present (affects antibiotic dosing)\n   - **Vascular assessment** if peripheral vascular disease suspected\n   - **Nutritional support** to optimize healing\n   - **Podiatry** for foot care and prevention of other diabetic complications\n\n8. **Organizational Aspects:**\n   - **Dedicated PJI clinic** for streamlined management\n   - **Care pathways** for standardized approach\n   - **Database/registry** for outcome tracking and quality improvement\n   - **Regular audit** of management and outcomes\n\n9. **Patient-Centered Approach:**\n   - **Patient education** about infection, treatment options, and prognosis\n   - **Shared decision-making** involving the patient in treatment choices\n   - **Psychological support** for chronic infection impact\n   - **Social services** if functional limitations affect independence\n\nThe success of PJI management depends on effective communication between these team members, with clear documentation, regular MDT discussions, and a coordinated approach to both surgical and medical management. For this 59-year-old diabetic patient, I would ensure early involvement of endocrinology for glycemic control optimization alongside the core PJI team.",
