    "examination_overview": "**Inspection**\n- Popeye deformity (distal migration of biceps muscle belly)\n- Ecchymosis\n- Muscle wasting\n- Comparison with contralateral side\n\n**Palpation**\n- Bicipital groove tenderness\n- Anterior shoulder tenderness\n- Assessment for associated AC joint pathology\n\n**Range of Movement**\n- Active and passive shoulder movements\n- Elbow flexion and supination strength\n\n**Special Tests**\n- Speed's test (biceps tendon)\n- Yergason's test (biceps tendon stability)\n- Hook test (distal biceps assessment)\n- Ludington's test (long head integrity)\n- Rotator cuff assessment (Jobe's, Hawkins, etc.)\n- O'Brien's test (SLAP lesion)\n\n**Neurovascular Assessment**\n- Distal pulses\n- Sensation in upper limb\n- Motor function of median, ulnar, radial nerves",
    "interview_questions": [
      {
        "answer": "The clinical presentation suggests a rupture of the long head of biceps tendon. The key features supporting this diagnosis include:\n\n- Sudden onset of pain with an audible/palpable 'pop' during exertion\n- Anterior shoulder pain\n- Visible deformity in the arm - likely a 'Popeye' deformity where the biceps muscle belly migrates distally\n- Occupational context involving heavy lifting\n\nThe long head of biceps tendon is particularly vulnerable at its origin at the supraglenoid tubercle and as it passes through the bicipital groove. In this 47-year-old patient, there may be underlying degenerative changes that predisposed to rupture during the acute loading event.\n\nIt's important to distinguish between proximal (more common) and distal biceps tendon ruptures, with the history and examination findings typically allowing differentiation. The proximal rupture typically presents with the characteristic Popeye deformity, while maintaining some elbow flexion strength due to the intact short head.",
        "question": "What do you see in this patient's clinical presentation and what is your primary diagnosis?"
