      {
        "answer": "I would perform a systematic examination:\n\n**Inspection:**\n- Compare both arms for asymmetry\n- Look specifically for the 'Popeye' deformity (distal migration of biceps muscle belly)\n- Assess for ecchymosis or swelling around the anterior shoulder\n- Observe overall posture and shoulder position\n\n**Palpation:**\n- Bicipital groove for tenderness and empty groove sign\n- Anterior shoulder structures\n- Assessment of associated structures (AC joint, rotator cuff insertions)\n\n**Range of Movement:**\n- Active and passive shoulder movements in all planes\n- Elbow flexion and supination strength testing (may be preserved due to intact short head)\n\n**Special Tests:**\n- Speed's test: arm extended, supinated and forward flexed against resistance\n- Yergason's test: elbow flexed to 90°, resistance to supination\n- Ludington's test: patient clasps hands behind head and alternately contracts biceps\n- Hook test: for distal biceps integrity\n\n**Rotator Cuff Assessment:**\n- Jobe's test (supraspinatus)\n- External rotation lag sign (infraspinatus)\n- Lift-off test (subscapularis)\n- Hawkins and Neer's impingement tests\n\n**SLAP Lesion Tests:**\n- O'Brien's active compression test\n- Anterior slide test\n\n**Neurovascular Examination:**\n- Distal pulses\n- Sensation in all dermatomes\n- Motor function of all major muscle groups\n\nThis comprehensive examination helps confirm the diagnosis and identify any associated pathology that may influence management decisions.",
        "question": "How would you examine this patient?"
