      {
        "answer": "For suspected biceps tendon rupture, I would request:\n\n**Initial Imaging:**\n- Plain radiographs of the shoulder (AP, lateral, axillary views)\n  - While not diagnostic for soft tissue injuries, these help rule out bony pathology\n  - May show associated findings like subacromial spurs or glenohumeral arthritis\n  - Can reveal subtle signs like the 'teardrop sign' (fluid in empty bicipital groove)\n\n**Ultrasound:**\n- Excellent first-line investigation for biceps pathology\n- Advantages:\n  - Dynamic assessment possible\n  - Non-invasive and relatively inexpensive\n  - Can visualize tendon discontinuity and retraction\n  - Can assess for fluid in bicipital groove\n  - Allows assessment of rotator cuff integrity\n\n**MRI Shoulder:**\n- Gold standard for definitive diagnosis\n- Provides detailed assessment of:\n  - Biceps tendon origin, course, and integrity\n  - Associated rotator cuff pathology (present in up to 90% of cases)\n  - SLAP lesions and other labral pathology\n  - Bicipital groove morphology\n  - Muscle quality and fatty infiltration\n\n**MR Arthrogram:**\n- Consider if SLAP lesion or intra-articular pathology suspected\n- Particularly useful in younger patients with suspected concomitant labral tears\n\n**Blood Tests:**\n- Not routinely required for traumatic ruptures\n- Consider inflammatory markers (ESR, CRP) if inflammatory arthropathy suspected\n- Thyroid function if risk factors present (thyroid disease associated with tendinopathy)\n\nThe choice between ultrasound and MRI often depends on local availability, cost considerations, and whether there is suspicion of associated pathology requiring surgical intervention.",
        "question": "What investigations would you request for this patient?"
