      {
        "answer": "Management of biceps tendon rupture should be tailored to the patient's age, activity level, occupation, and functional demands:\n\n**Initial Management:**\n1. Analgesia: Regular paracetamol, NSAIDs if not contraindicated\n2. Ice therapy: 20 minutes, 3-4 times daily for first 48-72 hours\n3. Activity modification: Avoid heavy lifting and overhead activities\n4. Temporary sling for comfort if needed\n\n**Conservative Management:**\nThis is appropriate for many patients, particularly:\n- Older patients (>60 years)\n- Sedentary individuals\n- Non-dominant arm involvement\n- Minimal functional deficit\n- Patients with significant comorbidities\n\nConservative approach includes:\n- Structured physiotherapy program focusing on:\n  - Rotator cuff strengthening\n  - Scapular stabilization\n  - Graduated return to activities\n- Expected outcomes: 70-80% good functional results with minimal strength deficit\n\n**Surgical Management:**\nIndications include:\n- Young, active patients (<50-55 years)\n- Athletes or manual laborers\n- Dominant arm (as in this postal worker)\n- Significant functional deficit\n- Associated pathology requiring repair\n\nSurgical options:\n1. **Tenotomy**: Simple release of remaining tendon\n   - Quicker recovery, less rehabilitation\n   - Suitable for older, less active patients\n   - May result in cosmetic deformity and mild supination weakness\n\n2. **Tenodesis**: Reattachment of tendon to proximal humerus\n   - Preserves length-tension relationship\n   - Reduces cosmetic deformity\n   - Better strength preservation\n   - Preferred in younger, active patients\n\n3. **Approach options**:\n   - Arthroscopic (less invasive, faster recovery)\n   - Open (better for complex cases)\n\n**For this 47-year-old postal worker with dominant arm involvement:**\nI would likely recommend surgical tenodesis given her age, occupation requiring lifting, and dominant arm involvement. I would discuss both conservative and surgical options, explaining the pros and cons of each approach, including recovery timelines and return to work expectations.",
