      {
        "answer": "**Complications of Untreated Biceps Tendon Rupture:**\n\n1. **Functional deficits:**\n   - Mild weakness in elbow flexion (approximately 8-15% reduction)\n   - More significant weakness in supination (up to 20-30%)\n   - Endurance deficits with repetitive activities\n\n2. **Cosmetic deformity:**\n   - Persistent 'Popeye' deformity due to distal migration of muscle belly\n   - May be psychologically distressing for some patients\n\n3. **Chronic pain:**\n   - Mechanical symptoms from tendon stump\n   - Associated rotator cuff or labral pathology may cause ongoing symptoms\n\n4. **Occupational impact:**\n   - Difficulty returning to manual labor or overhead activities\n   - Potential need for job modification or retraining\n\n**Complications of Conservative Management:**\n\n1. **Incomplete functional recovery**\n2. **Persistent deformity**\n3. **Delayed return to high-demand activities**\n\n**Complications of Surgical Management:**\n\n1. **General surgical complications:**\n   - Infection (1-2%)\n   - Neurovascular injury (musculocutaneous nerve at risk)\n   - Anesthetic risks\n   - DVT/PE (rare in upper limb surgery)\n\n2. **Tenotomy-specific complications:**\n   - Cosmetic deformity (Popeye sign)\n   - Cramping with activity\n   - Supination weakness\n\n3. **Tenodesis-specific complications:**\n   - Tenodesis failure/re-rupture (5-10%)\n   - Humeral fracture (rare)\n   - Persistent bicipital groove pain\n   - Complex regional pain syndrome (rare)\n\n4. **Approach-specific complications:**\n   - Arthroscopic: technical difficulty, incomplete treatment\n   - Open: larger scar, longer recovery\n\n5. **Rehabilitation complications:**\n   - Stiffness\n   - Adhesive capsulitis\n   - Persistent weakness\n\nThe literature suggests that surgical management (particularly tenodesis) in appropriate patients provides better cosmetic results, improved supination strength, and higher patient satisfaction compared to conservative management, but carries the inherent risks of surgical intervention. The decision should be individualized based on patient factors, expectations, and functional demands.",
