      {
        "answer": "**Biceps Brachii Anatomy:**\n\n**Origins:**\n- **Long head:** Supraglenoid tubercle and superior labrum\n  - Intra-articular course through the glenohumeral joint\n  - Passes through the bicipital groove (intertubercular sulcus)\n- **Short head:** Coracoid process of scapula\n  - Extra-articular course\n\n**Insertion:**\n- Bicipital tuberosity of radius\n- Bicipital aponeurosis to deep fascia of forearm\n\n**Function:**\n- Elbow flexion (both heads)\n- Supination of forearm (stronger when elbow flexed)\n- Long head: secondary stabilizer of humeral head\n- Short head: weak shoulder flexor\n\n**Blood Supply:**\n- Anterior circumflex humeral artery\n- Relatively hypovascular zone at the bicipital groove\n\n**Innervation:**\n- Musculocutaneous nerve (C5-C6)\n\n**Relationship to Patterns of Injury:**\n\n1. **Proximal Ruptures (96% of biceps ruptures):**\n   - Most commonly affects the long head at two locations:\n     a) **Origin at supraglenoid tubercle/superior labrum (SLAP region)**\n        - Associated with SLAP tears and overhead athletes\n     b) **Bicipital groove**\n        - Associated with stenosis, osteophytes, or degenerative changes\n   - Predisposing factors:\n     - Age-related degeneration (most common in 40-60 year olds)\n     - Impingement syndrome\n     - Rotator cuff tears (present in up to 90% of cases)\n     - Bicipital groove stenosis or irregularity\n     - Inflammatory arthropathies\n\n2. **Distal Ruptures (4% of biceps ruptures):**\n   - Typically occur at insertion on radial tuberosity\n   - More common in middle-aged men (40-60 years)\n   - Often associated with eccentric loading during elbow flexion\n   - Risk factors include smoking, steroid use, and fluoroquinolone antibiotics\n\n3. **Biceps Brachii Muscle Belly Tears:**\n   - Rare\n   - Usually traumatic in young athletes\n\n**Clinical Implications:**\n\n- The intra-articular course of the long head makes it vulnerable to degenerative changes and synovial fluid exposure\n- The hypovascular zone at the bicipital groove is a common site for degenerative rupture\n- The dual origin explains why function is partially preserved after isolated long head rupture\n- The anatomical relationship to the rotator cuff explains the high association with rotator cuff pathology\n- The biomechanical function in supination explains why this movement is more affected than elbow flexion after rupture\n\nUnderstanding this anatomy guides both diagnosis and treatment decisions, particularly regarding the need for surgical intervention and the choice between tenotomy and tenodesis.",
