      {
        "answer": "**Return to Work Planning for a Postal Worker with Biceps Tendon Rupture:**\n\n**Assessment Factors:**\n1. **Job demands analysis:**\n   - Weight of parcels typically handled\n   - Frequency of lifting/carrying\n   - Duration of shifts\n   - Need for overhead activities\n   - Delivery method (walking, driving)\n\n2. **Treatment pathway chosen:**\n   - Conservative management: typically 6-12 weeks for return to full duties\n   - Surgical management: typically 3-6 months for return to full duties\n\n3. **Dominant vs. non-dominant arm involvement:**\n   - Dominant arm (as in this case) typically requires more extensive rehabilitation\n\n4. **Associated pathology:**\n   - Presence of rotator cuff tears may prolong recovery\n\n**Staged Return to Work Plan:**\n\n**Conservative Management Pathway:**\n\n1. **Acute Phase (0-2 weeks):**\n   - Complete work restriction or administrative duties only\n   - Focus on pain control and protected movement\n\n2. **Early Rehabilitation (2-6 weeks):**\n   - Light duties with strict lifting restrictions (<2-5kg)\n   - Avoid overhead activities\n   - Consider reduced hours (4-6 hours/day)\n   - Workplace modifications (e.g., sorting mail at waist height)\n\n3. **Intermediate Rehabilitation (6-12 weeks):**\n   - Progressive increase in lifting capacity\n   - Graduated return to normal hours\n   - Introduction of overhead activities as tolerated\n\n4. **Advanced Rehabilitation (12+ weeks):**\n   - Return to full duties with ongoing monitoring\n   - Continuation of strengthening program\n\n**Surgical Management Pathway:**\n\n1. **Post-operative Phase (0-6 weeks):**\n   - Work restriction or administrative duties only\n   - Adherence to post-operative restrictions\n   - Protected range of motion as per protocol\n\n2. **Early Rehabilitation (6-12 weeks):**\n   - Light duties with strict lifting restrictions (<2-5kg)\n   - Avoid overhead activities\n   - Reduced hours as tolerated\n\n3. **Intermediate Rehabilitation (12-16 weeks):**\n   - Progressive increase in lifting capacity\n   - Graduated return to normal hours\n   - Introduction of job-specific tasks\n\n4. **Advanced Rehabilitation (16-24 weeks):**\n   - Progressive return to full duties\n   - Job-specific conditioning\n\n**Multidisciplinary Approach:**\n\n1. **Occupational therapy input:**\n   - Workplace assessment\n   - Ergonomic modifications\n   - Task adaptation strategies\n\n2. **Physiotherapy:**\n   - Work-hardening program\n   - Job-specific rehabilitation exercises\n   - Functional capacity evaluations\n\n3. **Employer engagement:**\n   - Regular communication with line managers\n   - Temporary redeployment options\n   - Graduated return to work planning\n\n4. **Documentation and monitoring:**\n   - Clear documentation of restrictions\n   - Regular review of progress\n   - Adjustment of plan based on clinical progress\n\nThis structured approach maximizes the chances of successful return to work while minimizing the risk of re-injury or development of chronic symptoms.",
