        "answer": "**Evidence Base for Management of Proximal Biceps Tendon Ruptures:**\n\nThe literature on proximal biceps tendon ruptures shows varying levels of evidence, with few high-quality randomized controlled trials. Here's a summary of the current evidence:\n\n**Non-surgical Management:**\n\n1. **Observational studies:**\n   - Multiple case series demonstrate satisfactory outcomes with conservative management\n   - Gilcreest (1926) first described good functional outcomes with non-operative treatment\n   - Mariani et al. (1988) reported 70-76% good/excellent results with conservative management\n\n2. **Functional outcomes:**\n   - Strength deficits: 8-21% reduction in elbow flexion, 15-40% reduction in supination\n   - Most studies show minimal impact on activities of daily living\n   - Greater impact on high-demand activities and manual labor\n\n3. **Patient satisfaction:**\n   - Generally high (70-80%) in appropriate patient populations\n   - Cosmetic deformity is the most common complaint\n\n**Surgical Management:**\n\n1. **Tenotomy vs. Tenodesis:**\n   - Slenker et al. (2012) meta-analysis: similar patient satisfaction (74% vs. 77%)\n   - Tenodesis showed lower rates of cosmetic deformity (8% vs. 43%)\n   - Tenodesis demonstrated better preservation of elbow flexion and supination strength\n\n2. **Technique comparison:**\n   - Arthroscopic vs. open: comparable functional outcomes\n   - Arthroscopic: less post-operative pain, faster recovery\n   - Open: potentially lower technical failure rate\n\n3. **Fixation methods:**\n   - Interference screws, suture anchors, and soft tissue tenodesis all show good outcomes\n   - No clear superiority of one fixation method over others\n\n**Comparative Studies:**\n\n1. **Randomized trials:**\n   - Limited high-quality RCTs comparing conservative vs. surgical management\n   - Zhang et al. (2015): surgical management showed better Constant scores and strength\n\n2. **Systematic reviews:**\n   - Frost et al. (2009): insufficient evidence to recommend routine surgical repair\n   - Slenker et al. (2012): surgical management may benefit younger, active patients\n\n**Evidence-Based Recommendations:**\n\n1. **Conservative management appropriate for:**\n   - Older patients (>60 years)\n   - Low physical demands\n   - Non-dominant arm\n   - Significant comorbidities\n   - Patient preference after informed discussion\n\n2. **Surgical management (tenodesis) may benefit:**\n   - Younger patients (<50-55 years)\n   - High physical demands (athletes, manual laborers)\n   - Dominant arm involvement\n   - Cosmetic concerns\n   - Associated pathology requiring repair\n\n3. **Tenotomy may be preferred in:**\n   - Elderly patients requiring surgery\n   - Significant comorbidities limiting rehabilitation\n   - When rapid recovery is prioritized\n\nThe current evidence suggests an individualized approach based on patient factors, with shared decision-making after thorough discussion of the risks, benefits, and expected outcomes of each approach. For this 47-year-old postal worker with dominant arm involvement, the literature would support consideration of surgical tenodesis, though a trial of conservative management remains a reasonable option.",
