        "answer": "My investigation approach would be comprehensive and urgent:\n\n**Immediate Investigations:**\n\n1. **Joint aspiration** - The gold standard diagnostic test\n   - Send for cell count (WCC >50,000 cells/mm³ with >90% neutrophils suggests septic arthritis)\n   - Gram stain and culture (positive in 60-80% of cases)\n   - Crystal analysis to rule out gout/pseudogout\n   - Glucose (lower in joint fluid than serum in infection)\n\n2. **Blood tests:**\n   - Full blood count (looking for leukocytosis)\n   - CRP and ESR (elevated inflammatory markers)\n   - Blood cultures (positive in 40-50% of septic arthritis cases)\n   - Urea and electrolytes (baseline renal function before antibiotics)\n   - Glucose and HbA1c (assess diabetic control)\n   - Uric acid (if considering gout)\n\n3. **Imaging:**\n   - Plain radiographs (AP and lateral knee) - may be normal initially but rule out other pathology\n   - Ultrasound - can confirm effusion and guide aspiration if needed\n   - MRI - if osteomyelitis is suspected or diagnosis unclear\n\n**Additional Investigations to Consider:**\n\n- Screening for distant sources of infection (urine, dental, respiratory)\n- Echocardiogram if endocarditis suspected\n- Specific PCR tests for gonococci or tuberculosis if clinically indicated\n\nThe key principle is not to delay treatment while waiting for all results. Joint aspiration and blood cultures should be obtained before starting antibiotics, but antibiotics should not be delayed if there will be significant time until surgical intervention.",
