        "answer": "Management of suspected septic arthritis requires urgent, aggressive intervention:\n\n**Immediate Management:**\n\n1. **Resuscitation and stabilization**\n   - IV access, fluid resuscitation if signs of sepsis\n   - Analgesia (titrated opioids often required)\n   - Optimize diabetic control (may need IV insulin sliding scale)\n\n2. **Joint aspiration**\n   - Performed urgently before antibiotics if possible\n   - Send samples as previously outlined\n\n3. **Empirical IV antibiotics**\n   - Start immediately after cultures obtained\n   - Following local guidelines, typically:\n     - Flucloxacillin 2g IV QDS + Gentamicin (dose based on weight/renal function)\n     - Alternative if penicillin-allergic: Clindamycin 600mg IV QDS or Vancomycin\n   - Cover for MRSA if risk factors present\n\n4. **Urgent surgical intervention**\n   - Arrange emergency arthroscopic washout (or open arthrotomy)\n   - Multiple tissue samples for microbiology\n   - Thorough lavage with at least 10L of fluid\n   - Potential for repeat washout if inadequate clinical response\n\n**Ongoing Management:**\n\n1. **Antibiotic therapy**\n   - Continue IV antibiotics for at least 2 weeks\n   - Adjust based on culture results and microbiology advice\n   - Consider switch to oral antibiotics when clinically improving with normalizing inflammatory markers\n   - Total duration typically 4-6 weeks\n\n2. **Monitoring**\n   - Daily clinical assessment\n   - Serial inflammatory markers (CRP, WCC)\n   - Blood glucose monitoring\n   - Consider repeat aspiration if not improving\n\n3. **Multidisciplinary approach**\n   - Microbiology input for antibiotic management\n   - Diabetic team involvement\n   - Early physiotherapy to prevent stiffness\n   - Infectious disease consultation if complex\n\n4. **Thromboprophylaxis**\n   - LMWH while immobile\n\nThe key principle is that septic arthritis requires both surgical drainage and appropriate antibiotics - neither alone is sufficient treatment.",
