        "answer": "**Common Causative Organisms in Adult Septic Arthritis:**\n\n1. **Staphylococcus aureus** (40-60% of cases)\n   - Most common overall pathogen\n   - Including MRSA in at-risk populations\n\n2. **Streptococcus species** (15-20%)\n   - Group A, B, C, G streptococci\n   - Streptococcus pneumoniae\n\n3. **Gram-negative organisms** (10-20%)\n   - More common in elderly, immunocompromised patients\n   - E. coli, Pseudomonas, Proteus, Klebsiella\n\n4. **Neisseria gonorrhoeae**\n   - More common in sexually active young adults\n   - Often associated with migratory polyarthralgia\n\n5. **Less common organisms:**\n   - Mycobacterium tuberculosis\n   - Fungal infections (immunocompromised)\n   - Borrelia (Lyme disease)\n   - Brucella\n\n**Influence on Antibiotic Choice:**\n\n1. **Empirical therapy** should cover the most likely organisms based on patient factors:\n   - For this diabetic patient: Flucloxacillin + Gentamicin to cover Staph aureus and potential gram negatives\n   - If MRSA risk: Add Vancomycin or Teicoplanin\n   - If immunocompromised: Broader spectrum with anti-pseudomonal coverage\n\n2. **Patient factors affecting choice:**\n   - Diabetes increases risk of gram-negative and S. aureus infections\n   - IV drug use: consider MRSA coverage\n   - Sexual history: consider gonococcal coverage (ceftriaxone)\n   - Recent hospitalization: consider resistant organisms\n   - Prosthetic joint: consider coagulase-negative staphylococci\n\n3. **Local resistance patterns** should guide empirical therapy\n   - Hospital antibiotic guidelines reflect local resistance\n\n4. **De-escalation** once culture results available\n   - Narrow spectrum based on sensitivities\n   - Switch to oral when clinically improving and CRP trending down\n\n5. **Duration of therapy:**\n   - Typically 2-4 weeks IV followed by 2-4 weeks oral\n   - Longer courses for specific organisms (e.g., tuberculosis)\n\nThe key principle is to start broad and narrow based on culture results, while ensuring adequate coverage for the most likely pathogens based on patient risk factors.",
