        "answer": "**Complications of Septic Arthritis:**\n\n1. **Local Complications:**\n\n   a) **Cartilage destruction**\n   - Occurs rapidly (within 24-48 hours)\n   - Bacterial enzymes and host inflammatory response damage cartilage\n   - Recognition: Persistent pain, reduced range of movement\n   - Management: Early surgical intervention, appropriate antibiotics, physiotherapy\n\n   b) **Osteomyelitis**\n   - Infection spreads to adjacent bone\n   - Recognition: Persistent pain, abnormal bone signal on MRI, elevated inflammatory markers despite treatment\n   - Management: Extended antibiotic course (6-12 weeks), possible further surgical debridement\n\n   c) **Joint stiffness and contractures**\n   - Due to inflammatory process and immobility\n   - Recognition: Reduced range of movement during recovery\n   - Management: Early physiotherapy, continuous passive motion, splinting\n\n   d) **Secondary osteoarthritis**\n   - Long-term consequence of cartilage damage\n   - Recognition: Persistent pain, radiographic changes months/years later\n   - Management: Standard OA management, possible arthroplasty if severe\n\n2. **Systemic Complications:**\n\n   a) **Sepsis/septic shock**\n   - Recognition: Hypotension, tachycardia, altered mental status, reduced urine output\n   - Management: Fluid resuscitation, vasopressors if needed, source control, broad-spectrum antibiotics\n\n   b) **Acute kidney injury**\n   - Due to sepsis or nephrotoxic antibiotics\n   - Recognition: Rising creatinine, reduced urine output\n   - Management: Fluid optimization, drug dose adjustment, renal replacement therapy if severe\n\n   c) **Endocarditis**\n   - Particularly with S. aureus bacteremia\n   - Recognition: New murmur, persistent bacteremia, embolic phenomena\n   - Management: Echocardiography, extended antibiotic course, possible valve surgery\n\n   d) **Metastatic abscesses**\n   - Spread of infection to other sites\n   - Recognition: New focal symptoms, imaging findings\n   - Management: Drainage if accessible, extended antibiotics\n\n3. **Treatment-related Complications:**\n\n   a) **Antibiotic side effects**\n   - C. difficile colitis, allergic reactions, nephrotoxicity\n   - Recognition: Diarrhea, rash, rising creatinine\n   - Management: Antibiotic change, supportive care\n\n   b) **Surgical complications**\n   - Bleeding, wound infection, neurovascular injury\n   - Recognition: Post-operative assessment\n   - Management: Return to theater if needed\n\n**Monitoring for Complications:**\n\n1. Regular clinical assessment of the joint (pain, swelling, movement)\n2. Serial inflammatory markers (CRP, WCC)\n3. Renal function monitoring\n4. Daily systemic assessment\n5. Follow-up imaging if inadequate response\n\n**Prevention Strategies:**\n\n1. Early diagnosis and treatment\n2. Adequate surgical debridement\n3. Appropriate antibiotic selection and duration\n4. Early mobilization and physiotherapy\n5. Optimization of comorbidities (especially diabetes control)\n\nThe mortality rate from septic arthritis remains 10-15%, particularly in elderly patients with comorbidities, highlighting the importance of prompt recognition and management of complications.",
