        "answer": "Differentiating septic arthritis from crystal arthropathy is crucial but challenging as they can present similarly and occasionally coexist (5-10% of cases).\n\n**Clinical Differentiation:**\n\n1. **History:**\n   - **Septic arthritis:**\n     - Rapid onset (hours to days)\n     - Single joint (monoarticular) in 80-90% of cases\n     - No previous episodes (unless recurrent risk factors)\n     - Often associated with fever and systemic symptoms\n   \n   - **Crystal arthropathy:**\n     - May have previous similar episodes\n     - Can be precipitated by specific triggers (alcohol, foods, medications)\n     - Gout typically affects 1st MTP joint, ankles, knees\n     - Pseudogout typically affects knees, wrists, shoulders\n\n2. **Examination:**\n   - **Septic arthritis:**\n     - Extreme tenderness, often cannot tolerate any movement\n     - Significant warmth and erythema extending beyond joint\n     - Patient appears systemically unwell\n   \n   - **Crystal arthropathy:**\n     - May have tophi (gout)\n     - Often less systemically unwell\n     - May have involvement of typical joints\n\n**Investigations:**\n\n1. **Joint aspiration** - The gold standard for differentiation:\n   - **Septic arthritis:**\n     - WCC >50,000 cells/mm³ (typically 100,000+)\n     - >90% neutrophils\n     - Positive Gram stain (60-80% sensitive)\n     - Positive culture (80-90% sensitive if not on antibiotics)\n     - Low glucose compared to serum\n   \n   - **Crystal arthropathy:**\n     - WCC typically 15,000-50,000 cells/mm³\n     - Presence of negatively birefringent crystals (gout)\n     - Positively birefringent crystals (pseudogout/CPPD)\n     - Sterile culture\n\n2. **Blood tests:**\n   - **Septic arthritis:**\n     - Higher CRP and WCC typically\n     - Positive blood cultures in 40-50%\n   \n   - **Crystal arthropathy:**\n     - Elevated uric acid in gout (but can be normal during acute attack)\n     - Less marked systemic inflammatory response\n\n3. **Imaging: