        "answer": "**BOAST Guidelines** (British Orthopaedic Association Standards for Trauma) for open fractures outline the following key principles:\n\n1. **Antibiotics** - Administer as soon as possible, ideally within 1 hour of injury\n   - I would give Co-amoxiclav 1.2g IV (or Cefuroxime 1.5g + Metronidazole 500mg IV if penicillin allergic)\n\n2. **Wound management** - Photograph, cover with sterile dressing, avoid exploring in ED\n   - I would ensure the wound is photographed, then covered with saline-soaked gauze and sterile dressing\n\n3. **Splintage** - Reduce gross deformity and splint the limb\n   - I would apply an above-knee backslab to stabilize the fracture\n\n4. **Tetanus prophylaxis** - Administer as appropriate\n   - I would check tetanus status and update if needed\n\n5. **Timing of surgery** - Debridement within 24 hours of injury\n   - For this contaminated wound, I would aim for surgery within 12 hours if possible\n\n6. **Combined orthoplastic approach** - For complex injuries (IIIB/C)\n   - I would involve plastic surgery at presentation for this case\n\n7. **Definitive skeletal stabilization** - At time of debridement or within 72 hours\n   - Options include external fixation, intramedullary nailing, or plate fixation depending on fracture pattern\n\n8. **Definitive soft tissue coverage** - Within 72 hours of injury\n   - For this case, primary closure, local flap, or free flap may be needed depending on wound assessment\n\n9. **Rehabilitation** - Early involvement of physiotherapy and rehabilitation team\n\nImplementation in this case:\n- Immediate IV antibiotics and tetanus prophylaxis\n- Photograph wound, apply sterile dressing, and splint\n- Urgent discussion with consultant and plastic surgery\n- Arrange emergency theatre for debridement and stabilization\n- Plan for definitive soft tissue coverage within 72 hours\n- Early physiotherapy involvement post-operatively",
