        "answer": "**Options for skeletal stabilization** in open tibial fractures include:\n\n1. **External fixation**\n   - **Temporary**: Damage control approach allowing wound access and soft tissue recovery\n   - **Definitive**: Frame completion with possible conversion to circular frame for complex fractures\n\n2. **Intramedullary (IM) nailing**\n   - Reamed or unreamed techniques\n   - Can be primary definitive treatment in selected cases\n\n3. **Plate fixation**\n   - Less commonly used in open fractures\n   - May be appropriate for certain fracture patterns (articular involvement)\n\n4. **Combined techniques**\n   - External fixation followed by delayed IM nailing or plating\n   - Temporary external fixation with planned conversion\n\n**Factors influencing my decision:**\n\n1. **Fracture pattern and location**\n   - Diaphyseal, metaphyseal, or intra-articular involvement\n   - Comminution and bone loss\n   - Segmental fractures may benefit from IM nailing\n\n2. **Soft tissue status**\n   - Gustilo-Anderson grade (higher grades may favor external fixation initially)\n   - Location and extent of wounds\n   - Need for plastic surgical procedures\n\n3. **Contamination level**\n   - Heavily contaminated wounds increase infection risk with internal fixation\n\n4. **Vascular status**\n   - Presence of vascular injury may necessitate external fixation initially\n\n5. **Patient factors**\n   - Age, bone quality, comorbidities\n   - Functional demands (this patient is a firefighter with high physical demands)\n\n6. **Surgeon experience and preference**\n\n7. **Available resources and implants**\n\n**Evidence-based approach:**\n- The LEAP study showed similar functional outcomes between IM nailing and external fixation for Grade III open fractures\n- Recent evidence suggests primary IM nailing is safe in Grade IIIA injuries with appropriate soft tissue management\n- For Grade IIIB/C injuries, temporary external fixation followed by definitive fixation after soft tissue coverage is often preferred\n\nFor this 39-year-old firefighter with likely Grade IIIA/B injury, I would consider:\n- Initial spanning external fixation if significant contamination or soft tissue injury\n- Possible conversion to IM nail once soft tissues permit\n- Discussing with plastic surgery colleagues regarding timing of definitive fixation relative to soft tissue coverage",
