        "answer": "**Seddon Classification (1943):**\n- **Neuropraxia:** Temporary conduction block without axonal disruption\n  * Preserved nerve continuity with local myelin damage\n  * Complete recovery expected within weeks to months\n  * Management: Observation, splinting, therapy\n\n- **Axonotmesis:** Axonal disruption with preserved endoneurium\n  * Wallerian degeneration occurs distal to injury\n  * Recovery occurs at 1-2mm/day as axons regenerate within intact endoneurial tubes\n  * Management: Usually non-operative, but may require exploration if no recovery\n\n- **Neurotmesis:** Complete nerve transection\n  * No spontaneous recovery\n  * Management: Surgical exploration and repair\n\n**Sunderland Classification (1951)** - More detailed 5-degree system:\n- **1st degree:** Equivalent to neuropraxia\n- **2nd degree:** Equivalent to axonotmesis\n- **3rd degree:** Disruption of axons and endoneurium, intact perineurium\n- **4th degree:** Disruption of axons, endoneurium, and perineurium, intact epineurium\n- **5th degree:** Complete transection (neurotmesis)\n\n**Management implications:**\n- In this case of a deep laceration with immediate sensory and motor loss, neurotmesis (Seddon) or 5th degree Sunderland injury is likely\n- This requires urgent surgical exploration and primary repair\n- BSSH guidelines recommend early exploration (ideally within 24-72 hours) for clean lacerations with suspected nerve injury\n- Delayed repair (>3 weeks) is associated with poorer outcomes due to nerve retraction, scarring, and muscle atrophy\n- The surgical approach depends on the nerve gap - primary neurorrhaphy for tension-free repair or nerve grafting for larger gaps\n- Postoperative rehabilitation protocols differ based on injury severity, with more intensive sensory re-education for higher-grade injuries",
