      {
        "answer": "The surgical options depend on several factors including the extent of nerve damage, size of the gap, and timing of surgery:\n\n**1. Primary Neurorrhaphy:**\n- Direct end-to-end repair of the nerve ends\n- Indicated when tension-free approximation is possible (typically gaps <1cm)\n- Technique:\n  * Microscopic magnification (2.5-4.0x loupes or microscope)\n  * Epineurial repair with 8-0 or 9-0 nylon sutures\n  * Minimal handling of nerve ends\n  * Proper fascicular alignment\n- Advantages: Best functional outcomes, single-stage procedure\n\n**2. Nerve Grafting:**\n- Indicated when primary repair would create tension (gaps >1cm)\n- Autologous options:\n  * Sural nerve (most common)\n  * Medial antebrachial cutaneous nerve\n  * Lateral antebrachial cutaneous nerve\n- Technique:\n  * Reversed graft orientation to minimize fascicular mismatch\n  * Multiple cable grafts to match diameter of median nerve\n  * Tension-free repair at both ends\n\n**3. Nerve Conduits/Allograft:**\n- Synthetic or processed nerve conduits\n- Typically used for smaller sensory nerves or short gaps (<3cm)\n- Limited evidence for use in major mixed nerves like median nerve\n\n**4. End-to-Side Repair:**\n- Distal stump sutured to side of adjacent intact nerve\n- Considered when proximal stump unavailable\n- Limited functional outcomes\n\n**5. Nerve Transfers:**\n- Transfer of redundant motor nerve to distal median nerve\n- Examples: Branch of ulnar nerve to thenar branch of median nerve\n- Considered for proximal injuries or delayed presentation\n\n**For this specific case:**\n- Given the acute presentation and forearm-level injury, I would plan for exploration and primary neurorrhaphy if possible\n- If a significant gap is encountered, sural nerve grafting would be my next choice\n- Associated tendon injuries would be repaired during the same procedure\n- The patient requires realistic counseling about outcomes, as even with optimal repair, recovery will be incomplete and prolonged",
