        "answer": "Post-operative rehabilitation following median nerve repair requires a comprehensive, phased approach:\n\n**Phase 1: Protection (0-3 weeks)**\n\n1. **Immobilization:**\n   - Wrist splinted in 20-30° flexion\n   - MCP joints in 40-60° flexion\n   - IP joints extended\n   - Duration: 3 weeks for neurorrhaphy, up to 6 weeks for nerve grafts\n\n2. **Edema management:**\n   - Elevation\n   - Compression garments\n   - Manual lymphatic drainage\n\n3. **Adjacent joint mobilization:**\n   - Gentle active and passive ROM of uninvolved joints\n   - Shoulder and elbow exercises to prevent stiffness\n\n**Phase 2: Early Mobilization (3-6 weeks)**\n\n1. **Controlled mobilization:**\n   - Transition to dynamic or static progressive splinting\n   - Gradual increase in wrist extension\n   - Protected active motion within pain limits\n\n2. **Tendon gliding exercises:**\n   - If concomitant tendon repairs, follow appropriate tendon protocol\n   - Differential tendon gliding to prevent adhesions\n\n3. **Scar management:**\n   - Silicone gel sheets\n   - Scar massage once wound healed\n   - Desensitization for hypersensitive scars\n\n**Phase 3: Reinnervation (6 weeks-6 months)**\n\n1. **Sensory reeducation:**\n   - Begins as soon as some sensation returns\n   - Localization exercises\n   - Texture discrimination\n   - Constant and moving touch discrimination\n\n2. **Motor reeducation:**\n   - Electrical stimulation (controversial but may prevent muscle atrophy)\n   - Mirror therapy\n   - Specific exercises for median-innervated muscles\n   - EMG biofeedback\n\n3. **Functional activities:**\n   - Graded activity program\n   - Compensatory techniques for ADLs\n   - Adaptive equipment as needed\n\n**Phase 4: Strengthening and Return to Function (6-24 months)**\n\n1. **Progressive strengthening:**\n   - Putty exercises\n   - Graded resistance training\n   - Focus on precision grip and opposition\n\n2. **Work hardening:**\n   - Task-specific training\n   - Simulated work activities\n   - Gradual increase in duration and intensity\n\n3. **Ongoing sensory reeducation:**\n   - Refinement of discriminative sensation\n   - Blindfolded object recognition\n   - Tactile gnosis training\n\n**Special Considerations:**\n\n- **Cortical reorganization:** Early sensory reeducation helps prevent cortical remapping\n- **Patient education:** Setting realistic expectations about recovery timeline\n- **Psychological support:** Addressing anxiety, depression, and body image concerns\n- **Regular assessment:** Using standardized measures (DASH, MHQ, Semmes-Weinstein monofilaments)\n- **Cold intolerance management:** Desensitization techniques, protective strategies\n\nThe rehabilitation program must be individualized based on the specific injury pattern, associated injuries, and patient factors. Close collaboration between surgeon, therapist, and patient is essential for optimizing outcomes.",
