        "answer": "Complications following median nerve injury and repair can be categorized into early and late complications:\n\n**Early Complications (0-6 weeks):**\n\n1. **Wound-related:**\n   - Infection (2-5% incidence)\n   - Dehiscence\n   - Hematoma formation\n   - Excessive scarring and adhesions\n\n2. **Technical:**\n   - Repair under tension\n   - Misalignment of fascicles\n   - Inadequate debridement of damaged nerve ends\n   - Suture granuloma formation\n\n3. **Associated structures:**\n   - Vascular compromise\n   - Tendon repair failure\n   - Joint stiffness\n   - Complex regional pain syndrome (CRPS) - up to 5% incidence\n\n**Late Complications (>6 weeks):**\n\n1. **Neuroma formation:**\n   - Incidence: 3-5% following repair\n   - Presents as painful, hypersensitive nodule at repair site\n   - Management: desensitization, targeted injections, surgical revision\n\n2. **Failed reinnervation:**\n   - Complete absence of recovery at expected timeframe\n   - May indicate repair failure or proximal nerve problem\n   - Requires EMG/NCS and consideration of revision surgery\n\n3. **Incomplete sensory recovery:**\n   - Persistent numbness (most common)\n   - Paresthesias\n   - Dysesthesias (unpleasant abnormal sensations)\n   - Two-point discrimination >10mm\n\n4. **Motor complications:**\n   - Persistent thenar atrophy\n   - Weakness of thumb opposition and abduction\n   - Clawing of index and middle fingers (if FDS/FDP function preserved)\n\n5. **Autonomic dysfunction:**\n   - Cold intolerance (30-50% of patients)\n   - Vasomotor instability\n   - Hyperhidrosis or anhidrosis\n   - Trophic changes to skin and nails\n\n6. **Functional impairment:**\n   - Difficulty with precision grip\n   - Impaired dexterity for fine manipulation\n   - Dropping objects due to sensory deficit\n   - Occupational disability\n\n7. **Psychological impact:**\n   - Depression and anxiety\n   - Body image concerns\n   - Chronic pain syndromes\n\n**Management strategies:**\n\n- Regular follow-up to identify complications early\n- Revision surgery for failed repairs (typically if no signs of recovery by 3-6 months)\n- Tendon transfers for persistent motor deficits (e.g., Opponensplasty using FDS of ring finger)\n- Targeted pain management for neuromas and neuropathic pain\n- Specialized hand therapy focusing on sensory reeducation and functional adaptation\n- Psychological support and pain management referrals as needed\n- Vocational rehabilitation for occupational adjustments\n\n**For this construction worker:**\n- Particular attention to occupational impact and potential need for workplace modifications\n- Higher risk of cold intolerance given outdoor work environment\n- Potential need for tendon transfers if thenar function remains poor\n- Consideration of protective gloves to compensate for sensory deficits",
