        "answer": "Tendon transfers are considered when nerve recovery is inadequate or impossible. The decision typically occurs 12-18 months post-injury when maximum nerve recovery has plateaued.\n\n**Indications for Tendon Transfers:**\n\n1. **Failed nerve repair** with no evidence of reinnervation after appropriate waiting period\n2. **Delayed presentation** (>12-18 months) where direct nerve repair would yield poor results\n3. **Proximal nerve injuries** with insufficient time for regeneration to reach end organs\n4. **Segmental nerve loss** where grafting is not feasible\n5. **Partial recovery** with specific functional deficits that can be addressed with transfers\n\n**Prerequisites for Successful Transfers:**\n\n- Supple joints with full passive range of motion\n- Stable, well-healed soft tissues\n- Adequate donor muscles (M4-M5 strength)\n- Motivated and compliant patient\n- Realistic expectations\n\n**Principles of Tendon Transfer:**\n\n1. **One function per transfer** - avoid trying to restore multiple functions with one transfer\n2. **Synergistic donor selection** - use muscles with similar activation patterns\n3. **Adequate strength** - donor should be at least M4 strength\n4. **Straight line of pull** - minimize angular redirection\n5. **Appropriate tension** - neither too tight nor too loose\n\n**Common Transfers for Median Nerve Palsy:**\n\n1. **Thumb Opposition:**\n   - **Riordan Opponensplasty:** FDS of ring finger → insertion into APB\n   - **Camitz Opponensplasty:** Palmaris longus with palmar fascia extension → APB\n   - **Huber Transfer:** Abductor digiti minimi → APB\n   - **Bunnell Transfer:** FDS of ring finger routed around FCU\n\n2. **Thumb Abduction:**\n   - **Extensor indicis proprius (EIP)** → APB\n   - **Extensor digiti minimi (EDM)** → APB\n\n3. **Thumb IP Flexion (AIN palsy):**\n   - **Brachioradialis** → FPL\n   - **ECRL** → FPL\n\n4. **Index/Middle Finger DIP Flexion:**\n   - **Superficialis to profundus (SPLASH):** FDS → FDP of same finger\n   - **Side-to-side tenodesis** of affected FDP to adjacent intact FDP\n\n**Specific Considerations for High Median Nerve Palsy:**\n\n- Requires addressing both extrinsic and intrinsic functions\n- May need multiple transfers (typically staged procedures)\n- Prioritize thumb opposition and key pinch over other functions\n- Consider tenodesis procedures for less critical functions\n\n**Postoperative Rehabilitation:**\n\n- Immobilization: 3-4 weeks in position of function\n- Early protected motion: 4-6 weeks\n- Motor reeducation: critical for synergistic control\n- Strengthening: begins at 8-12 weeks\n- Return to full activities: 4-6 months\n\n**Expected Outcomes:**\n\n- Opposition strength: 30-50% of normal\n- Key pinch: 40-60% of contralateral side\n- Patient satisfaction: generally high despite strength limitations\n- Return to manual labor: often possible but may require job modifications\n\nFor this construction worker, if median nerve recovery is inadequate, a Riordan or Camitz opponensplasty would likely be the primary procedure to restore thumb opposition, with additional transfers considered based on the specific pattern of persistent deficits.",
