        "answer": "Congenital talipes equinovarus (CTEV) or clubfoot consists of four main components that occur in a specific spatial relationship:\n\n1. **Hindfoot equinus**: Plantarflexion of the ankle and subtalar joint\n2. **Hindfoot varus**: Inversion of the calcaneus under the talus\n3. **Midfoot cavus**: High arch due to pronation of the forefoot relative to the hindfoot\n4. **Forefoot adductus and supination**: Medial deviation and supination of the forefoot\n\nThese deformities occur in a three-dimensional plane and are interrelated. The cavus is often the first deformity to be corrected in treatment, followed by adductus and varus, with equinus typically addressed last. The deformity involves abnormalities of the talus, calcaneus, navicular, and their articulations, with the navicular often severely displaced medially and the calcaneus positioned under the talus.",
