        "answer": "I would assess the severity using two validated classification systems:\n\n**1. Pirani Scoring System:**\nThis is a 6-point scoring system divided into two components (hindfoot and midfoot), each with three signs scored 0, 0.5, or 1:\n\nHindfoot components (3 points):\n- Posterior crease\n- Rigid equinus\n- Empty heel\n\nMidfoot components (3 points):\n- Medial crease\n- Curvature of lateral border\n- Position of lateral head of talus\n\nEach component is scored as:\n- 0: Normal\n- 0.5: Moderately abnormal\n- 1: Severely abnormal\n\nTotal scores range from 0 (normal) to 6 (most severe). This system is particularly useful for monitoring treatment progress with the Ponseti method.\n\n**2. Dimeglio Classification:**\nThis grades severity on a 20-point scale based on:\n- Four major components (equinus, varus, derotation of calcaneopedal block, adduction) - up to 16 points\n- Additional points for medial creases, posterior creases, cavus, and poor muscle condition - up to 4 additional points\n\nThe deformity is then classified as:\n- Grade I (mild): <5 points, >90% reducible\n- Grade II (moderate): 5-10 points, reducible but partially resistant\n- Grade III (severe): 10-15 points, resistant but partially reducible\n- Grade IV (very severe): 15-20 points, very stiff foot\n\nI would document both scores at initial assessment as they provide complementary information and help track treatment progress.",
