        "answer": "The Ponseti method is a primarily non-surgical approach to clubfoot treatment developed by Dr. Ignacio Ponseti at the University of Iowa. It's considered the gold standard because:\n\n**Key components of the Ponseti method:**\n\n1. **Sequential manipulation and casting:**\n   - Specific manipulation technique addressing deformities in a particular order\n   - Weekly cast changes for 5-8 weeks\n   - Cavus corrected first, followed by adductus and varus together\n   - Equinus addressed last\n\n2. **Percutaneous Achilles tenotomy:**\n   - Performed in approximately 90% of cases\n   - Minor procedure under local anesthesia\n   - Addresses residual equinus after casting\n   - Followed by 3 weeks in a final cast\n\n3. **Foot abduction orthosis (FAO) protocol:**\n   - Denis Browne bar with shoes set at 70° external rotation for affected foot(s)\n   - 23 hours/day for first 3 months\n   - Then 12-14 hours (nights and naps) until age 4-5 years\n\n**Evidence supporting gold standard status:**\n\n- **High success rates:** >95% initial correction in idiopathic cases\n- **Low recurrence rates:** 10-30% compared to 80% with previous methods\n- **Reduced need for extensive surgery:** Major surgical releases reduced from >90% to <5%\n- **Better long-term outcomes:** Improved function, less pain, fewer complications\n- **Global applicability:** Effective in various healthcare settings including low-resource environments\n- **Cost-effectiveness:** Lower overall treatment costs compared to surgical approaches\n- **Reproducible results:** Standardized protocol with consistent outcomes across centers\n\nThe method works by taking advantage of the viscoelastic properties of infant connective tissues, gradually remodeling joint surfaces and realigning bones without extensive surgical releases that can lead to stiffness and pain in later life.",
