      {
        "answer": "I would explain to the parents:\n\n**Treatment Timeline and Process:**\n\n1. **Initial Casting Phase (1-2 months):**\n   - Weekly visits for gentle manipulation and cast changes\n   - Each cast gradually corrects different aspects of the deformity\n   - Casts extend from toes to upper thigh with knee bent\n   - 5-8 casts typically needed\n\n2. **Achilles Tenotomy (if needed):**\n   - Minor procedure under local anesthesia (quick office procedure)\n   - Small nick in the Achilles tendon to complete correction\n   - Final cast for 3 weeks afterward\n\n3. **Bracing Phase (4-5 years):**\n   - Special boots connected by a bar (foot abduction orthosis)\n   - Initially worn 23 hours/day for 3 months\n   - Then nights and naps (12-14 hours/day) until age 4-5\n   - **Critical phase** - compliance directly linked to success\n\n**Expected Outcomes:**\n\n- **Success rate:** >95% initial correction with proper Ponseti treatment\n- **Functional results:** Normal-appearing foot with good function\n- **Activity level:** Participation in regular activities and sports\n- **Long-term:** Minimal or no pain, normal shoe wear\n\n**Potential Challenges:**\n\n- **Recurrence risk:** 10-30%, usually within first 2-3 years\n- **Recurrence causes:** Primarily non-compliance with bracing\n- **Signs of recurrence:** Return of adduction or supination during walking\n- **Management of recurrence:** May require repeat casting or minor procedures\n\n**Parental Role:**\n\n- **Critical to success:** Treatment outcomes depend significantly on parental compliance\n- **Commitment required:** Regular appointments and consistent brace use\n- **Skin care:** Checking for pressure areas or irritation\n- **Support available:** Clinic team, support groups, educational resources\n\nI would emphasize that with proper treatment and compliance, their child should have a functional, plantigrade foot allowing normal activities and shoe wear, though subtle differences in calf size or foot shape might persist.",
