        "answer": "**Management Differences for Complex/Atypical Clubfoot:**\n\n**1. Initial Approach:**\n\n*Idiopathic Clubfoot:*\n- Standard Ponseti protocol\n- Treatment initiation ideally <4 weeks of age\n- Weekly casting for 5-8 weeks\n- High success rate (>95%)\n\n*Complex/Atypical Clubfoot:*\n- Modified Ponseti approach still first-line\n- Longer casting period often needed (8-12+ casts)\n- Smaller, more incremental corrections\n- Cast changes may be less frequent (10-14 days)\n- Lower initial success rate (50-75%)\n- Earlier recognition of treatment resistance\n\n**2. Tenotomy Considerations:**\n\n*Idiopathic Clubfoot:*\n- Single percutaneous Achilles tenotomy (~90% of cases)\n- Performed when foot abduction reaches 60-70°\n\n*Complex/Atypical Clubfoot:*\n- May require repeat tenotomies\n- Additional tendon releases sometimes needed:\n  - Tibialis posterior tenotomy\n  - Flexor digitorum longus release\n  - Plantar fascia release for persistent cavus\n- May need open rather than percutaneous approach\n\n**3. Bracing Modifications:**\n\n*Idiopathic Clubfoot:*\n- Standard foot abduction orthosis (FAO)\n- 70° external rotation of affected foot\n\n*Complex/Atypical Clubfoot:*\n- Custom-molded orthoses often required\n- Modified rotation angles based on achievable correction\n- More frequent orthotic adjustments\n- Extended bracing duration (potentially beyond age 5)\n- Higher vigilance for skin problems\n\n**4. Surgical Interventions:**\n\n*Idiopathic Clubfoot:*\n- Surgery rarely needed (<5% with proper Ponseti treatment)\n- Tibialis anterior transfer for dynamic supination\n\n*Complex/Atypical Clubfoot:*\n- Higher surgical rates (25-50%)\n- Earlier consideration of limited releases\n- May require staged surgical approach\n- Potential procedures include:\n  - Posteromedial-lateral release\n  - Tendon transfers (multiple)\n  - External fixation for resistant cases\n  - Talectomy in extremely rigid cases\n  - Corrective osteotomies in older children\n\n**5. Follow-up Protocol:**\n\n*Idiopathic Clubfoot:*\n- Standard follow-up schedule\n- Discharge from regular follow-up possible at age 5-6\n\n*Complex/Atypical Clubfoot:*\n- More frequent monitoring\n- Multidisciplinary team approach\n- Long-term follow-up until skeletal maturity\n- Regular gait analysis\n- Functional assessments\n\n**6. Specific Considerations by Etiology:**\n\n- **Arthrogryposis:**\n  - Very rigid deformities\n  - May require 15-20+ casts\n  - Higher recurrence rates (50-80%)\n  - Often needs surgical intervention\n\n- **Myelomeningocele:**\n  - Treatment guided by neurological level\n  - Balance foot correction with bracing needs\n  - Consider impact of sensory deficits\n\n- **Syndromic cases:**\n  - Address associated conditions concurrently\n  - Coordinate with genetic/metabolic specialists\n  - Consider impact of overall health on treatment timing\n\nThe key principle remains attempting Ponseti method first, but with modified expectations, timeline, and readiness to implement alternative strategies when standard approaches fail.",
