        "answer": "**Risk factors for SUFE include:**\n\n- **Obesity** (most common risk factor, present in >50% of cases)\n- **Age and gender:** Peak age 12-15 years in boys, 10-13 years in girls\n- **Growth spurt during puberty**\n- **African or Polynesian ethnicity** (higher incidence)\n- **Previous contralateral SUFE** (20-40% risk of bilateral involvement)\n\n**Underlying conditions to consider:**\n\n- **Endocrine disorders:**\n  - Hypothyroidism\n  - Growth hormone deficiency\n  - Hypogonadism\n  - Hypopituitarism\n  - Hyperparathyroidism\n\n- **Renal disorders:**\n  - Chronic renal failure\n  - Renal osteodystrophy\n\n- **Other conditions:**\n  - Down syndrome\n  - Radiation therapy\n  - Previous radiotherapy\n  - Perthes disease\n\nEndocrine disorders are present in approximately 5-8% of SUFE cases. The presence of SUFE in atypical patients (very young, non-obese, or post-puberty) should prompt a thorough endocrine workup. Bilateral SUFE at presentation is also more suggestive of an underlying endocrinopathy.",
