      {
        "answer": "My surgical management would be as follows:\n\n**Pre-operative:**\n1. Full informed consent with patient and parents, discussing risks including AVN (5-10% in stable SUFE, up to 50% in unstable), chondrolysis, and need for future procedures\n2. Mark the correct side\n3. Ensure appropriate imaging is available\n4. Prophylactic antibiotics as per local protocol\n5. Position patient supine on a radiolucent table with C-arm access\n\n**Procedure for in situ fixation:**\n1. Under general anesthesia with muscle relaxation\n2. Prepare and drape the affected hip\n3. Obtain AP and lateral fluoroscopic views to confirm the slip\n4. Make a small lateral incision over the proximal femur\n5. Use a guide wire placed centrally in the femoral neck and head under fluoroscopic guidance\n6. Confirm appropriate positioning in both AP and lateral views\n7. Measure and select appropriate cannulated screw length\n8. Insert a single 6.5-7.0mm partially threaded cannulated screw\n9. Ensure the threads cross the physis but do not penetrate the joint\n10. Confirm final position with fluoroscopy in multiple views\n11. Close in layers\n\n**Considerations:**\n- For moderate to severe slips, I would discuss with my consultant about potential capital realignment procedures\n- For unstable SUFE, gentle closed reduction may be considered but with extreme caution due to AVN risk\n- I would consider prophylactic pinning of the contralateral hip if there are significant risk factors (young age, obesity, endocrinopathy)\n\n**Post-operative:**\n1. Protected weight-bearing for 6 weeks\n2. Regular radiographic follow-up until physeal closure\n3. Physiotherapy for gait training and strengthening\n4. Endocrinology and dietitian referrals",
        "question": "Describe your surgical management for this patient."
