      {
        "answer": "**Early complications:**\n\n1. **Avascular necrosis (AVN):**\n   - Incidence: 5-10% in stable SUFE, up to 50% in unstable SUFE\n   - Management: If early, consider core decompression; if established, may require hip reconstruction or arthroplasty depending on age and severity\n\n2. **Chondrolysis:**\n   - Incidence: 5-7% overall\n   - Management: Anti-inflammatory medication, protected weight-bearing, physical therapy; severe cases may require salvage procedures\n\n3. **Hardware complications:**\n   - Screw prominence, migration, or breakage\n   - Management: Revision surgery if symptomatic\n\n4. **Infection:**\n   - Management: Antibiotics, possible debridement if deep infection\n\n**Late complications:**\n\n1. **Femoroacetabular impingement (FAI):**\n   - Very common after SUFE due to altered femoral head-neck offset\n   - Management: Activity modification, physiotherapy; arthroscopic or open osteochondroplasty for persistent symptoms\n\n2. **Contralateral SUFE:**\n   - Incidence: 20-40%\n   - Management: Prophylactic pinning may be considered in high-risk patients; otherwise, close monitoring\n\n3. **Premature osteoarthritis:**\n   - Due to altered hip mechanics and cartilage damage\n   - Management: Initially conservative; may eventually require hip arthroplasty\n\n4. **Leg length discrepancy:**\n   - Management: Shoe lift for significant discrepancies; rarely requires surgical intervention\n\n5. **Residual deformity:**\n   - For severe slips with significant deformity\n   - Management: Proximal femoral osteotomy or surgical hip dislocation with capital realignment\n\nThe key to managing complications is regular follow-up until skeletal maturity, with clinical and radiological assessment. Early identification of complications allows for timely intervention. Patient education regarding activity modification and weight management is crucial for long-term outcomes.",
        "question": "What are the potential complications of SUFE and how would you manage them?"
