      {
        "answer": "Prophylactic pinning of the contralateral hip remains controversial, but there are specific scenarios where I would consider it:\n\n**Factors favoring prophylactic pinning:**\n\n1. **Patient-specific risk factors:**\n   - Young age at presentation (especially <10 years)\n   - Significant obesity (BMI >95th percentile)\n   - Confirmed endocrinopathy (hypothyroidism, growth hormone deficiency)\n   - Modified Oxford Bone Age Score indicating high risk\n   - Significant skeletal growth remaining\n\n2. **Radiographic indicators:**\n   - Early signs of contralateral slip (widened physis, decreased epiphyseal height)\n   - Posterior tilt angle >12° on lateral radiograph\n\n3. **Practical considerations:**\n   - Poor follow-up likelihood\n   - Geographic barriers to healthcare access\n   - Patient/family preference after thorough discussion\n\n**Factors against prophylactic pinning:**\n\n1. **Surgical risks:**\n   - AVN (rare but devastating in an otherwise normal hip)\n   - Chondrolysis\n   - Hardware complications\n   - Infection\n\n2. **Patient factors:**\n   - Near skeletal maturity\n   - Reliable follow-up\n   - No endocrinopathy\n   - Normal contralateral radiographs\n\nThe reported rate of bilateral involvement ranges from 20-40%, with most contralateral slips occurring within 18 months of the initial SUFE. Studies have shown that prophylactic pinning is safe with complication rates <2%, but the number needed to treat to prevent one contralateral slip is approximately 5-7 patients.\n\nI would discuss this initially with the managing consultant and then have a detailed discussion with the patient and family regarding risks and benefits, and make a shared decision based on their individual risk profile.",
