        "answer": "The ethical principles regarding authorship in medical publications are well-established:\n\n**Substantial contribution:** Authors should have made significant intellectual contributions to:\n- Conception or design of the work\n- Data acquisition, analysis, or interpretation\n- Drafting or critically revising the manuscript\n\n**Accountability:** Authors should be accountable for:\n- The accuracy and integrity of all aspects of the work\n- Ensuring questions related to accuracy are appropriately investigated and resolved\n\n**Approval:** All authors should approve the final version for publication\n\n**Agreement:** Authors should agree to be accountable for all aspects of the work\n\n**International Committee of Medical Journal Editors (ICMJE) criteria:** Most medical journals follow these guidelines, which state that authorship should be based on:\n1. Substantial contributions to conception/design, data acquisition/analysis, or interpretation\n2. Drafting or critically revising intellectual content\n3. Final approval of the version to be published\n4. Agreement to be accountable for all aspects of the work\n\n**Gift authorship:** Including individuals who have not met authorship criteria (often senior colleagues or supervisors) is considered unethical\n\n**Ghost authorship:** Omitting individuals who have met authorship criteria is also unethical\n\n**Acknowledgments section:** Those who contributed but don't meet full authorship criteria should be listed in the acknowledgments section\n\n**Transparency:** The specific contributions of each author should be transparent",
