        "answer": "My initial concerns for this adolescent patient with a high-energy mechanism of injury include:\n\n1. **Polytrauma assessment** - A fall from 8 meters warrants a thorough primary and secondary survey following ATLS principles\n\n2. **Calcaneal fracture characteristics:**\n   - Intra-articular vs extra-articular involvement\n   - Displacement and comminution\n   - Böhler's angle disruption\n   - Subtalar joint involvement\n\n3. **Compartment syndrome risk** - The calcaneus has distinct medial, lateral, and central compartments that can develop increased pressures, particularly following high-energy injuries\n\n4. **Associated injuries:**\n   - Spinal fractures (particularly thoracolumbar) occur in 10-15% of calcaneal fractures\n   - Bilateral calcaneal fractures (10-15% of cases)\n   - Other lower limb injuries (ankle, tibial plateau)\n   - Pelvic injuries\n\n5. **Soft tissue status** - Swelling, bruising, and skin integrity are critical for surgical planning\n\n6. **Age-specific considerations:**\n   - Open physes may affect fracture pattern\n   - Growth potential and remodeling capacity\n   - Long-term functional outcomes particularly important in young patients",
