        "answer": "When interpreting radiographs for a suspected calcaneal fracture, I would systematically assess:\n\n**Lateral View:**\n- **Böhler's angle** - Normally 20-40°, formed by lines drawn from posterior tuberosity to highest point of posterior facet and from highest point of posterior facet to anterior process. Reduction below 20° suggests fracture with depression of posterior facet\n- **Crucial angle of Gissane** - Normally 120-145°, formed at the intersection of the posterior facet and anterior facet\n- **Posterior facet integrity** - Assess for step-offs or depression\n- **Calcaneal height** - Look for shortening\n- **Subtalar joint congruity**\n- **Calcaneocuboid joint involvement**\n\n**Axial (Harris) View:**\n- **Width of calcaneus** - Assess for lateral wall blowout\n- **Sustentaculum tali position** - Usually maintained as it has strong ligamentous attachments (\"constant fragment\")\n- **Alignment of tuberosity fragment** - Varus/valgus malalignment\n\nThe Harris axial view is taken with the foot in dorsiflexion and the beam angled at 45 degrees cephalad. It allows visualization of the joint surface as well as loss of height, increase in width (important for footwear), and angulation of the tuberosity fragment.\n\n**Broden's Views (if available):**\n- Provides visualization of the posterior facet in multiple planes\n\n**AP and Oblique Foot Views:**\n- Assess for associated midfoot injuries\n- Evaluate calcaneocuboid joint\n\n**Ankle Views:**\n- Rule out associated ankle injuries\n\nI would classify the fracture using the **Sanders Classification** based on CT findings, which divides the posterior facet into three columns (medial, central, lateral) and assesses the number and location of fracture lines:\n- Type I: Non-displaced\n- Type II: Single fracture line creating two fragments\n- Type III: Two fracture lines creating three fragments\n- Type IV: Highly comminuted with three or more fracture lines\n\nThis classification has prognostic value, 