        "answer": "For this patient with a suspected calcaneal fracture from a high-energy mechanism, I would request:\n\n**CT Scan with Multiplanar Reconstruction:**\n- Gold standard for calcaneal fracture assessment\n- Provides detailed evaluation of:\n  * Posterior facet involvement and step-offs\n  * Number and orientation of fracture lines\n  * Degree of comminution\n  * Subtalar joint congruity\n  * Calcaneocuboid joint involvement\n  * Lateral wall displacement (\"blow-out\")\n  * Sustentaculum tali position\n- Allows for Sanders Classification which guides treatment and provides prognostic information\n- Enables precise surgical planning if operative intervention is indicated\n- 3D reconstructions particularly helpful for complex fracture patterns\n\n**Thoracolumbar Spine Imaging:**\n- Plain radiographs initially\n- Consider CT if suspicious for fracture\n- Essential as 10-15% of calcaneal fractures have associated spinal injuries, particularly at the thoracolumbar junction\n\n**Bilateral Foot/Ankle Imaging:**\n- Approximately 10-15% of calcaneal fractures are bilateral\n- Even if asymptomatic, contralateral calcaneal injury should be ruled out\n\n**Pelvic Radiograph:**\n- To rule out associated pelvic injuries in high-energy trauma\n\n**MRI (in selected cases):**\n- If suspicion of occult fracture with negative CT\n- To evaluate soft tissue injuries (ligaments, tendons)\n- To assess bone bruising or stress reactions\n- Particularly useful in pediatric/adolescent patients where growth plate injuries may be subtle\n\n**Weight-bearing CT (when appropriate during follow-up):**\n- Provides functional assessment of hindfoot alignment\n- Useful for evaluating subtalar joint congruity under physiological load\n- Helps in decision-making for late reconstructive procedures",
