      {
        "answer": "**Calcaneal Fracture Classifications:**\n\n1. **Essex-Lopresti Classification** (based on plain radiographs):\n   - **Joint Depression Type:** Primary fracture line runs posterior to subtalar joint with secondary fracture lines causing depression of posterior facet\n   - **Tongue Type:** Primary fracture line runs into posterior facet with secondary fracture extending posteriorly into tuberosity\n   - **Comminuted/Crush Type:** Severe comminution not fitting other patterns\n\n2. **Sanders Classification** (based on CT, most widely used):\n   - Divides posterior facet into three columns (medial, central, lateral)\n   - **Type I:** Non-displaced fractures (<2mm displacement)\n   - **Type II:** Single fracture line creating two fragments (IIA, IIB, IIC based on location)\n   - **Type III:** Two fracture lines creating three fragments (IIIAB, IIIAC, IIIBC)\n   - **Type IV:** Highly comminuted (>3 fragments)\n\n3. **Orthopaedic Trauma Association (OTA) Classification:**\n   - 82-A: Extra-articular\n   - 82-B: Intra-articular, involving subtalar joint only\n   - 82-C: Intra-articular, involving both subtalar and calcaneocuboid joints\n\n4. **Böhler's Angle Assessment:**\n   - Normal: 20-40°\n   - Reduction below 20° indicates significant depression\n\n**Influence on Management:**\n\n**Non-operative Management** is typically indicated for:\n- Sanders Type I (non-displaced) fractures\n- Extra-articular fractures without significant displacement\n- Minimally displaced fractures in low-demand patients\n- Patients with significant comorbidities or contraindications to surgery\n- Some pediatric fractures with remodeling potential\n**Non-operative treatment includes cast immobilisation for at least 10-12 weeks – this is for undisplaced fractures or poor surgical candidates**\n\n**Operative Management** is typically considered for:\n- Sanders Type II and III fractures (better outcomes with ORIF)\n- Displaced intra-articular fractures (>2mm step-off)\n- Significant loss of Böhler's angle\n- Lateral wall blowout causing peroneal impingement\n- Varus/valgus malalignment of tuberosity >10°\n- Associated open fracture\n\n**Surgical Approach Selection:**\n- **Extended Lateral Approach:** Most common, provides excellent exposure for ORIF of complex fractures\n- **Sinus Tarsi Approach:** Limited incision for less complex patterns, lower wound complication rates\n- **Percutaneous/Minimally Invasive:** For tongue-type fractures or simple patterns\n- **Medial Approach:** Rarely used, for specific medial wall or sustentaculum fractures\n\n**Fixation Method:**\n- Specialized calcaneal plates vs. screws based on fracture pattern\n- External fixation for severe soft tissue compromise\n\n**Timing of Surgery:**\n- Typically delayed until soft tissue swelling subsides (7-14 days)\n- Immediate surgery only for open fractures or compartment syndrome\n\n**In Adolescents (specific considerations):**\n- Greater remodeling potential may allow for less aggressive management\n- Growth plate considerations may modify fixation techniques\n- Long-term outcomes particularly important due to young age",
