        "answer": "**Early Complications:**\n\n1. **Compartment Syndrome**\n   - **Presentation:** Increasing pain, pain on passive stretch, paresthesia, pallor, paralysis\n   - **Management:** \n     * Clinical diagnosis primarily\n     * Compartment pressure measurement if equivocal\n     * Urgent fasciotomy of medial, lateral, and central compartments if confirmed\n     * Regular reassessment in high-risk cases\n\n2. **Soft Tissue Compromise**\n   - **Presentation:** Fracture blisters, skin necrosis, hematoma\n   - **Management:**\n     * Elevation, ice, compression\n     * Delay surgery until skin wrinkle sign present\n     * Consider minimally invasive approaches if surgery needed urgently\n     * Plastic surgery consultation for complex cases\n\n3. **Open Fractures**\n   - **Management:**\n     * Antibiotics per BOAST guidelines\n     * Urgent debridement\n     * Consider external fixation or primary arthrodesis\n     * Soft tissue coverage procedures as needed\n\n**Intermediate Complications:**\n\n1. **Wound Complications**\n   - **Incidence:** 10-25% with extended lateral approach\n   - **Management:**\n     * Superficial: Local wound care, antibiotics\n     * Deep: Debridement, culture-specific antibiotics\n     * Consider VAC therapy\n     * Timing surgery after swelling subsides reduces risk\n\n2. **Malunion**\n   - **Presentation:** Widened heel, decreased height, varus hindfoot\n   - **Management:**\n     * Orthotic devices for mild cases\n     * Corrective osteotomy for symptomatic cases\n     * Subtalar fusion may be required\n\n3. **Hardware Problems**\n   - **Presentation:** Prominence, irritation, peroneal impingement\n   - **Management:** Hardware removal after union (typically 6-12 months)\n\n**Late Complications:**\n\n1. **Post-traumatic Arthritis**\n   - **Incidence:** 30-40% of intra-articular fractures\n   - **Management:**\n     * Conservative: NSAIDs, activity modification, orthotics\n     * Surgical: Subtalar arthrodesis, triple arthrodesis\n\n2. **Chronic Pain**\n   - **Causes:** Arthritis, malunion, nerve impingement, complex regional pain syndrome\n   - **Management:**\n     * Multimodal pain management\n     * Targeted injections\n     * Corrective surgery for specific anatomical issues\n     * Pain specialist referral\n\n3. **Peroneal Tendinopathy**\n   - **Cause:** Lateral wall expansion causing impingement\n   - **Management:**\n     * Lateral wall decompression\n     * Peroneal tendon debridement or repair\n\n4. **Sural Nerve Injury**\n   - **Presentation:** Lateral foot numbness, neuroma\n   - **Management:**\n     * Careful surgical technique to avoid\n     * Neurolysis or neurectomy for symptomatic neuroma\n\n**Adolescent-Specific Considerations:**\n\n1. **Growth Disturbance**\n   - **Management:** Follow-up until skeletal maturity\n   - Corrective procedures if significant deformity develops\n\n2. **Long-term Functional Limitations**\n   - **Management:**\n     * Comprehensive rehabilitation\n     * Activity modification\n     * Orthotic support\n     * Career/sports counseling\n\n**Prevention Strategies:**\n- Anatomic reduction of articular surface\n- Restoration of calcaneal height and width\n- Careful soft tissue handling\n- Appropriate timing of surgery\n- Early mobilization and rehabilitation",
