        "answer": "**Initial Management:**\n\n1. **Assessment and Stabilization:**\n   - Complete trauma assessment (ATLS principles)\n   - Adequate analgesia (IV opioids titrated to effect)\n   - Posterior splint with ankle in neutral position\n   - Elevation and ice to reduce swelling\n   - Strict non-weight bearing status\n\n2. **Investigations:**\n   - Plain radiographs (lateral, axial, AP foot, ankle views)\n   - CT scan with multiplanar reconstruction\n   - Thoracolumbar spine imaging\n   - Appropriate blood tests\n\n3. **Soft Tissue Monitoring:**\n   - Regular neurovascular observations\n   - Compartment syndrome assessment\n   - Monitor for fracture blisters\n\n**Definitive Management Decision-Making:**\n\nFor this 15-year-old female, I would consider several factors:\n\n1. **Fracture Pattern Assessment:**\n   - If extra-articular or minimally displaced (Sanders Type I): Non-operative management\n   - If displaced intra-articular (Sanders Type II-III): Consider ORIF\n   - If severely comminuted (Sanders Type IV): Consider primary subtalar fusion vs. ORIF\n\n2. **Age-Specific Considerations:**\n   - Greater remodeling potential in adolescents\n   - Long-term functional outcomes particularly important\n   - Growth plate considerations (though calcaneal apophysis typically fuses by age 14-16)\n\n3. **Timing of Surgery:**\n   - Delay until soft tissue swelling subsides (typically 7-14 days)\n   - Use \"wrinkle sign\" to determine appropriate timing\n\n**Non-operative Management** (if appropriate):\n- Continued immobilization in posterior splint/cast\n- Non-weight bearing for 6-8 weeks\n- Early subtalar motion exercises when pain allows\n- Progressive weight bearing after 8-12 weeks\n- Physiotherapy for ROM, strength, and proprioception\n\n**Operative Management** (if indicated):\n\n1. **Approach Selection:**\n   - Extended lateral approach for complex fractures\n   - Sinus tarsi approach for less complex patterns (lower wound complication rate)\n   - Minimally invasive techniques for specific patterns\n\n2. **Surgical Goals:**\n   - Restore articular congruity (<2mm step-off)\n   - Restore Böhler's angle\n   - Restore calcaneal height and width\n   - Decompress lateral wall if expanded\n   - Stable fixation to allow early motion\n\n3. **Fixation Options:**\n   - Specialized calcaneal plates\n   - Cannulated screws\n   - K-wire fixation (may be more appropriate in younger patients)\n\n**Post-operative Protocol:**\n- Splint for 2 weeks until wound healing\n- Transition to removable boot\n- Early non-weight bearing ROM exercises\n- Non-weight bearing for 8-12 weeks\n- Progressive weight bearing as tolerated thereafter\n- Regular radiographic follow-up\n\n**Rehabilitation:**\n- Comprehensive physiotherapy program\n- Ankle and subtalar ROM exercises\n- Peroneal strengthening\n- Proprioception training\n- Gradual return to activities\n\n**Long-term Follow-up:**\n- Regular clinical and radiological assessment until fully recovered\n- Monitoring for growth-related issues until skeletal maturity\n- Assessment of functional outcomes using validated tools\n- Consideration of hardware removal if symptomatic (typically 6-12 months post-op)",
