        "answer": "The evidence base for calcaneal fracture management remains somewhat controversial, with several key studies providing guidance:\n\n**Major Randomized Controlled Trials:**\n\n1. **UK Heel Fracture Trial (2014):**\n   - Multicenter RCT with 151 patients\n   - Compared ORIF vs. non-operative treatment for displaced intra-articular fractures\n   - **Results:** No significant difference in Kerr-Atkins score at 2 years\n   - Higher complication rate in surgical group (19% wound complications)\n   - Subgroup analysis suggested better outcomes with surgery in anatomically reduced fractures and younger patients\n\n2. **Canadian Orthopaedic Trauma Society Trial (2002):**\n   - 424 patients with displaced intra-articular fractures\n   - **Results:** Better outcomes with ORIF in certain subgroups:\n     * Women\n     * Younger patients (<29 years)\n     * Joint depression pattern fractures\n     * Light labor workers\n     * Anatomic reduction achieved\n\n3. **Buckley et al. (2002):**\n   - Multicenter RCT with 424 patients\n   - **Results:** Overall no significant difference between operative and non-operative\n   - Subgroup analysis showed better outcomes with ORIF in:\n     * Böhler's angle restoration\n     * No heavy labor job requirements\n     * Anatomic reduction achieved\n\n**Meta-analyses:**\n\n1. **Zhang et al. (2016):**\n   - 10 studies including 891 patients\n   - **Conclusion:** ORIF showed better outcomes in:\n     * Böhler's angle restoration\n     * Calcaneal height and width\n     * Reduced subtalar arthritis\n     * Better functional scores\n   - But higher wound complication rates\n\n2. **Luo et al. (2016):**\n   - 4 RCTs with 966 patients\n   - **Conclusion:** No significant difference in functional outcomes\n   - Higher complication rates with surgery\n\n**Minimally Invasive Techniques:**\n\n1. **Xia et al. (2018):**\n   - Meta-analysis of 11 studies\n   - **Conclusion:** Minimally invasive techniques showed:\n     * Comparable functional outcomes to ORIF\n     * Lower wound complication rates\n     * Shorter operative time\n\n**Pediatric/Adolescent Evidence:**\n\n1. **Schneidmueller et al. (2010):**\n   - Retrospective review of 39 pediatric calcaneal fractures\n   - **Conclusion:** Good outcomes with non-operative management for most fractures\n   - Operative intervention reserved for significantly displaced intra-articular fractures\n\n2. **Pickle et al. (2004):**\n   - Review of 17 adolescent calcaneal fractures\n   - **Conclusion:** Better outcomes with ORIF for displaced intra-articular fractures\n\n**Current Evidence-Based Recommendations:**\n\n1. **Non-operative management** is generally indicated for:\n   - Extra-articular fractures\n   - Minimally displaced intra-articular fractures (Sanders Type I)\n   - Patients with significant comorbidities\n   - Most pediatric fractures\n\n2. **Operative management** may be beneficial for:\n   - Displaced intra-articular fractures (Sanders Type II-III) in younger, active patients\n   - Lateral wall expansion causing peroneal impingement\n   - Significant loss of calcaneal height/Böhler's angle\n   - Varus/valgus malalignment >10°\n\n3. **Minimally invasive techniques** show promise with:\n   - Lower wound complication rates\n   - Comparable functional outcomes to ORIF in selected fracture patterns\n\n4. **Primary subtalar fusion** may be considered for:\n   - Sanders Type IV fractures\n   - Elderly patients with osteoporotic bone\n\n**For adolescents specifically:**\n- Limited high-quality evidence\n- Greater consideration for non-operative management due to remodeling potential\n- Operative intervention for significantly displaced intra-articular fractures\n- Long-term functional outcomes particularly important due to young age",
