        "answer": "My immediate priorities follow a systematic approach based on ATLS principles and specific management for pelvic trauma with haemorrhage:\n\n1. **Primary Survey with simultaneous resuscitation**\n   - Airway management with cervical spine protection\n   - Breathing assessment and supplemental oxygen\n   - Circulation: Establish large-bore IV access (preferably two 14-16G cannulae)\n   - Activate major haemorrhage protocol\n   - Apply/confirm correct pelvic binder placement (over greater trochanters)\n\n2. **Haemorrhage control measures**\n   - Initiate balanced blood product resuscitation (1:1:1 PRBC:FFP:Platelets)\n   - Administer tranexamic acid 1g IV within 3 hours of injury (CRASH-2 trial evidence)\n   - Target permissive hypotension (systolic BP 80-90mmHg) until definitive haemorrhage control\n   - Avoid excessive crystalloid administration\n\n3. **Rapid assessment for bleeding source**\n   - FAST scan to identify other sources of haemorrhage\n   - Arrange immediate pelvic radiograph if not compromising resuscitation\n   - Prepare for potential CT angiogram if patient responds to initial resuscitation\n\n4. **Definitive haemorrhage control planning**\n   - Early activation of interventional radiology for potential angioembolization\n   - Prepare for potential damage control surgery (external fixation/pre-peritoneal packing)\n   - Coordinate with multidisciplinary team (orthopaedics, trauma surgery, IR, anaesthesia)\n\n5. **Prevent and treat coagulopathy**\n   - Monitor coagulation parameters (TEG/ROTEM if available)\n   - Maintain normothermia with active warming\n   - Correct acidosis and hypocalcaemia\n\nThis approach follows the BOAST guidelines for management of pelvic fractures and major haemorrhage protocols, prioritizing rapid identification and control of bleeding while supporting physiological parameters.",
