      {
        "answer": "**Potential Sources of Bleeding in Pelvic Fractures:**\n\n1. **Venous bleeding (70-80% of cases)**\n   - Disruption of the presacral and paravesical venous plexuses\n   - Bleeding from cancellous bone surfaces at fracture sites\n\n2. **Arterial bleeding (10-20% of cases)**\n   - Branches of the internal iliac artery, most commonly:\n     - Superior gluteal artery\n     - Internal pudendal artery\n     - Obturator artery\n     - Lateral sacral arteries\n\n3. **Associated injuries causing bleeding**\n   - Solid organ injuries (liver, spleen)\n   - Hollow viscus injuries with mesenteric bleeding\n   - Major vessel injuries (iliac vessels, aorta)\n   - Other fractures (femur, spine)\n\n**Prioritized Haemorrhage Control Interventions:**\n\n1. **Immediate mechanical stabilization**\n   - Confirm correct pelvic binder application (at level of greater trochanters)\n   - Reduces pelvic volume, stabilizes fracture fragments, and tamponades venous bleeding\n\n2. **Resuscitation and blood product administration**\n   - Balanced transfusion (1:1:1 PRBC:FFP:Platelets) as per major haemorrhage protocol\n   - Tranexamic acid 1g IV bolus (CRASH-2 trial evidence)\n   - Permissive hypotension (target SBP 80-90mmHg) until definitive control\n\n3. **Assessment of response to initial measures**\n   - Transient responder: Proceed to angioembolization if available\n   - Non-responder: Immediate surgical intervention\n\n4. **Definitive haemorrhage control based on resources and patient status:**\n\n   **If haemodynamically improving or stable enough for transfer:**\n   - Interventional radiology for selective angioembolization (particularly for arterial bleeding)\n   - Highest yield for patients with contrast extravasation on CT\n\n   **If persistently unstable or IR unavailable:**\n   - Damage control surgery with pre-peritoneal packing\n   - External fixation of pelvis in theatre\n   - Combined approach with both packing and subsequent angiography for refractory bleeding\n\n5. **Definitive pelvic stabilization**\n   - Once haemodynamically stable, definitive fixation of pelvic fractures\n   - Timing depends on associated injuries and overall patient condition\n\nThis approach follows the Western Trauma Association algorithm for management of pelvic fracture haemorrhage and BOAST guidelines, with interventions prioritized based on the patient's haemodynamic status and available resources. The decision between angioembolization and surgical packing should be made based on institutional resources and expertise, with the fastest route to haemorrhage control being preferred.",
