      {
        "answer": "**Role of Interventional Radiology in Pelvic Fracture Haemorrhage:**\n\nInterventional radiology (IR) plays a crucial role in the management of pelvic fracture-related haemorrhage through angiography and embolization. It offers several advantages:\n\n1. **Targeted haemorrhage control**\n   - Allows identification and selective embolization of specific bleeding vessels\n   - Particularly effective for arterial sources of bleeding (10-20% of pelvic fracture bleeding)\n\n2. **Minimally invasive approach**\n   - Avoids additional surgical trauma in polytrauma patients\n   - Can be performed under local anaesthesia with sedation if necessary\n\n3. **Diagnostic and therapeutic capabilities**\n   - Identifies specific bleeding vessels through angiography\n   - Allows immediate intervention once bleeding source is identified\n\n**Indications for Angioembolization:**\n\n1. **Primary indications:**\n   - Contrast extravasation on CT angiography (strongest predictor of need for embolization)\n   - Persistent haemodynamic instability despite mechanical stabilization and adequate resuscitation\n   - High-risk fracture patterns associated with arterial bleeding (vertical shear/APC-III injuries)\n\n2. **Secondary indications:**\n   - Ongoing transfusion requirements despite other interventions\n   - Recurrent hypotension after initial stabilization\n   - Following pre-peritoneal packing with ongoing bleeding\n   - Specific arterial injuries identified on imaging\n\n3. **Predictors of need for angioembolization:**\n   - Age >60 years\n   - Absence of long bone fractures (suggesting pelvis as primary bleeding source)\n   - Initial systolic BP <90mmHg\n   - Need for >2 units of blood in first hour\n   - Young and Burgess APC-II, APC-III, or vertical shear fracture patterns\n\n**Contraindications and Limitations:**\n\n1. **Absolute contraindications:**\n   - Unavailability of IR service or excessive delay to access (>30-60 minutes)\n   - Exsanguinating haemorrhage requiring immediate surgical intervention\n\n2. **Relative contraindications:**\n   - Severe contrast allergy (can be mitigated with premedication if time allows)\n   - Significant renal impairment (risk vs. benefit assessment needed)\n   - Coagulopathy (should be corrected concurrently but not a reason to delay)\n\n3. **Limitations:**\n   - Limited efficacy for venous bleeding (70-80% of pelvic fracture bleeding)\n   - Time required for IR suite preparation and procedure\n   - Need for specialized equipment and trained personnel\n   - Potential for rebleeding or missed bleeding sources\n\n**Practical Considerations:**\n\n- Success rates of 80-100% reported in literature for arterial bleeding control\n- Consideration of prophylactic embolization of bilateral internal iliac arteries in severe cases\n- Need for repeat angiography in 10-20% of cases due to rebleeding\n- Potential complications include tissue necrosis, bladder/sexual dysfunction, and femoral access site complications\n\nThe decision to proceed with angioembolization versus surgical intervention should be based on institutional resources, patient stability, and the suspected source of bleeding, following a protocol-driven approach as outlined in the Western Trauma Association and EAST guidelines for pelvic trauma management.",
