      {
        "answer": "**Resuscitation Management:**\n\n1. **Damage Control Resuscitation Principles**\n   - Limited crystalloid use (maximum 1-2L)\n   - Early activation of major haemorrhage protocol\n   - Balanced blood product administration in 1:1:1 ratio (PRBC:FFP:Platelets)\n   - Permissive hypotension (target SBP 80-90mmHg) until haemorrhage control\n   - Rapid access to blood products via massive transfusion protocol\n\n2. **Blood Product Administration**\n   - Initial 4-6 units PRBC with matched FFP and platelets\n   - Consider group O negative/emergency release blood for immediate needs\n   - Subsequent transfusion guided by clinical response and laboratory parameters\n   - Aim to maintain Hb >8 g/dL during active haemorrhage\n\n3. **Haemodynamic Monitoring**\n   - Establish invasive arterial monitoring\n   - Consider early central venous access\n   - Serial lactate measurements to assess adequacy of resuscitation\n   - Urinary catheter for output monitoring (after excluding urethral injury)\n\n4. **Adjuncts to Resuscitation**\n   - Tranexamic acid 1g IV bolus within 3 hours of injury, followed by 1g over 8 hours\n   - Calcium replacement (maintain ionized calcium >1.0 mmol/L)\n   - Consider vasopressors only after adequate volume resuscitation\n   - Active warming to prevent hypothermia\n\n**Prevention and Treatment of Trauma-Induced Coagulopathy:**\n\n1. **Understanding Pathophysiology**\n   - Trauma-induced coagulopathy (TIC) results from tissue injury, shock, haemodilution, hypothermia, and acidosis\n   - Characterized by systemic anticoagulation and hyperfibrinolysis\n   - Develops in 25-35% of severely injured trauma patients\n   - Associated with 4-fold increase in mortality\n\n2. **Prevention Strategies**\n   - **Damage control approach:**\n     - Expedite haemorrhage control (pelvic binder, angioembolization, surgery)\n     - Minimize crystalloid administration\n     - Prevent hypothermia (warm fluids, forced-air warming blankets, ambient temperature control)\n     - Correct acidosis (adequate tissue perfusion, judicious use of buffer solutions)\n\n   - **Haemostatic resuscitation:**\n     - Early balanced transfusion with 1:1:1 ratio of PRBC:FFP:Platelets (PROPPR trial evidence)\n     - Consider early fibrinogen replacement (cryoprecipitate or fibrinogen concentrate)\n     - Early administration of tranexamic acid (CRASH-2 trial evidence)\n\n3. **Monitoring and Targeted Treatment**\n   - **Laboratory monitoring:**\n     - Conventional coagulation tests (PT, APTT, fibrinogen, platelet count)\n     - Serial monitoring every 30-60 minutes during active resuscitation\n\n   - **Viscoelastic haemostatic assays (if available):**\n     - TEG (Thromboelastography) or ROTEM (Rotational Thromboelastometry)\n     - Provides rapid assessment of clot formation, strength, and lysis\n     - Allows targeted component therapy\n\n   - **Targeted therapy based on results:**\n     - Platelets: Maintain >50 × 10⁹/L (>100 × 10⁹/L for CNS injury)\n     - Fibrinogen: Maintain >1.5-2.0 g/L (cryoprecipitate or fibrinogen concentrate)\n     - Prothrombin complex concentrate for vitamin K antagonist reversal\n     - Additional antifibrinolytics for hyperfibrinolysis on TEG/ROTEM\n\n4. **Special Considerations**\n   - Reassess coagulation status after every 4-6 units of PRBC\n   - Consider recombinant Factor VIIa only as rescue therapy for refractory bleeding\n   - Adjust approach for patients on pre-injury anticoagulants or antiplatelets\n   - Monitor for and treat hypocalcaemia, which impairs coagulation\n\nThis approach follows the European guideline on management of major bleeding and coagulopathy following trauma and incorporates evidence from major trials including CRASH-2 and PROPPR, with the goal of preventing the lethal triad of hypothermia, acidosis, and coagulopathy.",
