      {
        "answer": "**Acute Complications and Management:**\n\n1. **Haemorrhage (most common cause of early mortality)**\n   - **Management:** Pelvic binder, resuscitation, angioembolization, pre-peritoneal packing\n   - **Monitoring:** Serial haemoglobin, lactate, base deficit, coagulation parameters\n   - **Prevention:** Early fracture stabilization, damage control resuscitation\n\n2. **Genitourinary Injuries (15-20%)**\n   - **Urethral injury:** Suspect with blood at meatus, high-riding prostate, scrotal hematoma\n     - Management: Suprapubic catheter, delayed urethroplasty\n   - **Bladder injury:** Extraperitoneal (conservative) vs. intraperitoneal (surgical repair)\n     - Management: CT cystogram for diagnosis, catheter drainage or surgical repair\n   - **Prevention:** Careful catheterization, high index of suspicion\n\n3. **Neurological Injury (10-15%)**\n   - **Lumbosacral plexus injury:** Associated with sacral fractures and SI joint disruption\n     - Management: Documentation, pain management, physiotherapy, potential surgical exploration\n   - **Cauda equina syndrome:** Emergent decompression if present\n   - **Prevention:** Anatomic reduction, careful screw placement\n\n4. **Gastrointestinal Injuries (2-4%)**\n   - **Open fractures with rectal involvement:** High mortality rate\n     - Management: Diverting colostomy, presacral drainage, antibiotics\n   - **Bowel entrapment in fracture sites**\n     - Management: Surgical exploration and repair\n   - **Prevention:** High index of suspicion, rectal examination\n\n5. **Infection and Sepsis**\n   - **Open fractures:** Increased risk of deep infection\n     - Management: Antibiotics, debridement, wound management\n   - **Retroperitoneal hematoma infection**\n     - Management: Drainage, targeted antibiotics\n   - **Prevention:** Early antibiotics, proper wound care, sterile technique during fixation\n\n6. **Venous Thromboembolism (DVT/PE)**\n   - **High risk due to immobility and pelvic vessel injury**\n     - Management: Chemical prophylaxis once bleeding controlled, mechanical prophylaxis\n     - Consideration of IVC filter in high-risk patients with contraindications to anticoagulation\n   - **Prevention:** Early mobilization, regular assessment\n\n**Long-term Complications and Management:**\n\n1. **Chronic Pain (30-40% of patients)**\n   - **Causes:** Malunion, nonunion, hardware issues, neurological injury, heterotopic ossification\n     - Management: Multimodal pain approach, consideration of hardware removal if symptomatic\n     - Referral to pain specialists for complex cases\n   - **Prevention:** Anatomic reduction, stable fixation\n\n2. **Malunion/Nonunion (5-15%)**\n   - **Malunion:** Leg length discrepancy, sitting problems, gait abnormalities\n     - Management: Osteotomies and revision surgery for symptomatic cases\n   - **Nonunion:** More common in sacral and pubic rami fractures\n     - Management: Revision surgery with bone grafting if symptomatic\n   - **Prevention:** Anatomic reduction, stable fixation, appropriate weight-bearing protocols\n\n3. **Sexual and Urinary Dysfunction (30-40%)**\n   - **Erectile dysfunction in males:** Associated with posterior ring injuries\n     - Management: Urology referral, medical management, psychological support\n   - **Dyspareunia in females**\n     - Management: Pelvic floor physiotherapy, pain management\n   - **Urinary incontinence/retention**\n     - Management: Urodynamic studies, specialist referral\n   - **Prevention:** Anatomic reduction, recognition and repair of genitourinary injuries\n\n4. **Gait Abnormalities and Functional Limitations**\n   - **Causes:** Leg length discrepancy, pelvic obliquity, muscle weakness, joint stiffness\n     - Management: Physiotherapy, gait training, shoe modifications, mobility aids\n     - Consideration of corrective surgery for severe cases\n   - **Prevention:** Early mobilization, intensive rehabilitation\n\n5. **Heterotopic Ossification (HO) (10-20%)**\n   - **Common locations:** Hip joint, symphysis pubis, sacroiliac joints\n     - Management: NSAIDs for prophylaxis, surgical excision if severely symptomatic\n     - Radiation therapy in selected cases\n   - **Prevention:** Consider prophylaxis in high-risk patients (head injury, prolonged ventilation)\n\n6. **Psychological Sequelae**\n   - **Post-traumatic stress disorder, depression, anxiety**\n     - Management: Psychological assessment, counseling, psychiatric referral if needed\n     - Support groups, cognitive behavioral therapy\n   - **Prevention:** Early psychological support, regular screening\n\n7. **Return to Work and Social Reintegration**\n   - **Only 60-70% return to previous employment**\n     - Management: Vocational rehabilitation, workplace adaptations\n     - Disability assessment and support\n   - **Prevention:** Comprehensive rehabilitation programs, social support\n\n8. **Hardware Complications (5-10%)**\n   - **Implant failure, prominence, or infection**\n     - Management: Hardware removal, revision surgery if needed\n   - **Prevention:** Appropriate implant selection, proper technique\n\nThe management of these complications requires a multidisciplinary approach involving orthopaedic surgeons, rehabilitation specialists, pain management, urology, and psychological support. Regular follow-up is essential to identify and address complications early, with functional outcome measures used to track progress and guide interventions.",
