        "answer": "On the AP pelvis radiograph, I can identify a lateral compression pelvic ring injury with disruption of the pubic symphysis and likely sacroiliac joint involvement on the left side. There appears to be medial displacement of the left hemipelvis consistent with a Young-Burgess lateral compression injury.\n\nMy immediate concerns are:\n\n1. **Haemodynamic stability** - Pelvic fractures can be associated with significant retroperitoneal bleeding (up to 3-4 units of blood). While lateral compression injuries typically have less bleeding than AP compression or vertical shear patterns, vigilance is still required.\n\n2. **Urological injury** - The history of blood at the urethral meatus is highly concerning for urethral or bladder injury. Approximately 15% of pelvic fractures have associated genitourinary injuries.\n\n3. **Associated injuries** - Given the high-energy mechanism, there's significant risk of additional injuries including abdominal solid organ injury, thoracic trauma, and extremity injuries.\n\n4. **Neurological status** - Potential for lumbosacral plexus injury or cauda equina syndrome with pelvic disruption.\n\n5. **Open fracture** - Need to assess for any communication between the fracture and external environment or bladder/bowels, which would significantly alter management.",
