        "answer": "The Young-Burgess classification is a mechanistic classification system for pelvic ring injuries that helps predict injury patterns, associated injuries, and guides management approaches.\n\n**Classification Categories:**\n\n1. **Lateral Compression (LC)** - Most common pattern (50-60%)\n   - **LC I**: Sacral compression on side of impact, pubic rami fractures\n   - **LC II**: LC I + posterior sacroiliac joint disruption with crescent fracture\n   - **LC III**: LC I + contralateral open book injury (rotationally unstable)\n\n2. **Anteroposterior Compression (APC)** - \"Open book\" injuries\n   - **APC I**: Minor symphyseal diastasis (<2.5cm), intact posterior elements\n   - **APC II**: Symphyseal diastasis >2.5cm, anterior SI disruption, intact posterior SI ligaments\n   - **APC III**: Complete disruption of SI joint (anterior and posterior), rotationally and vertically unstable\n\n3. **Vertical Shear (VS)**\n   - Vertical displacement through SI joint, iliac wing, or sacrum\n   - Complete disruption of pelvic floor\n   - Rotationally and vertically unstable\n\n4. **Combined Mechanism (CM)**\n   - Elements of multiple injury patterns\n\n**Management Implications:**\n\n- **Haemorrhage Risk**: Increases from LC I (lowest) to VS (highest)\n  - LC injuries: Venous bleeding predominates\n  - APC/VS injuries: Higher risk of arterial bleeding requiring embolisation\n\n- **Stability Considerations**:\n  - LC I: Usually stable, may need non-operative management\n  - LC II/III, APC II/III, VS: Unstable, typically require surgical fixation\n\n- **Temporary Stabilisation**:\n  - LC: Pelvic binder may worsen deformity in some cases\n  - APC: Pelvic binder highly effective\n  - VS: Pelvic binder helpful but traction may also be needed\n\n- **Definitive Treatment**:\n  - LC I: Often non-operative or percutaneous fixation\n  - LC II/III: Anterior plate fixation ± posterior fixation\n  - APC II: Anterior plate fixation often sufficient\n  - APC III/VS: Anterior and posterior fixation required\n\n- **Associated Injuries**:\n  - LC: Higher rate of urogenital and hollow viscus injuries\n  - APC/VS: Higher rate of major vessel injuries\n\nThe classification guides initial stabilisation methods, need for angiography, surgical approach, and fixation techniques while helping predict associated injuries requiring attention.",
