        "answer": "**Assessment of Urological Injury**\n\n1. **Clinical Evaluation**\n   - Blood at urethral meatus is highly suspicious for urethral injury\n   - Other signs include perineal/scrotal bruising, high-riding prostate (males), inability to void\n   - Assess for associated signs: vaginal bleeding, rectal bleeding\n\n2. **Imaging**\n   - **Retrograde Urethrogram (RUG)**: First-line investigation for suspected urethral injury\n     - Performed by injecting contrast into the urethral meatus\n     - Identifies location and extent of urethral disruption\n   \n   - **CT Cystogram**: Gold standard for bladder injury\n     - Requires adequate bladder distension (350-400ml of dilute contrast)\n     - Can identify extraperitoneal vs intraperitoneal rupture\n     - Should be performed after RUG if urethral injury suspected\n\n   - **CT with IV contrast**: May show contrast extravasation but less sensitive than dedicated studies\n\n3. **Classification of Injuries**\n   - **Urethral injuries**: Anterior vs posterior urethral disruption\n   - **Bladder injuries**: Contusion, extraperitoneal rupture, intraperitoneal rupture\n\n**Management Approach**\n\n1. **Immediate Management**\n   - **Avoid blind urethral catheterisation** - can convert partial tear to complete disruption\n   - Urgent urological consultation\n   - Consider suprapubic catheter placement if urinary diversion needed\n\n2. **Urethral Injury Management**\n   - **Partial tears**: May be managed with urethral catheterisation under urological guidance\n   - **Complete posterior urethral disruption**:\n     - Traditional approach: Suprapubic catheter and delayed urethroplasty (3-6 months)\n     - Alternative: Primary endoscopic realignment in selected cases\n   - **Anterior urethral injuries**: Often managed with urinary diversion and delayed repair\n\n3. **Bladder Injury Management**\n   - **Contusion**: Catheter drainage for 3-5 days\n   - **Extraperitoneal rupture**: Usually managed with catheter drainage for 10-14 days\n   - **Intraperitoneal rupture**: Requires surgical repair and catheter drainage\n\n4. **Coordination with Orthopaedic Management**\n   - Timing of pelvic stabilisation may need to be coordinated with urological interventions\n   - External fixation may be performed concurrently with urological procedures\n   - Internal fixation timing depends on urological injury severity and contamination risk\n\n5. **Follow-up**\n   - Cystogram prior to catheter removal\n   - Long-term follow-up for stricture development (urethral injuries)\n   - Urodynamic studies if bladder dysfunction persists\n\n",
