        "answer": "**Indications for Angiography and Embolisation in Pelvic Fractures**\n\n**Absolute Indications:**\n\n1. **Haemodynamic instability despite adequate resuscitation**\n   - Persistent hypotension (SBP <90mmHg) after initial fluid resuscitation\n   - Ongoing transfusion requirements (>2 units PRBC/hour) despite pelvic stabilisation\n\n2. **Active extravasation on CT**\n   - Contrast blush indicating arterial bleeding\n   - This finding has high specificity for arterial injury requiring embolisation\n\n3. **Expanding pelvic haematoma on serial imaging**\n   - Progressive increase in haematoma size despite stabilisation measures\n\n**Relative Indications:**\n\n1. **High-risk fracture patterns**\n   - Vertical shear injuries (Young-Burgess VS)\n   - Severe anteroposterior compression injuries (APC II/III)\n   - Fractures with significant displacement of posterior elements\n\n2. **Large pelvic haematoma on initial CT**\n   - Even without visible extravasation, large haematomas may indicate significant bleeding\n\n3. **Advanced age (>60 years) with significant pelvic fracture**\n   - Lower physiological reserve and higher mortality risk\n\n4. **Specific vessel injuries identified on CT**\n   - Corona mortis\n   - Superior gluteal artery\n   - Internal iliac artery branches\n\n5. **Failed non-operative management**\n   - Initial response to resuscitation followed by deterioration\n\n**Timing Considerations:**\n\n- **Immediate angiography** is indicated for haemodynamically unstable patients not responding to initial resuscitation and pelvic stabilisation\n\n- **Early angiography** (within 3 hours) is recommended for patients with active extravasation on CT, even if temporarily stabilised\n\n- **Delayed angiography** may be considered for patients who initially stabilise but later deteriorate\n\n**Procedural Approach:**\n\n- Selective internal iliac artery angiography is typically performed first\n\n- Embolisation strategies include:\n  - **Selective embolisation**: Targeting specific bleeding vessels\n  - **Non-selective embolisation**: Embolisation of the anterior division or entire internal iliac artery\n\n- Materials used include gelfoam, coils, or particles depending on vessel size and bleeding severity\n\n**Outcomes and Considerations:**\n\n- Success rates of 85-97% for controlling arterial haemorrhage\n\n- Potential complications include gluteal muscle necrosis, bladder necrosis, and sexual dysfunction (particularly with bilateral non-selective embolisation)\n\n- Should be part of a multidisciplinary approach including orthopaedic stabilisation and critical care management\n\nThis approach aligns with the Western Trauma Association and Eastern Association for the Surgery of Trauma guidelines for management of pelvic fracture haemorrhage.",
