        "answer": "**Principles of Definitive Surgical Management for Lateral Compression Pelvic Fractures**\n\n**Assessment and Classification Refinement**\n\n1. **Detailed Imaging Review**\n   - CT scan with 3D reconstruction to fully characterize:\n     - Sacral impaction/fracture pattern\n     - Pubic rami fractures\n     - SI joint disruption\n     - Presence of posterior crescent fracture (LC II)\n     - Contralateral injury (LC III)\n\n2. **Stability Assessment**\n   - LC I: Usually rotationally stable\n   - LC II: Rotationally unstable due to posterior injury\n   - LC III: Rotationally and sometimes vertically unstable\n\n**Timing of Surgery**\n\n1. **Emergency Surgery (within 24 hours)**\n   - Open fractures\n   - Associated vascular injuries requiring repair\n   - Neurological compromise from fracture displacement\n\n2. **Early Surgery (24-72 hours)**\n   - Most LC II and LC III injuries\n   - After resuscitation and stabilization of other injuries\n   - Before soft tissue contracture develops\n\n3. **Delayed Surgery (>72 hours)**\n   - Some stable LC I injuries that fail conservative management\n   - Patients requiring medical optimization\n\n**Surgical Approaches and Techniques**\n\n1. **LC I Management**\n   - Often non-operative if minimally displaced\n   - Percutaneous iliosacral screws for symptomatic cases\n   - Limited anterior fixation for symptomatic rami fractures\n\n2. **LC II Management**\n   - **Posterior Approach**:\n     - Direct reduction and fixation of posterior crescent fracture\n     - Options include:\n       - 3.5mm reconstruction plates\n       - Lag screws with washers\n       - Iliosacral screws (if adequate sacral corridor)\n\n   - **Anterior Approach**:\n     - Symphyseal plating or rami fixation if significantly displaced\n     - Usually through Pfannenstiel or modified Stoppa approach\n\n3. **LC III Management**\n   - Combined anterior and posterior fixation\n   - Posterior: Iliosacral screws or posterior plating\n   - Anterior: Symphyseal plating and/or rami fixation\n   - May require external fixation initially\n\n**Technical Considerations**\n\n1. **Reduction Techniques**\n   - LC injuries often require lateral compression for reduction\n   - Ball-spike pushers, reduction clamps, and Schanz pins as joysticks\n   - Specialized pelvic reduction frames may be helpful\n\n2. **Fixation Options**\n   - **Posterior**:\n     - 7.0mm or 7.3mm cannulated iliosacral screws\n     - 3.5mm reconstruction plates for crescent fractures\n     - Lumbopelvic fixation for comminuted sacral fractures\n\n   - **Anterior**:\n     - 3.5mm reconstruction plates for symphysis\n     - Intramedullary screws or plates for rami fractures\n\n3. **Minimally Invasive Options**\n   - Percutaneous iliosacral screws under fluoroscopic or navigation guidance\n   - Percutaneous anterior column screws\n   - Reduces soft tissue disruption and blood loss\n\n**Special Considerations with Urological Injury**\n\n1. **Surgical Sequence**\n   - Urological repair typically precedes definitive pelvic fixation\n   - External fixation may be used as temporary stabilization\n\n2. **Approach Modifications**\n   - Extended iliofemoral approach may be needed for combined orthopaedic and urological access\n   - Consider staged procedures if extensive contamination\n\n3. **Implant Selection**\n   - Minimize hardware near urological repairs\n   - Consider absorbable antibiotic beads in contaminated cases\n\n**Post-operative Protocol**\n\n1. **Mobilization**\n   - Toe-touch weight bearing for 6-8 weeks on affected side\n   - Progressive weight bearing based on radiographic healing\n\n2. **Thromboprophylaxis**\n   - Extended chemical prophylaxis (4-6 weeks)\n   - Consider IVC filter in high-risk patients\n\n3. **Follow-up**\n   - Radiographs at 2, 6, 12 weeks, then 6 and 12 months\n   - CT scan at 3 months to assess posterior ring healing\n\n",
