        "answer": "**Potential Complications of Pelvic Fractures**\n\n**Acute Complications**\n\n1. **Haemorrhage**\n   - **Management**: Pelvic binder, resuscitation, angioembolisation, preperitoneal packing\n   - **Prevention**: Early recognition and stabilization\n\n2. **Urogenital Injuries (10-25%)**\n   - **Urethral/bladder disruption**\n     - **Management**: Urological consultation, suprapubic catheter, delayed reconstruction\n     - **Prevention**: Avoid blind catheterization when suspected\n\n   - **Vaginal/perineal lacerations**\n     - **Management**: Early repair, antibiotics, consider diverting colostomy if rectal involvement\n     - **Prevention**: Thorough secondary survey\n\n3. **Neurological Injury (10-15%)**\n   - **Lumbosacral plexus injury**\n     - **Management**: Documentation, anatomic reduction, neurology consultation\n     - **Prevention**: Careful reduction techniques\n\n   - **Cauda equina syndrome**\n     - **Management**: Urgent decompression if due to canal compromise\n     - **Prevention**: Early recognition and imaging\n\n4. **Gastrointestinal Injuries (2-10%)**\n   - **Rectal tears/bowel injury**\n     - **Management**: General surgery consultation, repair, possible diversion\n     - **Prevention**: Rectal examination, high index of suspicion\n\n**Early Post-injury Complications**\n\n1. **Venous Thromboembolism (VTE) (35-60% without prophylaxis)**\n   - **Management**: Early chemical and mechanical prophylaxis, IVC filter if contraindicated\n   - **Prevention**: Risk assessment, early mobilization\n\n2. **Infection (2-5% closed, 30-50% open)**\n   - **Surgical site infection**\n     - **Management**: Antibiotics, debridement, hardware retention vs. removal\n     - **Prevention**: Perioperative antibiotics, meticulous technique\n\n   - **Urinary tract infection**\n     - **Management**: Culture-directed antibiotics, catheter management\n     - **Prevention**: Aseptic catheter care, removal when feasible\n\n3. **Compartment Syndrome**\n   - **Management**: Fasciotomy, delayed closure\n   - **Prevention**: High index of suspicion, regular assessment\n\n**Late Complications**\n\n1. **Malunion/Nonunion (5-15%)**\n   - **Management**: Revision surgery, bone grafting, adjusted fixation\n   - **Prevention**: Anatomic reduction, stable fixation\n\n2. **Post-traumatic Arthritis (SI joint, hip) (20-30% at 10 years)**\n   - **Management**: Analgesia, activity modification, SI joint fusion or THR if severe\n   - **Prevention**: Anatomic reduction of articular surfaces\n\n3. **Chronic Pain (30-40%)**\n   - **Management**: Multimodal pain approach, psychological support\n   - **Prevention**: Appropriate fixation, early rehabilitation\n\n4. **Sexual Dysfunction (30-40%)**\n   - **Male erectile dysfunction**\n     - **Management**: Urology referral, medical therapy, psychological support\n     - **Prevention**: Avoid nerve damage during approaches\n\n   - **Female dyspareunia**\n     - **Management**: Gynecology referral, pelvic floor therapy\n     - **Prevention**: Anatomic reduction\n\n5. **Urological Sequelae**\n   - **Urethral stricture (70-100% after complete disruption)**\n     - **Management**: Urethroplasty, dilatation\n     - **Prevention**: Appropriate initial management\n\n   - **Incontinence (5-20%)**\n     - **Management**: Pelvic floor therapy, urological intervention\n     - **Prevention**: Proper urethral repair\n\n6. **Leg Length Discrepancy**\n   - **Management**: Shoe lifts, osteotomy for severe cases\n   - **Prevention**: Restoration of pelvic height\n\n7. **Heterotopic Ossification (18-90%)**\n   - **Management**: NSAIDs, radiation therapy, surgical excision if symptomatic\n   - **Prevention**: Prophylactic NSAIDs or radiation in high-risk cases\n\n**Functional Complications**\n\n1. **Gait Abnormalities (30-70%)**\n   - **Management**: Physiotherapy, assistive devices, corrective surgery\n   - **Prevention**: Anatomic reduction, early mobilization\n\n2. **Return to Work/Activities**\n   - 60-80% return to previous employment by 12 months\n   - **Management**: Vocational rehabilitation, graduated return\n   - **Prevention**: Comprehensive rehabilitation program\n\n3. **Psychological Sequelae (PTSD, depression) (20-50%)**\n   - **Management**: Psychological assessment, therapy, medication if indicated\n   - **Prevention**: Early psychological support\n\nManagement should follow a multidisciplinary approach with input from orthopaedics, urology, general surgery, rehabilitation medicine, pain specialists, and psychological support as needed.",
