        "answer": "The duty of candour is a professional and statutory obligation to be open and honest with patients when something goes wrong with their treatment or care that causes, or has the potential to cause, harm or distress.\n\nIn this scenario, while there hasn't been a treatment error, the duty of candour principles still apply in terms of being open, honest and transparent with Mr. Wilson about:\n\n1. The suspected diagnosis of a bone tumor\n2. The uncertainty around the exact diagnosis until biopsy results are available\n3. The potential implications of the diagnosis\n4. The next steps in management\n5. The expected timeline for investigations and results\n\nKey elements of fulfilling the duty of candour in this case include:\n\n- Providing a truthful account of the findings without unnecessary delay\n- Explaining the situation in a way Mr. Wilson can understand\n- Offering an appropriate apology for the distress caused by the news\n- Documenting the conversation in detail in the medical records\n- Providing reasonable support, including written information and contact details\n- Ensuring appropriate follow-up is arranged\n\nThe duty of candour is enshrined in the Health and Social Care Act 2008 (Regulated Activities) Regulations 2014: Regulation 20, and is a key component of GMC Good Medical Practice guidance.",
