    "interview_questions": [
      {
        "answer": "The radiographs show a tibial plateau fracture of the right knee. Specifically, I can identify depression and splitting of the lateral tibial plateau with articular surface involvement. There appears to be significant displacement and depression of the articular surface (>2mm), which would be concerning for joint instability and future post-traumatic arthritis.\n\nMy primary concerns for this patient include:\n\n1. **Neurovascular status** - Tibial plateau fractures can be associated with popliteal artery injury and compartment syndrome, especially in high-energy mechanisms\n\n2. **Associated soft tissue injuries** - Up to 90% of tibial plateau fractures have associated soft tissue injuries including:\n   - Meniscal tears (especially lateral meniscus)\n   - Cruciate ligament injuries (particularly ACL)\n   - Collateral ligament injuries (MCL/LCL)\n   - Posterolateral corner injuries\n\n3. **Articular surface disruption** - The degree of depression and displacement will influence management decisions and long-term prognosis\n\n4. **Soft tissue envelope** - The condition of the soft tissues will dictate timing of definitive fixation",
        "question": "What do you see on these radiographs and what is your primary concern for this patient?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "I would classify this tibial plateau fracture using the **Schatzker classification**, which is the most widely used system for tibial plateau fractures. Based on the radiographs, this appears to be a Schatzker type II fracture (split depression of the lateral tibial plateau).\n\nThe complete Schatzker classification includes:\n\n- **Type I**: Pure split fracture of the lateral tibial plateau without depression\n- **Type II**: Split depression fracture of the lateral tibial plateau (split fracture combined with depression of the articular surface)\n- **Type III**: Pure depression of the lateral tibial plateau without split\n- **Type IV**: Medial tibial plateau fracture (split or depression)\n- **Type V**: Bicondylar tibial plateau fracture (both medial and lateral plateaus, with metaphysis and diaphysis remaining continuous)\n- **Type VI**: Tibial plateau fracture with dissociation between metaphysis and diaphysis\n\nI would also use the AO/OTA classification system as an alternative:\n- 41-B (partial articular fractures of the proximal tibia)\n  - B1: lateral plateau fracture\n  - B2: medial plateau fracture\n  - B3: both plateaus fractured\n\nFor complete assessment, I would request a CT scan with 3D reconstruction to better understand the fracture pattern, degree of depression, and comminution, which would guide my definitive management plan.",
        "question": "How would you classify this tibial plateau fracture?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "For this tibial plateau fracture, I would request the following additional imaging:\n\n1. **CT scan with 3D reconstruction**\n   - Essential for accurate classification and operative planning\n   - Provides detailed information about:\n     - Exact fracture pattern and comminution\n     - Precise measurement of articular depression (mm)\n     - Presence of posterior fragment involvement (often missed on plain radiographs)\n     - Degree of condylar widening\n     - Integrity of the cortical rim (important for screw placement)\n   - Helps determine optimal surgical approach and fixation strategy\n   - Guides the need for bone grafting\n\n2. **MRI scan**\n   - Indicated if suspicion of significant soft tissue injury\n   - Evaluates:\n     - Meniscal tears (present in up to 90% of cases)\n     - Cruciate ligament injuries (particularly ACL)\n     - Collateral ligament injuries\n     - Posterolateral corner injuries\n     - Occult fracture lines not visible on CT\n   - Influences timing and approach to surgery\n   - May change management if significant soft tissue injuries are identified\n\n3. **Stress radiographs** (if clinically indicated)\n   - To assess ligamentous stability if examination limited by pain\n   - Varus/valgus stress views to evaluate collateral ligament integrity\n\n4. **CT angiogram or formal angiography**\n   - If vascular injury is suspected based on clinical examination\n   - Particularly important in high-energy injuries or Schatzker V-VI fractures\n   - Essential if diminished pulses or expanding hematoma present",
        "question": "What additional imaging would you request and why?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Management of this 30-year-old male with a tibial plateau fracture would follow these principles:\n\n**Immediate Management:**\n1. **ATLS protocol** if polytrauma\n2. **Pain control** - IV opiates titrated to effect\n3. **Immobilization** - Above-knee backslab in slight flexion (15-20°)\n4. **Elevation and ice** to reduce swelling\n5. **Neurovascular assessment** and documentation\n6. **Compartment syndrome monitoring** - regular checks and low threshold for compartment pressure measurement\n7. **Antibiotics** if open fracture as per BOAST guidelines (co-amoxiclav or cefuroxime + gentamicin for grade I/II; add metronidazole for grade III)\n\n**Pre-operative Planning:**\n1. **Complete imaging** - CT scan with 3D reconstruction and possibly MRI\n2. **Fracture classification** - Schatzker and AO/OTA systems\n3. **Soft tissue assessment** - Determine if immediate ORIF is possible or if staged approach needed\n4. **Implant selection** - Based on fracture pattern (locking plates, screws, external fixation)\n\n**Definitive Management:**\n\nFor this Schatzker II fracture with significant articular depression (>2mm) and displacement, I would recommend operative intervention. The specific approach depends on soft tissue status:\n\n- **If soft tissues favorable:**\n  - ORIF with anatomic reduction of articular surface\n  - Lateral locking plate fixation\n  - Bone grafting for depression (autograft, allograft or bone substitute)\n  - Consider arthroscopic assistance for articular reduction\n\n- **If soft tissues compromised:**\n  - Staged approach with initial spanning external fixation\n  - Definitive ORIF when soft tissues permit (typically 7-14 days)\n\n**Surgical Technique:**\n1. Anterolateral approach to the proximal tibia\n2. Elevation of depressed fragments\n3. Bone grafting of the void\n4. Anatomic reduction of articular surface (aim for <2mm step-off)\n5. Buttress plate fixation (typically locking plate)\n6. Address any associated soft tissue injuries\n\n**Post-operative Care:**\n1. **Weight-bearing** - Non-weight-bearing for 6-12 weeks depending on fracture pattern and fixation\n2. **Range of motion** - Early ROM exercises when soft tissues permit\n3. **DVT prophylaxis** - LMWH until mobile\n4. **Physiotherapy** - Tailored rehabilitation program\n5. **Follow-up** - Regular clinical and radiological assessment\n\nThis management plan follows the principles outlined in NICE guidance for complex fractures and BOAST guidelines for open fractures if applicable.",
        "question": "How would you manage this patient?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "For a Schatzker type II tibial plateau fracture (split depression of the lateral plateau), several specific operative considerations are important:\n\n**Timing of Surgery:**\n- Ideally within 24-48 hours if soft tissues permit\n- Delay if significant swelling, fracture blisters, or compromised skin\n- Consider spanning external fixation if definitive surgery needs to be delayed\n\n**Surgical Approach:**\n1. **Anterolateral approach** - Standard for lateral plateau fractures\n   - Provides excellent visualization of the lateral plateau\n   - Allows for direct reduction of the split component\n   - Facilitates buttress plate application\n\n2. **Arthroscopic-assisted technique**\n   - Advantages: Better visualization of articular reduction, assessment and treatment of associated intra-articular injuries, less soft tissue dissection\n   - Disadvantages: Technical difficulty, fluid extravasation risk, longer operative time\n   - Best for simple depression patterns with intact cortical rim\n\n**Reduction Technique:**\n1. Exposure of the lateral plateau through a submeniscal arthrotomy\n2. Direct visualization of the articular surface\n3. Creation of a cortical window in the metaphysis below the depression\n4. Elevation of the depressed fragment using a bone tamp or elevator\n5. Temporary K-wire fixation of the split component\n\n**Void Management:**\n- **Bone grafting** essential to support the elevated articular surface\n- Options include:\n  - Autograft (iliac crest) - Gold standard but donor site morbidity\n  - Allograft - Good alternative without donor site morbidity\n  - Synthetic bone substitutes (calcium phosphate, calcium sulfate) - Provide immediate structural support\n\n**Fixation Strategy:**\n1. **Lateral locking plate** - Provides angular stability\n2. **Subchondral raft of screws** - Supports the elevated articular surface\n3. **Independent lag screws** - For the split component perpendicular to the fracture line\n4. **Consideration of dual plating** if there is posteromedial involvement\n\n**Management of Associated Injuries:**\n- Meniscal repair if tear identified (common with lateral plateau fractures)\n- ACL reconstruction typically delayed if needed\n- Collateral ligament injuries often heal with fracture treatment\n\n**Intraoperative Assessment:**\n- Fluoroscopic confirmation of reduction quality\n- Stress testing for stability after fixation\n- Range of motion assessment\n- Consideration of drain placement\n\n**Technical Pearls:**\n- Aim for anatomic reduction with <2mm step-off\n- Avoid lateral joint overcompression\n- Ensure adequate subchondral support\n- Consider posterior fragment fixation if present (may require additional approach)\n- Avoid screw penetration into the joint",
        "question": "What are the specific operative considerations for this fracture pattern?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Tibial plateau fractures can lead to numerous complications, both early and late:\n\n**Early Complications:**\n\n1. **Compartment Syndrome (5-17%)**\n   - Higher risk in Schatzker IV-VI and high-energy injuries\n   - Management: Emergent fasciotomy of all four compartments\n   - Monitoring: Regular clinical assessment, compartment pressure measurement if exam limited\n\n2. **Neurovascular Injury**\n   - Popliteal artery injury in 3-5% of cases (higher in high-energy trauma)\n   - Common peroneal nerve injury (especially with posterolateral corner injuries)\n   - Management: Vascular surgery consultation, urgent repair if arterial injury\n\n3. **Infection**\n   - Superficial: 3-8%, Deep: 2-4% (higher in open fractures)\n   - Risk factors: Open fracture, prolonged surgery, diabetes, smoking\n   - Management: Debridement, targeted antibiotics, possible implant removal if chronic\n\n4. **Wound Complications**\n   - Dehiscence, necrosis, fracture blisters\n   - Management: Delayed closure, VAC therapy, possible plastic surgery involvement\n\n5. **Venous Thromboembolism (DVT/PE)**\n   - Incidence: 10-20% without prophylaxis\n   - Management: Chemical prophylaxis (LMWH), mechanical methods, early mobilization\n\n**Late Complications:**\n\n1. **Post-traumatic Arthritis (20-44%)**\n   - Risk factors: Articular incongruity >2mm, meniscal injury, axial malalignment\n   - Management: Conservative (analgesia, activity modification, injections) or surgical (osteotomy, arthroplasty)\n\n2. **Malunion**\n   - Varus/valgus deformity, articular step-off, condylar widening\n   - Management: Corrective osteotomy if symptomatic and functional impairment\n\n3. **Nonunion (1-5%)**\n   - Risk factors: Smoking, diabetes, open fractures, infection\n   - Management: Revision ORIF with bone grafting, possible external fixation\n\n4. **Hardware Complications**\n   - Prominent hardware (10-15%)\n   - Screw penetration into joint\n   - Implant failure/breakage\n   - Management: Hardware removal after fracture healing if symptomatic\n\n5. **Knee Stiffness (5-10%)**\n   - Risk factors: Prolonged immobilization, complex fracture patterns, infection\n   - Management: Aggressive physiotherapy, possible manipulation under anesthesia, arthroscopic lysis of adhesions\n\n6. **Chronic Pain**\n   - Multifactorial: Hardware irritation, arthritis, complex regional pain syndrome\n   - Management: Multimodal approach including pain specialists\n\n**Prevention Strategies:**\n\n1. **Anatomic reduction** of articular surface (aim for <2mm step-off)\n2. **Stable fixation** allowing early range of motion\n3. **Early mobilization** and rehabilitation\n4. **Appropriate soft tissue handling** and timing of surgery\n5. **Meniscal preservation** whenever possible\n6. **Correction of mechanical alignment**\n7. **Prophylactic antibiotics** and strict aseptic technique\n8. **DVT prophylaxis**\n\n**Long-term Outcomes:**\n- 70-80% good to excellent results with appropriate management\n- Worse outcomes associated with: high-energy injuries, Schatzker V-VI patterns, associated soft tissue injuries, age >50 years, and residual articular incongruity",
        "question": "What are the potential complications of tibial plateau fractures and their management?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "**Role of Arthroscopy in Tibial Plateau Fractures**\n\nArthroscopy has emerged as a valuable adjunct in the management of selected tibial plateau fractures, offering several advantages while also having specific limitations:\n\n**Advantages:**\n\n1. **Superior Articular Visualization**\n   - Direct magnified view of the entire articular surface\n   - Better assessment of reduction quality compared to fluoroscopy alone\n   - Ability to identify small osteochondral fragments that may be missed on imaging\n\n2. **Diagnosis and Treatment of Associated Injuries**\n   - Meniscal tears (present in 50-90% of cases)\n   - Cruciate ligament injuries (particularly ACL)\n   - Chondral injuries\n   - Allows for immediate treatment (meniscal repair/partial meniscectomy)\n\n3. **Minimally Invasive Approach**\n   - Reduced soft tissue dissection compared to open arthrotomy\n   - Potentially lower infection rates\n   - Less post-operative pain and scarring\n   - Faster rehabilitation in some cases\n\n4. **Lavage of Hemarthrosis and Debris**\n   - Removal of loose bodies and fracture debris\n   - Thorough joint irrigation\n\n**Ideal Fracture Patterns for Arthroscopic Assistance:**\n\n- Schatzker types I-III (lateral plateau fractures)\n- Selected Schatzker type IV (medial plateau) if minimal displacement\n- Pure depression fractures with intact cortical rim\n- Fractures with suspected intra-articular pathology\n\n**Technique:**\n\n1. Standard arthroscopic portals (anteromedial and anterolateral)\n2. Diagnostic arthroscopy to assess all intra-articular structures\n3. Percutaneous or limited open reduction of split component\n4. Fluoroscopic guidance for subchondral bone elevation\n5. Arthroscopic visualization during reduction\n6. Percutaneous or limited open fixation\n7. Final arthroscopic assessment of reduction quality\n\n**Limitations and Contraindications:**\n\n1. **Fluid Extravasation Risk**\n   - Can lead to compartment syndrome\n   - Requires careful monitoring of compartment pressures\n   - Use of fluid pump with controlled pressure settings\n\n2. **Technical Difficulty**\n   - Steep learning curve\n   - Longer operative time initially\n   - Challenging in complex fracture patterns\n\n3. **Limited Application in Complex Fractures**\n   - Not ideal for Schatzker V-VI (bicondylar patterns)\n   - Difficult in highly comminuted fractures\n   - Challenging with significant displacement\n\n4. **Contraindications:**\n   - Open fractures\n   - Compartment syndrome\n   - Severe soft tissue injury\n   - Vascular injury requiring repair\n   - Highly comminuted fractures requiring extensive reconstruction\n\n**Evidence Base:**\n\nSystematic reviews and meta-analyses have shown:\n- Comparable or better functional outcomes compared to open techniques\n- Lower infection rates\n- Similar quality of reduction\n- Improved diagnosis of associated soft tissue injuries\n- Potential for faster rehabilitation\n\nHowever, most studies are retrospective or small prospective series, and high-quality RCTs are limited. The decision to use arthroscopy should be individualized based on fracture pattern, surgeon experience, and available resources.",
        "question": "What is the role of arthroscopy in the management of tibial plateau fractures?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "**Evidence on Weight-Bearing Protocols After Tibial Plateau Fracture Fixation**\n\nWeight-bearing protocols following tibial plateau fracture fixation have evolved significantly, with recent evidence challenging traditional non-weight-bearing restrictions:\n\n**Traditional Approach:**\n- Non-weight-bearing (NWB) for 6-12 weeks\n- Followed by progressive weight-bearing over additional 6-12 weeks\n- Based on theoretical concerns about fixation failure and articular collapse\n\n**Current Evidence:**\n\n1. **Biomechanical Studies:**\n   - Modern locking plate constructs can withstand physiological loads during partial weight-bearing\n   - Subchondral raft screws significantly increase construct strength\n   - Calcium phosphate cement augmentation provides immediate structural support allowing earlier loading\n\n2. **Clinical Studies:**\n\n   - **Segal et al. (2006)** - Retrospective study showing no difference in outcomes between early (<12 weeks) and late (>12 weeks) weight-bearing\n\n   - **Solomon et al. (2011)** - Prospective study demonstrating safety of early progressive weight-bearing at 2 weeks with locking plates\n\n   - **Williamson et al. (2019)** - RCT showing no difference in radiographic or functional outcomes between immediate partial weight-bearing and delayed weight-bearing groups\n\n   - **POSTIT Trial (2020)** - Multicenter RCT comparing early vs. delayed weight-bearing showing no difference in union rates or functional outcomes at 1 year\n\n3. **Systematic Reviews:**\n   - Recent meta-analyses suggest early progressive weight-bearing is safe with modern fixation techniques\n   - No significant increase in fixation failure, malunion, or articular subsidence with early weight-bearing protocols\n   - Potential benefits in muscle preservation, bone healing, and functional recovery\n\n**Current Recommendations Based on Evidence:**\n\n1. **Fracture Pattern-Specific Approach:**\n\n   - **Schatzker I-III (unicondylar):**\n     - Touch-down weight-bearing (TDWB) immediately post-op\n     - Progressive weight-bearing from 2-4 weeks if stable fixation achieved\n     - Full weight-bearing by 6-8 weeks\n\n   - **Schatzker IV (medial):**\n     - TDWB for 2-4 weeks\n     - Progressive weight-bearing from 4-6 weeks\n     - Full weight-bearing by 8-10 weeks\n\n   - **Schatzker V-VI (bicondylar):**\n     - TDWB for 4-6 weeks\n     - Progressive weight-bearing from 6-8 weeks\n     - Full weight-bearing by 10-12 weeks\n\n2. **Fixation-Dependent Approach:**\n\n   - **Locking plates with subchondral support:**\n     - Earlier progressive weight-bearing (2-4 weeks)\n\n   - **Conventional plating without locking technology:**\n     - More conservative approach (6-8 weeks NWB)\n\n   - **Calcium phosphate augmentation:**\n     - Allows earlier weight-bearing due to immediate structural support\n\n3. **Patient-Specific Factors:**\n\n   - **Bone quality** - Osteoporotic bone may require longer protection\n   - **Compliance** - Reliable patients may progress faster\n   - **BMI** - Higher BMI may necessitate longer protection\n   - **Associated injuries** - May limit weight-bearing independent of fracture healing\n\n**Practical Approach:**\n\nThe current evidence supports a more individualized approach to weight-bearing after tibial plateau fracture fixation:\n\n1. Immediate post-operative range of motion exercises\n2. Early partial/touch-down weight-bearing with modern locking constructs\n3. Progressive weight-bearing based on clinical and radiographic assessment\n4. Consideration of fracture pattern, fixation stability, bone quality, and patient factors\n\nThis approach balances the benefits of early mobilization (improved cartilage health, reduced muscle atrophy, enhanced bone healing) with the need to protect fixation until sufficient healing occurs.",
