        "answer": "Enhanced Recovery After Surgery (ERAS) in elective orthopaedic procedures is built around several evidence-based principles designed to improve patient outcomes and reduce length of stay:\n\n**Pre-operative Phase:**\n- **Patient education and expectation management** - Comprehensive information about the procedure, recovery timeline, and active patient participation\n- **Pre-habilitation** - Structured exercise programs to optimize physical condition before surgery\n- **Nutritional optimization** - Addressing malnutrition, anemia correction (target Hb >130 g/L), and carbohydrate loading\n- **Medical optimization** - Managing chronic conditions (diabetes, hypertension, etc.) to reduce perioperative risk\n\n**Perioperative Phase:**\n- **Minimally invasive surgical techniques** where appropriate\n- **Multimodal analgesia** - Combining regional techniques, opioid-sparing strategies, and scheduled non-opioid medications\n- **Rational fluid management** - Goal-directed therapy to maintain euvolemia\n- **Prevention of nausea and vomiting** - Risk assessment and prophylactic antiemetics\n- **Normothermia maintenance** - Active warming during surgery\n- **Minimizing surgical drains and urinary catheters**\n\n**Post-operative Phase:**\n- **Early mobilization** - Ideally within 4-6 hours post-surgery, but certainly day 0-1\n- **Early oral nutrition** - Return to normal diet as soon as possible\n- **Continued multimodal analgesia** with regular review\n- **Thromboprophylaxis** as per NICE guidelines\n- **Criteria-based discharge** rather than time-based\n\nThe BOA, BASK, and AAGBI have published guidelines supporting these principles, with evidence showing reduced complications, improved patient satisfaction, and shorter hospital stays (typically 1-3 days for hip and knee arthroplasty compared to traditional 5-10 days).",
        "question": "What are the key principles of Enhanced Recovery After Surgery (ERAS) in elective orthopaedic procedures?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Pre-operative optimization is crucial for successful enhanced recovery and involves a systematic approach across multiple domains:\n\n**Medical Optimization:**\n- **Anemia management**: Target Hb >130 g/L (both sexes). Iron supplementation (oral or IV) for iron-deficiency anemia at least 2 weeks pre-op\n- **Diabetes control**: Target HbA1c <69 mmol/mol (8.5%); involve diabetes team if poorly controlled\n- **Cardiovascular status**: Optimize blood pressure control (<160/90 mmHg); cardiology review for unstable cardiac conditions\n- **Respiratory function**: Pulmonary rehabilitation for COPD patients; smoking cessation at least 4 weeks pre-op (reduces complications by up to 50%)\n\n**Nutritional Optimization:**\n- Screen for malnutrition using validated tools (e.g., MUST score)\n- Protein supplementation for malnourished patients\n- Carbohydrate loading drinks 2-6 hours pre-operatively (reduces insulin resistance and catabolism)\n- Clear fasting guidelines (6 hours for solids, 2 hours for clear fluids)\n\n**Functional Optimization:**\n- **Pre-habilitation**: Structured exercise program focusing on strengthening, range of motion, and cardiovascular fitness\n- Baseline functional assessment (Oxford scores, TUG test, 6-minute walk test)\n- Gait training and mobility aid familiarization if needed\n\n**Psychosocial Preparation:**\n- **Patient education**: Joint school attendance explaining the pathway, expectations, and active participation\n- Anxiety management strategies\n- Discharge planning and home assessment early in the process\n- Social support network identification and mobilization\n\n**Medication Management:**\n- Review and optimize regular medications\n- Plan for perioperative anticoagulant management\n- Pre-emptive analgesia education and planning\n- MRSA decolonization if positive\n\n**Practical Preparation:**\n- Home modifications completed before admission\n- Essential items moved to accessible locations\n- Post-discharge transport arrangements confirmed\n- Caregiver education and preparation\n\nThis comprehensive approach has been shown to reduce complications by 30-50% and length of stay by 1-3 days in elective orthopaedic surgery.",
        "question": "How would you optimize a patient pre-operatively as part of an enhanced recovery pathway?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Multimodal analgesia is a cornerstone of enhanced recovery protocols, combining different analgesic mechanisms to maximize pain control while minimizing side effects, particularly from opioids:\n\n**Pre-operative Analgesia:**\n- **Patient education** on pain expectations and management strategies\n- **Pre-emptive analgesia**: Typically acetaminophen (paracetamol) 1g and gabapentinoid (e.g., pregabalin 75-150mg) 2 hours pre-operatively\n- **COX-2 inhibitors** (e.g., celecoxib 200-400mg) if no contraindications\n\n**Regional Anesthesia Techniques:**\n- **Spinal anesthesia** as the preferred technique over general anesthesia (reduces PONV, urinary retention, and cognitive dysfunction)\n- **For hip arthroplasty**: Fascia iliaca block or local infiltration analgesia (LIA)\n- **For knee arthroplasty**: Adductor canal block (preserves quadriceps function better than femoral nerve block) combined with LIA\n- **Local infiltration analgesia (LIA)**: Systematic infiltration of the surgical site with long-acting local anesthetics (e.g., ropivacaine 0.2%), often with adjuncts like ketorolac and adrenaline\n\n**Intraoperative Considerations:**\n- **Dexamethasone** 8-10mg IV (analgesic and antiemetic properties)\n- **Ketamine** low-dose (0.1-0.5 mg/kg) for opioid-tolerant patients\n- **Tranexamic acid** to reduce bleeding and associated pain\n- **Avoidance of long-acting opioids**\n\n**Post-operative Regimen:**\n1. **Regular scheduled medications**:\n   - Acetaminophen (paracetamol) 1g QDS\n   - NSAID/COX-2 inhibitor if not contraindicated\n   - Gabapentinoid for 3-5 days in selected patients\n\n2. **Opioid management**:\n   - Short-acting opioids for breakthrough pain only\n   - Patient-controlled analgesia (PCA) rarely needed with effective regional techniques\n   - Rapid transition to oral opioids when needed\n   - Opioid reduction plan from day 1\n\n3. **Adjunctive measures**:\n   - Ice therapy/cryotherapy\n   - Elevation\n   - Early mobilization (itself reduces pain)\n   - Cognitive behavioral approaches\n\nThis approach has demonstrated superior pain control compared to opioid-centric regimens, with studies showing 30-50% reduction in opioid consumption, faster mobilization, reduced PONV, and earlier discharge. The PROSPECT guidelines (procedure-specific pain management) provide evidence-based recommendations for specific procedures.",
        "question": "Describe the multimodal analgesia approach used in enhanced recovery protocols for elective hip and knee arthroplasty."
