    "examination_overview": "**General Inspection**\n- Swelling, bruising, deformity\n- Plantar ecchymosis (pathognomonic sign)\n- Midfoot widening\n\n**Palpation**\n- Point tenderness over tarsometatarsal joints\n- Gap between 1st and 2nd metatarsal bases\n\n**Special Tests**\n- Midfoot compression test\n- Abduction/pronation stress test\n- Piano key test (dorsal/plantar displacement of metatarsals)\n\n**Neurovascular Assessment**\n- Dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses\n- Capillary refill\n- Sensation in all dermatomes\n- Motor function assessment\n\n**Compartment Assessment**\n- Pain on passive stretch\n- Tense compartments\n- Pain out of proportion\n\n**Examination of Adjacent Joints**\n- Hindfoot and ankle\n- Forefoot and toes",
    "interview_questions": [
      {
        "answer": "On the AP radiograph, I can identify:\n- Widening between the base of the 1st and 2nd metatarsals (Fleck sign - small avulsion fragment may be present)\n- Loss of alignment between the medial border of the 2nd metatarsal and the medial border of the middle cuneiform\n- Possible fracture at the base of the 2nd metatarsal\n\nOn the lateral view:\n- Step-off between the dorsal cortex of the metatarsal bases and the corresponding cuneiforms\n- Possible dorsal displacement of metatarsals\n\nOn the oblique view:\n- Disruption of the normal parallel alignment of the tarsometatarsal joints\n- Possible avulsion fractures\n\nThese findings are consistent with a Lisfranc injury, which represents disruption of the tarsometatarsal joint complex. My primary concerns include:\n1. Potential vascular compromise to the midfoot\n2. Risk of compartment syndrome\n3. Long-term complications if not properly reduced and stabilized, including post-traumatic arthritis and chronic pain\n4. Functional impairment affecting gait and ability to return to work, especially given the patient's occupation as a construction worker",
