      {
        "answer": "The Lisfranc joint complex refers to the tarsometatarsal (TMT) joints, which connect the midfoot (cuneiforms and cuboid) to the metatarsal bases. The complex includes:\n\n**Bony Anatomy:**\n- 1st TMT joint: articulation between medial cuneiform and 1st metatarsal\n- 2nd TMT joint: middle cuneiform and 2nd metatarsal (keystone of the arch)\n- 3rd TMT joint: lateral cuneiform and 3rd metatarsal\n- 4th and 5th TMT joints: cuboid with 4th and 5th metatarsals\n\n**Ligamentous Support:**\n- Dorsal, plantar, and interosseous ligaments between adjacent metatarsals and cuneiforms\n- The Lisfranc ligament (most important stabilizer): strong oblique ligament connecting the medial cuneiform to the base of the 2nd metatarsal\n- No ligamentous connection between 1st and 2nd metatarsal bases (inherent weakness)\n\n**Clinical Importance:**\n1. **Structural Stability:** The complex forms the apex of the longitudinal and transverse arches of the foot\n2. **Load Transfer:** Critical for transferring forces from forefoot to hindfoot during gait\n3. **Midfoot Rigidity:** Essential for push-off phase of gait\n4. **Anatomical Vulnerability:** The 2nd metatarsal is recessed into the cuneiform row, creating a mortise and tenon arrangement that provides stability but is vulnerable to certain injury patterns\n5. **Vascular Considerations:** The dorsalis pedis artery traverses this region, making vascular injury a concern\n\nDisruption of this complex significantly affects foot biomechanics and, if not properly treated, leads to chronic pain, deformity, and post-traumatic arthritis.",
        "question": "Can you describe the anatomy of the Lisfranc joint complex and why it's important?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "The most widely used classification for Lisfranc injuries is the **Myerson Classification**, which guides management based on displacement pattern:\n\n**Type A: Total incongruity**\n- All five metatarsals are displaced in the same direction (laterally, medially, or dorsally)\n- **Management:** ORIF with screws/plates to restore anatomical alignment\n\n**Type B: Partial incongruity**\n- B1: Medial displacement of 1st metatarsal (most common)\n- B2: Lateral displacement of 2-5 metatarsals\n- **Management:** ORIF focusing on the specific displacement pattern; may require different approaches for different columns\n\n**Type C: Divergent**\n- C1: Partial divergent (1st metatarsal medial, some lateral displacement of lesser metatarsals)\n- C2: Total divergent (1st metatarsal medial, all lesser metatarsals lateral)\n- **Management:** Most unstable pattern, requires rigid fixation; higher risk of compartment syndrome and vascular compromise\n\n**Quénu and Küss Classification** (older system):\n- Divides the foot into three columns (medial, middle, lateral)\n- Categorizes based on which columns are affected\n\n**Management Principles Based on Classification:**\n\n1. **Stable, non-displaced injuries (radiographically occult):**\n   - Non-weight-bearing cast for 6 weeks\n   - Close radiographic follow-up to ensure no delayed displacement\n\n2. **Unstable or displaced injuries (all Myerson types):**\n   - Surgical intervention required\n   - ORIF with screws/plates for reducible injuries with good bone quality\n   - Primary arthrodesis for severely comminuted injuries, poor bone quality, or delayed presentation\n   - More rigid fixation for divergent patterns (Type C)\n\n3. **Severe soft tissue compromise:**\n   - Staged approach with temporary external fixation\n   - Definitive internal fixation once soft tissues permit\n\nThe classification also helps predict prognosis, with Type C injuries having worse outcomes and higher complication rates than Types A and B.",
        "question": "How would you classify Lisfranc injuries and how does this guide management?"
