      {
        "answer": "The surgical management of Lisfranc injuries includes several options, each with specific indications:\n\n**1. Open Reduction and Internal Fixation (ORIF)**\n- **Technique:** Anatomic reduction with screw or plate fixation\n- **Indications:** Acute, reducible injuries with good bone quality\n- **Approach:** Dorsal incisions (one or two) between 1st-2nd and 3rd-4th metatarsals\n- **Fixation Options:**\n  - Transarticular screws (4.0mm or 3.5mm cortical)\n  - Bridge plating with locking plates\n  - Suture button devices for ligamentous injuries\n- **Hardware Removal:** Often performed at 4-6 months to prevent hardware breakage and joint stiffness\n\n**2. Primary Arthrodesis**\n- **Technique:** Joint preparation, bone grafting if needed, and rigid fixation\n- **Indications:** Comminuted injuries, poor bone quality, delayed presentation, older patients\n- **Approach:** Similar to ORIF but with cartilage removal at joint surfaces\n- **Fixation:** Compression screws, dorsal plates, or combination\n\n**3. Temporary External Fixation**\n- **Indications:** Severe soft tissue injury, compartment syndrome, open injuries\n- **Technique:** Spanning external fixator to maintain length and alignment\n- **Timing:** Converted to definitive internal fixation when soft tissues permit\n\n**Evidence Base:**\n\nThe landmark **Ly and Coetzee RCT (2006)** compared ORIF vs. primary arthrodesis for acute Lisfranc injuries:\n- 41 patients randomized to ORIF or primary arthrodesis\n- Primary arthrodesis showed better AOFAS midfoot scores at 2 years (68.6 vs. 88.0)\n- Lower reoperation rate in arthrodesis group (27.5% vs. 7.3%)\n- Return to work was similar between groups\n\n**Henning et al. systematic review (2015)** of 421 patients across 11 studies found:\n- Primary arthrodesis had better functional outcomes than ORIF\n- No significant difference in complication rates\n- Both techniques had similar return to work rates\n\n**Smith et al. meta-analysis (2018)** of 7 studies (193 patients) reported:\n- Primary arthrodesis had higher union rates\n- ORIF had higher rate of secondary procedures\n- No difference in wound complications\n\n**Current Consensus:**\n- Purely ligamentous injuries may benefit more from primary arthrodesis\n- Younger patients with simple fracture patterns may benefit from ORIF\n- Patient-specific factors (age, activity level, comorbidities) should guide decision-making\n- Anatomic reduction is the most important predictor of outcome regardless of fixation method\n\nThe **British Orthopaedic Foot and Ankle Society (BOFAS)** recommends consideration of both techniques based on injury pattern and patient factors, with a trend toward primary arthrodesis for unstable injuries.",
        "question": "What are the surgical options for managing Lisfranc injuries and what evidence supports your approach?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Lisfranc injuries can lead to numerous complications, both acute and chronic:\n\n**Acute Complications:**\n\n1. **Compartment Syndrome**\n   - **Presentation:** Pain out of proportion, pain on passive stretch, tense compartments, paresthesia\n   - **Management:** Emergency fasciotomy of all nine foot compartments\n   - **Prevention:** High index of suspicion, regular neurovascular checks, compartment pressure monitoring in high-risk cases\n\n2. **Vascular Compromise**\n   - **Presentation:** Diminished pulses, poor capillary refill, pallor\n   - **Management:** Urgent reduction, vascular surgery consultation if needed\n   - **Investigation:** Ankle-brachial index, CT angiography if concerns persist\n\n3. **Skin Necrosis/Wound Complications**\n   - **Risk Factors:** High-energy injuries, diabetes, smoking\n   - **Management:** Wound care, debridement, VAC therapy, possible plastic surgery input\n   - **Prevention:** Careful soft tissue handling, staged procedures if significant swelling\n\n**Subacute Complications:**\n\n4. **Malreduction/Loss of Reduction**\n   - **Presentation:** Post-operative radiographs showing >2mm displacement\n   - **Management:** Revision surgery if identified early\n   - **Prevention:** Stable fixation, non-weight-bearing protocol\n\n5. **Hardware Failure/Irritation**\n   - **Presentation:** Pain with hardware prominence, broken screws on imaging\n   - **Management:** Hardware removal once healing confirmed\n   - **Prevention:** Proper hardware selection, consideration of routine removal at 4-6 months\n\n**Chronic Complications:**\n\n6. **Post-traumatic Arthritis**\n   - **Incidence:** Up to 50% of cases regardless of treatment\n   - **Presentation:** Midfoot pain with activity, stiffness\n   - **Management:** NSAIDs, orthotics, activity modification, selective injections\n   - **Surgical Options:** Delayed arthrodesis of affected joints\n   - **Prevention:** Anatomic reduction, stable fixation\n\n7. **Chronic Pain/Complex Regional Pain Syndrome**\n   - **Management:** Multimodal pain approach, pain specialist referral, physiotherapy\n   - **Interventions:** Nerve blocks, desensitization therapy\n\n8. **Midfoot Instability/Arch Collapse**\n   - **Presentation:** Flattening of longitudinal arch, midfoot sag on weight-bearing\n   - **Management:** Custom orthotics, supportive footwear\n   - **Surgical Options:** Corrective osteotomy, arthrodesis, tendon transfers\n\n9. **Non-union/Delayed Union**\n   - **Risk Factors:** Smoking, diabetes, poor reduction\n   - **Management:** Bone stimulation, revision surgery with bone grafting\n\n**Evidence-Based Management Strategies:**\n\n- **Schepers et al. systematic review** showed 40% of patients develop post-traumatic arthritis\n- **Mulier et al.** demonstrated that anatomic reduction to within 2mm reduces arthritis risk by 60%\n- **Myerson's long-term follow-up study** showed that 25% of patients require secondary procedures\n\n**Prevention Strategies:**\n1. Anatomic reduction (most important factor)\n2. Stable fixation\n3. Appropriate post-operative protocols\n4. Patient education regarding long-term expectations\n5. Early recognition and management of complications",
