      {
        "answer": "Counseling this construction worker about return to work following a Lisfranc injury requires a comprehensive, honest discussion:\n\n**Short-term Expectations (0-3 months):**\n- Complete inability to work in construction for at least 6-12 weeks\n- Non-weight-bearing period of 6-8 weeks minimum\n- Need for frequent follow-up appointments and possibly physical therapy\n- Potential for surgical hardware removal at 4-6 months requiring additional time off\n\n**Medium-term Recovery (3-6 months):**\n- Graduated return to weight-bearing in a supportive boot\n- Transition to supportive footwear with custom orthotics\n- Physical therapy focusing on proprioception, strength, and gait training\n- Possible light duties or modified work around 4-6 months depending on healing\n- Continued activity restrictions (no climbing ladders, carrying heavy loads, or uneven terrain)\n\n**Long-term Prognosis (6-12+ months):**\n- **Evidence-based outcomes to discuss:**\n  - Ly and Coetzee study: 65% return to pre-injury level of activity by 12 months\n  - Mulier et al.: 40-60% develop some degree of post-traumatic arthritis\n  - Reinhardt et al.: 17% require secondary procedures within 5 years\n  - Myerson's series: Average AOFAS score of 78/100 at 2 years (good but not excellent)\n\n- **Specific construction work considerations:**\n  - May need permanent work modifications for:\n    * Climbing ladders/scaffolding\n    * Working on uneven surfaces\n    * Heavy lifting/carrying\n    * Prolonged standing/walking\n  - Permanent custom orthotic use likely\n  - Supportive, protective footwear requirements\n  - Weather-related symptoms (cold/damp conditions may exacerbate pain)\n\n**Practical Advice:**\n1. **Vocational rehabilitation** assessment to identify specific job demands and modifications\n2. **Staged return to work** plan with employer involvement\n3. **Consideration of retraining** for less physically demanding role within construction\n4. **Disability benefits** information if unable to return to previous position\n5. **Regular follow-up** to monitor for developing arthritis or other complications\n\n**Psychological Support:**\n- Acknowledge the significant impact on identity and financial security\n- Discuss realistic expectations while maintaining optimism\n- Consider referral for psychological support if adjustment difficulties arise\n\n**Final Recommendations:**\n- Best-case scenario: Return to modified construction duties at 6 months, full duties by 12 months with permanent modifications\n- Worst-case scenario: Inability to return to construction work due to persistent pain/limitations\n- Most likely outcome: Return to modified construction work with permanent restrictions and possible need for career adjustment over time\n\nThis balanced approach provides realistic expectations while acknowledging the significant impact of this injury on a physically demanding occupation.",
