    "management_overview": "**Initial Management**\n- PRICE protocol (Protection, Rest, Ice, Compression, Elevation)\n- Analgesia (NSAIDs, paracetamol)\n- Activity modification\n- Consider knee brace for symptomatic relief\n\n**Conservative Management**\n- Physiotherapy referral\n  - Quadriceps strengthening\n  - Hamstring strengthening\n  - Proprioception exercises\n  - Core stability work\n  - Return to sport program if appropriate\n\n- Patient education\n  - Natural history of meniscal tears\n  - Activity modification\n  - Weight management if appropriate\n\n**Surgical Management Considerations**\n- Based on BASK/BOA guidelines for arthroscopic meniscal surgery\n- Indications for surgery:\n  1. Persistent mechanical symptoms (locking, catching)\n  2. Failure of conservative management (6-12 weeks)\n  3. Significant functional limitation\n  4. Specific tear patterns more amenable to repair\n\n- Surgical options:\n  1. Arthroscopic partial meniscectomy\n  2. Meniscal repair (if in vascular zone and appropriate tear pattern)\n  3. Consider concomitant procedures if associated injuries\n\n**Post-operative Rehabilitation**\n- Tailored based on procedure performed\n- More protected protocol for meniscal repair vs meniscectomy\n- Graduated return to activities\n\n**Follow-up**\n- Clinical review at 2-6 weeks post-intervention\n- Physiotherapy progress assessment\n- Return to work/sport planning",
