      {
        "answer": "**MRI Interpretation for Suspected Meniscal Tear**\n\n**1. Technical Aspects**\n- Standard knee MRI protocol includes T1, T2, and proton density (PD) sequences\n- Fat-suppressed PD or T2 sequences are most sensitive for meniscal pathology\n- Images should be reviewed in all three planes (axial, coronal, sagittal)\n\n**2. Normal Meniscal Appearance**\n- Homogeneous low signal intensity on all sequences\n- Triangular shape on coronal images\n- Bow-tie appearance on sequential sagittal images\n\n**3. Key Features to Assess in Meniscal Tears**\n\n**Tear Characteristics:**\n- **Signal Pattern**: High signal extending to articular surface indicates tear\n- **Location**: Medial vs lateral meniscus\n- **Zone**: Anterior horn, body, or posterior horn (posterior horn medial meniscus most common)\n- **Tear Pattern**:\n  - Horizontal/oblique tears (parallel to tibial plateau)\n  - Vertical/longitudinal tears (perpendicular to tibial plateau)\n  - Radial tears (from inner to outer margin)\n  - Complex/degenerative tears (multiple tear patterns)\n  - Bucket-handle tears (longitudinal tear with displaced fragment)\n\n**Reparability Assessment:**\n- **Vascularity Zone**:\n  - Red-red zone (peripheral 10-30%, vascular, good healing potential)\n  - Red-white zone (middle 10-30%, limited vascularity)\n  - White-white zone (inner 40%, avascular, poor healing potential)\n- **Tear Length**: Typically measured in millimeters\n- **Chronicity**: Acute vs chronic changes\n\n**4. Associated Pathologies**\n- ACL or other ligamentous injuries\n- Chondral damage or defects\n- Bone marrow edema or contusions\n- Synovitis or joint effusion\n\n**5. Grading System for Meniscal Signal**\n- **Grade 1**: Intrameniscal globular signal not extending to surface\n- **Grade 2**: Linear intrameniscal signal not extending to surface\n- **Grade 3**: Signal extending to articular surface (true tear)\n\n**6. Diagnostic Pitfalls**\n- Magic angle phenomenon (artifactual high signal)\n- Meniscal variants (discoid meniscus, meniscal flounce)\n- Normal intrameniscal vasculature in children/adolescents\n- Post-surgical changes in previously operated knees\n\nIn this 40-year-old patient with mechanical symptoms, I would specifically look for a grade 3 signal indicating a true tear, assess its location and pattern to determine surgical approach, and evaluate reparability based on vascularity zone and tear configuration. I would also carefully examine for any associated injuries that might influence management decisions.",
