      {
        "answer": "**Current Guidelines and Evidence for Meniscal Tear Management**\n\n**1. BASK/BOA Guidelines for Arthroscopic Meniscal Surgery**\n- Recommend shared decision-making approach\n- Emphasize appropriate patient selection for surgical intervention\n- Advocate for meniscal preservation when possible\n\n**2. Evidence Base for Management**\n\n**Conservative Management:**\n- Multiple RCTs (including FIDELITY trial) show similar outcomes between arthroscopic partial meniscectomy and sham surgery for degenerative tears in middle-aged patients\n- ESCAPE trial showed physiotherapy should be first-line treatment for degenerative meniscal tears\n- However, evidence supports surgery for **traumatic tears with mechanical symptoms** in younger patients (like our case)\n\n**Surgical Management:**\n- **Meniscal Repair vs Meniscectomy:**\n  - Long-term studies show increased OA risk following meniscectomy\n  - MOON Cohort study showed 76.9% success rate for meniscal repairs at 6-year follow-up\n  - Repair success rates higher in concurrent ACL reconstruction (90% vs 60-70% for isolated repairs)\n  - Repair success factors: younger age, acute tears, peripheral location, vertical tear pattern\n\n**3. Management Approach for This Case**\n\n**Initial Assessment:**\n- Confirm diagnosis with MRI\n- Assess tear pattern, location, and reparability\n- Evaluate for associated injuries\n\n**Decision-Making Factors:**\n- **Patient factors**: 40-year-old, active, recreational athlete\n- **Symptom factors**: Mechanical symptoms, functional limitations\n- **Tear characteristics**: Based on MRI findings\n\n**Management Plan:**\n\n1. **Initial Conservative Trial (unless locked knee)**\n   - Structured physiotherapy program (6-12 weeks)\n   - Activity modification\n   - Anti-inflammatory medication\n   - Consider unloader brace for symptom relief\n\n2. **Surgical Consideration if Conservative Management Fails**\n   - **Repair indications**: Peripheral tear (red-red or red-white zone), vertical/longitudinal pattern, acute injury, young patient\n   - **Partial meniscectomy indications**: White-white zone tears, complex/degenerative patterns, horizontal tears\n\n3. **Surgical Technique Selection**\n   - **Repair techniques**: Inside-out, outside-in, all-inside techniques based on tear location\n   - **Meniscectomy principle**: Remove unstable fragment while preserving as much functional meniscus as possible\n\n4. **Post-operative Rehabilitation**\n   - **After repair**: More protected protocol (limited weight-bearing, restricted ROM initially)\n   - **After meniscectomy**: Accelerated protocol with earlier return to activities\n\n**For this specific patient:**\nGiven her age (40), acute traumatic mechanism, mechanical symptoms, and impact on recreational activities, I would:\n1. Obtain MRI to confirm diagnosis and assess tear pattern\n2. Consider trial of conservative management unless significant mechanical blocking symptoms\n3. If mechanical symptoms persist or MRI shows clearly repairable tear, proceed with arthroscopic intervention\n4. Prioritize repair over resection if tear pattern and location are amenable\n5. Implement sport-specific rehabilitation with goal of return to tennis\n\nThis approach aligns with current evidence showing better long-term outcomes with meniscal preservation, particularly in younger, active patients with traumatic tears.",
