      {
        "answer": "**Types of Meniscal Tears and Management Implications**\n\n**1. Vertical/Longitudinal Tears**\n- **Characteristics**: Tear runs parallel to long axis of meniscus, perpendicular to tibial plateau\n- **Common mechanism**: Traumatic, rotational forces\n- **Management implications**:\n  - **Highly repairable** if in red-red or red-white zone\n  - Inside-out repair technique often preferred for optimal suture placement\n  - Excellent healing potential (70-90% success rate)\n  - **Bucket-handle variant** (displaced longitudinal tear) requires urgent repair if causing locking\n\n**2. Horizontal/Oblique Tears**\n- **Characteristics**: Tear runs parallel to tibial plateau, splitting meniscus into superior and inferior flaps\n- **Common mechanism**: Degenerative or chronic repetitive microtrauma\n- **Management implications**:\n  - **Less amenable to repair** due to shear forces across repair site\n  - Partial meniscectomy typically preferred, removing unstable flap\n  - Consider repair only if large, peripheral, and in younger patients\n  - Trephination may be added to improve blood supply to repair site\n\n**3. Radial Tears**\n- **Characteristics**: Tear extends from inner free margin toward periphery\n- **Common mechanism**: Acute traumatic or chronic degenerative\n- **Management implications**:\n  - Disrupts circumferential fibers (\"hoop stress\" distribution)\n  - **Complete radial tears** significantly compromise meniscal function\n  - Repair challenging but indicated for peripheral tears in young patients\n  - Partial meniscectomy for irreparable tears, preserving as much tissue as possible\n\n**4. Complex/Degenerative Tears**\n- **Characteristics**: Combination of multiple tear patterns, often with tissue degeneration\n- **Common mechanism**: Age-related degeneration, often atraumatic\n- **Management implications**:\n  - **Rarely repairable**\n  - Selective partial meniscectomy to stable rim\n  - Conservative management often appropriate first-line (especially in middle-aged patients)\n  - Consider underlying OA in treatment planning\n\n**5. Root Tears**\n- **Characteristics**: Avulsion of meniscal root attachment\n- **Common mechanism**: Acute trauma or chronic degeneration\n- **Management implications**:\n  - **Functionally equivalent to total meniscectomy** if left untreated\n  - Repair strongly recommended, especially in younger patients\n  - Transosseous tunnel repair technique common\n  - Protected weight-bearing post-repair (4-6 weeks)\n\n**Key Decision-Making Factors**\n\n**Tear-Related Factors:**\n- **Location**: Peripheral (vascular) tears more amenable to repair\n- **Pattern**: Vertical/longitudinal most repairable; complex least repairable\n- **Size**: Larger tears (>3cm) may have poorer healing potential\n- **Chronicity**: Acute tears have better repair outcomes than chronic tears\n\n**Patient-Related Factors:**\n- **Age**: Younger patients have better healing potential and longer-term consequences of meniscectomy\n- **Activity level**: Higher demand patients benefit more from successful repair\n- **Comorbidities**: Smoking, diabetes may impair healing\n- **Associated injuries**: Concurrent ACL reconstruction improves meniscal repair healing rates\n\n**Management Algorithm Based on Tear Pattern:**\n1. **Peripheral tears in red-red/red-white zone**: Repair in most cases\n2. **Vertical/longitudinal tears**: Repair if in vascular zone\n3. **Bucket-handle tears**: Urgent repair if causing locking\n4. **Root tears**: Repair to prevent accelerated joint degeneration\n5. **Horizontal, radial, complex tears in white-white zone**: Partial meniscectomy\n6. **Degenerative tears in middle-aged/older patients**: Trial of conservative management first\n\nThe primary goal is always meniscal preservation when possible, as long-term studies consistently show better outcomes and reduced OA progression compared to meniscectomy.",
