      {
        "answer": "**Surgical Techniques for Meniscal Repair**\n\n**1. Inside-Out Technique**\n- **Description**: Sutures passed from inside the joint to outside through cannulas\n- **Advantages**: \n  - Gold standard with longest track record\n  - Precise suture placement\n  - Strong vertical mattress configuration possible\n- **Disadvantages**:\n  - Requires accessory incisions\n  - Risk to neurovascular structures (requires careful retractor placement)\n- **Best applications**: Middle and posterior third tears, especially in medial meniscus\n\n**2. Outside-In Technique**\n- **Description**: Sutures/needles passed from outside the joint to inside\n- **Advantages**:\n  - Reduced neurovascular risk\n  - Good for anterior horn tears\n- **Disadvantages**:\n  - Technical difficulty with posterior horn tears\n  - May require accessory incisions\n- **Best applications**: Anterior horn tears, tears in children\n\n**3. All-Inside Technique**\n- **Description**: Specialized devices (e.g., meniscal arrows, FasT-Fix, RapidLoc) placed entirely arthroscopically\n- **Advantages**:\n  - No accessory incisions\n  - Reduced operative time\n  - Reduced neurovascular risk\n- **Disadvantages**:\n  - Higher implant cost\n  - Potential for implant-related complications\n  - May have lower pullout strength than traditional sutures\n- **Best applications**: Posterior horn tears, lateral meniscus tears\n\n**4. Hybrid Techniques**\n- **Description**: Combination of different repair methods\n- **Advantages**: Optimizes approach based on tear location and pattern\n- **Best applications**: Complex or extensive tears\n\n**5. Augmentation Techniques**\n- **Trephination/Needling**: Creating vascular channels to improve healing\n- **Fibrin clot/PRP application**: Biological augmentation to enhance healing\n- **Synovial abrasion**: Promoting vascular ingrowth\n\n**Post-operative Rehabilitation Protocol**\n\n**Phase 1: Protection Phase (0-6 weeks)**\n- **Weight-bearing status**:\n  - Simple repairs: Partial weight-bearing with brace locked in extension (0-2 weeks), progressing to full weight-bearing\n  - Complex/root repairs: Non-weight-bearing or toe-touch weight-bearing for 4-6 weeks\n- **Range of motion**:\n  - Simple repairs: 0-90° for first 2-4 weeks\n  - Complex repairs: More restricted ROM (0-60° for first 4 weeks)\n- **Brace**: Hinged knee brace with ROM limitations\n- **Exercises**:\n  - Quadriceps sets, straight leg raises\n  - Ankle pumps, gluteal sets\n  - Patella mobilizations\n  - Upper body and core conditioning\n\n**Phase 2: Intermediate Phase (6-12 weeks)**\n- **Weight-bearing**: Full weight-bearing without brace\n- **Range of motion**: Progress to full ROM\n- **Exercises**:\n  - Closed-chain strengthening exercises\n  - Stationary cycling\n  - Pool-based rehabilitation\n  - Proprioceptive training\n  - Progressive resistance training\n- **Restrictions**: Avoid deep squatting, pivoting, cutting\n\n**Phase 3: Advanced Phase (3-6 months)**\n- **Functional progression**:\n  - Sport-specific exercises\n  - Agility drills\n  - Plyometric training\n  - Running progression (straight-line → cutting → pivoting)\n- **Return to sport criteria**:\n  - Full, pain-free ROM\n  - 90% strength compared to contralateral side\n  - Successful completion of functional tests\n  - No effusion or pain with activities\n  - Typically 4-6 months for full return\n\n**Special Considerations**\n\n**1. Tear-Specific Modifications**\n- **Root repairs**: More prolonged protection (6 weeks non-weight-bearing)\n- **Radial tears**: More restricted ROM progression\n- **Bucket-handle repairs**: Careful monitoring for re-displacement\n\n**2. Concomitant Procedures**\n- **With ACL reconstruction**: Follow more restrictive protocol\n- **With cartilage procedures**: Modified weight-bearing based on cartilage procedure\n\n**3. Patient-Specific Factors**\n- **Age**: More gradual progression in older patients\n- **Tissue quality**: Poorer quality may require extended protection\n- **Compliance**: Adjust based on patient reliability\n\n**4. Evidence-Based Considerations**\n- Recent studies suggest accelerated protocols may be safe for certain repair types\n- MRI evidence shows healing typically requires 3-6 months for completion\n- Return to pivoting sports before 4-6 months associated with higher failure rates\n\nThe rehabilitation protocol should be individualized based on repair technique, tear characteristics, patient factors, and surgeon preference, with regular clinical assessment to guide progression.",
