      {
        "answer": "**Complications of Meniscal Tears and Their Management**\n\n**I. Complications of Untreated or Conservatively Managed Meniscal Tears**\n\n**1. Persistent Mechanical Symptoms**\n- **Manifestation**: Continued locking, catching, giving way\n- **Management**:\n  - Reassessment of diagnosis and tear pattern\n  - Consider surgical intervention if significantly affecting function\n  - Activity modification and bracing as temporary measures\n\n**2. Progression of Tear**\n- **Manifestation**: Worsening symptoms, extension of tear on follow-up imaging\n- **Management**:\n  - Surgical intervention often indicated for progressive tears\n  - More extensive surgery may be required compared to initial presentation\n\n**3. Secondary Chondral Damage**\n- **Manifestation**: Development of articular cartilage lesions due to altered biomechanics\n- **Management**:\n  - Address meniscal pathology to prevent further damage\n  - Consider chondral procedures if significant damage present\n  - Unloader bracing, injections for symptom management\n\n**4. Early Osteoarthritis**\n- **Manifestation**: Joint space narrowing, osteophyte formation\n- **Management**:\n  - Weight management, activity modification\n  - Physical therapy focusing on muscle strengthening\n  - Pharmacological management (NSAIDs, injections)\n  - Consider meniscal transplantation in select young patients with total/subtotal meniscectomy\n\n**II. Complications of Surgical Management**\n\n**A. Complications of Meniscal Repair**\n\n**1. Repair Failure**\n- **Incidence**: 20-40% for isolated repairs, 10-20% with concurrent ACL reconstruction\n- **Risk factors**: White-white zone tears, complex patterns, older patients, smoking\n- **Management**:\n  - Revision repair if appropriate tear pattern\n  - Conversion to partial meniscectomy\n  - Consider biological augmentation for revision repairs\n\n**2. Neurovascular Injury**\n- **Structures at risk**: \n  - Medial repair: Saphenous nerve/vein\n  - Lateral repair: Peroneal nerve, popliteal vessels\n- **Management**:\n  - Immediate recognition and repair if identified intraoperatively\n  - Neurology referral for persistent deficits\n  - Careful technique with proper retractor placement\n\n**3. Implant-Related Complications**\n- **Types**: Migration, breakage, synovitis, cartilage damage\n- **Management**:\n  - Removal of prominent or migrated implants\n  - Chondral procedures if significant cartilage damage\n\n**4. Arthrofibrosis**\n- **Manifestation**: Stiffness, limited ROM post-operatively\n- **Management**:\n  - Aggressive early ROM exercises\n  - Manipulation under anesthesia if persistent\n  - Arthroscopic lysis of adhesions in refractory cases\n\n**B. Complications of Meniscectomy**\n\n**1. Long-term Joint Degeneration**\n- **Incidence**: Relative risk of OA increased 14x after total meniscectomy\n- **Risk factors**: Amount of meniscus removed, alignment, age, activity level\n- **Management**:\n  - Joint preservation strategies (activity modification, weight management)\n  - Cartilage procedures in select cases\n  - Meniscal allograft transplantation in young patients with significant deficiency\n\n**2. Persistent Pain**\n- **Causes**: Inadequate debridement, unaddressed chondral lesions, OA\n- **Management**:\n  - Identify source with advanced imaging\n  - Revision arthroscopy if mechanical source identified\n  - Comprehensive pain management approach\n\n**3. Iatrogenic Cartilage Injury**\n- **Causes**: Instrument-related during arthroscopy\n- **Management**:\n  - Immediate recognition and treatment if possible\n  - Chondral procedures for significant lesions\n\n**III. General Surgical Complications**\n\n**1. Infection**\n- **Incidence**: 0.1-0.5% in knee arthroscopy\n- **Management**:\n  - Early arthroscopic lavage and debridement\n  - Appropriate antibiotic therapy\n  - Serial inflammatory marker monitoring\n\n**2. Deep Vein Thrombosis/Pulmonary Embolism**\n- **Incidence**: 0.1-0.5% without prophylaxis\n- **Management**:\n  - Risk stratification and appropriate prophylaxis\n  - Early mobilization\n  - Therapeutic anticoagulation if diagnosed\n\n**3. Persistent Effusion**\n- **Management**:\n  - RICE protocol\n  - Consider aspiration if significant\n  - Rule out infection if persistent\n\n**Prevention Strategies**\n\n**For Conservative Management:**\n- Clear patient education regarding activity modification\n- Structured rehabilitation program\n- Regular follow-up to assess for progression\n- Appropriate bracing for unstable tears\n\n**For Surgical Management:**\n- Proper patient selection\n- Meticulous surgical technique\n- Appropriate repair technique for tear pattern\n- Structured rehabilitation protocol\n- Biological augmentation in high-risk repairs\n\nThe management of complications should be individualized based on patient factors, activity demands, and the specific complication encountered.",
