        "answer": "This injury can be classified using several systems:\n\n1. **Mayfield Classification** of progressive perilunar instability:\n   - Stage I: Scapholunate dissociation\n   - Stage II: Capitolunate disruption\n   - Stage III: Lunotriquetral disruption\n   - Stage IV: Lunate dislocation from radial fossa (complete lunate dislocation)\n\nThis case represents a Stage III injury where the lunate remains aligned with the radius but the remainder of the carpus is dislocated.\n\n2. We can also classify by **direction of displacement**:\n   - Dorsal perilunate dislocation (most common, ~80%)\n   - Volar perilunate dislocation (less common)\n\n3. **Associated fractures** create variants:\n   - Pure ligamentous injury (lesser arc)\n   - Trans-scaphoid perilunate dislocation (greater arc)\n   - Trans-radial styloid, trans-capitate, or trans-triquetral variants\n\nThe **pathomechanism** typically involves:\n- Fall on an outstretched hand\n- Wrist in extension, ulnar deviation, and intercarpal supination\n- Axial loading causing sequential failure of the stabilizing ligaments in a radial-to-ulnar direction\n- The dorsal radiocarpal ligaments act as a fulcrum, resulting in dorsal displacement of the carpus relative to the lunate",
