      {
        "answer": "Perilunate dislocations carry significant risk of both acute and chronic complications:\n\n**Acute complications:**\n\n1. **Neurovascular injury:**\n   - Median nerve compression or contusion (most common) - can lead to acute carpal tunnel syndrome\n   - Vascular compromise to the hand\n\n2. **Failed reduction or recurrent instability**\n\n3. **Infection** (particularly with open injuries or after surgical intervention)\n\n4. **Compartment syndrome** (rare)\n\n**Chronic complications:**\n\n1. **Avascular necrosis:**\n   - Particularly of the lunate (Kienböck's disease) or scaphoid\n   - Risk increases with delayed treatment\n\n2. **Post-traumatic arthritis:**\n   - Radiocarpal arthritis\n   - Midcarpal arthritis\n   - Reported in up to 50-60% of cases at long-term follow-up\n\n3. **Chronic carpal instability:**\n   - SLAC (Scapholunate Advanced Collapse)\n   - SNAC (Scaphoid Nonunion Advanced Collapse)\n\n4. **Chronic pain and stiffness**\n\n5. **Reduced grip strength** (typically 60-80% of contralateral side)\n\n6. **Complex Regional Pain Syndrome**\n\n**Treatment-specific complications:**\n\n1. **K-wire related:**\n   - Migration\n   - Breakage\n   - Pin tract infection\n\n2. **Surgical approach related:**\n   - Scar tenderness\n   - Tendon adhesions\n   - Neuroma formation\n\n3. **Immobilization related:**\n   - Stiffness\n   - Muscle atrophy\n   - Adjacent joint stiffness\n\nEven with optimal management, many patients have residual symptoms and functional limitations. Literature suggests only about 50% return to their previous occupation without restrictions.",
