        "answer": "The surgical technique for ORIF of a perilunate dislocation involves:\n\n**Preoperative Planning:**\n- Review all imaging\n- Ensure appropriate equipment (K-wires, mini fragment set, headless compression screws)\n- Consider need for both dorsal and volar approaches\n\n**Anesthesia and Positioning:**\n- General anesthesia with muscle relaxation\n- Supine position with arm on hand table\n- Tourniquet application\n- Fluoroscopy available\n\n**Surgical Approach:**\n\n1. **Dorsal Approach (Primary):**\n   - Longitudinal incision centered over the Lister tubercle\n   - Identify and protect the dorsal sensory branches of the radial nerve\n   - Open the retinaculum between the 3rd and 4th compartments\n   - Elevate the extensor pollicis longus\n   - Create a capsular flap with radial base\n   - Identify the scapholunate and lunotriquetral intervals\n\n2. **Volar Approach (If needed for lunate reduction or carpal tunnel release):**\n   - Extended carpal tunnel approach\n   - Release transverse carpal ligament\n   - Protect median nerve\n   - Access volar capsule\n\n**Reduction Technique:**\n\n1. Debride hematoma and damaged tissue\n2. Reduce the carpus relative to the lunate:\n   - Apply traction\n   - Use joystick K-wires in the scaphoid and triquetrum if needed\n   - Reduce the scaphoid to the lunate first\n   - Then reduce the capitate into the lunate concavity\n   - Finally, reduce the triquetrum to the lunate\n\n**Fixation:**\n\n1. Provisional K-wire fixation under fluoroscopic guidance:\n   - Scapholunate joint (1-2 wires)\n   - Lunotriquetral joint (1 wire)\n   - Scaphocapitate (1 wire)\n   - Consider capitolunate wire\n\n2. Definitive fixation options:\n   - K-wires alone (most common)\n   - Headless compression screws for scaphoid fractures\n   - Suture anchors for ligament repair where tissue quality permits\n\n**Ligament Repair:**\n- Repair the scapholunate and lunotriquetral ligaments where possible\n- Consider dorsal capsulodesis for additional stability\n\n**Closure:**\n- Repair the dorsal capsule\n- Close retinaculum with care not to compress extensor tendons\n- Skin closure\n- Apply well-padded above-elbow cast with wrist in slight extension\n\n**Post-operative Protocol:**\n- Above-elbow cast for 6 weeks\n- K-wire removal at 8-12 weeks\n- Progressive mobilization under hand therapy guidance",
