      {
        "answer": "On these radiographs, I can see widening of the acromioclavicular joint with superior displacement of the distal clavicle relative to the acromion. There appears to be an increased coracoclavicular distance compared to the contralateral side on the weight-bearing views.\n\nThis injury would be classified using the Rockwood classification for AC joint injuries:\n\n**Rockwood Classification:**\n- **Grade I:** AC ligament sprain with intact ligaments and normal radiographs\n- **Grade II:** AC ligament disruption with intact coracoclavicular ligaments; AC joint widening but normal coracoclavicular distance\n- **Grade III:** Disruption of both AC and coracoclavicular ligaments; 25-100% increase in coracoclavicular distance with superior displacement of distal clavicle\n- **Grade IV:** Grade III injury plus posterior displacement of distal clavicle into/through trapezius\n- **Grade V:** Grade III injury but with severe superior displacement (>100% increase in coracoclavicular distance)\n- **Grade VI:** Inferior displacement of distal clavicle beneath coracoid or acromion (rare)\n\nBased on the radiographic findings, this appears to be a **Grade III** AC joint injury with complete disruption of both the AC and coracoclavicular ligaments, resulting in superior displacement of the distal clavicle and increased coracoclavicular distance.",
