      {
        "answer": "**Surgical Options for AC Joint Disruption:**\n\n1. **Hook Plate Fixation**\n   - **Technique:** Plate fixed to acromion with hook under acromion and screws into distal clavicle\n   - **Advantages:**\n     - Relatively simple technique\n     - Good initial stability\n     - No need for graft harvesting\n   - **Disadvantages:**\n     - Requires second surgery for plate removal (typically 3-6 months)\n     - Risk of acromial erosion/osteolysis\n     - Subacromial impingement\n     - Restricted ROM until plate removal\n     - Potential for plate breakage or screw loosening\n\n2. **Coracoclavicular (CC) Ligament Reconstruction**\n   - **Technique:** Synthetic or biological grafts to reconstruct CC ligaments\n   - **Advantages:**\n     - Anatomic reconstruction of primary stabilizers\n     - No need for implant removal\n     - Earlier rehabilitation possible\n   - **Disadvantages:**\n     - Technically more demanding\n     - Risk of coracoid fracture\n     - Potential for graft failure or stretching\n     - Donor site morbidity if autograft used\n\n3. **Anatomic AC Joint Reconstruction**\n   - **Technique:** Reconstruction of both AC and CC ligaments\n   - **Advantages:**\n     - Most anatomic repair\n     - Addresses horizontal and vertical stability\n     - Better biomechanical outcomes\n   - **Disadvantages:**\n     - Technically challenging\n     - Longer operative time\n     - More extensive dissection\n\n4. **Arthroscopic-Assisted Techniques**\n   - **Technique:** Minimally invasive approach using suture buttons or grafts\n   - **Advantages:**\n     - Minimally invasive\n     - Less soft tissue disruption\n     - Better cosmesis\n     - Allows assessment of glenohumeral joint\n   - **Disadvantages:**\n     - Steep learning curve\n     - Technical challenges\n     - Higher implant costs\n\n5. **Modified Weaver-Dunn Procedure**\n   - **Technique:** Transfer of coracoacromial ligament to distal clavicle\n   - **Advantages:**\n     - Established technique\n     - No foreign material\n   - **Disadvantages:**\n     - Biomechanically inferior to native ligaments\n     - Higher failure rates compared to newer techniques\n     - Sacrifices coracoacromial ligament\n\n**Current Evidence and Trends:**\n- Modern techniques favor anatomic reconstruction over non-anatomic procedures\n- Biomechanical studies show superior strength with CC ligament reconstruction compared to Weaver-Dunn\n- Arthroscopic techniques are gaining popularity but require specialized training\n- No clear consensus on optimal technique, with surgeon experience often guiding choice\n\n**Complications Common to All Techniques:**\n- Hardware failure or migration\n- Loss of reduction\n- Infection\n- Persistent pain\n- Stiffness\n- Osteoarthritis of AC joint",
