        "answer": "**Recovery Timeline and Rehabilitation Protocol for AC Joint Injury**\n\n**Conservative Management (Grade I-III):**\n\n**Phase 1: Acute Phase (0-2 weeks)**\n- **Goals:**\n  - Pain and inflammation control\n  - Protection of injured structures\n  - Maintain mobility of uninvolved joints\n- **Interventions:**\n  - Sling immobilization for comfort (1-2 weeks)\n  - Ice therapy (15-20 minutes every 2-3 hours)\n  - Gentle pendulum exercises starting day 3-5\n  - Pain-free passive ROM exercises\n  - Scapular setting exercises\n\n**Phase 2: Intermediate Phase (2-6 weeks)**\n- **Goals:**\n  - Restore full passive ROM\n  - Initiate active ROM\n  - Begin light strengthening\n- **Interventions:**\n  - Progressive active-assisted ROM exercises\n  - Isometric rotator cuff strengthening\n  - Light resistance band exercises\n  - Scapular stabilization exercises\n  - Postural correction\n\n**Phase 3: Advanced Strengthening (6-12 weeks)**\n- **Goals:**\n  - Restore full active ROM\n  - Progressive strengthening\n  - Improve neuromuscular control\n- **Interventions:**\n  - Progressive resistance training\n  - Functional movement patterns\n  - Sport-specific exercises\n  - Proprioceptive training\n  - Plyometric exercises (if appropriate)\n\n**Phase 4: Return to Activity (12+ weeks)**\n- **Goals:**\n  - Sport-specific conditioning\n  - Return to pre-injury level of function\n- **Interventions:**\n  - Advanced strengthening\n  - Sport-specific drills\n  - Gradual return to training\n  - Protective taping/bracing as needed\n\n**Post-Surgical Rehabilitation:**\n\n**Phase 1: Protection Phase (0-4 weeks)**\n- **Goals:**\n  - Protect surgical repair\n  - Control pain and inflammation\n  - Maintain mobility of uninvolved joints\n- **Interventions:**\n  - Sling immobilization (2-4 weeks depending on procedure)\n  - Strict ROM limitations based on surgical technique\n  - Gentle passive ROM within safe limits\n  - Elbow, wrist, and hand exercises\n\n**Phase 2: Motion Phase (4-8 weeks)**\n- **Goals:**\n  - Gradually restore ROM\n  - Protect healing tissues\n  - Minimize pain\n- **Interventions:**\n  - Progressive passive and active-assisted ROM\n  - Light isometric exercises\n  - Scapular stabilization\n  - Postural exercises\n\n**Phase 3: Strengthening Phase (8-12 weeks)**\n- **Goals:**\n  - Normalize ROM\n  - Progressive strengthening\n  - Improve neuromuscular control\n- **Interventions:**\n  - Progressive resistance training\n  - Rotator cuff strengthening\n  - Scapulothoracic exercises\n  - Core stability training\n\n**Phase 4: Functional Phase (12-20 weeks)**\n- **Goals:**\n  - Sport-specific training\n  - Return to pre-injury activities\n- **Interventions:**\n  - Advanced strengthening\n  - Sport-specific drills\n  - Plyometric training\n  - Return to play progression\n\n**Return to Play Criteria:**\n1. Full, pain-free ROM\n2. Strength at least 90% of contralateral side\n3. Successful completion of sport-specific functional tests\n4. Psychological readiness\n5. Surgeon clearance (if post-surgical)\n\n**Expected Timelines:**\n- **Grade I:** Return to sport in 1-2 weeks\n- **Grade II:** Return to sport in 2-4 weeks\n- **Grade III (Conservative):** Return to sport in 6-12 weeks\n- **Surgical Management:** Return to sport in 12-24 weeks depending on procedure\n\n**Persistent Issues:**\n- 20-40% of conservatively managed Grade III injuries may have residual symptoms\n- Persistent deformity is common but often not functionally limiting\n- Late surgical intervention remains an option for symptomatic patients",
