      {
        "answer": "**Potential Complications of AC Joint Injuries**\n\n**Acute Complications:**\n\n1. **Associated Injuries**\n   - **Clavicle fractures:** Particularly distal third fractures\n   - **Scapular fractures:** Including glenoid, coracoid, or acromion\n   - **Rotator cuff tears:** Especially in older patients or high-energy injuries\n   - **Brachial plexus injuries:** More common in high-grade dislocations\n   - **Management:** Comprehensive initial assessment with appropriate imaging; address associated injuries concurrently\n\n2. **Neurovascular Injuries**\n   - **Suprascapular nerve injury:** Can cause infraspinatus weakness\n   - **Vascular compromise:** Rare but requires urgent assessment\n   - **Management:** Thorough neurovascular examination; vascular studies if compromise suspected\n\n**Chronic/Late Complications:**\n\n1. **Persistent Pain**\n   - **Causes:**\n     - Chronic instability\n     - AC joint arthritis\n     - Osteolysis of distal clavicle\n     - Impingement syndromes\n   - **Management:**\n     - Activity modification\n     - Targeted physiotherapy\n     - Corticosteroid injections\n     - Late reconstruction for symptomatic instability\n     - Distal clavicle excision for arthritis/osteolysis\n\n2. **Chronic Instability**\n   - **Presentation:** Persistent pain, clicking, weakness, cosmetic deformity\n   - **Management:**\n     - Functional rehabilitation focusing on periscapular strengthening\n     - Late surgical reconstruction if significantly symptomatic\n     - Surgical options similar to acute setting but technically more challenging\n\n3. **Post-traumatic Arthritis**\n   - **Incidence:** 8-42% following AC joint injuries\n   - **Management:**\n     - Conservative: NSAIDs, activity modification, injections\n     - Surgical: Distal clavicle excision (open or arthroscopic)\n\n4. **Distal Clavicle Osteolysis**\n   - **Presentation:** Pain, tenderness, radiographic changes\n   - **Management:**\n     - Rest and activity modification\n     - Distal clavicle excision if persistent symptoms\n\n**Surgery-Specific Complications:**\n\n1. **Hardware-Related Issues**\n   - **Hook plate complications:**\n     - Acromial erosion/fracture\n     - Subacromial impingement\n     - Hardware failure\n     - Pain requiring early removal\n   - **Management:** Hardware removal; revision surgery if needed\n\n2. **Coracoid Fracture**\n   - **Risk factors:** Drill holes, overtightening of fixation\n   - **Management:** Conservative for most; revision surgery for symptomatic cases\n\n3. **Loss of Reduction**\n   - **Incidence:** 15-80% depending on technique\n   - **Management:** Revision surgery if symptomatic; otherwise observation\n\n4. **Infection**\n   - **Incidence:** 0.5-3%\n   - **Management:** Antibiotics; debridement if necessary; hardware removal if persistent\n\n5. **Adhesive Capsulitis**\n   - **Risk factors:** Prolonged immobilization\n   - **Management:** Early ROM exercises; physiotherapy; rarely manipulation under anesthesia\n\n**Prevention Strategies:**\n\n1. **Appropriate initial management** based on injury grade and patient factors\n2. **Structured rehabilitation protocol** with gradual progression\n3. **Proper surgical technique** when indicated\n4. **Adequate protection** during return to activities\n5. **Patient education** regarding expectations and potential complications\n\n**Long-term Outcomes:**\n- Most Grade I-II injuries: Excellent outcomes with conservative management\n- Grade III injuries: 80-85% good-excellent results regardless of treatment\n- Grade IV-VI injuries: Better outcomes with surgical management\n- Persistent deformity is common but often not functionally limiting",
