l. (2012): Retrospective review of 41 athletes\n     - Faster return to sport with surgical treatment (10.2 vs. 16.8 weeks)\n     - No difference in final functional outcomes\n\n2. **Manual Laborers:**\n   - Gstettner et al. (2008): Comparative study of 41 patients\n     - Better Constant scores in surgical group (90.4 vs. 80.7)\n     - Particularly beneficial in heavy manual laborers\n\n**Long-term Outcomes:**\n\n1. **Schlegel et al. (2001):**\n   - 10-year follow-up of non-operatively treated Grade III injuries\n   - 42% had mild pain with activities\n   - 26% had moderate strength deficits\n   - Most returned to previous level of function despite persistent deformity\n\n2. **Tauber et al. (2016):**\n   - Long-term follow-up (mean 7.4 years) comparing surgical vs. non-surgical\n   - Better ASES and Constant scores in surgical group\n   - Lower rate of AC joint arthritis in surgical group\n\n**Current Evidence-Based Recommendations:**\n\n1. **General Population:**\n   - Initial non-operative management is reasonable first-line approach\n   - Surgical consideration for persistent symptoms after failed conservative treatment\n\n2. **Young, Athletic Patients:**\n   - Consider surgical management for:\n     - Overhead athletes\n     - Contact sport participants\n     - Manual laborers\n     - Patients concerned with cosmesis\n\n3. **Surgical Timing:**\n   - Acute surgery (within 3 weeks) if indicated\n   - Chronic reconstruction more technically challenging but viable for symptomatic patients\n\n**Evidence Limitations:**\n\n1. Heterogeneity in surgical techniques makes comparison difficult\n2. Lack of standardized outcome measures across studies\n3. Limited high-quality RCTs with long-term follow-up\n4. Insufficient data on specific subpopulations (elite athletes, manual laborers)\n\n**Conclusion:**\nCurrent evidence suggests similar functional outcomes between surgical and non-surgical management for most Grade III AC joint injuries, with higher complication rates in 