        "answer": "My immediate concerns for this patient include:\n\n1. **Metastatic spinal cord compression (MSCC)** - a neurological emergency requiring urgent assessment and management\n2. **Neurological deterioration** - progressive weakness suggesting ongoing cord compression\n3. **Pain control** - significant thoracic pain likely related to metastatic disease\n4. **Spinal instability** - risk of pathological fracture and further neurological compromise\n\nInitial management would include:\n\n**Immediate actions:**\n- Commence high-dose dexamethasone 16mg orally or IV as per NICE CG75 guidelines\n- Ensure adequate analgesia following WHO pain ladder principles\n- Maintain spinal immobilization if any concern about instability\n- Urgent whole spine MRI within 24 hours (as per NICE CG75)\n- Notify the MSCC coordinator as per local pathway\n\n**Urgent referrals:**\n- Spinal surgical team for assessment regarding surgical intervention\n- Oncology team for input on systemic treatment options and radiotherapy\n- Specialist rehabilitation team\n\n**Supportive care:**\n- Thromboprophylaxis if immobile\n- Pressure area care\n- Bladder and bowel management (catheterization if retention present)\n- Regular neurological observations\n\nThe management pathway should follow NICE CG75 guidelines for MSCC with decisions made through multidisciplinary discussion involving spinal surgeons, oncologists, and the patient regarding goals of care.",
