        "answer": "The decision between surgical intervention and radiotherapy for MSCC requires careful consideration of multiple factors:\n\n**Factors favoring surgical intervention:**\n\n1. **Neurological status**\n   - Rapidly deteriorating neurology\n   - Incomplete cord compression with residual function\n\n2. **Spinal stability**\n   - High SINS score (Spinal Instability Neoplastic Score >7)\n   - Pathological fracture with instability\n   - Mechanical pain improved with recumbency\n\n3. **Tumor characteristics**\n   - Radioresistant histology (e.g., pancreatic adenocarcinoma is relatively radioresistant)\n   - Solitary or limited metastatic disease\n   - Previous radiotherapy to same region (reaching tissue tolerance)\n\n4. **Patient factors**\n   - Good performance status (ECOG 0-2)\n   - Life expectancy >3 months (Tokuhashi score)\n   - Medically fit for surgery\n   - Patient preference for surgical intervention\n\n**Factors favoring radiotherapy:**\n\n1. **Disease extent**\n   - Multiple levels of cord compression\n   - Widespread metastatic disease\n   - Poor prognosis (Tokuhashi score)\n\n2. **Neurological status**\n   - Complete paraplegia >24 hours\n   - Slowly progressive symptoms\n\n3. **Tumor characteristics**\n   - Highly radiosensitive tumors (e.g., lymphoma, myeloma, germ cell)\n   - Multiple spinal metastases\n\n4. **Patient factors**\n   - Poor performance status (ECOG 3-4)\n   - Limited life expectancy (<3 months)\n   - Significant comorbidities increasing surgical risk\n   - Patient preference for non-surgical approach\n\n**Evidence base:**\nThe Patchell trial (2005) demonstrated that surgery followed by radiotherapy was superior to radiotherapy alone in maintaining walking ability in selected patients. However, this applies to carefully selected patients with good prognostic factors.\n\nFor this 72-year-old woman with pancreatic cancer (which typically has a poor prognosis), the decision would require MDT discussion considering her overall 