        "answer": "**Spinal Instability Neoplastic Score (SINS)**\n\nThe SINS is a validated classification system developed by the Spine Oncology Study Group to assess instability in spinal metastatic disease. It evaluates six parameters:\n\n1. **Location** (0-3 points)\n   - Junctional regions (occiput-C2, C7-T2, T11-L1, L5-S1): 3 points\n   - Mobile spine (C3-C6, L2-L4): 2 points\n   - Semi-rigid spine (T3-T10): 1 point\n   - Rigid spine (S2-S5): 0 points\n\n2. **Pain** (0-3 points)\n   - Mechanical pain (worse with movement/loading): 3 points\n   - Occasional pain but not mechanical: 1 point\n   - Pain-free: 0 points\n\n3. **Bone lesion** (0-2 points)\n   - Lytic: 2 points\n   - Mixed lytic/blastic: 1 point\n   - Blastic: 0 points\n\n4. **Radiographic spinal alignment** (0-4 points)\n   - Subluxation/translation present: 4 points\n   - New deformity (kyphosis/scoliosis): 2 points\n   - Normal alignment: 0 points\n\n5. **Vertebral body collapse** (0-3 points)\n   - >50% collapse: 3 points\n   - <50% collapse: 2 points\n   - No collapse but >50% body involved: 1 point\n   - None of the above: 0 points\n\n6. **Posterolateral involvement** (0-3 points)\n   - Bilateral: 3 points\n   - Unilateral: 1 point\n   - None: 0 points\n\n**Interpretation:**\n- Score 0-6: Stable\n- Score 7-12: Potentially unstable (consider bracing and surgical consultation)\n- Score 13-18: Unstable (surgical intervention likely needed)\n\n**Additional assessment:**\n\n1. **Clinical examination**\n   - Percussion tenderness\n   - Step deformity\n   - Neurological deterioration with movement\n\n2. **Dynamic imaging**\n   - Flexion/extension radiographs (if safe and appropriate)\n\n3. **Advanced imaging**\n   - CT for detailed bony anatomy\n   - MRI for soft tissue and neural involvement\n\n4. **Biomechanical considerations**\n   - Three-column involvement (Denis classification)\n   - Involvement of pedicles and posterior elements\n   - Percentage of vertebral body destruction\n\nThe SINS score guides management decisions and helps standardize communication between specialists. Patients with potentially unstable or unstable spines may benefit from surgical stabilization, especially if they have good performance status and prognosis.",
