        "answer": "**Rehabilitation in MSCC**\n\nRehabilitation plays a crucial role in optimizing function, quality of life, and independence for patients with MSCC. The approach should be multidisciplinary and initiated early.\n\n**Key principles:**\n\n1. **Early intervention**\n   - Rehabilitation assessment should begin immediately after diagnosis\n   - Prevents complications of immobility\n   - Sets realistic goals based on prognosis and neurological status\n\n2. **Multidisciplinary approach**\n   - Physiotherapy: mobility, transfers, strengthening exercises\n   - Occupational therapy: ADL assessment, equipment provision\n   - Speech and language therapy (if cervical MSCC affects swallowing)\n   - Specialist nurses: continence management, skin care\n   - Psychologists: adjustment to disability, mood management\n   - Social workers: discharge planning, community support\n\n3. **Goal-directed rehabilitation**\n   - Tailored to individual patient's prognosis\n   - Short-term achievable goals for those with limited life expectancy\n   - More intensive rehabilitation for those with better prognosis\n\n**Specific interventions:**\n\n1. **Mobility and transfers**\n   - Bed mobility training\n   - Transfer techniques (bed to chair/commode)\n   - Wheelchair skills and prescription\n   - Gait re-education and walking aids if appropriate\n\n2. **Activities of daily living**\n   - Self-care strategies\n   - Adaptive equipment\n   - Home modifications\n\n3. **Bladder and bowel management**\n   - Intermittent catheterization training\n   - Bowel regime establishment\n   - Continence aids\n\n4. **Pressure care**\n   - Education on pressure relief techniques\n   - Appropriate mattress/cushion provision\n   - Skin inspection routine\n\n5. **Psychological support**\n   - Adjustment to disability\n   - Coping strategies\n   - Support for family/caregivers\n\n**Rehabilitation settings:**\n\n- **Acute hospital**: Initial assessment and early intervention\n- **Specialist spinal rehabilitation unit**: For patients with good prognosis and rehabilitation potential\n- **Palliative care setting**: Focused rehabilitation for symptom control and quality of life\n- **Community**: Ongoing support after discharge\n\n**Evidence and guidelines:**\n\nNICE CG75 recommends that patients with MSCC should have access to a full range of rehabilitation services and that rehabilitation should be started as soon as possible after treatment. The guidelines emphasize the importance of considering prognosis when setting rehabilitation goals.\n\nFor this 72-year-old woman with pancreatic cancer and MSCC, rehabilitation would focus on maximizing independence while recognizing the likely limited prognosis. Goals might include safe transfers, wheelchair mobility, bladder/bowel management, and support for both patient and family in adapting to changed functional status.",
