      {
        "answer": "**Prognostic Scoring Systems for Spinal Metastases**\n\nSeveral validated scoring systems help predict survival and guide treatment decisions in patients with spinal metastases:\n\n1. **Tokuhashi Score (Revised)**\n   - Evaluates six parameters (0-15 points total):\n     - Performance status (KPS)\n     - Number of extraspinal metastases\n     - Number of vertebral metastases\n     - Visceral metastases\n     - Primary tumor type\n     - Neurological status\n   - Interpretation:\n     - 0-8 points: Conservative treatment (predicted survival <6 months)\n     - 9-11 points: Palliative surgery (predicted survival 6-12 months)\n     - 12-15 points: Excisional surgery (predicted survival >12 months)\n   - For pancreatic cancer (poor prognosis tumor), the score would likely be lower\n\n2. **Tomita Score**\n   - Simpler system (2-10 points) based on:\n     - Primary tumor growth rate\n     - Visceral metastases\n     - Bone metastases\n   - Interpretation:\n     - 2-3 points: Wide/marginal excision (long survival expected)\n     - 4-5 points: Marginal/intralesional excision (medium survival)\n     - 6-7 points: Palliative surgery (short survival)\n     - 8-10 points: Supportive care (very short survival)\n   - Pancreatic cancer would score high (aggressive primary)\n\n3. **SORG Nomogram (Skeletal Oncology Research Group)**\n   - More recent model using:\n     - Age\n     - Primary tumor diagnosis\n     - ECOG performance status\n     - Presence of visceral metastases\n     - Number of spine metastases\n     - Location of spine metastases\n   - Provides 30-day, 90-day, and 1-year mortality risk\n\n4. **Spinal Instability Neoplastic Score (SINS)**\n   - Assesses risk of instability (not survival)\n   - Guides decisions about stabilization procedures\n\n5. **Bilsky Scale**\n   - Grades epidural spinal cord compression on MRI\n   - Helps determine urgency of decompression\n\n**Influence on Management:**\n\nFor this 72-year-old woman with pancreatic cancer (typically poor prognosis):\n\n1. **If poor prognosis scores (Tokuhashi <8, Tomita >7)**\n   - Focus on palliative approaches\n   - Radiotherapy rather than extensive surgery\n   - Short course radiotherapy regimens\n   - Early palliative care involvement\n   - Rehabilitation focused on short-term quality of life goals\n\n2. **If intermediate prognosis scores**\n   - Consider less invasive surgical options (e.g., percutaneous stabilization)\n   - Single-stage posterior decompression and stabilization\n   - Followed by adjuvant radiotherapy\n\n3. **If unexpectedly good prognosis scores**\n   - More aggressive surgical approach might be justified\n   - Consider en bloc resection if solitary metastasis\n   - More intensive rehabilitation program\n\nIt's important to note that these scoring systems are guides and should be used alongside clinical judgment and patient preferences. The MDT discussion should incorporate these prognostic indicators while recognizing their limitations. For pancreatic cancer with metastatic disease, the prognosis is typically poor (median survival often <6 months), which would generally favor less invasive approaches focused on quality of life.",
