        "answer": "**Management of MSCC in a Patient on Anticoagulation**\n\nThe presence of anticoagulation therapy complicates management due to increased bleeding risk during interventions and potential delays in treatment. Here's how I would approach this:\n\n**1. Anticoagulation Assessment**\n- Identify specific anticoagulant (warfarin, DOAC, LMWH)\n- Check recent INR if on warfarin\n- Determine indication and risk of thromboembolism (CHA₂DS₂-VASc score)\n- Assess bleeding risk (HAS-BLED score)\n\n**2. Immediate Management**\n- Continue dexamethasone 16mg daily as per NICE CG75\n- Urgent hematology consultation for anticoagulation management\n\n**3. Anticoagulation Reversal/Bridging for Interventions**\n\n**For Warfarin:**\n- If surgical intervention likely: reverse with vitamin K (5-10mg IV) and PCC (Prothrombin Complex Concentrate)\n- Target INR <1.5 for surgery\n- Consider IVC filter if very high thrombotic risk\n\n**For DOACs:**\n- Omit at least 48 hours before surgery (longer for dabigatran with renal impairment)\n- Consider specific reversal agents if urgent surgery needed:\n  - Idarucizumab for dabigatran\n  - Andexanet alfa for factor Xa inhibitors\n\n**For LMWH:**\n- Omit dose 24 hours before procedure\n\n**4. Surgical Considerations**\n- May influence surgical approach (less invasive techniques)\n- Meticulous hemostasis during surgery\n- Consider cell salvage techniques\n- Close postoperative monitoring for hemorrhagic complications\n\n**5. Radiotherapy Planning**\n- Radiotherapy can generally proceed without anticoagulation reversal\n- May influence decision between surgery vs. radiotherapy (favoring radiotherapy)\n\n**6. Postoperative Anticoagulation**\n- Restart prophylactic anticoagulation 24-48 hours post-surgery if hemostasis achieved\n- Delayed restart of therapeutic anticoagulation (typically 72 hours post-surgery)\n- Consider bridging with LMWH when restarting warfarin\n\n**7. Monitoring**\n- Close neurological monitoring\n- Vigilance for epidural hematoma (can worsen cord compression)\n- Regular hemoglobin checks\n- Monitoring for signs of thromboembolism during interruption\n\n**8. MDT Discussion**\n- Balanced decision-making involving:\n  - Spinal surgeons\n  - Hematologists\n  - Anesthesiologists\n  - Oncologists\n  - Cardiologists (for AF management)\n\nThe presence of anticoagulation may shift the risk-benefit balance toward non-surgical management with radiotherapy, particularly in a patient with pancreatic cancer who likely has limited life expectancy. However, if neurological deterioration is rapid or spinal instability is present, the benefits of surgical intervention may still outweigh the risks of temporarily reversing anticoagulation.",
