      {
        "answer": "Surgical management of flexor tendon sheath infection requires urgent intervention:\n\n**Preoperative Preparation**:\n1. Informed consent explaining risks including potential for permanent stiffness, adhesions, and in severe cases, amputation\n2. Mark the patient and affected digit\n3. Appropriate anesthesia (typically axillary block or general anesthesia)\n4. Prophylactic antibiotics if not already administered\n5. Tourniquet control\n\n**Surgical Approach**:\n1. **Brunner incisions** (zigzag) or mid-lateral incisions to access the flexor sheath\n   - For the middle finger, I would use mid-axial incisions to avoid crossing flexion creases\n   - Extend incisions as needed to visualize the entire tendon sheath from A1 pulley to distal insertion\n\n2. **Exploration and Debridement**:\n   - Careful identification and protection of neurovascular bundles\n   - Opening of the tendon sheath between pulleys (preserving A2 and A4 pulleys if possible)\n   - Thorough irrigation with copious amounts of saline\n   - Assessment of tendon viability and debridement of necrotic tissue\n   - Collection of fluid and tissue samples for microbiology\n\n3. **Irrigation System**:\n   - In severe cases, placement of irrigation catheter for continuous or intermittent post-operative irrigation\n   - Typically using a small catheter (e.g., pediatric feeding tube) inserted proximally\n   - Exit site distally for through-and-through irrigation\n\n4. **Wound Management**:\n   - Decision for primary closure vs. delayed closure based on severity\n   - If significant contamination, consider leaving wound partially open\n   - Placement of small drain if needed\n   - Sterile dressing\n\n**Postoperative Management**:\n1. Elevation of the hand above heart level\n2. Continuation of appropriate IV antibiotics\n3. Regular wound assessment\n4. Early mobilization when appropriate to prevent adhesions\n5. Hand therapy involvement from early stages\n\n**Follow-up**:\n1. Daily wound checks initially\n2. Adjustment of antibiotics based on culture results\n3. Removal of irrigation system when infection controlled\n4. Progressive mobilization program\n5. Regular assessment of function and complications",
