      {
        "answer": "Flexor tendon sheath infections can lead to several serious complications:\n\n1. **Tendon Adhesions and Stiffness**:\n   - **Management**: Early protected mobilization, intensive hand therapy, tenolysis in severe cases after infection resolution\n   - **Prevention**: Appropriate surgical technique, early motion protocols when safe\n\n2. **Tendon Necrosis and Rupture**:\n   - **Management**: Debridement of necrotic tendon, potential staged tendon reconstruction or grafting once infection cleared\n   - **Prevention**: Early diagnosis and treatment, adequate debridement\n\n3. **Osteomyelitis**:\n   - **Management**: Extended antibiotic course (6+ weeks), possible bone debridement\n   - **Prevention**: Thorough initial debridement, appropriate antibiotic coverage\n\n4. **Joint Contracture**:\n   - **Management**: Intensive hand therapy, static/dynamic splinting, possible capsulotomy\n   - **Prevention**: Early protected motion when appropriate\n\n5. **Spread to Deep Spaces**:\n   - **Management**: Extended surgical exploration of involved spaces, broader debridement\n   - **Prevention**: Early adequate surgical intervention of the primary infection\n\n6. **Digital Ischemia/Necrosis**:\n   - **Management**: Urgent fasciotomy if compartment syndrome, possible amputation if irreversible\n   - **Prevention**: Early surgical decompression, monitoring of compartment pressures\n\n7. **Complex Regional Pain Syndrome**:\n   - **Management**: Pain management, desensitization, physiotherapy, possible referral to pain specialist\n   - **Prevention**: Adequate pain control, early mobilization\n\n8. **Amputation**:\n   - **Management**: Appropriate level selection, rehabilitation, prosthetic consideration\n   - **Prevention**: Early aggressive treatment of infection\n\n9. **Persistent Infection**:\n   - **Management**: Re-exploration, cultures for resistant or unusual organisms, extended/adjusted antibiotics\n   - **Prevention**: Adequate initial debridement, appropriate antibiotic coverage\n\n10. **Functional Impairment**:\n    - **Management**: Occupational therapy, adaptive equipment, vocational rehabilitation\n    - **Prevention**: Comprehensive rehabilitation program\n\nThe key principles in managing these complications include:\n1. Early recognition through vigilant monitoring\n2. Multidisciplinary approach involving hand surgeons, infectious disease specialists, and hand therapists\n3. Patient education regarding expected outcomes and rehabilitation requirements\n4. Staged reconstruction approaches after complete resolution of infection\n5. Psychological support for patients with significant functional impairment",
