  - Lower threshold for repeat imaging or exploration if not improving\n   - More frequent inflammatory marker monitoring\n   - Enhanced vigilance for spreading infection\n\n4. **Rehabilitation**:\n   - Modified hand therapy protocols considering potentially delayed healing\n   - Education regarding diabetic hand care\n   - Longer follow-up period\n\n5. **Multidisciplinary Approach**:\n   - Endocrinology involvement throughout\n   - Vascular surgery consultation if concerns about perfusion\n   - Infectious disease input for antibiotic management\n\nThe prognosis is generally worse in diabetic patients, with higher rates of complications including amputation. I would counsel the patient accordingly and maintain a lower threshold for aggressive intervention if the infection is not responding to initial treatment.",
