      {
        "answer": "I would approach this situation with professionalism and respect for the ongoing procedure:\n\n**Wait for an appropriate moment:** I would wait until Mr. Richardson reaches a suitable pause in the closure process or until closure is complete to avoid disrupting a critical part of the procedure.\n\n**Request a brief discussion:** I would politely ask, 'Mr. Richardson, could I please clarify something about the implants used for the NJR form when convenient?'\n\n**Present the information objectively:** 'I've noticed that the implants documented by the scrub nurse differ from what we had in our template. The form shows we've used Brand X femoral and tibial components rather than the planned Brand Y components.'\n\n**Ask for clarification:** 'Was there a specific reason for the change? I want to ensure the NJR documentation is accurate.'\n\n**Offer solutions:** 'Would you like me to update our records accordingly, or should we discuss this further after we complete the procedure?'\n\n**Maintain a constructive tone:** Throughout the conversation, I would maintain a respectful, non-accusatory tone focused on accurate documentation and patient care rather than suggesting an error has been made.",
        "question": "How would you approach this situation with your consultant?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Accurate NJR documentation is crucial for multiple reasons:\n\n**Patient safety and traceability:**\n- Enables identification of patients with specific implants if recalls occur\n- Creates a permanent record of exactly what components are in the patient\n- Allows appropriate follow-up based on the specific implant characteristics\n\n**Quality improvement:**\n- Provides data on implant performance and longevity\n- Helps identify underperforming implants earlier\n- Contributes to evidence-based decision making for future patients\n\n**Professional and legal requirements:**\n- NJR submission is mandatory for all eligible joint replacements in the UK\n- Accurate documentation is a GMC requirement under Good Medical Practice\n- Forms part of the medical record which has legal standing\n\n**Research and development:**\n- Contributes to national datasets that inform implant design and surgical technique\n- Supports research into outcomes and complications\n\n**Service evaluation:**\n- Allows benchmarking of surgeon and hospital performance\n- Contributes to revalidation data for surgeons\n\n**Consent implications:**\n- Patients should be informed about what implant they have received\n- Particularly important if different from what was discussed during consent",
        "question": "Why is accurate NJR documentation important?"
