      {
        "answer": "As a trainee, I have several specific responsibilities regarding the NJR:\n\n**Documentation accuracy:**\n- Ensuring all fields are completed correctly and truthfully\n- Double-checking implant details with scrub staff if uncertain\n- Verifying patient details to prevent misidentification\n\n**Consent for NJR:**\n- Ensuring the patient has consented to have their data submitted to the NJR\n- Documenting this consent appropriately\n- Understanding that NJR consent is separate from surgical consent\n\n**Timely submission:**\n- Completing forms promptly while details are fresh\n- Ensuring forms are submitted within the required timeframe\n- Following up on any queries or incomplete forms\n\n**Understanding implant systems:**\n- Familiarizing myself with commonly used implant systems\n- Recognizing different components and their catalog numbers\n- Being aware of the templating process and its importance\n\n**Learning from NJR data:**\n- Using NJR reports to understand outcomes and best practices\n- Incorporating evidence from the NJR into clinical decisions\n- Discussing NJR findings in departmental meetings\n\n**Supporting consultants:**\n- Assisting in the accurate completion of forms\n- Flagging any discrepancies or concerns\n- Understanding that ultimate responsibility lies with the consultant surgeon\n\n**Professional development:**\n- Including NJR-related activities in my portfolio\n- Reflecting on cases and outcomes as part of learning\n- Understanding the importance of registry data in orthopaedic practice",
        "question": "What are your responsibilities regarding the NJR as a trainee?"
