      {
        "answer": "I would document this situation comprehensively while maintaining professionalism:\n\n**In the NJR form:**\n- Record the actual implants used (not the planned ones)\n- Include all required details: manufacturer, catalog numbers, sizes, batch numbers\n- Ensure the consultant surgeon verifies and signs the completed form\n- Note any special circumstances that led to the implant change if relevant\n\n**In the operation note:**\n- Document the procedure performed with the actual implants used\n- Include specific details of components: \"Total knee replacement using Brand X femoral component size 5 and Brand X tibial component size 4 with a 10mm polyethylene insert\"\n- Avoid any judgmental language about the change from planned implants\n- Ensure the note is factual and objective\n\n**In the patient's medical record:**\n- Update the implant record card with the correct implant details\n- Ensure implant stickers are affixed to the appropriate documentation\n- Document any discussions with the consultant about the implant change\n- Record any specific post-operative instructions related to the actual implants used\n\n**For personal reflection:**\n- Make a note in my portfolio about the learning points from this case\n- Reflect on the importance of clear communication and documentation\n- Consider how templates and actual components might sometimes differ and why\n\n**For departmental purposes:**\n- Update any relevant inventory or stock records\n- Ensure the hospital's implant database is updated with the correct information\n- Follow local protocols for recording implant usage",
