      {
        "answer": "If my consultant dismisses my concerns about the implant discrepancy, I would approach the situation carefully:\n\n**During the immediate interaction:**\n- Remain respectful and professional at all times\n- Clarify my specific concern: \"I'm concerned about the accuracy of the NJR submission and patient records\"\n- Frame it as a patient safety and documentation issue rather than questioning clinical judgment\n- Ask directly: \"How would you like me to proceed with completing the NJR form?\"\n\n**If concerns persist:**\n- Seek a private follow-up conversation: \"Could we discuss this further after the list?\"\n- Document the actual implants used based on scrub nurse records and implant stickers\n- Consult the theater documentation to verify what was actually implanted\n\n**Escalation if necessary:**\n- Discuss with another consultant or the department lead if I remain concerned\n- Speak with my educational supervisor about the situation\n- Contact the hospital's NJR lead if specific guidance is needed\n\n**Documentation:**\n- Record factually what implants were used based on the best available information\n- Document any discussions had regarding the discrepancy\n- Avoid attributing blame or making accusatory statements in records\n\n**GMC considerations:**\n- Remember my duty to act with honesty and integrity (GMC Good Medical Practice)\n- Consider patient safety as the primary concern\n- Recognize the hierarchy while also acknowledging my professional responsibility\n\n**Learning opportunity:**\n- Reflect on the experience in my portfolio\n- Discuss in an anonymized way during educational supervision\n- Consider whether departmental processes could be improved to prevent similar situations",
