      {
        "answer": "The duty of candour in this situation requires careful consideration:\n\n**Definition of duty of candour:**\n- A professional and statutory obligation to be open and honest with patients when something goes wrong with their treatment or care that causes, or has the potential to cause, harm or distress\n- Regulated by the GMC for individual practitioners and by the CQC for organizations under Regulation 20 of the Health and Social Care Act 2008\n\n**Application to this scenario:**\n- First, I need to determine if there has been harm or potential for harm\n- A change in implant is not necessarily harmful if the alternative is clinically appropriate\n- The key question is whether the change represents a patient safety incident or simply a variation in practice\n\n**If no harm or potential harm:**\n- Duty of candour is not formally triggered\n- However, good practice still involves transparency with the patient about what implant they received\n- This would typically be part of routine post-operative information\n\n**If potential harm exists:**\n- The consultant has primary responsibility as the lead clinician\n- I should discuss with the consultant the need to inform the patient\n- The patient should be told in a timely manner about the change and any implications\n- An apology should be offered if appropriate\n- The conversation should be documented\n- Support should be offered to the patient\n\n**My responsibilities as a trainee:**\n- Raise concerns appropriately with the consultant\n- Document accurately what implants were actually used\n- Support the consultant in fulfilling their duty of candour obligations\n- Escalate concerns if I believe duty of candour obligations are not being met\n\n**Documentation:**\n- Record any discussions about duty of candour considerations\n- Document what the patient has been told about their implants\n- Ensure the medical record accurately reflects the components used",
