        "answer": "If the consultant suggests I attempt the procedure with telephone guidance only:\n\n**Acknowledge their suggestion respectfully:**\n- \"Thank you for your confidence in me, but I have concerns about proceeding without direct supervision\"\n\n**Clearly communicate my limitations:**\n- \"I have assisted with external fixators but have not performed one independently\"\n- \"Given the neurovascular compromise and urgency of this case, I don't believe telephone guidance would be sufficient for patient safety\"\n\n**Emphasize patient safety:**\n- \"The patient has signs of neurovascular compromise which may worsen during the procedure\"\n- \"This is a complex injury requiring experienced hands, especially with the comminution and displacement\"\n- \"I'm concerned that any delay or technical error could lead to permanent neurovascular damage\"\n\n**Refer to GMC guidance:**\n- According to GMC Good Medical Practice, I must \"recognize and work within the limits of [my] competence\" and \"ask for help when necessary\"\n- This situation represents a clear need for senior input based on my current skill level\n\n**Propose alternatives:**\n- \"Would it be possible for you to come in, or is there another consultant or experienced registrar available?\"\n- \"I'm happy to prepare everything so we can proceed immediately upon your arrival\"\n- \"If you're unable to attend, should we consider transferring the patient to another center?\"\n\n**Document the conversation:**\n- I would document this discussion in detail, including my expressed concerns about competence, the patient safety issues raised, and the final decision made.\n\nThis approach maintains professional respect while prioritizing patient safety and acknowledging my limitations in accordance with GMC guidance.",
