      {
        "answer": "**Duty of Candour in This Situation:**\n\nThe statutory duty of candour applies when a notifiable safety incident occurs where treatment has resulted in, or could result in, significant harm. In this scenario:\n\n**Recognition of a notifiable incident:**\n- The failed closed reduction followed by neurovascular compromise represents a situation where harm has occurred or could occur\n- The delay in definitive treatment (external fixation) due to my inability to perform the procedure independently could potentially exacerbate this harm\n\n**My professional duty of candour requires:**\n\n1. **Timely disclosure to the patient:**\n   - Inform Mr. Thompson as soon as reasonably practicable that:\n     - The initial reduction attempt was unsuccessful\n     - There are signs of neurovascular compromise requiring urgent intervention\n     - I need to involve a consultant for the external fixator application\n     - This may result in some delay to definitive treatment\n\n2. **Provide a truthful account:**\n   - Explain in clear, straightforward language what has happened\n   - Avoid technical jargon or defensive language\n   - Do not attribute blame but focus on facts\n\n3. **Apologize:**\n   - Offer a genuine apology for the unsuccessful reduction and the need to escalate care\n   - This is not an admission of liability but an expression of regret\n\n4. **Provide reasonable support:**\n   - Ensure regular monitoring of neurovascular status while awaiting consultant arrival\n   - Manage pain appropriately\n   - Keep the patient informed of timeframes and next steps\n   - Address any questions or concerns\n\n5. **Document the disclosure:**\n   - Record the conversation with the patient including:\n     - Information provided\n     - Apology given\n     - Patient's questions and responses\n     - Plan for ongoing care\n\n6. **Follow-up communication:**\n   - Provide written follow-up if required by local policy\n   - Ensure the patient has a point of contact for further questions\n\nThis fulfills both the statutory duty of candour (Health and Social Care Act 2008 Regulation 20) and the professional duty of candour as outlined in GMC guidance. It maintains trust with the patient while acknowledging the clinical situation honestly and transparently.",
