        "answer": "The British Orthopaedic Association Standards for Trauma (BOAST) guidelines for ankle fracture management include:\n\n**Assessment:**\n- Full clinical examination including neurovascular status\n- Documentation of soft tissue status\n- Appropriate radiographs (AP, lateral, mortise views)\n- Classification of fracture pattern\n\n**Timing of Surgery:**\n- Emergency surgery for open fractures, compromised skin, or neurovascular compromise\n- Surgery within 24-48 hours for closed fractures with acceptable soft tissues\n- Delayed surgery if significant swelling (consider temporary external fixation)\n\n**Surgical Principles:**\n- Anatomic reduction of articular surface\n- Stable fixation of fractures\n- Assessment of syndesmotic stability intraoperatively\n- Appropriate soft tissue handling\n\n**Post-operative Care:**\n- Appropriate immobilization\n- DVT prophylaxis as per local protocol\n- Early mobilization and physiotherapy\n- Regular follow-up with radiographic assessment\n\n**Special Considerations:**\n- Diabetic patients require more careful soft tissue assessment\n- Higher risk of complications in elderly, diabetics, smokers\n- Consider non-operative management in low-demand patients with stable fractures\n\nThe guidelines emphasize the importance of:  \n1. Timely intervention based on soft tissue status\n2. Anatomic reduction of the ankle mortise\n3. Stable fixation to allow early mobilization\n4. Recognition of patient factors that increase complication risk",
