        "answer": "**Classification Systems for Ankle Fractures:**\n\n1. **Weber/Danis-Weber Classification** (based on level of fibular fracture relative to syndesmosis):\n   - Type A: Below syndesmosis (stable, often managed non-operatively)\n   - Type B: At level of syndesmosis (potentially unstable, requires assessment)\n   - Type C: Above syndesmosis (unstable, usually requires surgery)\n\n2. **Lauge-Hansen Classification** (based on mechanism of injury):\n   - Supination-External Rotation (SER): Most common (60-70%)\n   - Pronation-External Rotation (PER): Second most common\n   - Supination-Adduction (SA): Transverse medial or vertical lateral malleolus fracture\n   - Pronation-Abduction (PA): Rare, comminuted lateral malleolus\n   - Pronation-Dorsiflexion (PD): Rare, high fibular fracture with posterior malleolus\n\n3. **AO/OTA Classification**:\n   - Type 44-A: Infrasyndesmotic fibula fracture\n   - Type 44-B: Transsyndesmotic fibula fracture\n   - Type 44-C: Suprasyndesmotic fibula fracture\n\n**For this trimalleolar fracture:**\n- Weber B/AO 44-B classification (fibular fracture at level of syndesmosis)\n- Likely Lauge-Hansen SER stage IV (lateral malleolus, posterior malleolus, medial malleolus)\n\n**How Classification Guides Management:**\n\n1. **Stability Assessment:**\n   - Weber B fractures require assessment of medial structures\n   - Trimalleolar pattern indicates instability requiring surgical fixation\n   - Posterior malleolus involvement >25% requires direct fixation\n\n2. **Fixation Strategy:**\n   - Lateral malleolus: Plate and screws for Weber B\n   - Medial malleolus: Lag screws or tension band wiring\n   - Posterior malleolus: Direct posterior approach for large fragments\n   - Syndesmosis: Intraoperative assessment after bony fixation\n\n3. **Approach and Sequence:**\n   - Typically lateral approach first (fibula)\n   - Then medial approach (medial malleolus)\n   - Posterolateral or posteromedial approach for posterior malleolus\n   - Sequence may be modified based on specific fracture pattern\n\n4. **Rehabilitation Protocol:**\n   - Weber B with syndesmotic injury: Protected weight-bearing for 6-12 weeks\n   - Trimalleolar fractures: More conservative protocol, especially in diabetics\n   - Posterior malleolus fixation: Modified ankle ROM exercises\n\n5. **Diabetic Considerations:**\n   - More robust fixation regardless of classification\n   - Extended non-weight-bearing period\n   - Higher threshold for surgical intervention in borderline cases\n   - More frequent follow-up to detect early complications\n\nThe classification helps determine stability, need for surgery, fixation strategy, and rehabilitation protocol, but must be modified based on patient factors like diabetes which significantly increases complication risks.",
