        "answer": "Differentiating necrotising fasciitis from other soft tissue infections is critical but challenging. Key distinguishing features include:\n\n**Clinical Features Suggestive of NF:**\n- Pain out of proportion to visible findings (hallmark sign)\n- Rapid progression over hours rather than days\n- Systemic toxicity disproportionate to visible skin changes\n- \"Wooden-hard\" induration of subcutaneous tissues\n- Crepitus (subcutaneous gas) in 30-50% of cases\n- Skin changes: initially minimal, progressing to dusky, then purple patches, then bullae and necrosis\n- Failure to respond to initial antibiotics\n- Anesthesia of affected skin (due to nerve destruction)\n\n**Differentiating from Cellulitis:**\n- Cellulitis typically has well-defined margins and responds to antibiotics\n- Cellulitis rarely causes systemic toxicity early in its course\n- Cellulitis pain is proportionate to visible inflammation\n- Cellulitis progresses over days rather than hours\n\n**Laboratory Findings:**\n- LRINEC score ≥6 (CRP, WBC, Hemoglobin, Sodium, Creatinine, Glucose)\n- Marked leukocytosis with left shift\n- Elevated CK (muscle involvement)\n- Metabolic acidosis and elevated lactate\n\n**Imaging:**\n- Plain radiographs: subcutaneous gas (sensitivity only 25%)\n- CT/MRI: fascial thickening, fluid collections, gas in soft tissues\n\nThe gold standard for diagnosis remains surgical exploration with the characteristic \"dishwater pus\" and lack of bleeding from fascial planes, along with easy separation of fascia from underlying tissue with blunt dissection.",
        "question": "How would you differentiate necrotising fasciitis from other soft tissue infections?"
