      {
        "answer": "**Microbiology:**\n\nNecrotising fasciitis is classified into three types based on microbiology:\n\n1. **Type I (Polymicrobial)** - 70-80% of cases\n   - Mixed aerobic and anaerobic bacteria\n   - Common organisms: Streptococci, Staphylococci, Enterobacteriaceae, Bacteroides, Peptostreptococcus\n   - Often seen in diabetic patients, post-surgical wounds, pressure sores\n\n2. **Type II (Monomicrobial)** - 20-30% of cases\n   - Group A Streptococcus (S. pyogenes) with or without Staphylococcus aureus\n   - Often affects previously healthy individuals\n   - Can follow minor trauma, surgical procedures, or occur spontaneously\n   - Associated with streptococcal toxic shock syndrome in 50% of cases\n\n3. **Type III (Marine-related)**\n   - Vibrio vulnificus and other marine organisms\n   - Associated with seawater or seafood exposure\n   - Rare but highly virulent\n\n**Pathophysiology:**\n\nThe disease progression involves several key mechanisms:\n\n1. **Initial Entry Point**\n   - Bacteria enter through breaks in skin (trauma, surgery, insect bites)\n   - Can be cryptogenic (no obvious entry point) in up to 20% of cases\n\n2. **Fascial Spread**\n   - Bacteria spread along fascial planes due to lower blood supply and resistance\n   - Horizontal spread exceeds visible skin changes (\"tip of the iceberg\" phenomenon)\n\n3. **Toxin Production**\n   - Bacterial exotoxins (particularly from GAS) cause direct tissue damage\n   - Superantigens trigger massive cytokine release causing systemic inflammatory response\n\n4. **Vascular Compromise**\n   - Bacterial enzymes (hyaluronidase, collagenase) destroy fascial barriers\n   - Thrombosis of perforating vessels to skin and subcutaneous tissues\n   - Leads to tissue ischemia, necrosis, and gangrene\n\n5. **Systemic Effects**\n   - Toxin-mediated shock\n   - Cytokine storm\n   - Multi-organ dysfunction\n\nThe rapid progression is due to several factors:\n- Fascial planes have poor blood supply (limited immune response)\n- Bacterial synergy in polymicrobial infections\n- Toxin-mediated tissue destruction\n- Thrombosis of blood vessels causing ischemia\n\nThis explains the classic findings of pain out of proportion (due to nerve involvement), rapid progression, and systemic toxicity disproportionate to visible skin changes.",
        "question": "What are the microbiology and pathophysiology of necrotising fasciitis?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "**Surgical Approach for Debridement:**\n\n1. **Timing:**\n   - Immediate surgical exploration once diagnosis is suspected\n   - Delays >24 hours associated with significantly increased mortality\n\n2. **Incision Planning:**\n   - Extensive incisions that extend beyond visible affected areas\n   - Longitudinal incisions following fascial planes\n   - Must extend into healthy tissue proximally and distally\n\n3. **Exploration Findings:**\n   - Diagnostic features: \"dishwater\" gray fluid, lack of bleeding, easy separation of fascia from underlying tissue with blunt dissection\n   - Necrotic fascia appears gray, dull, and non-bleeding\n\n4. **Extent of Debridement:**\n   - Radical excision of ALL necrotic tissue until healthy, bleeding tissue is reached\n   - May require multiple fasciotomies\n   - Often more extensive than initially apparent (\"tip of the iceberg\")\n   - Sacrifice of functional structures may be necessary to save life\n\n5. **Tissue Sampling:**\n   - Multiple deep tissue samples for microbiology\n   - Frozen section histology if available\n\n6. **Re-examination:**\n   - Planned re-look at 24 hours (or earlier if clinical deterioration)\n   - Serial debridements often required (average 3-4 procedures)\n\n**Principles of Wound Management After Debridement:**\n\n1. **Initial Management:**\n   - Leave wounds open after initial debridement\n   - Loose packing with saline-soaked gauze or\n   - Negative Pressure Wound Therapy (NPWT) once infection controlled\n\n2. **Negative Pressure Wound Therapy:**\n   - Benefits: removes exudate, reduces edema, increases granulation\n   - Contraindicated in active infection with necrotic tissue\n   - Can be used after adequate debridement and infection control\n   - Typically changed every 48-72 hours\n\n3. **Dressing Changes:**\n   - Regular inspection (at least daily initially)\n   - Assess for residual infection/necrosis\n   - Consider bedside debridement of small areas of necrosis\n\n4. **Definitive Wound Closure:**\n   - Delayed primary closure rarely possible\n   - Split-thickness skin grafts for superficial defects\n   - Local or free flaps for complex defects with exposed tendons/bones\n   - Timing: once wound is clean with healthy granulation (typically 1-3 weeks)\n\n5. **Amputation Considerations:**\n   - May be necessary for life-threatening infection despite debridement\n   - Consider early if extensive muscle necrosis, joint involvement, or vascular compromise\n   - Functional outcome must be balanced with life-saving measures\n\n6. **Rehabilitation Planning:**\n   - Early physiotherapy involvement\n   - Occupational therapy assessment\n   - Psychological support\n\nThe key principles are early, aggressive, and complete debridement of all necrotic tissue, followed by appropriate wound management and reconstruction planning once infection is controlled.",
        "question": "What surgical approach would you take for debridement and what are the principles of wound management after debridement?"
