      {
        "answer": "**Prognostic Factors:**\n\n1. **Timing of Intervention:**\n   - Delay in diagnosis >24 hours: significantly increased mortality\n   - Delay in surgical debridement: strongest modifiable risk factor for mortality\n\n2. **Patient Factors:**\n   - Advanced age (>65 years)\n   - Comorbidities (diabetes, immunosuppression, renal failure, liver disease)\n   - Extent of tissue involvement\n   - Presence of septic shock or multi-organ failure at presentation\n   - Elevated lactate levels (>2 mmol/L)\n   - Elevated LRINEC score (≥8 associated with higher mortality)\n\n3. **Microbiology:**\n   - Group A Streptococcal infection with toxic shock syndrome (higher mortality)\n   - Clostridial infection (gas gangrene)\n   - Polymicrobial infections (generally worse prognosis than monomicrobial)\n\n4. **Anatomical Location:**\n   - Perineal/genital involvement (Fournier's gangrene)\n   - Cervical necrotising fasciitis (risk of airway compromise)\n   - Truncal involvement (difficult to debride adequately)\n\n**Potential Complications:**\n\n1. **Acute Complications:**\n   - Septic shock (30-70% of cases)\n   - Multi-organ dysfunction syndrome\n   - Acute kidney injury\n   - Acute respiratory distress syndrome\n   - DIC (disseminated intravascular coagulation)\n   - Compartment syndrome\n\n2. **Surgical Complications:**\n   - Significant tissue loss requiring complex reconstruction\n   - Amputation (5-25% of limb cases)\n   - Functional impairment\n   - Cosmetic disfigurement\n\n3. **Long-term Complications:**\n   - Chronic pain\n   - Contractures and scarring\n   - Lymphedema\n   - Neuropathic pain\n   - Psychological sequelae (PTSD, depression, body image issues)\n   - Reduced quality of life\n   - Long-term disability and inability to return to previous occupation\n\n4. **Mortality:**\n   - Overall mortality: 20-40% even with optimal management\n   - Mortality with delayed diagnosis/treatment: up to 70%\n   - Mortality with toxic shock syndrome: up to 60%\n\n**Predictors of Poor Outcome:**\n- Admission lactate >2 mmol/L\n- White cell count >30,000 cells/mm³\n- Creatinine >2 mg/dL\n- Age >65 years\n- Heart rate >110 bpm\n- Temperature <36°C\n- Presence of hemorrhagic bullae\n\nThe key to improving outcomes is early recognition, prompt surgical intervention, appropriate antibiotics, and supportive care. Despite optimal management, necrotising fasciitis remains a condition with high morbidity and mortality.",
