        "answer": "The surgical options for this olecranon fracture include:\n\n1. **Tension Band Wiring (TBW):**\n   - Converts tensile forces to compressive forces across the fracture site\n   - Ideal for simple transverse fractures\n   - Technically straightforward and cost-effective\n   - Limitations: hardware prominence, migration requiring removal (up to 80%)\n\n2. **Plate Fixation:**\n   - Pre-contoured olecranon plates or posterior plates\n   - Better for comminuted fractures, oblique patterns, or fractures extending distally\n   - Provides more stable fixation but more extensive dissection\n   - Lower rates of hardware removal (10-20%)\n\n3. **Fragment Excision with Triceps Advancement:**\n   - For comminuted fractures involving <50% of articular surface in elderly patients\n   - Avoids hardware complications\n   - Potential for some extension weakness\n\n4. **Intramedullary Screw Fixation:**\n   - Less prominent hardware\n   - Limited to specific fracture patterns\n\n5. **Total Elbow Arthroplasty:**\n   - Reserved for severely comminuted fractures in elderly with poor bone quality\n   - Significant activity restrictions post-operatively\n\nThe decision factors include:\n\n- **Patient factors:**\n  - Age (74 years - consider bone quality)\n  - Activity level and functional demands\n  - Comorbidities (COPD - anesthetic considerations)\n  - Hand dominance and occupation\n\n- **Fracture characteristics:**\n  - Displacement (>2mm generally requires fixation)\n  - Comminution (simple vs complex)\n  - Articular involvement (percentage and step-off)\n  - Bone quality (osteoporotic vs normal)\n\nFor this 74-year-old man, I would consider:\n- If it's a simple transverse fracture with good bone quality: TBW\n- If comminuted or poor bone quality: plate fixation\n\n\nThe decision would be made after thorough discussion with the patient regarding functional expectations, risks, and benefits of each approach.",
