    "interview_questions": [
      {
        "answer": "The radiographs show a posterolateral dislocation of the right elbow with the radius and ulna displaced posteriorly and laterally relative to the distal humerus. The radiocapitellar and ulnohumeral relationships are disrupted.\n\nMy primary concerns are:\n\n1. **Neurovascular status** - particularly the status of the brachial artery and median, ulnar, and radial nerves which can be compromised in elbow dislocations\n\n2. **Associated fractures** - I would carefully assess for:\n   - Coronoid process fractures (seen in 10-15% of dislocations)\n   - Radial head fractures (present in approximately 5-10% of cases)\n   - Capitellum or trochlea fractures\n\n3. **Joint stability** - determining if this is a simple dislocation (purely ligamentous) or complex dislocation (with associated fractures)\n\n4. **Soft tissue injury** - including potential damage to the medial and lateral collateral ligament complexes",
        "question": "What do you see on these radiographs and what is your primary concern for this patient?"
