      {
        "answer": "Elbow dislocations are classified in several ways, each influencing management:\n\n**1. Directional Classification:**\n- **Posterior/Posterolateral (90-95%)** - most common, as seen in this case\n- **Anterior (2-5%)** - rare, often associated with significant trauma and olecranon fractures\n- **Medial/Lateral/Divergent** - uncommon variants with specific injury patterns\n\n**2. Stability-based Classification:**\n- **Simple dislocations (80%)** - purely ligamentous injuries without significant fractures\n   - Management: Closed reduction, brief immobilization (7-10 days), early controlled mobilization\n\n- **Complex dislocations (20%)** - associated with fractures that contribute to instability\n   - Management: Often requires surgical intervention to address fractures and restore stability\n\n**3. The \"Terrible Triad\"** - specific complex pattern including:\n   - Elbow dislocation\n   - Radial head fracture\n   - Coronoid process fracture\n   - Management: Usually requires surgical fixation of fractures and ligament repair\n\n**4. O'Driscoll Classification** for posterolateral rotatory instability:\n   - Stage 1: Posterolateral subluxation\n   - Stage 2: Incomplete posterolateral dislocation\n   - Stage 3: Complete dislocation\n\nThe classification directly impacts management:\n- Simple stable dislocations: Closed reduction and early mobilization\n- Complex or unstable dislocations: Surgical intervention\n- Terrible triad injuries: Almost always require surgical management\n\nStability testing after reduction is crucial to determine the appropriate treatment pathway.",
        "question": "How would you classify elbow dislocations and how does this affect your management approach?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "My approach to reducing this posterolateral elbow dislocation would be:\n\n**Pre-reduction:**\n1. **Adequate analgesia and sedation**\n   - Procedural sedation with monitoring (typically propofol or midazolam with fentanyl)\n   - Regional block consideration (supracondylar or axillary block)\n\n2. **Thorough neurovascular assessment and documentation**\n   - Document median, ulnar, and radial nerve function\n   - Assess and record brachial, radial pulses\n\n3. **Review imaging** to understand the exact displacement pattern and any associated fractures\n\n**Reduction technique:**\n1. **Positioning**\n   - Patient supine\n   - Assistant stabilizing the proximal humerus\n\n2. **In-line traction**\n   - Apply gentle longitudinal traction to the forearm\n   - Maintain slight flexion (15-20°) during initial traction\n\n3. **Olecranon manipulation**\n   - Apply direct pressure to the olecranon process\n   - Push anteriorly and medially to reverse the displacement\n\n4. **Flexion maneuver**\n   - While maintaining traction and olecranon pressure, gently flex the elbow\n   - This helps disengage the coronoid from the olecranon fossa\n\n5. **Supination of the forearm**\n   - Particularly important in posterolateral dislocations\n\n**Post-reduction:**\n1. **Immediate neurovascular reassessment**\n\n2. **Stability testing**\n   - Gentle examination through range of motion to assess stability\n   - Varus/valgus stress testing if appropriate\n\n3. **Post-reduction radiographs**\n   - Confirm concentric reduction\n   - Assess for previously unrecognized fractures\n\n4. **Immobilization**\n   - Posterior splint with elbow at 90° flexion and forearm in neutral or slight pronation\n   - Avoid full extension which may compromise reduction\n\nIf closed reduction fails or is incomplete, I would consider reduction under general anesthesia or open reduction if necessary.",
