      {
        "answer": "**Acute Complications:**\n\n1. **Neurovascular Injury (10-20% of cases)**\n   - **Brachial artery injury** - Management: Urgent vascular surgery consultation if pulse absent or diminished after reduction\n   - **Nerve injuries** (median most common, followed by ulnar and radial)\n     - Management: Document, monitor, consider EMG/NCS at 3-4 weeks if no improvement\n\n2. **Compartment Syndrome**\n   - Management: Regular compartment checks, fasciotomy if pressures >30mmHg or within 30mmHg of diastolic pressure\n\n3. **Irreducible Dislocation**\n   - Causes: Soft tissue interposition (MCL, brachialis, radial nerve), buttonholing through capsule\n   - Management: Open reduction\n\n4. **Recurrent Instability**\n   - Management: Hinged external fixator or surgical repair of ligamentous structures\n\n**Intermediate Complications:**\n\n1. **Heterotopic Ossification (3-5%)**\n   - Management: Prophylaxis with NSAIDs (indomethacin 25mg TID for 3 weeks) in high-risk cases\n   - Surgical excision if mature and causing functional limitation (typically after 6-12 months)\n\n2. **Missed Associated Injuries**\n   - Essex-Lopresti lesion (radial head fracture with disruption of interosseous membrane)\n   - Management: High index of suspicion, forearm and wrist imaging if symptoms\n\n**Long-term Complications:**\n\n1. **Stiffness and Contracture (most common)**\n   - Management: Structured physiotherapy, static progressive splinting\n   - Consider manipulation under anesthesia or arthroscopic release if severe\n\n2. **Post-traumatic Arthritis (20% at 5 years)**\n   - Management: Analgesia, activity modification, intra-articular steroid injections\n   - Surgical options: arthroscopic debridement, arthroplasty in severe cases\n\n3. **Chronic Instability**\n   - Management: Ligament reconstruction (typically lateral collateral ligament complex)\n   - Consideration of hinged external fixation in complex cases\n\n4. **Ulnar Neuropathy (delayed)**\n   - Management: Padded protection, activity modification\n   - Surgical transposition if persistent or progressive\n\nThe key to managing complications is prevention through appropriate initial treatment, early recognition of complications, and structured rehabilitation protocols.",
