        "answer": "Several features in this patient's history strongly suggest an ACL injury:\n\n1. **Mechanism of injury** - A non-contact pivoting/cutting movement is the classic mechanism for ACL tears, accounting for approximately 70% of ACL injuries\n\n2. **Audible/sensory 'pop'** - This is reported in around 50-70% of ACL injuries and represents the actual tearing of the ligament\n\n3. **Immediate swelling** - Rapid hemarthrosis typically occurs within 2-4 hours due to bleeding from the torn ligament's vessels\n\n4. **Functional instability** - The report of the knee 'giving way' indicates loss of the ACL's stabilizing function\n\n5. **Inability to continue activity** - Most athletes cannot continue playing after an ACL tear due to pain, swelling, and instability\n\nThe combination of these features in a young athlete participating in a high-risk sport (football involves cutting, pivoting, and landing movements) makes ACL injury highly likely. The history has approximately 80-85% diagnostic accuracy when all these elements are present, though clinical examination and MRI are required for confirmation.",
