        "answer": "**Initial Imaging:**\n\n1. **Plain Radiographs** - Standard series including:\n   - AP weight-bearing view\n   - Lateral view\n   - Intercondylar notch view\n   - Skyline/patellofemoral view\n\n   Expected findings:\n   - Usually normal in isolated ACL injuries\n   - May show lateral capsular sign (Segond fracture) - avulsion of lateral tibial plateau\n   - Possible tibial spine avulsion in certain ACL injury patterns\n   - Deep lateral femoral notch sign (>2mm depression) suggests ACL injury with pivot shift\n   - Rule out other fractures or degenerative changes\n\n2. **MRI Scan** - Gold standard non-invasive investigation:\n\n   Expected primary findings in ACL tear:\n   - Complete or partial disruption of ACL fibers\n   - Abnormal course/orientation of the ACL\n   - Increased signal intensity within the ligament\n   - Empty notch sign in chronic cases\n\n   Associated findings (important for treatment planning):\n   - Bone bruising pattern (typically lateral femoral condyle and posterolateral tibial plateau)\n   - Meniscal tears (present in 50-75% of ACL injuries)\n   - Chondral damage\n   - Other ligamentous injuries (MCL most common)\n   - Capsular avulsions\n\n**Sensitivity and Specificity:**\n- MRI has approximately 86-95% sensitivity and 95-98% specificity for ACL tears\n- T2-weighted and proton density sequences are most useful\n\n**Additional Considerations:**\n- Stress radiographs may be considered in complex instability patterns\n- CT scan if bony involvement suspected\n- Contralateral knee comparison if needed\n\nThe classic bone bruising pattern on MRI (lateral femoral condyle and posterolateral tibial plateau) reflects the pivot shift mechanism and helps confirm diagnosis even when ACL visualization is challenging.",
