        "answer": "**Surgical Technique for ACL Reconstruction:**\n\n1. **Patient Positioning and Preparation:**\n   - Supine position with leg holder or lateral post\n   - Examination under anesthesia to confirm diagnosis\n   - Application of tourniquet (optional)\n\n2. **Diagnostic Arthroscopy:**\n   - Standard anterolateral and anteromedial portals\n   - Systematic assessment of all compartments\n   - Address concomitant pathology (meniscal tears, chondral lesions)\n\n3. **Graft Harvest** (depends on chosen graft):\n   - Performed before or after diagnostic arthroscopy\n\n4. **Tunnel Placement:**\n   - **Femoral Tunnel Options:**\n     - Transtibial technique (less anatomic)\n     - Anteromedial portal technique (more anatomic)\n     - Outside-in technique\n   - **Tibial Tunnel:**\n     - Centered at native ACL footprint\n     - Typically 7-10mm diameter based on graft size\n     - Proper angle (45-55°) to avoid roof impingement\n\n5. **Graft Preparation and Passage:**\n   - Appropriate sizing and preparation\n   - Passage through tunnels using passing sutures\n\n6. **Graft Fixation:**\n   - **Femoral Fixation Options:**\n     - Suspensory fixation (EndoButton, TightRope)\n     - Interference screw\n     - Cross-pins\n   - **Tibial Fixation Options:**\n     - Interference screw\n     - Post fixation with screw and washer\n     - Staples\n\n7. **Final Assessment:**\n   - Confirm stability with Lachman test\n   - Check for impingement in full extension\n   - Ensure full range of motion\n\n**Graft Options:**\n\n1. **Hamstring Tendon Autograft:**\n   - **Technique:** Harvest of semitendinosus ± gracilis tendons\n   - **Advantages:**\n     - Less anterior knee pain\n     - Smaller incision\n     - Less extensor mechanism disruption\n     - Good for female athletes (less strength deficit impact)\n   - **Disadvantages:**\n     - Slightly higher revision rates in some studies\n     - Longer healing time at bone-tendon interface\n     - Potential hamstring weakness\n\n2. **Bone-Patellar Tendon-Bone (BPTB) Autograft:**\n   - **Technique:** Central third of patellar tendon with bone blocks\n   - **Advantages:**\n     - Gold standard historically\n     - Bone-to-bone healing (6-8 weeks)\n     - Lower revision rates in high-level athletes\n     - Excellent initial fixation strength\n   - **Disadvantages:**\n     - Anterior knee pain (up to 30%)\n     - Risk of patellar fracture\n     - Quadriceps weakness\n     - Potential for patellofemoral issues\n\n3. **Quadriceps Tendon Autograft:**\n   - **Technique:** Central portion of quadriceps tendon ± bone block\n   - **Advantages:**\n     - Large cross-sectional area\n     - Good biomechanical properties\n     - Less donor site morbidity than BPTB\n     - Emerging evidence of excellent outcomes\n   - **Disadvantages:**\n     - Less long-term data compared to other grafts\n     - Technical challenges in harvest\n\n4. **Allograft Options:**\n   - **Types:** Achilles tendon, tibialis anterior/posterior, BPTB\n   - **Advantages:**\n     - No donor site morbidity\n     - Shorter operative time\n     - Good for revision cases\n   - **Disadvantages:**\n     - Higher failure rates in young athletes\n     - Slower incorporation\n     - Risk of disease transmission (minimal)\n     - Cost and availability issues\n\n**Current Evidence:**\nSystematic reviews show similar functional outcomes between hamstring and BPTB grafts, with BPTB having slightly lower revision rates but higher anterior knee pain. For a 24-year-old female footballer, either hamstring or BPTB would be appropriate, with the final decision based on patient preference after discussion of the specific advantages and disadvantages.",
