        "answer": "**ACL Rehabilitation Protocol:**\n\nThe rehabilitation follows a criterion-based progression rather than strictly time-based, though general timeframes guide expectations. The protocol follows BASK/BOA guidelines with phases:\n\n**Phase 1: Early Post-operative (0-2 weeks)**\n- **Goals:** Control pain/swelling, protect graft, restore basic ROM\n- **Interventions:**\n  - PRICE principles (Protection, Rest, Ice, Compression, Elevation)\n  - Early weight-bearing as tolerated with crutches\n  - Patella mobilizations\n  - Ankle pumps and gentle ROM exercises\n  - Quadriceps activation exercises (isometric sets)\n  - Straight leg raises when able\n- **Criteria for progression:** Minimal effusion, 0-90° ROM, quadriceps activation\n\n**Phase 2: Intermediate (2-6 weeks)**\n- **Goals:** Full extension, progressive flexion, normalize gait\n- **Interventions:**\n  - Progress to full weight-bearing without crutches\n  - Closed-chain exercises (mini squats, step-ups)\n  - Stationary cycling when ROM allows\n  - Proprioception exercises (balance board)\n  - Core stability training\n  - Gait re-education\n- **Criteria for progression:** Full extension, >120° flexion, normal gait pattern\n\n**Phase 3: Strengthening (6-12 weeks)**\n- **Goals:** Full ROM, progressive strengthening, basic neuromuscular control\n- **Interventions:**\n  - Advanced closed-chain exercises\n  - Introduction of controlled open-chain exercises\n  - Proprioception progression (unstable surfaces)\n  - Cross-training (swimming, elliptical)\n  - Straight-line jogging towards end if criteria met\n- **Criteria for progression:** Full ROM, good quadriceps control, minimal pain with activities\n\n**Phase 4: Advanced Strengthening (3-6 months)**\n- **Goals:** Sport-specific strength, advanced neuromuscular control\n- **Interventions:**\n  - Progressive plyometric training\n  - Running progression (straight → curves → acceleration/deceleration)\n  - Agility drills\n  - Sport-specific movement patterns\n  - Continued strength training with focus on quadriceps/hamstrings\n- **Criteria for progression:** 80% strength compared to contralateral side, good landing mechanics\n\n**Phase 5: Return to Sport (6-9+ months)**\n- **Goals:** Sport-specific conditioning, psychological readiness\n- **Interventions:**\n  - Advanced agility training\n  - Sport-specific drills at progressive intensities\n  - Controlled contact/collision introduction\n  - Simulated game situations\n  - Psychological readiness training\n\n**Return to Sport Criteria:**\n\n1. **Time-based considerations:**\n   - Minimum 9 months post-surgery for pivoting sports like football\n   - Evidence shows higher re-injury rates with return before 9 months\n\n2. **Objective measures:**\n   - **Strength testing:**\n     - Quadriceps and hamstring strength ≥90% of contralateral side\n     - Hamstring:quadriceps ratio of 60-70%\n     - Isokinetic testing at multiple speeds\n\n   - **Functional performance tests:**\n     - Limb Symmetry Index (LSI) ≥90% on hop test battery:\n       * Single hop for distance\n       * Triple hop for distance\n       * Crossover hop test\n       * 6m timed hop test\n     - Y-Balance test ≥90% of contralateral side\n\n   - **Movement quality assessment:**\n     - Landing Error Scoring System (LESS)\n     - Tuck jump assessment\n     - Drop vertical jump with video analysis\n     - Assessment of valgus collapse during cutting maneuvers\n\n3. **Subjective measures:**\n   - Patient-reported outcome measures:\n     - International Knee Documentation Committee (IKDC) score\n     - Knee Injury and Osteoarthritis Outcome Score (KOOS)\n     - ACL-Return to Sport after Injury (ACL-RSI) scale ≥70%\n\n4. **Psychological readiness:**\n   - Confidence in knee function\n   - Absence of fear of re-injury (kinesiophobia)\n   - Psychological readiness questionnaires\n\n5. **Sport-specific assessment:**\n   - Football-specific movement patterns\n   - Fatigue-resistance testing\n   - Decision-making under