        "answer": "**Potential Complications Following ACL Reconstruction:**\n\n**1. Early Complications:**\n\n- **Infection (0.5-1%):**\n  - **Presentation:** Increased pain, erythema, warmth, effusion, fever\n  - **Management:**\n    - Arthroscopic lavage and debridement\n    - Appropriate antibiotic therapy based on culture results\n    - Graft retention if possible (especially with early intervention)\n    - Serial inflammatory markers (CRP, ESR, WCC) to monitor response\n\n- **Venous Thromboembolism (DVT/PE) (0.5-2%):**\n  - **Presentation:** Calf pain, swelling, Homan's sign; dyspnea (PE)\n  - **Management:**\n    - Duplex ultrasound for diagnosis\n    - Therapeutic anticoagulation (LMWH, DOACs)\n    - Consider IVC filter for PE with contraindication to anticoagulation\n    - Thromboprophylaxis in high-risk patients\n\n- **Hemarthrosis:**\n  - **Presentation:** Tense, painful effusion\n  - **Management:**\n    - Aspiration if causing significant pain or limiting ROM\n    - PRICE principles\n    - Consider arthroscopic evacuation if recurrent/severe\n\n**2. Intermediate Complications:**\n\n- **Arthrofibrosis (2-5%):**\n  - **Presentation:** Limited ROM, particularly extension deficit\n  - **Management:**\n    - Aggressive physiotherapy\n    - Consider manipulation under anesthesia if <6 weeks post-op\n    - Arthroscopic lysis of adhesions for established cases\n    - Prevention: early ROM exercises, avoiding surgery in acute phase\n\n- **Cyclops Lesion (1-3%):**\n  - **Presentation:** Extension block, typically 3-6 months post-op\n  - **Management:**\n    - Arthroscopic excision of nodule\n    - Post-excision rehabilitation focusing on extension\n\n- **Hardware Complications:**\n  - **Presentation:** Pain over fixation sites, mechanical symptoms\n  - **Management:**\n    - Hardware removal if symptomatic\n    - Revision of fixation if graft compromise\n\n**3. Late Complications:**\n\n- **Graft Failure/Re-rupture (5-15%):**\n  - **Risk factors:** Young age, early return to sport, poor rehabilitation\n  - **Presentation:** Instability, giving way, effusion after new trauma\n  - **Management:**\n    - MRI confirmation\n    - Revision ACL reconstruction\n    - Address contributing factors (tunnel position, graft choice)\n\n- **Donor Site Morbidity:**\n  - **Patellar tendon:** Anterior knee pain, patellar fracture, tendinopathy\n  - **Hamstring:** Hamstring weakness, saphenous nerve injury\n  - **Management:**\n    - Targeted rehabilitation\n    - Activity modification\n    - Occasional surgical intervention for persistent issues\n\n- **Osteoarthritis (30-50% at 10-15 years):**\n  - **Risk factors:** Meniscal damage, chondral injury, malalignment\n  - **Management:**\n    - Weight management\n    - Activity modification\n    - Physiotherapy\n    - Standard OA treatment algorithm\n\n**4. Technical Complications:**\n\n- **Tunnel Malposition:**\n  - **Presentation:** Instability, impingement, limited ROM\n  - **Management:**\n    - Revision surgery with tunnel grafting if needed\n    - Single vs. two-stage approach based on tunnel expansion\n\n- **Graft Impingement:**\n  - **Presentation:** Extension deficit, pain, progressive graft attrition\n  - **Management:**\n    - Notchplasty\n    - Graft debridement or revision if damaged\n\n**5. Neurovascular Complications:**\n\n- **Saphenous Nerve Injury (Hamstring harvest):**\n  - **Presentation:** Medial knee/leg paresthesia\n  - **Management:** Usually self-limiting, neuropathic pain medications if persistent\n\n- **Infrapatellar Branch Injury:**\n  - **Presentation:** Anterior knee numbness\n  - **Management:** Reassurance, rarely requires intervention\n\n**Prevention Strategies:**\n\n1. **Surgical technique optimization:**\n   - Anatomic tunnel placement\n   - Appropriate graft tensioning\n   - Meticulous closure\n\n2. **Rehabilitation protocols:**\n   - Early ROM exercises\n   - Criterion-based progression\n   - Comprehensive return-to-sport testing\n\n3. **Patient education:**\n   - Realistic expectations\n   - Compliance with rehabilitation\n   - Gradual return to activities\n\n4. **Risk stratification:**\n   - Thromboprophylaxis for high-risk patients\n   - Modified rehabilitation for complex cases\n   - Delayed return to sport for young athletes\n\nThe overall complication rate following ACL reconstruction is approximately 10-15%, with major complications occurring in <5% of cases.",
