        "answer": "This presentation is concerning for septic arthritis of the hip, which is a surgical emergency in children. My approach would be systematic:\n\n**Initial Assessment:**\n- Rapid clinical evaluation focusing on vital signs, general appearance, and hip examination\n- Assess for signs of sepsis using age-appropriate criteria\n- Evaluate the position of the affected limb (typically held in flexion, abduction, and external rotation)\n\n**Differential Diagnosis:**\n1. **Septic arthritis** - Most concerning diagnosis requiring urgent intervention\n2. **Transient synovitis** - More benign, often following viral illness, but a diagnosis of exclusion\n3. **Perthes disease** - Usually more insidious onset, less systemic features\n4. **Slipped Capital Femoral Epiphysis (SCFE)** - Typically in older children (8-15 years), often overweight\n5. **Osteomyelitis** of proximal femur - Can present similarly, may coexist\n6. **Psoas abscess** - Can cause similar positioning\n7. **Appendicitis** - Can refer pain to hip region\n\n**Kocher's Criteria** would guide my diagnostic reasoning. These include:\n- Non-weight bearing\n- Temperature >38.5°C\n- ESR >40mm/hr or CRP >20mg/L\n- WCC >12,000/mm³\n\nThe presence of all four criteria gives >99% probability of septic arthritis. With three criteria, probability is approximately 93%.\n\nI would proceed urgently with investigations while preparing for likely surgical intervention.",
