      {
        "answer": "I would request the following investigations:\n\n**Blood Tests:**\n- **Full Blood Count** - Elevated WCC (>12,000) with neutrophilia suggests bacterial infection\n- **CRP and ESR** - Inflammatory markers, typically elevated in infection (CRP >20mg/L, ESR >40mm/hr)\n- **Blood Cultures** - To identify causative organism before antibiotics administered\n- **Renal and Liver Function** - Baseline before antibiotics and to assess for systemic impact\n\n**Imaging:**\n- **Plain Radiographs (AP and frog lateral of pelvis)** - May show joint effusion, widened joint space, or soft tissue swelling. Also rules out other pathologies like Perthes or SCFE\n- **Ultrasound** - Highly sensitive for detecting hip effusion, can guide aspiration. Non-invasive and no radiation exposure\n- **MRI** - Gold standard if diagnosis uncertain. Can detect early osteomyelitis, soft tissue infection, and differentiate from transient synovitis\n\n**Joint Aspiration:**\n- Ultrasound-guided aspiration is both diagnostic and therapeutic\n- Send fluid for:\n  - **Gram stain and culture** - Direct visualization and identification of organism\n  - **Cell count** - WCC >50,000 cells/mm³ with >90% polymorphs strongly suggests septic arthritis\n  - **Glucose** - Lower in septic fluid compared to serum\n  - **Crystal analysis** - To exclude other causes\n\n**Other:**\n- **Throat swab** - If tonsillitis/pharyngitis suspected as primary source\n- **Urine culture** - To identify potential primary source\n\nThese investigations help confirm diagnosis, identify causative organism, guide antibiotic therapy, and rule out differential diagnoses. The combination of clinical assessment, laboratory findings, and imaging provides the most accurate diagnosis.",
