        "answer": "Management of confirmed septic arthritis of the hip in a child is a surgical emergency requiring prompt multidisciplinary care:\n\n**1. Immediate Measures:**\n- **IV access** and fluid resuscitation if signs of sepsis\n- **Analgesia** - Weight-appropriate dosing of paracetamol, ibuprofen, and opiates if needed\n- **NBM** in preparation for surgical intervention\n- **Broad-spectrum IV antibiotics** after cultures taken (don't delay >1 hour if septic)\n  - Typically flucloxacillin + ceftriaxone or according to local protocol\n  - Cover Staphylococcus aureus, Streptococcus species, and Kingella kingae (common in this age group)\n\n**2. Surgical Management:**\n- **Emergency arthrotomy and washout** - Ideally within 6 hours of diagnosis\n- **Surgical approach options:**\n  - Anterior approach (Smith-Petersen) - Most common\n  - Arthroscopic washout in selected cases\n- **Procedure includes:**\n  - Copious irrigation with normal saline\n  - Debridement of infected/necrotic tissue\n  - Consideration of drain placement\n  - Samples for microbiology\n\n**3. Post-operative Care:**\n- **Antibiotics:**\n  - Continue IV antibiotics for minimum 48-72 hours\n  - Convert to oral when clinically improved (afebrile, improved inflammatory markers, tolerating oral intake)\n  - Total course typically 3-4 weeks, guided by clinical response and inflammatory markers\n  - Adjust based on culture results and sensitivities\n\n**4. Monitoring and Follow-up:**\n- **Daily clinical assessment** - Vital signs, pain levels, hip examination\n- **Serial inflammatory markers** (CRP, WCC) to track response\n- **Mobilize** when comfortable, with physiotherapy input\n- **Regular outpatient follow-up** at 2 weeks, 6 weeks, 3 months, and 6 months\n- **Radiographs** at follow-up to monitor for complications\n\n**5. Multidisciplinary Input:**\n- **Paediatric team** for overall care and antibiotic management\n- **Microbiology** input for targeted antibiotic therapy\n- **Physiotherapy** for 