        "answer": "**Causative Organisms in Paediatric Septic Arthritis by Age:**\n\n**Overall Most Common Organism:**\n- **Staphylococcus aureus** is the predominant pathogen across all age groups (30-60% of cases)\n  - Including community-acquired MRSA in some regions\n  - Associated with higher rates of complications\n\n**Age-Specific Patterns:**\n\n**Neonates (0-2 months):**\n- **Group B Streptococcus**\n- **Escherichia coli** and other gram-negative bacilli\n- **Staphylococcus aureus**\n- **Candida** species (especially in premature infants or those with central lines)\n\n**Infants and Toddlers (2 months-4 years):**\n- **Kingella kingae** - Increasingly recognized as a major pathogen in this age group (10-50% depending on detection methods)\n  - Often associated with concurrent or recent upper respiratory infections\n  - Typically causes milder disease than S. aureus\n- **Staphylococcus aureus**\n- **Streptococcus pneumoniae** (decreasing due to vaccination)\n- **Streptococcus pyogenes** (Group A Strep)\n- **Haemophilus influenzae type B** (rare in vaccinated populations)\n\n**Older Children (>4 years):**\n- **Staphylococcus aureus** (predominant)\n- **Streptococcus pyogenes**\n- **Neisseria gonorrhoeae** (in sexually active adolescents)\n\n**Special Considerations:**\n\n- **Salmonella** species - Associated with sickle cell disease\n- **Pseudomonas aeruginosa** - Puncture wounds through shoes/trainers\n- **Mycobacterium tuberculosis** - Consider in high-risk populations or endemic areas\n- **Brucella** species - In endemic areas or with unpasteurized dairy consumption\n\n**Diagnostic Challenges:**\n- Culture-negative septic arthritis occurs in 30-70% of cases\n- PCR techniques have improved detection rates, particularly for K. kingae\n- Blood cultures positive in only 40-50% of cases\n\nThis age-specific pattern guides empiric antibiotic choices while awaiting culture results. For the 4-year-old in this case, coverage should include S. aureus and K. kingae as 