      {
        "answer": "**Complications of Paediatric Septic Arthritis of the Hip:**\n\n**1. Short-term Complications:**\n\n- **Avascular Necrosis (AVN) of Femoral Head**\n  - Most serious complication (5-15% of cases)\n  - Results from increased intracapsular pressure compromising blood supply\n  - Risk factors: delayed diagnosis (>4 days), young age (<3 years), virulent organism (MRSA)\n\n- **Osteomyelitis**\n  - Extension of infection into adjacent bone\n  - May require extended antibiotic course and additional surgical procedures\n\n- **Sepsis/Systemic Inflammatory Response Syndrome**\n  - Life-threatening complication requiring intensive care support\n\n- **Persistent Infection/Treatment Failure**\n  - Inadequate surgical drainage or antibiotic resistance\n  - May require repeat surgical intervention\n\n**2. Long-term Complications:**\n\n- **Growth Disturbance**\n  - Damage to proximal femoral growth plate\n  - Can lead to leg length discrepancy or angular deformity\n\n- **Chondrolysis**\n  - Destruction of articular cartilage\n  - Results in joint space narrowing and early degenerative changes\n\n- **Secondary Osteoarthritis**\n  - Long-term sequela from cartilage damage\n  - May present in adolescence or early adulthood\n\n- **Recurrent Infection**\n  - Uncommon but possible, especially with inadequate initial treatment\n\n- **Joint Stiffness/Contractures**\n  - Due to capsular scarring or muscle contractures\n\n**Monitoring Protocol:**\n\n**Inpatient Monitoring:**\n- **Daily clinical assessment**\n  - Vital signs, pain levels, range of motion, neurovascular status\n- **Serial inflammatory markers**\n  - CRP (most responsive to treatment changes)\n  - ESR (slower to normalize)\n  - WBC count\n- **Post-operative radiographs**\n  - To confirm adequate drainage and rule out osteomyelitis\n\n**Outpatient Follow-up:**\n\n**Short-term (2-6 weeks):**\n- Clinical examination for pain, range of motion, gait\n- Inflammatory markers if not normalized at discharge\n- Plain radiographs to assess for early AVN or osteomyelitis\n\n**Medium-term (3-6 months):**\n- Detailed hip examination including range of motion\n- Gait assessment\n- Radiographs to assess femoral head contour and density\n- Consider MRI if concerns about AVN\n\n**Long-term (1-5 years):**\n- Annual follow-up for minimum 2 years\n- Assessment of growth (leg length measurement)\n- Radiographs to monitor for AVN, growth disturbance, or early arthritis\n- Functional assessment including sports participation\n\n**Indications for Additional Imaging:**\n- Persistent pain or limited range of motion\n- Abnormal gait\n- Radiographic changes concerning for AVN\n- Leg length discrepancy\n\n**MRI Indications:**\n- Gold standard for early detection of AVN (before radiographic changes)\n- Persistent symptoms despite normal radiographs\n- Assessment of extent of osteonecrosis\n\nEarly diagnosis and prompt surgical intervention remain the most important factors in preventing these complications, particularly AVN of the femoral head.",
