        "answer": "Several system improvements could help reduce on-the-day cancellations:\n\n**Theatre list management:**\n- More realistic scheduling that accounts for case complexity and potential complications\n- Buffer time between cases to absorb unexpected delays\n- Regular audit of procedure durations to inform more accurate scheduling\n\n**Consultant availability:**\n- Dedicated elective and trauma consultant teams where possible\n- Clear escalation policy for when trauma cases require consultant presence\n- On-call consultant backup for elective lists when the primary consultant is called away\n\n**Communication systems:**\n- Earlier warning systems for potential cancellations\n- Regular updates throughout the day on list progress\n- Improved communication between theatres to anticipate delays\n\n**Resource allocation:**\n- Protected elective theatre time separate from trauma theatres\n- Flexible staffing models to respond to unexpected demands\n- Evening/weekend lists to accommodate rescheduled cases\n\n**Patient preparation:**\n- Managing expectations during pre-assessment about potential cancellations\n- Prioritization system for rebooking cancelled cases\n- Support systems for patients affected by cancellations\n\n**Quality improvement:**\n- Regular review of cancellation data to identify patterns\n- Multidisciplinary approach to developing solutions\n- PDSA (Plan-Do-Study-Act) cycles to test and refine improvements\n\nI would reflect on this experience in my portfolio and consider discussing it at departmental meetings or quality improvement forums to contribute to system-level changes.",
