      {
        "answer": "**Duty of candour** is both a professional and statutory obligation for healthcare professionals to be open and honest with patients when something goes wrong with their treatment or care that causes, or has the potential to cause, harm or distress.\n\n**The statutory duty of candour** was introduced following the Francis Report into Mid Staffordshire NHS Foundation Trust and is enshrined in the Health and Social Care Act 2008 (Regulated Activities) Regulations 2014: Regulation 20.\n\n**In this scenario, the duty of candour applies as follows:**\n\n1. **Notification:** I must inform Mrs. Wilson and her son about the aseptic loosening of the acetabular component as soon as reasonably practicable.\n\n2. **Honest explanation:** I need to provide a truthful account of what has happened, explaining that aseptic loosening is a recognized complication that can occur even with optimal surgical technique.\n\n3. **Apology:** I should offer a genuine apology for the distress and need for further surgery, regardless of whether there was any error in the initial procedure.\n\n4. **Support:** I must outline what support is available to Mrs. Wilson, including the plan for revision surgery, pain management, and rehabilitation.\n\n5. **Documentation:** I need to document the discussion comprehensively in the medical records, including the information provided, questions asked, and responses given.\n\n6. **Written notification:** Following the verbal discussion, written notification should be provided that includes the same information.\n\n7. **Investigation:** If appropriate, I should explain that a review of the case will be conducted to identify any learning points.\n\nThe duty of candour helps maintain trust in the doctor-patient relationship and demonstrates respect for patient autonomy. It's important to note that the duty applies regardless of whether there was any error or negligence in the original procedure.",
