        "answer": "The key principles of breaking bad news that I would apply in this scenario are structured around the **SPIKES protocol**:\n\n**S - Setting:**\n- Ensure a private, quiet environment without interruptions\n- Arrange comfortable seating for Mrs. Wilson and her son\n- Allocate sufficient uninterrupted time (at least 20-30 minutes)\n- Have relevant imaging available to aid explanation\n- Silence my phone and ask not to be disturbed\n- Consider having a senior colleague present if appropriate\n\n**P - Perception:**\n- Assess what Mrs. Wilson and her son already understand about the situation\n- Ask open questions: \"What's your understanding of what's happening with your hip?\"\n- Identify any misconceptions early to address them appropriately\n- Gauge Mr. Wilson's specific concerns about his mother's initial surgery\n\n**I - Invitation:**\n- Determine how much information they want\n- Ask permission: \"I'd like to explain what the X-rays show and discuss options. Is that okay?\"\n- Respect their preferences for information detail\n- Consider Mrs. Wilson's wishes if they differ from her son's\n\n**K - Knowledge:**\n- Provide information in clear, non-technical language\n- Use a logical sequence: what happened, why it happened, what it means\n- Explain aseptic loosening as a recognized complication (1-2% incidence)\n- Avoid jargon and check understanding frequently\n- Use visual aids (X-rays, diagrams) to enhance understanding\n- Give information in manageable chunks with pauses\n\n**E - Empathy:**\n- Acknowledge emotions as they arise: \"I can see this is upsetting news\"\n- Validate their feelings: \"It's completely understandable to feel frustrated\"\n- Use empathic statements: \"I'm sorry you're facing this situation\"\n- Respond to anger with understanding rather than defensiveness\n- Maintain appropriate eye contact and body language\n- Allow silences when emotions are expressed\n\n**S - Strategy/Summary:**\n- Outline clear next steps for revision surgery\n- Provide realistic expectations about outcomes\n- Involve Mrs. Wilson in decision-making\n- Check understanding of the plan\n- Offer written information to supplement the discussion\n- Arrange appropriate follow-up\n- Provide contact details for questions\n\nThroughout this process, I would maintain focus on Mrs. Wilson as the patient while acknowledging her son's concerns. I would practice patient-centered communication, balancing honesty with hope, and ensuring both feel supported through this challenging situation.",
