    "clinical_scenario": "Mrs. Margaret Wilson, a 72-year-old female, underwent a total hip replacement 6 months ago for osteoarthritis. She has been experiencing increasing pain and difficulty walking over the past 2 months. Recent radiographs show aseptic loosening of the acetabular component requiring revision surgery. Her son, Mr. James Wilson (48), is accompanying her today and is visibly upset about the \"failure\" of the initial surgery. He has expressed concerns about the quality of care his mother received and has mentioned considering a formal complaint. You are the registrar who needs to explain the situation and discuss the revision surgery with both Mrs. Wilson and her son.",
    "clinical_scenario_title": "Failed Total Hip Replacement Requiring Revision",
    "consent_approach": "**Establish rapport:** Begin by introducing yourself to both Mrs. Wilson and her son. Acknowledge their concerns and create a comfortable environment for discussion.\n\n**Explain the situation:** Using clear, non-technical language, explain what has happened with the hip replacement:\n- The acetabular component (the socket part of the artificial hip) has become loose\n- This is a recognized complication that occurs in approximately 1-2% of cases within the first year\n- It is not necessarily due to any error or substandard care\n\n**Explain the revision procedure:** \n- Removal of the loose component\n- Possible bone grafting if there is bone loss\n- Insertion of a new acetabular component\n- Possible exchange of the femoral component if necessary\n\n**Discuss benefits:**\n- Relief of pain\n- Improved mobility and function\n- Prevention of further damage to surrounding bone\n\n**Discuss risks:**\n- General surgical risks (infection 1-2%, bleeding, DVT/PE 1-5%)\n- Specific risks to revision surgery:\n  - Higher infection rate compared to primary surgery (2-4%)\n  - Dislocation (5-10%)\n  - Leg length discrepancy\n  - Nerve injury (1%)\n  - Fracture during component removal (3-5%)\n  - Potential need for further revision in future\n\n**Recovery expectations:**\n- Longer hospital stay (5-7 days)\n- Extended rehabilitation period\n- Possible blood transfusion\n- Slower return to function compared to primary surgery\n\n**Answer questions:** Provide ample time for both Mrs. Wilson and her son to ask questions. Address concerns about why this happened and what will be done differently.\n\n**Confirm understanding:** Ask Mrs. Wilson to explain back her understanding of the procedure, risks, and benefits.\n\n**Obtain consent:** If proceeding, complete consent Form 1 for adults with capacity, ensuring Mrs. Wilson understands all aspects of the procedure.",
