    "breaking_bad_news_framework": "**Setting:** Ensure a private, quiet room with comfortable seating for both Mrs. Wilson and her son. Minimize interruptions by silencing your phone and asking not to be disturbed. Have relevant imaging available to explain the situation.\n\n**Perception:** Begin by assessing their understanding of the current situation.\n\"Mrs. Wilson, I'd like to discuss your hip replacement. First, could you tell me what you understand about what's happening with your hip right now?\"\n\"Mr. Wilson, I understand you have some concerns about your mother's care. Could you share what your main concerns are?\"\n\n**Invitation:** Determine how much information they want.\n\"I'd like to explain what we've found on your recent X-rays and what options we have moving forward. Is that okay with you?\"\n\n**Knowledge:** Provide information clearly and honestly.\n\"The X-rays show that the cup part of your hip replacement has started to come loose from the bone. This is called aseptic loosening and it's one of the known complications that can happen after hip replacement surgery. It's not due to infection, and it occurs in about 1-2% of cases within the first year, even when the surgery is performed to the highest standards.\"\n\n\"This explains the pain you've been experiencing. Unfortunately, this means we need to consider a second operation to replace this component.\"\n\n**Empathy:** Acknowledge their emotions and respond with empathy.\n\"I can see this is upsetting news, especially after you've already gone through one operation. I'm truly sorry you're facing this situation.\"\n\n\"Mr. Wilson, I understand your frustration and concern for your mother. It's natural to question what happened, and I want to address all your concerns.\"\n\n**Strategy/Summary:** Outline the plan moving forward.\n\"The good news is that we can fix this problem with a revision surgery. This involves replacing the loose component with a new one. The recovery may take a bit longer than your first surgery, but most patients experience good pain relief and improved function afterward.\"\n\n\"I'd like to discuss the details of this revision surgery, including the risks and benefits, so you can make an informed decision about how to proceed. We also have a specialist revision hip surgeon who would be involved in your care.\"\n\n\"Do you have any questions about what I've explained so far?\"",
