        "answer": "The Gartland classification for paediatric supracondylar fractures:\n\n**Type I:** Non-displaced or minimally displaced fractures. The anterior humeral line still intersects the capitellum. Management is typically non-operative with above-elbow cast immobilisation in 60-90° of flexion for 3-4 weeks.\n\n**Type II:** Displaced fractures with intact posterior cortex. The anterior humeral line passes anterior to the capitellum, but the posterior cortex remains intact providing some stability. Management may be closed reduction and casting if a stable reduction can be achieved, or closed reduction and percutaneous K-wire fixation.\n\n**Type III:** Completely displaced fractures with no cortical contact. These are unstable injuries requiring closed reduction and percutaneous K-wire fixation. Type III fractures have a higher incidence of neurovascular complications.\n\nModified classification includes:\n**Type IV:** Multidirectional instability with periosteal disruption circumferentially. These are highly unstable and often require open reduction.\n\nThe classification guides management decisions:\n- Type I: Non-operative management\n- Type II: Closed reduction ± K-wire fixation\n- Type III/IV: Closed/open reduction with K-wire fixation\n\nIn this case with vascular compromise, emergency reduction and stabilization is indicated regardless of classification, though the complete displacement confirms this as a Type III injury.",
        "question": "Can you describe the Gartland classification for supracondylar fractures and how it guides management?"
