      {
        "answer": "**Assessment of vascular compromise:**\n\n1. Detailed vascular examination:\n   - Presence/absence of radial and ulnar pulses\n   - Capillary refill time\n   - Hand colour, temperature, and perfusion\n   - Doppler assessment if pulses not palpable\n   - Pain on passive extension of fingers (compartment syndrome check)\n\n2. Categorize as:\n   - Pulseless but well-perfused hand (pink pulseless)\n   - Pulseless and poorly perfused hand (white pulseless)\n\n**Management approach:**\n\n1. **Immediate actions:**\n   - Gentle alignment in position of comfort (~30° flexion)\n   - Above-elbow backslab\n   - Elevation\n   - Avoid excessive manipulation pre-theatre\n   - Urgent theatre notification - surgical emergency\n\n2. **Definitive management:**\n   - Emergency closed reduction and K-wire fixation\n   - Reassess vascular status after reduction\n\n3. **If perfusion returns after reduction:**\n   - Continue regular neurovascular observations\n   - Standard post-operative care\n\n4. **If remains pulseless but well-perfused:**\n   - Close observation with 30-minute neurovascular checks\n   - Consider vascular surgery consultation\n\n5. **If remains pulseless and poorly perfused:**\n   - Immediate vascular exploration\n   - Brachial artery may require thrombectomy, repair, or bypass\n   - Fasciotomy if signs of compartment syndrome\n\nThe British Orthopaedic Association Standards for Trauma (BOAST) guidelines recommend emergency treatment for supracondylar fractures with vascular compromise, with reduction as the first step in management, as most vascular issues resolve with anatomic reduction of the fracture.",
