      {
        "answer": "**Neurovascular complications of supracondylar fractures:**\n\n1. **Vascular complications:**\n   - Brachial artery injury (entrapment, spasm, transection, intimal tear)\n   - Compartment syndrome\n   - Volkmann's ischaemic contracture (late complication)\n\n2. **Neurological complications:**\n   - Anterior interosseous nerve injury (most common) - assess by pinch sign\n   - Median nerve injury - assess by thumb/index finger opposition\n   - Radial nerve injury - assess by wrist/finger extension\n   - Ulnar nerve injury - assess by intrinsic hand muscle function\n\n**Monitoring protocol:**\n\n1. **Pre-operative monitoring:**\n   - Detailed neurovascular assessment on presentation\n   - Regular observations (15-30 minute intervals)\n   - Document using 5 P's: Pain, Pallor, Pulselessness, Paraesthesia, Paralysis\n\n2. **Specific assessments:**\n   - Vascular: pulses, capillary refill, colour, temperature\n   - Neurological: specific nerve function tests\n   - Compartment syndrome: pain on passive extension, pain out of proportion\n\n3. **Post-reduction/post-operative monitoring:**\n   - Immediate reassessment after reduction\n   - Regular observations (hourly initially)\n   - Maintain elevation and appropriate cast padding\n\n4. **Indications for intervention:**\n   - Persistent vascular compromise after reduction\n   - Progressive neurological deficit\n   - Increasing pain despite appropriate analgesia\n   - Pain on passive extension of fingers\n\n5. **Documentation:**\n   - Clear, timed entries\n   - Escalation pathway documented\n   - Communication with senior team members\n\nThe incidence of neurovascular complications is approximately 10-20% in displaced supracondylar fractures, with the anterior interosseous nerve most commonly affected. Most neurological injuries are neurapraxias that resolve spontaneously, but vascular compromise requires urgent intervention.",
