        "answer": "**Compartment syndrome definition:**\nCompartment syndrome occurs when increased pressure within a closed fascial compartment compromises the circulation and function of tissues within that space. In the forearm, there are volar and dorsal compartments that can be affected following supracondylar fractures.\n\n**Diagnosis:**\n\n1. **Clinical signs (6 P's):**\n   - **Pain:** Severe, out of proportion to injury, exacerbated by passive stretch\n   - **Pallor:** Pale or dusky appearance\n   - **Pulselessness:** Late sign, may have normal pulses initially\n   - **Paraesthesia:** Sensory changes in nerve distribution\n   - **Paralysis:** Late sign, motor weakness\n   - **Pressure:** Tense, firm compartment on palpation\n\n2. **Most reliable early signs:**\n   - Pain on passive extension of fingers\n   - Pain out of proportion to injury\n   - Pain unresponsive to appropriate analgesia\n\n3. **Compartment pressure measurement:**\n   - Not typically required in children with clear clinical signs\n   - If measured, absolute pressure >30mmHg or delta pressure (diastolic BP minus compartment pressure) <30mmHg is concerning\n\n**Management:**\n\n1. **Immediate actions:**\n   - Remove constrictive dressings/casts\n   - Maintain limb at heart level (not elevated)\n   - Optimize blood pressure to improve perfusion\n   - Urgent orthopaedic consultation\n\n2. **Definitive treatment:**\n   - Emergency fasciotomy of affected compartments\n   - Combined with fracture stabilization\n   - Volar and dorsal forearm fasciotomies may be required\n   - Leave wounds open initially\n\n3. **Post-fasciotomy care:**\n   - Regular wound inspection\n   - Delayed primary closure or skin grafting\n   - Physiotherapy\n   - Monitor for reperfusion injury\n\n4. **Timing is critical:**\n   - Muscle necrosis begins after 4-6 hours of ischemia\n   - Irreversible damage after 6-8 hours\n   - Volkmann's ischemic contracture is the end result of untreated compartment syndrome\n\nIn children with supracondylar fractures, compartment syndrome risk is highest with vascular injury, significant swelling, and after reperfusion following vascular repair. The threshold for fasciotomy should be low when clinical signs are present.",
