        "answer": "**K-wire fixation technique for supracondylar fracture:**\n\n1. **Patient positioning:**\n   - Supine position with arm on radiolucent table/arm board\n   - C-arm positioned for AP and lateral views\n\n2. **Reduction technique:**\n   - Longitudinal traction with elbow in extension for 10 minutes (time yourself!)\n   - Correct medial/lateral translation\n   - Flex elbow while applying anterior pressure on olecranon\n   - Confirm reduction with fluoroscopy\n\n3. **Standard K-wire configuration:**\n   - **Lateral entry:** 2 divergent lateral K-wires (most common)\n     - Entry at lateral epicondyle, directed medially and proximally\n     - Divergent pattern for rotational stability\n   - **Cross-pinning:** 1 lateral and 1 medial K-wire\n     - Provides better biomechanical stability\n     - Risk to ulnar nerve with medial pin (may need to do a mini-open procedure to visualize and protect ulnar nerve)\n\n4. **Technical considerations:**\n   - K-wires 2.0 mm diameter\n   - Both wires should engage opposite cortex\n   - Maintain 30-45° of elbow flexion during pinning\n   - Avoid ulnar nerve injury with medial pinning (mini-incision technique)\n   - Confirm stability under fluoroscopy\n\n5. **Post-fixation:**\n   - Cut and bend K-wires outside skin\n   - Above-elbow cast in 60-90° flexion\n   - K-wires typically removed at 3–4 weeks\n\n**Potential complications:**\n\n1. **Technique-related:**\n   - Ulnar nerve injury (7-15% with medial pinning)\n   - Inadequate fixation/loss of reduction\n   - Pin migration/infection\n   - Iatrogenic fracture\n\n2. **Early complications:**\n   - Vascular injury/compromise\n   - Compartment syndrome\n   - Nerve injuries (median, radial, anterior interosseous)\n   - Pin tract infection (1-6%)\n\n3. **Late complications:**\n   - Malunion (cubitus varus - \"gunstock deformity\")\n   - Growth disturbance\n   - Elbow stiffness\n   - Myositis ossificans\n\n4. **Prevention strategies:**\n   - Anatomic reduction\n   - Adequate pin spread at fracture site\n   - Bicortical fixation\n   - If medial pin needed, use mini-open technique with elbow in less flexion\n   - Appropriate post-operative immobilization\n   - Regular follow-up with radiographs\n\nThe complication rate for K-wire fixation of supracondylar fractures is approximately 10-15%, with pin tract infection being most common. Cubitus varus is the most common long-term complication, occurring in 5-10% of cases.",
