        "answer": "**Potential Complications:**\n\n1. **Avascular Necrosis (AVN):**\n   - Incidence varies by Hawkins classification (0-13% Type I, 20-50% Type II, 60-100% Type III, >90% Type IV)\n   - Results from disruption of blood supply to talar body\n   - May lead to collapse and severe arthritis\n\n2. **Post-traumatic Arthritis:**\n   - Affects 30-90% of patients with talar fractures\n   - Can involve ankle, subtalar, and/or talonavicular joints\n   - More common with displaced fractures, joint incongruity, and AVN\n\n3. **Malunion/Nonunion:**\n   - Malunion rates 5-25%, nonunion 5-10%\n   - Varus malunion most common and problematic\n   - Contributes to altered biomechanics and accelerated arthritis\n\n4. **Infection:**\n   - Higher risk with open fractures (10-20%)\n   - Can lead to osteomyelitis, which is difficult to eradicate\n\n5. **Complex Regional Pain Syndrome (CRPS):**\n   - Reported in 5-10% of cases\n   - Can significantly impair rehabilitation\n\n6. **Soft Tissue Complications:**\n   - Wound healing problems\n   - Sural nerve injury\n   - Tendon adhesions or impingement\n\n7. **Compartment Syndrome:**\n   - Less common than with calcaneal fractures but requires vigilance\n\n**Monitoring:**\n\n1. **For AVN:**\n   - Serial radiographs at 6, 12, 26, and 52 weeks\n   - Look for Hawkins sign at 6-8 weeks (subchondral lucency in talar dome indicating preserved blood flow)\n   - MRI at 3-6 months if concerned about AVN\n   - CT to assess extent of collapse if AVN develops\n\n2. **For Arthritis:**\n   - Weight-bearing radiographs at 6 and 12 months\n   - Clinical assessment of pain, stiffness, and function\n   - CT if concerned about subtle joint incongruity\n\n3. **For Malunion/Nonunion:**\n   - Serial radiographs to assess healing\n   - CT at 3-4 months if union is questionable\n   - Assessment of alignment (varus/valgus, dorsiflexion/plantarflexion)\n\n4. **For Infection:**\n   - Regular wound checks\n   - Monitor inflammatory markers if concerned\n   - Low threshold for aspiration/biopsy if infection suspected\n\n5. **For CRPS:**\n   - Regular pain assessment\n   - Evaluation for disproportionate pain, vasomotor changes, sudomotor changes, and trophic changes\n\n**Management:**\n\n1. **AVN Management:**\n   - Observation for small, asymptomatic lesions\n   - Protected weight-bearing if early AVN without collapse\n   - For established AVN with collapse:\n     - Young patient: Talar body bone grafting or vascularized bone graft\n     - Limited AVN: Osteochondral allograft\n     - Extensive AVN: Tibiotalocalcaneal arthrodesis or custom talar replacement\n\n2. **Arthritis Management:**\n   - Conservative: Activity modification, orthoses, bracing, analgesics\n   - Injections: Corticosteroid, hyaluronic acid\n   - Surgical: Arthroscopic debridement, distraction arthroplasty\n   - End-stage: Isolated fusion (subtalar, talonavicular, ankle) or pantalar fusion\n\n3. **Malunion/Nonunion Management:**\n   - Symptomatic malunion: Corrective osteotomy if deformity is significant\n   - Nonunion: Revision ORIF with bone grafting\n   - Salvage: Arthrodesis for unsalvageable cases\n\n4. **Infection Management:**\n   - Surgical debridement\n   - Culture-specific antibiotics\n   - Consider temporary antibiotic-impregnated cement spacer\n   - May require staged reconstruction\n\n5. **CRPS Management:**\n   - Multidisciplinary approach\n   - Early physical therapy\n   - Pain management (gabapentinoids, SNRIs)\n   - Consider stellate ganglion blocks\n   - Psychological support",
