      {
        "answer": "**Return to Sport Considerations After Talus Fracture:**\n\n1. **Timeline Expectations:**\n   - Minimum 6-9 months for return to non-contact sports\n   - 9-12+ months for return to contact/collision sports like rugby\n   - Some athletes may never return to previous level of competition\n   - Professional literature shows 50-70% return to pre-injury level for talar fractures\n\n2. **Healing Milestones:**\n   - **Radiographic union** - Complete fracture healing on imaging (typically 12-16 weeks)\n   - **Absence of AVN** - Serial imaging to rule out avascular necrosis\n   - **Functional recovery** - Restoration of strength, range of motion, proprioception\n   - **Sport-specific capacity** - Ability to perform sport-specific movements without pain\n\n3. **Functional Assessment Criteria:**\n   - **Range of motion** - At least 80% of contralateral side\n   - **Strength** - At least 90% of contralateral side for ankle dorsiflexion, plantarflexion, inversion, eversion\n   - **Single-leg balance** - Comparable to uninjured side\n   - **Hop testing** - Single hop, triple hop, crossover hop (>85% of uninjured side)\n   - **Agility testing** - Figure-8 runs, cutting maneuvers without pain or apprehension\n\n4. **Graduated Return Protocol:**\n   - **Phase 1:** Walking without limp or pain\n   - **Phase 2:** Jogging on flat surface\n   - **Phase 3:** Running with directional changes\n   - **Phase 4:** Sport-specific drills without contact\n   - **Phase 5:** Full practice participation\n   - **Phase 6:** Competitive play (often starting with limited minutes)\n\n5. **Protective/Supportive Measures:**\n   - **Taping/bracing** - Consider semi-rigid ankle brace for initial return\n   - **Footwear modification** - Proper cleats with adequate support\n   - **Orthotic consideration** - Custom orthoses if alignment issues persist\n   - **Impact modification** - Gradual increase in training volume and intensity\n\n6. **Psychological Readiness:**\n   - **Confidence assessment** - Tampa Scale for Kinesiophobia or ACL-RSI adapted for ankle\n   - **Fear of re-injury** - Address through graduated exposure\n   - **Realistic expectations** - Honest discussion about potential limitations\n   - **Mental health support** - Consider sports psychology referral if adjustment difficulties\n\n7. **Long-term Career Planning:**\n   - **Position modification** - Consider less demanding role if appropriate\n   - **Longevity discussion** - Potential impact on career duration\n   - **Alternative training** - Modified conditioning to reduce impact\n   - **Post-career considerations** - Education about potential for early-onset arthritis\n\n8. **Monitoring During Return:**\n   - **Pain monitoring** - Visual analog scale before/during/after activity\n   - **Swelling assessment** - Figure-of-8 measurements\n   - **Recovery time** - How long symptoms persist after activity\n   - **Functional progression** - Regular reassessment of performance metrics\n\n9. **Red Flags to Delay Return:**\n   - Persistent pain with activity\n   - Progressive or recurrent swelling\n   - Decreasing range of motion\n   - Signs of developing AVN on imaging\n   - Inability to complete sport-specific movements\n   - Psychological unreadiness\n\n10. **Maintenance Program:**\n    - Ongoing proprioceptive training\n    - Regular strength maintenance\n    - Monitoring for early signs of post-traumatic arthritis\n    - Consideration of activity modification for long-term joint preservation",
