        "answer": "**Blood Supply to the Talus:**\n\nThe talus has a unique and tenuous blood supply that significantly influences fracture management and prognosis:\n\n1. **Anatomical Considerations:**\n   - Approximately 60% of the talar surface is covered by articular cartilage\n   - No muscular or tendinous attachments to the talar body\n   - Blood supply enters primarily through three sources:\n\n2. **Primary Vascular Sources:**\n   - **Posterior tibial artery** → artery of the tarsal canal (posterolateral)\n   - **Anterior tibial artery** → artery of the tarsal sinus (lateral)\n   - **Peroneal artery** → perforating branches (posterolateral)\n\n3. **Regional Blood Supply:**\n   - **Talar head:** Primarily from dorsalis pedis and artery of tarsal sinus\n   - **Talar neck:** Branches from all three major sources (richest supply)\n   - **Talar body:** Primarily from artery of tarsal canal and sinus, entering through the neck\n   - **Posterior process:** Direct branches from posterior tibial artery\n\n4. **Intraosseous Pattern:**\n   - Blood vessels enter primarily at non-articular surfaces\n   - Intraosseous anastomoses are limited\n   - Retrograde flow from head to body is critical when neck vessels are disrupted\n\n**Influence on Management:**\n\n1. **Urgency of Reduction:**\n   - Displaced fractures require urgent reduction (ideally <24 hours) to minimize vascular compromise\n   - Prolonged displacement increases AVN risk by compromising remaining blood flow\n\n2. **Surgical Approach Considerations:**\n   - Minimize soft tissue stripping, particularly at the talar neck\n   - Preserve capsular attachments where possible\n   - Consider dual approaches rather than extensive single approach\n   - Medial malleolar osteotomy may be preferred over extensive soft tissue dissection\n\n3. **Fixation Strategy:**\n   - Achieve anatomic reduction to restore normal vascular channels\n   - Minimize periosteal stripping during fixation\n   - Consider headless compression screws to reduce prominence\n   - Avoid extensive hardware that may further compromise blood supply\n\n4. **Post-operative Protocol:**\n   - Non-weight bearing period to allow revascularization\n   - Regular imaging to monitor for AVN\n   - Protected weight bearing once allowed\n\n5. **Bone Grafting Considerations:**\n   - Primary bone grafting may be considered for comminuted areas\n   - Vascularized bone grafts may be used for established AVN\n\n**Influence on Prognosis:**\n\n1. **AVN Risk Stratification:**\n   - Hawkins classification directly correlates with AVN risk based on disruption of blood supply\n   - Type I (non-displaced): 0-13% AVN risk\n   - Type II (subtalar dislocation): 20-50% AVN risk\n   - Type III (subtalar and ankle dislocation): 60-100% AVN risk\n   - Type IV (adds talonavicular dislocation): >90% AVN risk\n\n2. **Prognostic Indicators:**\n   - **Hawkins sign:** Subchondral lucency in talar dome at 6-8 weeks indicates preserved blood flow\n   - **Fracture location:** Neck fractures have higher AVN risk than head or process fractures\n   - **Time to reduction:** Longer displacement correlates with higher AVN risk\n   - **Degree of comminution:** More comminution disrupts more vascular channels\n\n3. **Long-term Outcomes:**\n   - Even with optimal management, the compromised blood supply leads to:\n     - Higher rates of delayed union and nonunion\n     - Increased risk of post-traumatic arthritis\n     - Potential for late collapse even years after injury\n\n4. **Monitoring Requirements:**\n   - Extended follow-up needed (minimum 12-24 months)\n   - Serial imaging to detect early AVN\n   - Lifetime awareness of potential late complications",
