    "examination_overview": "**General Inspection**\n- Thenar muscle wasting\n- Skin changes/dryness in median nerve distribution\n\n**Provocative Tests**\n- Tinel's test (tapping over carpal tunnel)\n- Phalen's test (wrist flexion for 60 seconds)\n- Durkan's test (direct compression over carpal tunnel)\n- Reverse Phalen's test\n\n**Sensory Assessment**\n- Light touch and two-point discrimination in median nerve distribution\n- Compare with ulnar and radial nerve distributions\n- Compare with contralateral hand\n\n**Motor Assessment**\n- Thumb abduction strength (APB)\n- Opposition strength\n- Grip strength measurement\n\n**Differential Diagnosis Assessment**\n- Cervical spine examination\n- Thoracic outlet assessment\n- Cubital tunnel assessment\n- Pronator teres syndrome assessment\n\n**Contralateral Hand**\n- Examination for comparison and bilateral disease",
    "interview_questions": [
      {
        "answer": "Carpal tunnel syndrome (CTS) is an entrapment neuropathy of the median nerve at the wrist as it passes through the carpal tunnel. The carpal tunnel is formed by the carpal bones posteriorly and the transverse carpal ligament anteriorly.\n\nTypical presenting features include:\n\n- **Sensory symptoms** in the median nerve distribution (thumb, index, middle finger, and radial half of ring finger)\n  - Numbness, tingling, burning sensations\n  - Nocturnal symptoms often waking the patient from sleep\n  - Relief with shaking or moving the hand ('flick sign')\n\n- **Motor symptoms** in advanced cases\n  - Weakness of thumb abduction and opposition\n  - Thenar muscle wasting\n  - Dropping objects\n  - Fine motor difficulty\n\n- **Distribution** that typically spares the palm (palmar cutaneous branch of median nerve passes superficial to the transverse carpal ligament)\n\n- **Exacerbating factors**\n  - Repetitive wrist movements\n  - Sustained wrist flexion or extension\n  - Vibration exposure\n\nThe condition is often bilateral but typically worse in the dominant hand. It's more common in women, with peak incidence between 40-60 years of age. There are numerous associated conditions including pregnancy, diabetes, hypothyroidism, rheumatoid arthritis, and occupations involving repetitive wrist movements.",
