        "answer": "My examination for suspected carpal tunnel syndrome would be systematic:\n\n**1. Inspection:**\n- Thenar muscle wasting (advanced cases)\n- Skin changes in median nerve distribution (dryness, altered sweating)\n- Hand positioning and any obvious deformity\n\n**2. Provocative Tests:**\n- **Tinel's test** - Tapping over the carpal tunnel to elicit paresthesia in median nerve distribution\n- **Phalen's test** - Holding wrists in complete flexion for 60 seconds to reproduce symptoms\n- **Reverse Phalen's test** - Wrist extension for 60 seconds\n- **Durkan's test** (carpal compression test) - Direct pressure over the carpal tunnel for 30 seconds\n\n**3. Sensory Assessment:**\n- Light touch sensation in median, ulnar, and radial nerve distributions\n- Two-point discrimination (normal <6mm in fingertips)\n- Compare with contralateral side\n\n**4. Motor Assessment:**\n- Thumb abduction strength (abductor pollicis brevis)\n- Opposition strength\n- Grip and pinch strength measurements\n\n**5. Assess for Differential Diagnoses:**\n- Cervical spine examination (neck movements, Spurling's test)\n- Thoracic outlet assessment (Adson's test, elevated arm stress test)\n- Elbow examination for cubital tunnel syndrome (Tinel's at elbow, elbow flexion test)\n- Forearm examination for pronator teres syndrome\n\n**6. Bilateral Examination:**\n- Always examine both hands for comparison and to identify bilateral disease\n\nI would document my findings systematically, noting positive and negative findings to help establish the diagnosis and severity of carpal tunnel syndrome.",
        "question": "How would you examine a patient with suspected carpal tunnel syndrome?"
