        "answer": "I would discuss the following aspects of outcomes and recovery with this patient:\n\n**Expected Outcomes:**\n\n1. **Symptom Relief:**\n   - Nocturnal symptoms typically improve immediately or within days after surgery\n   - Tingling/numbness improves in 70-90% of patients\n   - Complete resolution in approximately 70-75% of cases\n   - Patients with severe/longstanding compression or thenar wasting may have incomplete recovery\n\n2. **Success Rates:**\n   - Overall satisfaction rate of 80-90%\n   - Better outcomes associated with shorter duration of symptoms before surgery\n   - Poorer outcomes associated with normal preoperative nerve conduction studies, workers' compensation cases, and secondary CTS (diabetes, rheumatoid arthritis)\n\n**Recovery Timeline:**\n\n1. **Immediate Postoperative Period (0-2 weeks):**\n   - Bulky dressing for 2-3 days, followed by simple dressing\n   - Keep wound clean and dry until healed (typically 10-14 days)\n   - Elevation of hand to reduce swelling\n   - Early finger and wrist movement encouraged to prevent stiffness\n   - Pain management with regular analgesics\n\n2. **Early Recovery (2-6 weeks):**\n   - Suture removal at 10-14 days\n   - Gradual increase in hand use for light activities\n   - Scar management (massage, silicone gel sheets if needed)\n   - Driving typically possible after 1-2 weeks (when able to grip steering wheel safely)\n   - Return to desk-based work possible at 2-3 weeks\n\n3. **Later Recovery (6-12 weeks):**\n   - Gradual return to heavier activities\n   - Pillar pain and scar tenderness typically improve\n   - Return to manual work typically at 4-6 weeks\n   - Full grip strength recovery may take 3-6 months\n\n**Specific Considerations for this Patient:**\n\n- As an accountant with keyboard-intensive work, he could likely return to modified duties at 2-3 weeks\n- May need ergonomic assessment of workstation upon return\n- Being right-hand dominant with right CTS, he should prepare for temporary assistance with writing and computer use\n- Should arrange for help with driving for at least 1-2 weeks postoperatively\n\n**Follow-up:**\n- Wound check at 2 weeks\n- Clinical review at 6 weeks to assess recovery\n- Further follow-up only if symptoms persist or recur\n\nI would emphasize that while most patients experience significant improvement, complete resolution of all symptoms is not guaranteed, particularly if there was longstanding compression with muscle wasting before surgery.",
