        "answer": "**Risk Factors for Carpal Tunnel Syndrome:**\n\n1. **Demographic Factors:**\n   - Female gender (3-4 times more common than in males)\n   - Age 40-60 years (peak incidence)\n   - Pregnancy (especially third trimester)\n   - Menopause\n\n2. **Medical Conditions:**\n   - **Endocrine disorders:**\n     - Diabetes mellitus (15-20% of diabetic patients develop CTS)\n     - Hypothyroidism\n     - Acromegaly\n   - **Inflammatory conditions:**\n     - Rheumatoid arthritis\n     - Gout\n     - Pseudogout\n   - **Fluid retention states:**\n     - Pregnancy\n     - Renal failure\n     - Congestive heart failure\n   - **Others:**\n     - Obesity (BMI >30)\n     - Amyloidosis\n     - Sarcoidosis\n\n3. **Anatomical Factors:**\n   - Previous wrist fracture or dislocation\n   - Congenitally small carpal tunnel\n   - Space-occupying lesions (ganglion, lipoma, etc.)\n   - Anomalous muscles or vessels\n\n4. **Occupational/Activity Factors:**\n   - Repetitive wrist movements\n   - Prolonged wrist flexion or extension\n   - Vibration exposure\n   - High-force manual tasks\n   - Occupations: assembly line workers, keyboard users, cashiers, hairdressers, musicians\n\n**Influence on Management:**\n\n1. **Addressing Underlying Conditions:**\n   - Optimize glycemic control in diabetic patients\n   - Treat hypothyroidism\n   - Manage inflammatory arthritis\n   - Weight loss counseling for obese patients\n\n2. **Pregnancy-related CTS:**\n   - Often resolves postpartum\n   - Conservative management preferred (splinting)\n   - Surgery typically deferred unless severe symptoms\n\n3. **Occupational CTS:**\n   - Workplace modifications and ergonomic assessment\n   - Activity modification and breaks\n   - Liaison with occupational health\n   - Return-to-work planning post-treatment\n\n4. **Bilateral Disease:**\n   - Common in systemic conditions\n   - May require staged bilateral surgery\n   - Consider dominant hand first\n\n5. **Recurrent or Resistant CTS:**\n   - More common with systemic risk factors\n   - May require more extensive release\n   - Consider alternative diagnoses\n\n6. **Prognosis:**\n   - Patients with systemic risk factors have poorer outcomes\n   - Higher recurrence rates in diabetes, rheumatoid arthritis\n   - May require longer follow-up\n\nFor this 58-year-old male accountant, I would specifically assess for diabetes, hypothyroidism, and inflammatory conditions. His occupation involves repetitive keyboard use, so ergonomic assessment and workplace modifications would be important components of management. If surgery is performed, a detailed return-to-work plan would be essential, potentially including temporary workplace accommodations or modified duties.",
