        "answer": "**AO/OTA Classification for Proximal Humerus Fractures:**\n\nThe AO/OTA classification uses a alphanumeric system where proximal humerus fractures are designated as 11- (1 for humerus, 1 for proximal segment).\n\n**Type A: Extra-articular, unifocal fractures**\n- 11-A1: Tuberosity fractures\n  - 11-A1.1: Greater tuberosity\n  - 11-A1.2: Lesser tuberosity\n  - 11-A1.3: Both tuberosities\n\n- 11-A2: Impacted metaphyseal fractures\n  - 11-A2.1: Without displacement\n  - 11-A2.2: With valgus displacement\n  - 11-A2.3: With varus displacement\n\n- 11-A3: Non-impacted metaphyseal fractures\n  - 11-A3.1: Simple\n  - 11-A3.2: Multifragmentary with lateral hinge\n  - 11-A3.3: Multifragmentary without lateral hinge\n\n**Type B: Extra-articular, bifocal fractures**\n- 11-B1: With metaphyseal impaction\n  - 11-B1.1: With lesser tuberosity\n  - 11-B1.2: With greater tuberosity\n  - 11-B1.3: With both tuberosities\n\n- 11-B2: Without metaphyseal impaction\n  - 11-B2.1: With lesser tuberosity\n  - 11-B2.2: With greater tuberosity\n  - 11-B2.3: With both tuberosities\n\n- 11-B3: With glenohumeral dislocation\n  - 11-B3.1: With lesser tuberosity\n  - 11-B3.2: With greater tuberosity\n  - 11-B3.3: With both tuberosities\n\n**Type C: Articular fractures**\n- 11-C1: Slight displacement\n  - 11-C1.1: With tuberosity involvement\n  - 11-C1.2: Without tuberosity involvement\n  - 11-C1.3: With glenohumeral dislocation\n\n- 11-C2: Impacted with marked displacement\n  - 11-C2.1: With tuberosity involvement\n  - 11-C2.2: Without tuberosity involvement\n  - 11-C2.3: With glenohumeral dislocation\n\n- 11-C3: Dislocated (head split)\n  - 11-C3.1: With tuberosity involvement\n  - 11-C3.2: Without tuberosity involvement\n  - 11-C3.3: With glenohumeral dislocation\n\n**Comparison with Neer Classification:**\n\n1. **Conceptual Differences:**\n   - **Neer:** Based on displaced segments (>1cm displacement or >45° angulation)\n   - **AO/OTA:** Based on fracture location, pattern, and articular involvement\n\n2. **Complexity:**\n   - **Neer:** Simpler system (1, 2, 3, 4-part + special categories)\n   - **AO/OTA:** More comprehensive with 27 subtypes\n\n3. **Focus:**\n   - **Neer:** Emphasizes displacement as key factor for treatment decisions\n   - **AO/OTA:** More detailed anatomical description of fracture patterns\n\n4. **Clinical Utility:**\n   - **Neer:** More commonly used in clinical practice, easier to apply\n   - **AO/OTA:** Better for research, more detailed for complex patterns\n\n5. **Prognostic Value:**\n   - **Neer:** Directly correlates with risk of AVN (4-part = highest risk)\n   - **AO/OTA:** Type C (articular) fractures generally have worse prognosis\n\n6. **Treatment Guidance:**\n   - **Neer:** More directly guides treatment (more parts = more likely surgical)\n   - **AO/OTA:** Provides detailed fracture morphology but less direct treatment algorithm\n\nBoth classifications have value, with Neer being more practical for everyday clinical use and treatment decisions, while AO/OTA provides more detailed fracture characterization useful for research and complex cases.",
