      {
        "answer": "**Duty of candour** is a professional and statutory obligation to be open and honest with patients when something goes wrong with their treatment or care that causes, or has the potential to cause, harm or distress.\n\nIn this scenario, the duty of candour applies in several ways:\n\n1. **Transparency about the clinical situation:** I must be completely honest with Mr. Johnson about the severity of his injury, the failure of limb salvage attempts, and the necessity for amputation. I should not minimize the gravity of the situation or provide false reassurance.\n\n2. **Disclosure of risks:** I must clearly explain all material risks of both proceeding with amputation and any alternative options, including the risks of not proceeding with amputation.\n\n3. **Acknowledgment of emotional impact:** I need to recognize and validate the significant psychological and life impact this will have on the patient.\n\n4. **Documentation:** I must thoroughly document our discussions, including information provided, questions asked, and the patient's understanding and decisions.\n\n5. **Support provision:** I should ensure appropriate psychological, social, and rehabilitation support is arranged.\n\n6. **Follow-up:** I must ensure there is a clear plan for ongoing care and communication.\n\nThe duty of candour is enshrined in law through the Health and Social Care Act 2008 (Regulated Activities) Regulations 2014: Regulation 20, and is emphasized in GMC guidance. It requires healthcare professionals to:\n\n- Tell the patient when something has gone wrong\n- Apologize to the patient\n- Offer an appropriate remedy or support to put matters right (if possible)\n- Explain fully the short and long-term effects of what has happened\n\nWhile the amputation is not a medical error but rather a necessary intervention due to trauma, the principles of openness, honesty, and transparency still apply fully in communicating with the patient about his condition and treatment options.",
        "question": "What is the duty of candour in this scenario?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "I would document this discussion comprehensively in the medical notes as follows:\n\n**Date, Time, Location**\n\n**Present:** Mr. Robert Johnson (patient), Mrs. Johnson (wife), Dr. [My Name] (Orthopaedic Registrar)\n\n**Discussion regarding need for above knee amputation of right lower limb**\n\n**Clinical context:** 42-year-old male, day 3 post motorcycle accident with severe right lower limb trauma. Despite initial limb salvage attempts (vascular repair, external fixation), patient has developed progressive tissue necrosis and compartment syndrome. MDT decision (orthopaedics, vascular surgery, plastic surgery) that above knee amputation is indicated due to non-viable limb and risk of sepsis.\n\n**Discussion content:**\n- SPIKES framework used for breaking bad news\n- Patient's current understanding of condition assessed\n- Explained severity of injury and failure of limb salvage attempts\n- Discussed need for above knee amputation based on MDT decision\n- Explained procedure, including level of amputation and anesthetic requirements\n\n**Benefits discussed:**\n- Prevention of life-threatening sepsis\n- Removal of non-viable, painful tissue\n- Potential for rehabilitation and prosthetic fitting\n- Long-term functional recovery possibilities\n\n**Risks discussed:**\n- Surgical risks: bleeding, infection, wound healing problems\n- Phantom limb pain and stump pain\n- Psychological impact\n- Need for potential revision surgery\n- Prosthetic-related complications\n- General anesthesia risks\n\n**Alternatives discussed:**\n- Further limb salvage attempts (explained poor prognosis)\n- Palliative approach (explained risks of sepsis and mortality)\n\n**Patient questions:**\n- [Document specific questions asked by patient and responses given]\n\n**Patient's reaction and understanding:**\n- [Document patient's emotional response and level of understanding]\n\n**Decision:**\n- [Document whether patient consented to procedure or requested more time]\n- [If consented] Consent form 1 completed and signed\n\n**Plan:**\n- Scheduled for surgery on [date/time]\n- Anesthetic assessment arranged\n- Amputation rehabilitation team referral made\n- Psychological support referral made\n- Pain team consultation requested\n\n**Follow-up plan:**\n- Post-operative review\n- Rehabilitation pathway discussed\n\nSigned: [My Name], Orthopaedic Registrar\nDate and Time\nGMC Number",
        "question": "How would you document this discussion in the medical notes?"
