      {
        "answer": "Supporting Mr. Johnson psychologically through this life-changing process requires a comprehensive approach:\n\n**Immediate support:**\n- **Acknowledge the emotional impact:** Validate that feelings of shock, grief, anger, and fear are normal responses to limb loss.\n- **Active listening:** Allow Mr. Johnson to express his emotions and concerns without rushing or dismissing them.\n- **Honest communication:** Provide clear information while maintaining realistic hope focused on adaptation and rehabilitation rather than false reassurance.\n- **Involve family:** With Mr. Johnson's permission, include his wife in discussions and support planning, recognizing her own emotional needs.\n\n**Professional psychological support:**\n- **Early referral:** Make an urgent referral to clinical psychology specialized in trauma and amputation adjustment.\n- **Screening:** Monitor for signs of adjustment disorder, depression, PTSD, which are common after traumatic amputation.\n- **Peer support:** Offer connection with the amputee peer support network when appropriate.\n\n**Multidisciplinary approach:**\n- **Amputation rehabilitation team:** Ensure early involvement of specialized rehabilitation physicians, nurses, physiotherapists, occupational therapists, and prosthetists.\n- **Pain management team:** Proactive management of both acute post-surgical pain and phantom limb pain.\n- **Social worker:** Address practical concerns about work, finances, home adaptations, and family support.\n\n**Ongoing support strategies:**\n- **Staged information:** Provide information at a pace Mr. Johnson can process, recognizing that emotional shock may limit initial comprehension.\n- **Goal setting:** Work with Mr. Johnson to establish realistic short and long-term rehabilitation goals.\n- **Functional focus:** Emphasize capabilities and adaptive strategies rather than limitations.\n- **Body image support:** Address concerns about changed appearance and self-perception.\n- **Return to meaningful activities:** Discuss modified approaches to his previous interests like coaching football.\n\n**Long-term considerations:**\n- **Transition points:** Recognize that psychological support needs may increase at key transitions (hospital discharge, prosthetic fitting, return to community activities).\n- **Family adaptation:** Support the entire family unit, including helping his teenage children understand and adjust.\n- **Vocational rehabilitation:** Explore return-to-work options and necessary accommodations for his construction career.\n\nThroughout this process, I would maintain regular communication with Mr. Johnson, checking in on his psychological wellbeing alongside his physical recovery, and adjusting support as needed.",
        "question": "How would you support this patient psychologically through this process?"
      },
      {
        "answer": "Rehabilitation following an above knee amputation is comprehensive and multidisciplinary, involving several key phases and elements:\n\n**Pre-operative preparation (if possible):**\n- Psychological preparation and education about the rehabilitation journey\n- Baseline functional assessment\n- Introduction to the rehabilitation team\n\n**Immediate post-operative phase (0-2 weeks):**\n- **Wound management:** Monitoring healing, dressing changes, infection prevention\n- **Pain control:** Managing both surgical pain and phantom limb sensations\n- **Positioning:** Preventing hip flexion contractures by avoiding prolonged sitting with the hip flexed\n- **Residual limb care:** Proper bandaging/shrinker application to shape the stump for prosthetic fitting\n- **Early mobilization:** Bed transfers, sitting balance, wheelchair skills\n- **Psychological support:** Addressing initial adjustment and grief response\n\n**Early rehabilitation phase (2-6 weeks):**\n- **Strengthening exercises:** Focus on core, upper body, and remaining lower limb\n- **Residual limb conditioning:** Desensitization, scar mobilization, edema control\n- **Balance training:** Progressing from supported to unsupported standing\n- **Functional activities:** Transfer techniques, single-limb mobility, activities of daily living\n- **Cardiovascular fitness:** Modified exercise programs to maintain conditioning\n\n**Pre-prosthetic training (typically 4-8 weeks post-op):**\n- **Residual limb maturation:** Continued use of compression garments\n- **Prosthetic assessment:** Evaluation for appropriate prosthetic prescription\n- **Weight-bearing preparation:** Gradual loading of the residual limb\n- **Psychological preparation:** Setting realistic expectations for prosthetic use\n\n**Prosthetic rehabilitation phase:**\n- **Prosthetic fitting and adjustment:** Working with prosthetist for optimal socket fit\n- **Gait training:** Progressive walking program from parallel bars to various terrains\n- **Advanced mobility skills:** Stairs, ramps, uneven surfaces, getting up from falls\n- **Activities of daily living:** Adapting to perform self-care, domestic, and community activities\n- **Vocational rehabilitation:** Work-specific skills and adaptations\n\n**Long-term rehabilitation (ongoing):**\n- **Prosthetic refinement:** Regular reviews and adjustments as the residual limb changes\n- **Advanced functional training:** Return to specific recreational activities\n- **Complication management:** Addressing skin issues, volume fluctuations, mechanical problems\n- **Psychological support:** Ongoing adjustment, body image, and social reintegration\n- **Peer support:** Connection with amputee support groups\n\n**Key multidisciplinary team members:**\n- Rehabilitation physician (leading the team)\n- Physiotherapist (mobility, strength, prosthetic training)\n- Occupational therapist (ADLs, equipment, home modifications)\n- Prosthetist (prosthesis design, fitting, adjustment)\n- Psychologist (adjustment, coping strategies)\n- Nurse specialist (wound care, residual limb management)\n- Social worker (community resources, financial support)\n- Dietitian (nutritional support for healing and weight management)\n\nThe rehabilitation process is highly individualized based on Mr. Johnson's age, pre-injury fitness level, comorbidities, social support, and personal goals. As a 42-year-old previously active individual, he has good potential for successful prosthetic use and return to modified activities, including potentially adapted versions of cycling and coaching.",
