    "consent_approach": "**Establish rapport:** Introduce yourself and ensure Mr. Johnson is comfortable. Acknowledge the difficult situation and check if he would like his wife to be present during the consent discussion.\n\n**Explain the condition:** \"The trauma from your accident has caused severe damage to the blood vessels, muscles, nerves, and bones in your right leg. Despite our initial attempts to save the limb, the tissues are not receiving adequate blood supply and are beginning to die. This puts you at risk of life-threatening infection and sepsis.\"\n\n**Explain the procedure:** \"An above knee amputation involves surgically removing the damaged leg above the knee joint. We will carefully shape the remaining thigh to allow for optimal prosthetic fitting later. The procedure will be performed under general anesthesia.\"\n\n**Discuss benefits:**\n- Prevention of life-threatening infection and sepsis\n- Removal of painful, non-viable tissue\n- Opportunity for rehabilitation and prosthetic fitting\n- Potential for return to independence and some activities\n\n**Discuss risks:**\n- Surgical risks: bleeding, infection, wound healing problems (5-10%)\n- Phantom limb pain (up to 80% of patients experience some degree)\n- Stump pain (20-30%)\n- Psychological impact including adjustment disorder, depression\n- Need for revision surgery (5-15%)\n- Prosthetic-related complications\n- General anesthesia risks\n\n**Alternative options:**\n- Further limb salvage attempts (explaining why this is not recommended due to poor prognosis)\n- Palliative approach (explaining risks of sepsis and mortality)\n\n**Answer questions:** Encourage Mr. Johnson to ask questions and address his concerns honestly.\n\n**Check understanding:** Ask Mr. Johnson to explain back his understanding of the procedure, risks, and benefits.\n\n**Obtain consent:** Provide consent form 1 for Mr. Johnson to sign if he agrees to proceed. Emphasize that he can take time to consider his decision and discuss with family.",
