    "breaking_bad_news_framework": "**Setting:** Find a quiet side room, sit at eye level, turn phone to silent.\n\n**Perception:** “Mr. Morris, I understand you’re upset. Could you share what you’ve been told about today’s surgery so I can see where the confusion lies?”\n\n**Invitation:** “Would you like me to explain why the team has recommended an arthroscopy rather than a full replacement and discuss what we can do next?”\n\n**Knowledge:** “Your recent X-rays show only moderate wear in the inner part of the knee. At this stage a knee replacement would likely be more invasive than necessary and may not relieve all of your pain. A key-hole arthroscopy lets us look inside, trim any torn cartilage and wash out debris, which can reduce symptoms and delay the need for replacement.”\n\n**Empathy:** “I appreciate this is very different from what you believed would happen. I’m sorry for the anxiety and inconvenience this misunderstanding has caused you.”\n\n**Summarize & Strategy:** “Today we have two options: proceed with the arthroscopy after you’ve had time to consider the information, or postpone the operation so you can see your consultant again. Whatever you decide, I’ll document our discussion and, if you feel standards were not met, I can guide you to our Patient Advice and Liaison Service (PALS) to make a complaint or seek further clarification.”",
