    "consent_approach": "1. **Establish rapport:** Greet Mr. Morris, introduce yourself, ensure he is seated comfortably and ask if he would like a relative present.\n\n2. **Confirm understanding & expectations:** Ask, “Can you tell me what operation you were expecting today?” – to gauge his current perception.\n\n3. **Explain the planned procedure:** In plain language describe diagnostic knee arthroscopy – key-hole camera examination, debridement if necessary – duration ~30 min, usually day-case.\n\n4. **Discuss benefits:**\n   - Direct visual assessment of joint structures\n   - Ability to treat minor meniscal or chondral lesions immediately\n   - Smaller incisions, faster recovery than replacement\n\n5. **Discuss alternatives:**\n   - Conservative management (physio, injections, weight optimisation)\n   - Arthroplasty (partial or total) – why MDT felt not yet indicated based on X-ray Kellgren–Lawrence grade 2 changes only\n\n6. **Outline material risks:**\n   - Infection (~1%)\n   - Bleeding/haemarthrosis\n   - Thrombosis (DVT/PE)\n   - Anaesthetic complications\n   - Ongoing pain / no improvement (15-20%)\n   - Need for future knee replacement\n\n7. **Check understanding & address questions:** “Could you summarise what you understand so far?”  Clarify misconceptions.\n\n8. **Confirm capacity (adult, presumed) & voluntary decision:** Offer time to consider; ensure no coercion.\n\n9. **Document & obtain written consent:** If he agrees, complete **Consent Form 1** (adult with capacity). Provide copy.\n\n10. **Provide post-operative information:** Rehab, driving, work, red-flag symptoms.",
