        "answer": "When consenting Thomas for his elbow arthroscopy, I would follow a structured approach:\n\n**Establish rapport:** I would introduce myself to both Thomas and his father, ensuring they feel comfortable and addressing any initial concerns they might have.\n\n**Explain the procedure:** Using age-appropriate language, I would describe the elbow arthroscopy process, explaining how small instruments will be inserted through tiny incisions to visualize and remove the loose bodies from his elbow joint.\n\n**Discuss benefits:**\n- Improved elbow function and range of motion\n- Reduction in pain and discomfort\n- Prevention of further joint damage\n- Quicker recovery compared to open surgery\n\n**Discuss risks:**\n- Pain and swelling (common)\n- Infection (1-2%)\n- Bleeding\n- Nerve injury (particularly ulnar nerve, 1-2%)\n- Stiffness\n- Portal site pain\n- Compartment syndrome (rare)\n- Anaesthetic risks\n\n**Answer questions:** I would encourage both Thomas and his father to ask any questions they might have about the procedure, risks, or recovery process, addressing each thoroughly.\n\n**Ensure understanding:** I would confirm that both Thomas and his father understand the information provided, using open-ended questions to assess their comprehension.\n\n**Obtain consent:** I would provide consent form 2 for minors, explaining that his father will need to sign as Thomas's legal guardian, while also involving Thomas in the decision-making process appropriate to his maturity level.",
