        "answer": "**Gillick competence** refers to the legal concept that allows minors under 16 years of age to consent to their own medical treatment without parental permission or knowledge, provided they have sufficient understanding and intelligence to comprehend fully what is proposed.\n\nKey aspects of Gillick competence include:\n\n1. The minor must demonstrate sufficient maturity and intelligence to understand the nature, purpose, and possible consequences of the proposed treatment.\n\n2. The assessment of Gillick competence is specific to the particular decision at hand and the complexity of the treatment being considered.\n\n3. The concept originated from the case of Gillick v West Norfolk and Wisbech Area Health Authority (1985), where the House of Lords ruled that doctors could provide contraceptive advice and treatment to girls under 16 without parental consent if the girl had sufficient maturity and understanding.\n\n4. Gillick competence operates alongside Fraser guidelines, which are more specific to contraceptive advice but are often referenced in similar contexts.\n\n5. Healthcare professionals must assess each case individually, considering the minor's age, maturity, understanding, and the complexity of the medical decision.\n\nIn Thomas's case, at 15 years old, he may potentially be Gillick competent for decisions regarding his elbow arthroscopy if he demonstrates sufficient understanding of the procedure, its risks, benefits, and alternatives. However, it's good practice to involve parents whenever possible, especially for surgical procedures, even when a minor is deemed Gillick competent.",
