      {
        "answer": "I would use the SPIKES protocol for delivering difficult news:\n\n**Setting:**\nI would ensure privacy by finding a quiet room where Thomas's father and I could sit down comfortably without interruptions. I would make sure we have adequate time for the discussion and that my pager is on silent mode.\n\n**Perception:**\nI would begin by asking, \"Mr. Wilson, how do you feel Thomas's surgery went today?\" This helps me understand his current level of awareness and expectations before proceeding.\n\n**Invitation:**\nI would say, \"I need to discuss something important about Thomas's surgery with you. Is this a good time?\" This ensures he is ready to receive the information.\n\n**Knowledge:**\nI would clearly explain what happened: \"During Thomas's elbow arthroscopy, there was an unexpected complication. The ulnar nerve, which runs close to the elbow joint, was injured during the procedure. This nerve controls sensation to the little finger and part of the ring finger, as well as some hand muscles. We identified this during the surgery, and while the nerve wasn't completely cut, it has been injured and may cause the numbness and tingling Thomas is experiencing.\"\n\n**Empathy:**\nI would acknowledge the father's feelings: \"I understand this is concerning news and not what either of you expected. I'm truly sorry that this has happened during Thomas's surgery.\"\n\n**Summarize:**\nI would outline the next steps: \"Most ulnar nerve injuries from arthroscopy are temporary and resolve within weeks to months. We'll need to monitor Thomas closely. I'll arrange for nerve conduction studies to assess the extent of the injury and refer Thomas to our hand therapy team. We'll schedule regular follow-ups to track his progress. Do you have any questions about what I've explained or about the plan moving forward?\"\n\nThroughout this conversation, I would maintain eye contact, use a calm and empathetic tone, and give Mr. Wilson time to process the information and