        "answer": "To prevent ulnar nerve injury during elbow arthroscopy in the future, I would implement several strategies:\n\n**Reflect on the case:**\n- I would thoroughly analyze what factors contributed to the nerve injury in Thomas's case\n- Consider whether there were any warning signs or technical challenges that I could better anticipate in future cases\n- Complete a Personal Benefit Assessment (PBA) or detailed journal entry to document my reflection and learning points\n\n**Improve technical approach:**\n- Review and refine my portal placement technique, ensuring safe distances from neurovascular structures\n- Consider using more conservative approaches when working near the medial aspect of the elbow where the ulnar nerve is vulnerable\n- Practice meticulous soft tissue handling and maintain constant awareness of the ulnar nerve's location\n\n**Enhance preoperative planning:**\n- Carefully review preoperative imaging to identify any anatomical variations\n- Consider additional imaging if needed to better understand the patient's specific anatomy\n- Plan portal placements with particular attention to neurovascular structures\n\n**Improve patient selection:**\n- Be more cautious with patients who have had previous elbow surgery or trauma that might alter normal anatomy\n- Consider alternative approaches for patients with conditions that increase surgical complexity\n\n**Seek additional training:**\n- Attend specialized courses on elbow arthroscopy techniques\n- Observe experienced surgeons performing similar procedures\n- Practice in skills labs or with cadaveric specimens if available\n\n**Modify intraoperative approach:**\n- Consider routine ulnar nerve identification before beginning the arthroscopic procedure\n- Use appropriate arm positioning to minimize tension on the nerve\n- Employ careful retractor placement and management\n- Consider using nerve monitoring in high-risk cases\n\n**Team approach:**\n- Discuss the case at departmental morbidity and mortality meetings\n- Share learning points with colleagues to improve collective knowledge\n- Implement any departmental protocol changes that might reduce risk\n\n**Update consent process:**\n- Ensure ulnar nerve injury is explicitly discussed during the consent process\n- Use this case as an example to improve how I communicate risks to patients\n\nBy implementing these strategies, I would work to minimize the risk of similar complications in future procedures while using this experience as a valuable learning opportunity to improve my surgical practice.",
