        "answer": "**Duty of candour** refers to the professional and statutory obligation for healthcare professionals, including surgeons, to be open, honest, and transparent with patients when something goes wrong with their treatment or care that causes, or has the potential to cause, harm or distress.\n\nThe duty of candour has both statutory and professional components:\n\n**Statutory duty of candour:**\n- Established under the Health and Social Care Act 2008 (Regulated Activities) Regulations 2014\n- Applies to healthcare organizations rather than individuals\n- Requires organizations to inform patients about incidents where harm has occurred\n- Mandates a formal process of notification, apology, and explanation\n\n**Professional duty of candour:**\n- Outlined by the General Medical Council (GMC) in Good Medical Practice\n- Applies to individual healthcare professionals\n- Requires doctors to be open and honest with patients when things go wrong\n\n**Key elements of the duty of candour include:**\n\n1. **Disclosure:** Promptly informing the patient (or their representative) when something has gone wrong that causes or could lead to significant harm\n\n2. **Apology:** Providing a genuine apology for what has happened\n\n3. **Explanation:** Giving a full explanation of what happened and why\n\n4. **Support:** Offering appropriate support to the patient\n\n5. **Documentation:** Recording the incident and the discussion with the patient\n\n6. **Learning:** Using the incident as an opportunity for reflection and improvement\n\n**In Thomas's case, my duty of candour would require:**\n- Promptly informing Thomas and his father about the ulnar nerve injury\n- Apologizing for the complication\n- Explaining how it happened, the potential consequences, and the management plan\n- Providing ongoing support throughout his recovery\n- Documenting our discussions and the incident thoroughly\n- Using this experience to improve my practice and prevent similar incidents\n\nThe duty of candour is fundamental to maintaining trust in the doctor-patient relationship and is an essential aspect of ethical surgical practice. It acknowledges that healthcare professionals have a responsibility to be honest about mistakes or complications, which ultimately improves patient safety and the quality of care.",
