        "answer": "My professional duty in a case of suspected non-accidental injury is comprehensive and guided by GMC principles:\n\n**Primary duty to the child:**\n- The child's safety and welfare must be my paramount concern\n- I must act in the best interests of the child at all times\n\n**Duty to report:**\n- I have a professional obligation to report any reasonable suspicion of NAI\n- This duty overrides patient confidentiality when a child's safety is at risk\n- I must follow local safeguarding protocols and national guidelines\n\n**Immediate actions required:**\n1. Document all findings meticulously with diagrams, photographs (with appropriate consent), and detailed descriptions\n2. Inform the consultant on call and the hospital's named safeguarding lead\n3. Make a referral to social services\n4. Ensure the child is admitted to hospital for safety and further assessment\n5. Arrange appropriate investigations (skeletal survey, ophthalmology review, etc.)\n\n**Multi-disciplinary approach:**\n- Engage with the safeguarding team, social services, and police as appropriate\n- Participate in strategy meetings and case conferences as required\n- Provide objective medical evidence and professional opinions\n\n**Documentation requirements:**\n- Record all findings, discussions, and decisions contemporaneously\n- Ensure documentation is factual, objective, and comprehensive\n- Clearly distinguish between observed facts and professional opinions\n\n**Ongoing responsibilities:**\n- Contribute to the child protection plan\n- Provide medical follow-up as needed\n- Be prepared to provide evidence for legal proceedings if required\n\nThe GMC's Good Medical Practice and specific guidance on protecting children emphasize that doctors must act if they believe a child may be at risk of abuse or neglect, even when this creates challenges in the doctor-patient relationship.",
