        "answer": "In this case of suspected non-accidental injury, I would take the following immediate actions:\n\n**Clinical care of the child:**\n1. Ensure Oliver receives appropriate medical treatment for his femur fracture and any other injuries\n2. Admit him to the pediatric ward (not the orthopaedic ward) for safety and further assessment\n3. Request a full skeletal survey to identify any other occult fractures\n4. Arrange ophthalmology review to check for retinal hemorrhages\n5. Consider blood tests including clotting screen to rule out medical causes of bruising\n\n**Safeguarding procedures:**\n1. Inform my consultant immediately\n2. Contact the hospital's named safeguarding lead or on-call pediatrician with safeguarding responsibility\n3. Make an urgent referral to social services\n4. Document the need for a strategy meeting\n5. Consider whether police involvement is required immediately\n\n**Documentation:**\n1. Document all injuries meticulously with measurements, diagrams, and descriptions\n2. Arrange for medical photography (with appropriate consent)\n3. Record the history given by the mother verbatim, noting any inconsistencies or changes\n4. Document my concerns and the rationale for suspecting NAI\n5. Record all communications with other professionals\n\n**Communication:**\n1. Speak with the mother using the SPIKES framework to explain concerns and next steps\n2. Liaise with the pediatric team who will take over primary care\n3. Communicate with social services, providing clear medical information\n4. Participate in the initial strategy discussion\n\n**Hospital management:**\n1. Ensure Oliver is not discharged until a safety plan is in place\n2. Consider whether contact with the mother should be supervised\n3. Determine if there are other children in the household who may be at risk\n\nAll these actions would be taken promptly while maintaining a professional, non-judgmental approach with the family.",
        "question": "What immediate actions would you take in this case?"
