      {
        "answer": "The multi-disciplinary team (MDT) approach is essential in managing suspected non-accidental injury cases, ensuring comprehensive assessment and protection of the child:\n\n**Core MDT members and their roles:**\n\n**Orthopaedic team (my role):**\n- Identify suspicious fracture patterns and other injuries\n- Provide expert opinion on mechanism of injury and consistency with history\n- Manage orthopaedic injuries and treatment\n- Contribute medical evidence to the safeguarding process\n\n**Paediatricians:**\n- Take over as the lead medical specialty for the child\n- Coordinate the medical assessment and investigations\n- Provide holistic assessment of the child's health and development\n- Often include specialists in child protection with expertise in NAI\n\n**Safeguarding team:**\n- Hospital safeguarding lead provides expertise and guidance\n- Coordinate the hospital's response to safeguarding concerns\n- Liaise between healthcare professionals and external agencies\n- Ensure protocols are followed correctly\n\n**Social services:**\n- Lead the child protection investigation\n- Assess the child's home environment and family circumstances\n- Coordinate strategy meetings and case conferences\n- Develop and implement child protection plans\n- Make decisions about the need for legal proceedings\n\n**Police:**\n- Investigate potential criminal aspects\n- Gather forensic evidence\n- Interview family members and potential witnesses\n- Determine if criminal charges are warranted\n\n**Additional MDT members:**\n\n- **Radiologists:** Provide expert interpretation of imaging studies\n- **Nursing staff:** Observe parent-child interactions and provide care\n- **Psychologists/psychiatrists:** Assess psychological impact and family dynamics\n- **Health visitors:** Provide background information on family and home situation\n- **Legal representatives:** Advise on legal aspects of child protection\n\n**MDT processes:**\n\n1. **Strategy meetings:** Initial multi-agency discussion to share information and plan investigation\n2. **Case conferences:** Formal meeting to determine if child protection plan is needed\n3. **Regular MDT reviews:** Ongoing assessment of the child's situation\n4. **Information sharing:** Continuous communication between agencies\n\n**Benefits of the MDT approach:**\n\n- Comprehensive assessment from multiple perspectives\n- Shared decision-making based on collective expertise\n- Prevents important information being missed\n- Ensures appropriate balance between child protection and family support\n- Provides checks and balances in the decision-making process\n\nAs the orthopaedic registrar, I would actively participate in this MDT process, providing my medical expertise while recognizing the valuable contributions of other team members in ensuring Oliver's safety and wellbeing.",
