      {
        "answer": "In this case, I would look for several key indicators of non-accidental injury (NAI) that raise suspicion and warrant further investigation:\n\n**Injury characteristics inconsistent with history:**\n- Spiral femur fracture in a 3-year-old is concerning when the history is a simple fall\n- Spiral fractures often result from twisting forces rather than direct impact\n- The mechanism described (fall while playing) may not generate sufficient force to cause a femoral fracture in a healthy child\n\n**Multiple injuries at different stages of healing:**\n- Multiple bruises of different ages on back and buttocks suggest repeated trauma\n- Healing clavicle fracture that the mother was unaware of indicates previous significant injury without medical attention\n- The combination suggests a pattern of injuries rather than a single incident\n\n**Concerning injury locations:**\n- Bruises on the back and buttocks are less common in accidental injuries\n- These areas are typically protected during normal falls but are common sites for inflicted injuries\n- Bruises in these locations in non-mobile children are particularly concerning\n\n**Delay in seeking medical attention:**\n- Any significant delay between injury and presentation (would need to establish this)\n- Previous injuries (clavicle fracture) that weren't medically assessed\n\n**Behavioral indicators:**\n- Mother's anxious and defensive behavior when questioned\n- Any inconsistencies or changes in the history provided\n- Inappropriate response to the child's distress\n\n**Additional features to assess:**\n1. **Child's developmental stage:** Is Oliver's developmental level consistent with the ability to generate the described injury?\n2. **Previous presentations:** Check for previous ED attendances or GP visits for injuries\n3. **Social factors:** Any known safeguarding concerns, domestic violence, substance misuse\n4. **Interaction:** Observe the interaction between Oliver and his mother\n\n**Further investigations to consider:**\n- Full skeletal survey to identify occult fractures\n- Ophthalmology assessment for retinal hemorrhages\n- Coagulation studies to rule out bleeding disorders\n\nIt's important to note that while these indicators raise suspicion, they don't definitively prove NAI. A thorough, objective assessment through the multi-disciplinary safeguarding process is essential to determine the cause of Oliver's injuries and ensure his safety.",
