        "answer": "The legal framework for child protection in cases of suspected non-accidental injury in the UK is comprehensive and places the child's welfare as paramount:\n\n**Primary legislation:**\n\n**Children Act 1989:**\n- Provides the foundation for child protection in England and Wales\n- Section 47 places a duty on local authorities to investigate when a child is suspected of suffering significant harm\n- Establishes the principle that the child's welfare is paramount\n- Defines significant harm as ill-treatment or impairment of health or development\n- Provides for Child Protection Orders and Emergency Protection Orders\n\n**Children Act 2004:**\n- Strengthened the original Act following the Victoria Climbié inquiry\n- Established Local Safeguarding Children Boards (now Partnerships)\n- Placed duty on agencies to cooperate to safeguard children\n- Created the role of Children's Commissioner\n\n**Children and Social Work Act 2017:**\n- Updated safeguarding arrangements\n- Established new local safeguarding partnerships\n- Strengthened focus on learning from serious case reviews\n\n**Statutory guidance:**\n\n**Working Together to Safeguard Children:**\n- Comprehensive guidance on inter-agency working\n- Outlines roles and responsibilities of different professionals\n- Details procedures for assessment and intervention\n- Provides framework for strategy discussions and child protection conferences\n\n**Professional obligations:**\n\n**GMC guidance:**\n- Doctors have a professional duty to act if they believe a child may be at risk\n- This duty may override the normal requirement for patient confidentiality\n- Doctors must be able to recognize signs of abuse and neglect\n- Doctors must know how to respond appropriately to safeguarding concerns\n\n**Legal processes in NAI cases:**\n\n1. **Initial protection:**\n   - Emergency Protection Order (up to 8 days)\n   - Police Protection Powers (up to 72 hours)\n   - Child Assessment Order\n\n2. **Care proceedings:**\n   - Local authority may initiate care proceedings in Family Court\n   - Interim Care Orders while full assessment takes place\n   - Potential outcomes include Care Orders, Supervision Orders, or return to family\n\n3. **Criminal proceedings:**\n   - Police investigation may run parallel to care proceedings\n   - Criminal prosecution may occur if evidence supports charges\n   - Different standard of proof (beyond reasonable doubt vs. balance of probabilities)\n\n**My responsibilities within this framework:**\n\n- Recognize and report suspected NAI promptly\n- Provide detailed, objective medical evidence\n- Participate in strategy discussions and case conferences\n- Potentially provide expert testimony in court proceedings\n- Maintain comprehensive documentation\n- Continue to act in the best interests of the child throughout\n\nThis legal framework ensures a structured, multi-agency approach to protecting children while maintaining appropriate checks and balances in the system.",
