    "management_overview": "**Immediate Management**\n- PRICE protocol:\n  - Protection\n  - Rest\n  - Ice (20 minutes every 2 hours)\n  - Compression (elastic bandage)\n  - Elevation\n\n- Analgesia:\n  - Regular paracetamol\n  - NSAIDs if not contraindicated\n  - Consider stronger analgesia if required\n\n- Reduction:\n  - Usually spontaneous\n  - If still dislocated: gentle extension with medial pressure on patella\n\n**Initial Treatment**\n1. Aspiration of significant hemarthrosis if causing pain\n2. Hinged knee brace (0-30° initially) or cricket pad splint\n3. Weight-bearing as tolerated with crutches\n4. Early physiotherapy referral\n\n**Rehabilitation Protocol**\n- Phase 1 (0-2 weeks):\n  - Quadriceps activation exercises\n  - Straight leg raises\n  - Gentle range of motion exercises\n  - Patella mobilization\n\n- Phase 2 (2-6 weeks):\n  - Progressive weight-bearing\n  - Closed chain exercises\n  - Proprioception training\n  - VMO strengthening\n\n- Phase 3 (6-12 weeks):\n  - Sport-specific rehabilitation\n  - Functional strengthening\n  - Plyometric training\n  - Return to sport assessment\n\n**Surgical Considerations**\n- Indications for acute surgical intervention:\n  - Osteochondral fracture\n  - Large chondral fragments\n  - Persistent instability despite reduction\n\n- Indications for delayed surgical intervention:\n  - Recurrent dislocations\n  - Significant anatomical risk factors\n  - Failed conservative management\n\n**Surgical Options**\n- MPFL repair or reconstruction\n- Tibial tuberosity osteotomy\n- Trochleoplasty (for severe trochlear dysplasia)\n\n\n**Follow-up**\n- Orthopaedic clinic review at 2 weeks\n- Regular physiotherapy assessment\n- Repeat imaging if symptoms persist\n- Consider sports medicine referral for elite athletes",
