        "answer": "**Anatomical Risk Factors for Patellar Instability**\n\n1. **Trochlear Dysplasia**\n   - Abnormal trochlear morphology with flattened or convex trochlear\n   - Classified using Dejour classification (Types A-D)\n   - Assessment: Lateral radiograph (crossing sign, supratrochlear spur), MRI, CT\n   - Measurement: Trochlear depth, trochlear angle\n\n2. **Patella Alta**\n   - High-riding patella that engages with trochlear later in flexion\n   - Assessment: Lateral radiograph, MRI\n   - Measurement: Insall-Salvati ratio (>1.2 indicates patella alta), Caton-Deschamps index\n\n3. **Increased Tibial Tuberosity-Trochlear Groove (TT-TG) Distance**\n   - Lateral position of tibial tuberosity relative to trochlear groove\n   - Assessment: CT or MRI\n   - Measurement: TT-TG distance >15mm considered abnormal, >20mm often requires surgical correction\n\n4. **Excessive Lateral Patellar Tilt**\n   - Abnormal patellar tilt in relation to femoral trochlea\n   - Assessment: Skyline/Merchant radiographs, CT, MRI\n   - Measurement: Lateral patellofemoral angle, patellar tilt angle\n\n5. **Genu Valgum (Knock-knees)**\n   - Increases Q-angle and lateral vector on patella\n   - Assessment: Clinical examination, long-leg alignment radiographs\n   - Measurement: Mechanical axis, Q-angle (>20° in females, >15° in males is abnormal)\n\n6. **Femoral Anteversion**\n   - Increased femoral neck anteversion\n   - Assessment: Clinical (Craig's test), CT rotational profile\n   - Measurement: Femoral anteversion angle\n\n7. **External Tibial Torsion**\n   - Increased external rotation of tibia\n   - Assessment: Clinical, CT rotational profile\n   - Measurement: Tibial torsion angle\n\n8. **Generalized Ligamentous Laxity**\n   - Beighton score ≥4 indicates hypermobility\n   - Assessment: Clinical examination of multiple joints\n\n9. **Vastus Medialis Obliquus (VMO) Dysplasia or Atrophy**\n   - Inadequate dynamic medial stabilization\n   - Assessment: Clinical examination, MRI\n\n10. **Previous MPFL Injury**\n    - Compromised primary soft tissue restraint\n    - Assessment: MRI, clinical examination (medial tenderness)\n\n**Clinical Assessment**\n- Q-angle measurement\n- Patellar tracking during active knee flexion/extension (J-sign)\n- Patellar apprehension test\n- Lateral patellar glide test (>75% translation indicates instability)\n- Patellar tilt test\n- Beighton score for generalized laxity\n\n**Radiological Assessment**\n- Standard radiographs (AP, lateral, skyline views at 30° and 45°)\n- Long-leg alignment films\n- MRI for soft tissue and cartilage assessment\n- CT for precise bony measurements and rotational assessment\n\nIn this 33-year-old tennis player, I would perform a comprehensive assessment of all these factors to determine her risk of recurrent instability and guide management decisions, particularly given her high functional demands.",
