        "answer": "**Surgical Options for Recurrent Patellar Instability**\n\n1. **Medial Patellofemoral Ligament (MPFL) Reconstruction**\n   - Gold standard for recurrent instability without significant anatomical abnormalities\n   - Techniques: Autograft (gracilis/semitendinosus), allograft, or synthetic graft\n   - Fixation options: Suture anchors to patella, interference screw to femur\n   - Considerations: Anatomic femoral attachment critical to prevent over-constraint\n\n2. **Tibial Tuberosity Osteotomy (TTO)**\n   - Medialization: For increased TT-TG distance (>15-20mm)\n   - Distalization: For patella alta (Insall-Salvati >1.2)\n   - Anteriorization: For patellofemoral arthritis with anterior knee pain\n   - Combined procedures based on specific pathology\n\n3. **Trochleoplasty**\n   - For severe trochlear dysplasia (Dejour types B, C, D)\n   - Techniques: Deepening trochleoplasty, lateral facet elevation\n   - Significant procedure with higher complication rates\n   - Reserved for cases with severe dysplasia and failed previous surgeries\n\n4. **Lateral Retinacular Procedures**\n   - Lateral release: Rarely performed in isolation\n   - May be combined with other procedures if lateral tightness present\n   - Risk of iatrogenic medial instability if performed alone\n\n5. **Medial Soft Tissue Procedures**\n   - Medial reefing/imbrication\n   - VMO advancement\n   - Generally insufficient alone for recurrent instability\n\n6. **Combined Procedures**\n   - Often necessary to address multiple pathologies\n   - Common combinations: MPFL reconstruction + TTO, MPFL + trochleoplasty\n\n**Decision-Making Factors**\n\n1. **Anatomical Factors**\n   - Trochlear dysplasia type and severity\n   - TT-TG distance measurement\n   - Patella alta (Insall-Salvati ratio)\n   - Patellar tilt and subluxation\n   - Limb alignment (valgus)\n   - Rotational abnormalities\n\n2. **Patient Factors**\n   - Age (33 years - mature skeleton)\n   - Activity level (semi-professional athlete with high demands)\n   - Sport-specific requirements (tennis involves cutting, pivoting)\n   - Number of previous dislocations\n   - Presence of patellofemoral arthritis\n   - Expectations and goals\n\n3. **Surgical Considerations**\n   - Recovery time and rehabilitation protocol\n   - Return to sport timeline\n   - Complication rates of different procedures\n   - Surgeon experience with specific techniques\n\n**For this 33-year-old tennis player:**\n\nI would recommend an individualized approach based on her specific pathoanatomy:\n\n1. If normal anatomy with isolated MPFL tear:\n   - MPFL reconstruction alone\n\n2. If increased TT-TG distance (>15mm) or patella alta:\n   - MPFL reconstruction + appropriate TTO\n\n3. If severe trochlear dysplasia:\n   - Consider MPFL reconstruction + trochleoplasty\n\n4. If multiple factors:\n   - Combined procedures addressing each component\n\nGiven her athletic career, I would discuss:\n- Realistic recovery timeline (6-9 months for return to competitive tennis)\n- Rehabilitation milestones and expectations\n- Success rates and potential complications\n- Alternative treatments if she declines surgery\n\nThe final decision would be made collaboratively, considering her competitive timeline, career goals, and specific anatomical factors identified on imaging.",
