      {
        "answer": "**MPFL Anatomy**\n\nThe medial patellofemoral ligament (MPFL) is the primary soft tissue restraint against lateral patellar displacement, contributing approximately 50-60% of the total restraining force.\n\n**Origin (Femoral Attachment)**\n- Attaches to the medial femoral condyle\n- Located between the medial epicondyle and adductor tubercle\n- Slightly anterior and distal to the adductor tubercle\n- Approximately 10mm distal to the femoral physis\n- Isometric point that maintains consistent tension throughout knee flexion\n\n**Insertion (Patellar Attachment)**\n- Broad insertion along the superomedial border of the patella\n- Covers approximately 30-40% of the proximal medial patellar border\n- Merges with the vastus medialis obliquus (VMO) aponeurosis\n- Blends with layer 2 of the medial retinaculum\n\n**Structural Characteristics**\n- Length: 40-60mm\n- Width: 10-30mm (fan-shaped structure)\n- Thickness: 0.5-1.2mm\n- Tensile strength: 208N (average)\n- Composed of dense, regular connective tissue\n\n**Histology**\n- Type I collagen predominance\n- Parallel fiber orientation\n- Relatively hypovascular compared to other ligaments\n- Contains mechanoreceptors suggesting proprioceptive function\n\n**Relationships**\n- Superficial to the joint capsule\n- Deep to the vastus medialis obliquus (VMO)\n- Crosses the medial patellofemoral joint line\n- Intimately related to the second layer of the medial retinaculum\n\n**Biomechanical Role in Patellar Stability**\n\n1. **Primary Static Restraint**\n   - Provides 50-60% of restraining force against lateral displacement\n   - Most important in early flexion (0-30°) before trochlear engagement\n   - Functions as a checkrein rather than a primary stabilizer in deeper flexion\n\n2. **Tension Profile**\n   - Near-isometric behavior during knee flexion\n   - Maximum tension at 15-30° of knee flexion\n   - Decreases in tension as the patella engages the trochlea in deeper flexion\n\n3. **Force Transmission**\n   - Transmits medially directed force to counteract lateral vectors\n   - Works with VMO to provide dynamic medial stability\n   - Guides patella into trochlear groove during early flexion\n\n4. **Injury Implications**\n   - MPFL rupture occurs in approximately 90-100% of acute patellar dislocations\n   - Most common site of injury is at or near the femoral attachment (70%)\n   - Patellar-based injuries account for approximately 20%\n   - Mid-substance tears are least common (10%)\n   - Healing potential is limited due to poor vascularity and continuous stress\n\n5. **Functional Synergy**\n   - Works with other stabilizers:\n     - Dynamic: VMO, quadriceps mechanism\n     - Static: Medial patellotibial ligament, medial patellomeniscal ligament\n     - Osseous: Trochlear geometry, patella morphology\n\nUnderstanding this detailed anatomy is crucial for:\n- Accurate diagnosis of injury patterns\n- Proper surgical reconstruction technique\n- Anatomic graft placement during reconstruction\n- Avoiding over-constraint or non-isometric reconstruction\n\nIn this 33-year-old tennis player, accurate assessment of the MPFL injury pattern (location, extent) would be essential for planning appropriate treatment, especially if surgical reconstruction becomes necessary.",
