      {
        "answer": "**Standard Rehabilitation Protocol Following Patellar Dislocation**\n\n**Phase 1: Acute Phase (0-2 weeks)**\n- Goals:\n  - Control pain and swelling\n  - Protect healing tissues\n  - Maintain quadriceps activation\n  - Prevent atrophy\n  - Restore basic range of motion\n\n- Interventions:\n  - PRICE protocol (Protection, Rest, Ice, Compression, Elevation)\n  - Hinged knee brace (0-30° initially)\n  - Partial weight-bearing with crutches as tolerated\n  - Quadriceps setting/isometric exercises\n  - Straight leg raises (with brace locked)\n  - Gentle passive range of motion exercises (0-90°)\n  - Patella mobilization (avoid lateral glide)\n  - Ankle pumps and hip strengthening\n\n**Phase 2: Intermediate Phase (2-6 weeks)**\n- Goals:\n  - Progress to full weight-bearing\n  - Restore full range of motion\n  - Improve quadriceps strength\n  - Begin proprioceptive training\n  - Normalize gait pattern\n\n- Interventions:\n  - Progressive weight-bearing as tolerated\n  - Wean from brace (typically by 4-6 weeks)\n  - Closed chain exercises (mini squats, step-ups)\n  - VMO strengthening (biased exercises)\n  - Stationary cycling with minimal resistance\n  - Balance/proprioception exercises\n  - Gait training\n  - Swimming/water therapy (if wounds healed)\n\n**Phase 3: Advanced Strengthening (6-12 weeks)**\n- Goals:\n  - Restore normal strength and endurance\n  - Improve neuromuscular control\n  - Prepare for return to functional activities\n  - Address any biomechanical issues\n\n- Interventions:\n  - Progressive resistance training\n  - Single-leg closed chain exercises\n  - Multi-directional lunges\n  - Eccentric quadriceps training\n  - Advanced proprioception exercises\n  - Elliptical trainer/cross-trainer\n  - Jogging in straight lines (if appropriate)\n\n**Phase 4: Return to Sport (12+ weeks)**\n- Goals:\n  - Sport-specific conditioning\n  - Restore confidence\n  - Safe return to pre-injury activities\n  - Prevent recurrence\n\n- Interventions:\n  - Plyometric training\n  - Agility drills\n  - Sport-specific movement patterns\n  - Cutting and pivoting exercises\n  - Return to sport testing\n\n**Modifications for High-Level Tennis Player**\n\n1. **Accelerated Timeline with Caution**\n   - More frequent therapy sessions (potentially 4-5x/week initially)\n   - Earlier introduction of tennis-specific movements\n   - Careful monitoring for setbacks or signs of recurrence\n\n2. **Sport-Specific Considerations**\n   - Tennis movement analysis and biomechanical assessment\n   - Focus on lateral movement patterns essential for tennis\n   - Serve mechanics assessment (knee loading patterns)\n   - Court surface-specific training progression (clay vs hard court)\n\n3. **Advanced Neuromuscular Training**\n   - Earlier introduction of proprioceptive training\n   - Tennis-specific balance exercises (simulating reaching for shots)\n   - Deceleration training for court coverage\n   - Reactive agility drills\n\n4. **Psychological Components**\n   - Confidence building exercises\n   - Visualization techniques\n   - Graduated exposure to fear-inducing movements\n   - Competitive simulations\n\n5. **Return to Tennis Progression**\n   - Structured on-court rehabilitation:\n     - Stationary hitting → controlled movement → full court → match play\n   - Gradual increase in training volume and intensity\n   - Monitored practice matches before competitive return\n   - Potential use of protective bracing initially\n\n6. **Ongoing Maintenance Program**\n   - Long-term prehabilitation program\n   - Regular assessment of quadriceps/VMO strength\n   - Continued proprioceptive training\n   - Monitoring for signs of instability\n\n7. **Multidisciplinary Approach**\n   - Sports medicine physician oversight\n   - Physiotherapist with tennis experience\n   - Strength and conditioning coach\n   - Tennis coach involvement in later stages\n   - Sports psychologist if needed\n\n8. **Objective Testing for Return to Play**\n   - Isokinetic strength testing (quadriceps:hamstring ratio)\n   - Single-leg hop tests (distance and quality)\n   - Y-balance test\n   - Agility T-test\n   - Tennis-specific movement assessment\n\n9. **Timeline Adjustments**\n   - Phase 1: Similar timeline (0-2 weeks)\n   - Phase 2: Potentially accelerated (2-4 weeks)\n   - Phase 3: Tennis-specific focus (4-8 weeks)\n   - Phase 4: Graduated return to competition (8-12 weeks)\n   - Full competitive return: 12-16 weeks (vs 16-24 weeks standard)\n\nThe key is balancing the athlete's desire for rapid return with the biological healing timeframes and risk of recurrence. I would emphasize that rushing the process could lead to recurrent instability and potentially career-threatening complications.",
