        "answer": "**Common Complications Following Patellar Dislocation**\n\n1. **Recurrent Instability/Redislocation**\n   - Incidence: 15-50% after first-time dislocation\n   - Risk factors:\n     - Age <25 years at first dislocation\n     - Female gender\n     - Anatomical abnormalities (trochlear dysplasia, patella alta, increased TT-TG)\n     - Generalized ligamentous laxity\n     - Inadequate rehabilitation\n\n   - Management:\n     - Comprehensive assessment of anatomical risk factors\n     - MRI to evaluate MPFL and chondral status\n     - Targeted physiotherapy focusing on VMO strengthening\n     - Consider surgical intervention:\n       - MPFL reconstruction\n       - Tibial tuberosity osteotomy if anatomical abnormalities present\n       - Trochleoplasty for severe trochlear dysplasia\n\n2. **Osteochondral Injuries**\n   - Incidence: 25-70% (higher with first-time dislocations)\n   - Common locations:\n     - Medial patellar facet (shear injury)\n     - Lateral femoral condyle (impact injury)\n\n   - Management:\n     - MRI assessment of size and location\n     - Small, stable lesions (<1cm): Conservative management\n     - Large or unstable fragments: Surgical intervention\n       - Fixation if viable fragment\n       - Debridement for small, non-fixable fragments\n       - Cartilage restoration procedures (microfracture, OATS, ACI) for larger defects\n\n3. **Patellofemoral Osteoarthritis**\n   - Incidence: Up to 50% at long-term follow-up\n   - Risk factors:\n     - Chondral injury at time of dislocation\n     - Recurrent dislocations\n     - Delayed or inadequate treatment\n\n   - Management:\n     - Activity modification\n     - Weight management\n     - Physiotherapy focusing on quadriceps strengthening\n     - Unloader bracing\n     - Intra-articular injections (corticosteroid, hyaluronic acid)\n     - Surgical options for advanced cases:\n       - Arthroscopic debridement\n       - Patellofemoral arthroplasty\n       - Total knee arthroplasty\n\n4. **Chronic Anterior Knee Pain**\n   - Incidence: 40-60% following patellar dislocation\n   - Causes:\n     - Chondral injury\n     - Patellofemoral maltracking\n     - Quadriceps weakness\n     - Altered biomechanics\n\n   - Management:\n     - Targeted physiotherapy\n     - VMO strengthening\n     - Patellar taping/bracing\n     - Activity modification\n     - Analgesics and NSAIDs\n     - Consider surgical intervention if structural abnormality identified\n\n5. **Quadriceps Weakness/Atrophy**\n   - Incidence: Common following immobilization\n   - Causes:\n     - Arthrogenic muscle inhibition\n     - Disuse atrophy\n     - Pain inhibition\n\n   - Management:\n     - Early rehabilitation\n     - Neuromuscular electrical stimulation\n     - Biofeedback training\n     - Progressive resistance training\n     - Blood flow restriction training for early strengthening\n\n6. **Complications Following Surgical Treatment**\n   - MPFL reconstruction complications:\n     - Over-constraint (10-15%)\n     - Patellar fracture (1-2%)\n     - Hardware complications\n     - Graft failure (5-10%)\n\n   - Tibial tuberosity osteotomy complications:\n     - Non-union (rare)\n     - Hardware irritation (5-10%)\n     - Tibial fracture (rare)\n     - Over/under correction\n\n   - Management:\n     - Revision surgery if indicated\n     - Hardware removal if symptomatic\n     - Targeted rehabilitation\n\n7. **Apprehension and Psychological Impact**\n   - Fear of redislocation\n   - Activity avoidance\n   - Reduced sports participation\n\n   - Management:\n     - Graduated exposure to fear-inducing activities\n     - Psychological support\n     - Confidence-building exercises\n     - Protective bracing during return to sport\n\n**Specific Considerations for the 33-year-old Tennis Player**\n\nFor this high-level athlete, I would be particularly vigilant about:\n\n1. **Osteochondral Injuries**\n   - Expedited MRI assessment\n   - Low threshold for surgical intervention to preserve articular surface\n   - Consider advanced cartilage procedures if significant defects present\n\n2. **Recurrent Instability Risk**\n   - Comprehensive assessment of all anatomical risk factors\n   - Early surgical intervention if high risk identified\n   - Custom bracing for return to sport\n\n3. **Return to Performance**\n   - Address quadriceps weakness aggressively\n   - Sport-specific rehabilitation\n   - Biomechanical analysis of tennis movements\n   - Gradual return to competition protocol\n\n4. **Long-term Career Implications**\n   - Discuss potential for accelerated patellofemoral arthritis\n   - Career longevity considerations\n   - Preventative strategies for future seasons\n\nThe management approach would be multidisciplinary, involving sports medicine, physiotherapy, and potentially sports psychology to address both the physical and psychological aspects of recovery in this elite athlete.",
