        "answer": "**Evidence Base for Management of Patellar Dislocations**\n\n**1. First-Time (Primary) Dislocations**\n\n**Conservative vs. Surgical Management**\n- **Cochrane Review (Smith et al, 2015)**:\n  - No significant difference in redislocation rates between surgical and non-surgical treatment\n  - Insufficient evidence to recommend routine surgical repair after first-time dislocation\n  - Quality of included studies was low to moderate\n\n- **PAPI Study (Sillanpää et al, 2009)**:\n  - RCT comparing operative vs. non-operative treatment\n  - Surgical group showed lower redislocation rates (0% vs. 20%)\n  - Better functional outcomes in surgical group at 7-year follow-up\n  - Limited to military population (not generalizable)\n\n- **Meta-analysis (Zheng et al, 2019)**:\n  - Pooled analysis of 22 studies (1,984 patients)\n  - Surgical treatment showed lower redislocation rates (OR 0.44)\n  - Better Kujala scores with surgical treatment\n  - Heterogeneity in surgical techniques limits conclusions\n\n**Current Consensus**:\n- Conservative management recommended for most first-time dislocations without significant risk factors\n- Consider surgical intervention for:\n  - Large osteochondral fragments\n  - Significant anatomical risk factors\n  - High-demand athletes (relevant to our tennis player)\n\n**2. Recurrent Dislocations**\n\n**Surgical Techniques**\n\n- **MPFL Reconstruction**:\n  - **Systematic Review (Schneider et al, 2016)**:\n    - 85-90% success rate in preventing recurrence\n    - Mean Kujala score improvement of 15-20 points\n    - Various graft options show similar outcomes\n    - Anatomic femoral tunnel placement critical\n\n  - **Long-term Outcomes (Nomura et al, 2007)**:\n    - 96% good/excellent results at 11.9 years\n    - 5% redislocation rate\n    - Progressive patellofemoral arthritis in 15%\n\n- **Tibial Tuberosity Osteotomy (TTO)**:\n  - **Systematic Review (Caton & Dejour, 2010)**:\n    - Medialization effective for TT-TG >15-20mm\n    - Distalization effective for patella alta\n    - Combined procedures show 80-90% success rates\n\n- **Combined Procedures**:\n  - **MPFL + TTO (Schöttle et al, 2010)**:\n    - Superior outcomes compared to isolated procedures in patients with combined pathologies\n    - Redislocation rates <5%\n\n  - **Trochleoplasty (Dejour et al, 2013)**:\n    - Reserved for severe trochlear dysplasia\n    - 85-90% success rate when combined with other procedures\n    - Higher complication rates (15-20%)\n\n**3. Rehabilitation Evidence**\n\n- **Early Mobilization vs. Immobilization**:\n  - **RCT (Rood et al, 2015)**:\n    - Early mobilization showed better quadriceps recovery\n    - No difference in redislocation rates\n    - Improved patient satisfaction\n\n- **Bracing**:\n  - **Systematic Review (Cheng et al, 2014)**:\n    - Limited evidence for optimal duration\n    - 3-6 weeks commonly recommended\n    - No clear advantage of rigid vs. hinged bracing\n\n- **Return to Sport**:\n  - **Systematic Review (Moiz et al, 2018)**:\n    - Average return to sport: 5.4 months after conservative treatment\n    - Average return to sport: 6.7 months after surgical treatment\n    - 84% return to pre-injury level after surgery\n\n**4. Prognostic Factors**\n\n- **Risk of Recurrence**:\n  - **Prospective Cohort (Fithian et al, 2004)**:\n    - Age <25 years: 7x increased risk\n    - Female gender: 3x increased risk\n    - Trochlear dysplasia: 3.8x increased risk\n    - Patella alta: 2.5x increased risk\n\n- **Nottingham Patellar Instability Score**:\n  - Validated prediction tool for recurrence risk\n  - Factors: age, bilateral symptoms, family history, trochlear dysplasia, patella alta, TT-TG distance\n\n**How This Evidence Influences Treatment Decisions for the 33-year-old Tennis Player**\n\n1. **Initial Management**:\n   - Evidence supports early mobilization rather than prolonged immobilization\n   - Short period (1-3 weeks) of bracing in extension with early quadriceps activation\n\n2. **Surgical Decision-Making**:\n   - As a high-demand athlete, evidence suggests she may benefit from early surgical intervention even after first-time dislocation\n   - Her age (33) is protective against recurrence compared to younger patients\n   - Decision would be heavily influenced by:\n     - MRI findings (MPFL tear pattern, chondral injury)\n     - Anatomical risk factors (trochlear dysplasia, TT-TG distance, patella alta)\n     - Nottingham Patellar Instability Score calculation\n\n3. **Surgical Technique Selection**:\n   - If surgery indicated, evidence supports:\n     - Isolated MPFL reconstruction if normal anatomy\n     - Combined procedures (MPFL + TTO) if anatomical abnormalities present\n     - Avoid isolated lateral release (poor evidence for effectiveness)\n\n4. **Rehabilitation Protocol**:\n   - Evidence supports accelerated rehabilitation with early weight-bearing\n   - Focus on quadriceps/VMO strengthening has strongest evidence base\n   - Return to sport timeline of 5-7 months aligns with evidence\n   - Consider longer rehabilitation for cutting/pivoting sports like tennis\n\n5. **Long-term Considerations**:\n   - Evidence shows 40-60% risk of patellofemoral arthritis at 20+ years\n   - Discuss career longevity implications\n   - Consider preventative strategies based on evidence\n\nThe evidence base would be discussed with the patient as part of shared decision-making, acknowledging that her status as a semi-professional athlete may justify a more aggressive approach than would be recommended for the general population, while still being guided by her specific anatomical and injury characteristics.",
