    "consent_approach": "**Establish rapport:**\n- Introduce yourself and confirm Mr. Clark’s preferred name.\n- Ensure privacy, sit at eye level, and invite his daughter to stay if Mr. Clark wishes.\n\n**Confirm capacity & baseline understanding:**\n- Check orientation (time, place, person) and ask what he already knows about his injury.\n\n\n**Explain the diagnosis & proposed procedure:**\n- Simple language: “You have broken the ball part of your left hip. We plan to replace that ball with a metal implant fixed with cement – called a hemiarthroplasty.”\n\n**Discuss benefits:**\n- Pain relief\n- Earlier mobilisation and reduced complications of bed rest\n- More predictable result than fixation in the elderly\n- Shorter operation than total hip replacement\n\n**Discuss material risks (emphasise those relevant to Mr. Clark):**\n- Infection (deep ≈1–2 %)\n- Dislocation (≈2–4 %)\n- Periprosthetic fracture\n- Bleeding / transfusion\n- DVT/PE despite prophylaxis\n- Leg-length difference or persistent limp\n- Loosening or wear requiring revision\n- Medical risks: MI, stroke, anaesthetic complications, death (<5 % at 30 days in this age group)\n\n**Discuss alternatives:**\n- Total hip replacement (higher dislocation risk, longer surgery but better long-term function)\n- Internal fixation with screws/sliding hip screw (higher failure rate)\n- Non-operative treatment (rarely recommended – pain, immobility, high mortality)\n\n**Post-operative expectations & rehabilitation:**\n- Full weight bearing with physiotherapy from day 1\n- Hospital stay 3–5 days on average\n- Hip precautions for six weeks to minimise dislocation risk\n\n**Answer questions & check understanding:**\n- Encourage both Mr. Clark and his daughter to ask anything.\n- Ask Mr. Clark to repeat key points in his own words.\n\n**Confirm decision & obtain consent:**\n- “Given everything we have discussed, are you happy to proceed?”\n- Complete **Consent Form 1** (adult with capacity). Ensure legible completion, sign yourself, and witness signature if trust policy requires.\n\n**Documentation & information leaflets:**\n- Record the discussion in the notes, give the trust hip fracture booklet, and document that written information was provided.\n\n**Offer time:**\n- Allow a short period for consideration; confirm theatre schedule; provide buzzer if further questions arise.",
