        "answer": "Knee dislocations are classified using several systems, with the Kennedy Classification being the most widely used:\n\n\n**Kennedy Classification: Direction of Tibial Displacement:**\n- Anterior (most common, ~60%)\n- Posterior (~30%)\n- Medial (~4%)\n- Lateral (~4%)\n- Rotatory (~2%)\n\n**Schenck Classification:**\n- **KD-I**: Single cruciate tear with collateral ligament tear\n- **KD-II**: Both cruciate ligaments torn, collaterals intact\n- **KD-III**: Both cruciates torn plus one collateral torn\n  - **KD-IIIM**: Both cruciates plus MCL complex\n  - **KD-IIIL**: Both cruciates plus LCL/PLC complex\n- **KD-IV**: Both cruciates and both collaterals torn\n- **KD-V**: Knee dislocation with periarticular fracture\n\n\n**Management Guidance Based on Classification:**\n\n1. **Directional classification** guides the reduction technique:\n   - Posterior dislocations (as in this case): Apply anterior force to proximal tibia with knee flexed\n   - Anterior dislocations: Apply posterior force to proximal tibia\n\n2. **Schenck classification** guides surgical planning:\n   - KD-I/II: May be managed with repair/reconstruction of the involved ligaments\n   - KD-III/IV: Require comprehensive multi-ligament reconstruction, often staged\n   - KD-V: Requires fixation of fractures before ligament reconstruction\n\n3. **Vascular considerations** override classification-based management:\n   - With vascular injury: Immediate reduction and vascular repair take precedence\n   - Without vascular injury: More deliberate planning of ligament reconstruction\n\n4. **Timing of surgery** is influenced by classification:\n   - Avulsions/fractures: Early repair (within 2 weeks)\n   - Isolated cruciate tears: Can be delayed 3-6 weeks\n   - Multi-ligament injuries: Controversy exists, but many surgeons prefer early surgery for lateral/posterolateral injuries (within 2 weeks) while medial-sided injuries can be delayed\n\nIn this motorcross accident with posterior dislocation, I would anticipate a KD-III or KD-IV injury pattern with high risk of vascular compromise, necessitating urgent reduction and vascular assessment before definitive ligament reconstruction planning.",
