        "answer": "**Surgical Options for Multi-ligamentous Knee Injuries:**\n\n**Timing Options:**\n\n1. **Acute repair/reconstruction** (within 2-3 weeks)\n   - Advantages: Easier identification of tissue planes, potential for direct repair of avulsions\n   - Disadvantages: Higher risk of arthrofibrosis, operating through traumatized tissues\n\n2. **Delayed reconstruction** (>3 weeks)\n   - Advantages: Reduced risk of arthrofibrosis, improved soft tissue condition\n   - Disadvantages: More difficult tissue identification, loss of repair opportunities\n\n3. **Staged approach**\n   - First stage: Repair/reconstruct posterolateral corner (PLC) and collaterals\n   - Second stage (3-6 months later): Cruciate reconstructions\n\n**Reconstruction Options:**\n\n1. **ACL Reconstruction**\n   - Autograft options: Hamstring, patellar tendon, quadriceps tendon\n   - Allograft options: Achilles, tibialis anterior, patellar tendon\n\n2. **PCL Reconstruction**\n   - Single vs. double bundle techniques\n   - Transtibial vs. tibial inlay techniques\n   - Typically requires stronger graft (Achilles allograft or quadriceps autograft)\n\n3. **MCL Complex**\n   - Direct repair for acute injuries with good tissue quality\n   - Reconstruction with autograft/allograft for chronic injuries\n   - Consideration of posteromedial corner repair/reconstruction\n\n4. **LCL/PLC Complex**\n   - Anatomic reconstruction of key structures (LCL, popliteus, popliteofibular ligament)\n   - Modified Larson, Arciero, or LaPrade techniques\n\n**Decision-Making Factors:**\n\n1. **Patient factors**\n   - Age and activity level\n   - Comorbidities and healing potential\n   - Occupational and athletic demands\n   - Compliance with rehabilitation\n\n2. **Injury pattern**\n   - Schenck classification (KD-I through KD-V)\n   - Direction of dislocation (posterior dislocations have higher incidence of PLC injuries)\n   - Presence of avulsions vs. midsubstance tears\n   - Associated fractures or chondral injuries\n\n3. **Vascular status**\n   - History of vascular repair (may influence surgical approach)\n   - Timing may be dictated by vascular intervention\n\n4. **Soft tissue condition**\n   - Swelling and skin integrity\n   - Presence of open wounds\n   - Compartment syndrome history\n\n5. **Surgeon factors**\n   - Experience with multi-ligament reconstruction\n   - Preferred techniques and graft choices\n   - Available resources (allograft availability)\n\n**Evidence-Based Considerations:**\n\n- The MOON Knee Dislocation Study suggests better outcomes with early (<3 weeks) surgery for lateral-sided injuries\n- Higher risk of arthrofibrosis with acute surgery, particularly if full ROM not achieved preoperatively\n- Growing evidence supports single-stage multi-ligament reconstruction when possible\n- Repair vs. reconstruction debate continues, but reconstruction shows more reliable outcomes in most studies\n\nFor this motorcross patient with posterior dislocation, I would likely recommend early reconstruction of the PCL and posterolateral corner (if involved) once vascular status is secured, with consideration of a staged approach if significant medial injury is also present.",
