        "answer": "**Potential Complications Following Knee Dislocation:**\n\n**Early Complications (0-2 weeks):**\n\n1. **Vascular Injury**\n   - Incidence: 10-40% (higher in posterior dislocations)\n   - Management:\n     - Immediate vascular surgery consultation\n     - Arterial repair/bypass within 6-8 hours of injury\n     - Fasciotomy if compartment syndrome present or prolonged ischemia\n     - Amputation rates 10-15% with popliteal artery injury\n\n2. **Neurological Injury**\n   - Common peroneal nerve: 20-30% incidence\n   - Management:\n     - Documentation and monitoring\n     - Ankle-foot orthosis for foot drop\n     - Consider nerve exploration if complete laceration suspected\n     - Tendon transfers for permanent deficits (posterior tibialis transfer)\n\n3. **Compartment Syndrome**\n   - Management:\n     - Clinical monitoring (pain out of proportion, pain on passive stretch)\n     - Compartment pressure measurement if suspected\n     - Urgent four-compartment fasciotomy if confirmed\n\n4. **Venous Thromboembolism (VTE)**\n   - Management:\n     - Chemical thromboprophylaxis (LMWH)\n     - Mechanical prophylaxis\n     - Consider IVC filter in high-risk patients with contraindications to anticoagulation\n\n**Intermediate Complications (2 weeks-6 months):**\n\n1. **Stiffness/Arthrofibrosis**\n   - Incidence: 20-60%\n   - Management:\n     - Aggressive early ROM when safe\n     - Continuous passive motion\n     - Consider manipulation under anesthesia if <90° flexion by 6-8 weeks\n     - Arthroscopic lysis of adhesions for refractory cases\n\n2. **Heterotopic Ossification**\n   - Management:\n     - NSAIDs prophylaxis\n     - Surgical excision if mature and limiting function\n\n3. **Wound Complications**\n   - Management:\n     - Meticulous surgical technique\n     - Staged procedures if soft tissues compromised\n     - Negative pressure wound therapy for dehiscence\n     - Plastic surgery involvement for complex wounds\n\n**Late Complications (>6 months