        "answer": "**Evidence on Timing of Surgery for Multi-ligamentous Knee Injuries:**\n\n**Key Studies and Findings:**\n\n1. **Levy et al. (2010) - Systematic Review**\n   - Compared early (<3 weeks) vs. late (>3 weeks) surgery\n   - Early surgery: Better functional outcomes, higher return to sport rates\n   - Early surgery: Higher risk of arthrofibrosis (8% vs. 3%)\n   - Recommended early surgery for most multi-ligament knee injuries\n\n2. **Mook et al. (2013) - Meta-analysis**\n   - Early surgery: Better Lysholm scores, greater stability, higher return to work rates\n   - No significant difference in range of motion between early and late groups\n   - Concluded early surgery preferable when possible\n\n3. **MOON Knee Dislocation Study (2019)**\n   - Prospective multicenter study\n   - Early surgery (<3 weeks) showed better outcomes for lateral-sided injuries\n   - Timing less critical for medial-sided injuries\n   - Supported early surgery particularly for posterolateral corner injuries\n\n4. **Jiang et al. (2018) - Meta-analysis**\n   - Early repair/reconstruction: Better subjective outcomes and stability\n   - No significant difference in complication rates between early and late groups\n   - Supported early intervention when possible\n\n**Current Evidence-Based Recommendations:**\n\n1. **Timing Based on Injury Pattern:**\n   - Lateral/posterolateral injuries: Early surgery (<3 weeks) recommended\n   - Medial-sided injuries: Can be delayed if necessary with comparable outcomes\n   - Vascular injuries: Immediate surgical exploration required\n\n2. **Factors Favoring Early Surgery (<3 weeks):**\n   - Avulsion injuries amenable to direct repair\n   - Young, active patients\n   - Lateral-sided/posterolateral injuries\n   - Good soft tissue condition\n   - Experienced surgical team\n\n3. **Factors Favoring Delayed Surgery (>3 weeks):**\n   - Significant soft tissue swelling/damage\n   - Limited preoperative range of motion\n   - Predominantly medial-sided injuries\n   - Limited surgical resources/experience\n   - Medical comorbidities requiring optimization\n\n4. **Staged Approach Evidence:**\n   - Hirschmann et al. (2010): Supported staged approach for complex injuries\n   - First stage: Collateral ligament and PLC reconstruction\n   - Second stage (3-6 months later): Cruciate reconstruction\n   - Reduced arthrofibrosis risk but requires two surgeries\n\n**Practical Application:**\n\nThe current evidence suggests a nuanced approach to timing:\n\n1. Vascular injuries require immediate intervention regardless of other factors\n\n2. For isolated multi-ligament injuries without vascular compromise:\n   - Early surgery (<3 weeks) generally produces better outcomes\n   - Particularly important for posterolateral corner injuries\n   - Must balance against arthrofibrosis risk\n\n3. Consider patient-specific factors:\n   - Activity demands\n   - Injury pattern\n   - Soft tissue condition\n   - Surgical resources\n\nFor this motorcross patient with posterior dislocation, assuming no vascular injury requiring immediate intervention, the evidence would support early reconstruction (within 3 weeks) particularly if posterolateral corner involvement is identified on MRI.",
