        "answer": "**Rehabilitation Following Surgical Management of Knee Dislocation:**\n\nRehabilitation must be carefully structured and individualized based on the specific injury pattern, surgical procedure, and patient factors. The following is a phase-based approach:\n\n**Phase 1: Protection Phase (0-6 weeks)**\n\n**Goals:**\n- Protect surgical repairs/reconstructions\n- Control pain and swelling\n- Maintain quadriceps activation\n- Gradually restore range of motion within safe limits\n- Prevent complications (DVT, arthrofibrosis)\n\n**Interventions:**\n- Hinged knee brace locked in extension for weight-bearing\n- Partial weight-bearing with crutches (protocol varies by surgeon)\n- Passive ROM exercises within safe limits:\n  - Typically 0-90° by 2-4 weeks for most reconstructions\n  - More conservative for PLC reconstructions (0-30° for 2 weeks, progressing slowly)\n- Quadriceps sets, straight leg raises with brace locked\n- Patella mobilizations\n- Ankle pumps and upper body conditioning\n- Cryotherapy and elevation\n- Neuromuscular electrical stimulation for quadriceps if needed\n\n**Phase 2: Motion Phase (6-12 weeks)**\n\n**Goals:**\n- Progress to full ROM\n- Improve quadriceps and hamstring strength\n- Normalize gait pattern\n- Maintain cardiovascular fitness\n\n**Interventions:**\n- Progress to full weight-bearing (typically by 8-10 weeks)\n- Unlock brace progressively for ambulation\n- Advance ROM exercises to achieve full motion by 12 weeks\n- Closed-chain strengthening exercises (wall slides, mini-squats)\n- Open-chain exercises with limited ranges (avoid terminal extension for ACL, terminal flexion for PCL)\n- Stationary cycling with progressive resistance\n- Pool therapy\n- Proprioceptive training\n- Gait training\n\n**Phase 3: Strengthening Phase (3-6 months)**\n\n**Goals:**\n- Normalize strength (goal: 80% of contralateral limb)\n- Improve neuromuscular control\n- Enhance proprioception\n- Prepare for return to functional activities\n\n**Interventions:**\n- Discontinue brace for daily activities (may continue for exercise)\n- Progressive resistance training\n- Eccentric training programs\n- Advanced closed-chain exercises\n- Balance and proprioceptive progression\n- Elliptical trainer, stair climber\n- Straight-line jogging when criteria met (typically 4-6 months)\n- Sport-specific movement patterns without cutting/pivoting\n\n**Phase 4: Return to Function Phase (6-12+ months)**\n\n**Goals:**\n- Achieve full strength and power\n- Restore sport-specific or occupational function\n- Safe return to pre-injury activities\n\n**Interventions:**\n- Advanced strengthening and power development\n- Plyometric training\n- Agility drills with progressive intensity\n- Sport-specific or job-specific training\n- Gradual return to cutting and pivoting sports (9-12+ months)\n- Psychological readiness assessment\n- Return-to-sport testing (hop tests, strength testing, agility assessments)\n\n**Special Considerations:**\n\n1. **PCL Reconstruction:**\n   - Avoid hamstring strengthening for 8-12 weeks\n   - Avoid open-chain knee flexion exercises initially\n   - Posterior tibial support during exercises\n\n2. **PLC Reconstruction:**\n   - More conservative ROM progression\n   - Avoid varus stress and tibial external rotation for 12 weeks\n   - No resisted hamstring exercises for 8-12 weeks\n\n3. **MCL Repair/Reconstruction:**\n   - Avoid valgus stress for 8-12 weeks\n   - Brace may be continued longer for activities\n\n4. **Vascular Repair History:**\n   - Modified weight-bearing progression\n   - Monitoring for claudication symptoms\n   - Avoiding prolonged knee flexion initially\n\n**Outcome Measures:**\n- Regular strength testing (isokinetic if available)\n- Functional testing (single-leg hop, crossover hop, etc.)\n- Patient-reported outcome measures (IKDC, Lysholm, KOOS)\n- Return-to-sport criteria: 90% limb symmetry on strength and hop tests\n\nThe timeline must be individualized based on surgical procedure, tissue healing, and patient progress rather than strictly time-based. Communication between surgeon, physiotherapist, and patient is essential throughout the rehabilitation process.",
