        "answer": "Perthes disease, or Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease, is an idiopathic avascular necrosis of the femoral head that typically affects children between 4-8 years of age, with a male predominance (4:1 male to female ratio).\n\nThe typical presentation includes:\n\n- Insidious onset of hip or referred knee pain\n- Limping or antalgic gait that worsens with activity\n- Limited hip range of motion, particularly abduction and internal rotation\n- Often unilateral (bilateral in 10-15% of cases)\n- Symptoms that fluctuate but persist over months\n- No history of significant trauma\n- May have quadriceps wasting due to disuse\n\nThe condition progresses through four radiological stages (Waldenström staging):\n1. **Initial/necrosis stage**: Increased density of the femoral head\n2. **Fragmentation stage**: Subchondral fracture and fragmentation of the femoral head\n3. **Reossification stage**: New bone formation\n4. **Healing/remodeling stage**: Restoration of normal bone architecture (though often with residual deformity)\n\nThe entire disease process typically spans 2-4 years from onset to completion.",
