        "answer": "When interpreting radiographs for suspected Perthes disease, I would systematically assess:\n\n**AP Pelvis and Frog Lateral Views:**\n\n1. **Femoral head density and contour:**\n   - Initial increased density of the femoral head (sclerosis)\n   - Subchondral fracture line (Caffey's sign or crescent sign)\n   - Fragmentation of the femoral head\n   - Head flattening or collapse\n\n2. **Joint space:**\n   - Widening due to synovitis and effusion\n   - Lateral subluxation of the femoral head\n\n3. **Metaphyseal changes:**\n   - Cysts or radiolucent areas\n   - Widening of the femoral neck\n\n4. **Epiphyseal size:**\n   - Comparison with the contralateral side\n   - Small or fragmented epiphysis\n\n5. **Staging (Waldenström):**\n   - Initial/necrosis: Increased density, preserved height\n   - Fragmentation: Subchondral fracture, fragmentation\n   - Reossification: New bone formation\n   - Healing/remodeling: Restoration toward normal architecture\n\n6. **Classification systems:**\n   - **Herring (Lateral Pillar):** Based on the height of the lateral pillar of the femoral head\n     - Group A: No involvement of lateral pillar\n     - Group B: >50% of lateral pillar height maintained\n     - Group B/C border: Exactly 50% of lateral pillar height maintained\n     - Group C: <50% of lateral pillar height maintained\n\nI would also note that early changes may not be visible on plain radiographs, and MRI is more sensitive for early diagnosis.",
