      {
        "answer": "There are three main classification systems used in Perthes disease, each with specific implications for management:\n\n**1. Herring Lateral Pillar Classification:**\n- Most widely used and with the best prognostic value\n- Based on the height of the lateral pillar of the femoral head during fragmentation phase\n- **Group A:** No involvement of lateral pillar - Excellent prognosis, typically managed conservatively\n- **Group B:** >50% of lateral pillar height maintained - Good prognosis in children <8-9 years, may need containment\n- **Group B/C border:** Exactly 50% of lateral pillar height maintained - Intermediate prognosis, often needs containment\n- **Group C:** <50% of lateral pillar height maintained - Poor prognosis, surgical containment often indicated\n\n**2. Catterall Classification:**\n- Based on extent of femoral head involvement on AP and lateral radiographs\n- **Group I:** Anterior head involvement only (<25%) - Good prognosis, observation usually sufficient\n- **Group II:** Anterior head and partial central segment (25-50%) - Good prognosis if <6 years, may need containment\n- **Group III:** Most of head except posterior segment (50-75%) - Containment often indicated\n- **Group IV:** Total head involvement (>75%) - Poor prognosis, surgical containment typically indicated\n\n**3. Waldenström Staging:**\n- Describes the temporal progression rather than extent\n- **Initial/necrosis stage:** Increased density, preserved height - Symptomatic treatment, activity modification\n- **Fragmentation stage:** Subchondral fracture, fragmentation - Key stage for containment decisions\n- **Reossification stage:** New bone formation - Containment less effective at this stage\n- **Healing/remodeling stage:** Restoration toward normal - Focus on managing residual deformity\n\n**Management Guidance Based on Classification:**\n\n1. **Age considerations:**\n   - <6 years: Better remodeling potential, more likely to respond to conservative management\n   - 6-8 years: Intermediate, decision based on classification and risk factors\n   - >8-9 years: Poorer prognosis, lower threshold for surgical intervention\n\n2. **Containment principles:**\n   - Goal: Keep femoral head contained within acetabulum during healing to maintain sphericity\n   - Conservative containment: Bracing (Scottish Rite, Petrie cast)\n   - Surgical containment: Femoral osteotomy, pelvic osteotomy, shelf procedure\n\n3. **Decision algorithm:**\n   - Herring A: Usually observation regardless of age\n   - Herring B: <8 years - observation or conservative containment; >8 years - consider surgical containment\n   - Herring B/C border: <8 years - conservative or surgical containment; >8 years - surgical containment\n   - Herring C: Surgical containment typically indicated regardless of age\n\n4. **Head-at-risk signs** (additional factors indicating need for more aggressive management):\n   - Lateral subluxation\n   - Calcification lateral to epiphysis\n   - Gage's sign\n   - Horizontal growth plate\n   - Diffuse metaphyseal reaction\n\nThe classification systems, particularly Herring, combined with age and presence of head-at-risk signs, provide the framework for treatment decisions in Perthes disease.",
        "question": "What are the classification systems used in Perthes disease and how do they guide management?"
