      {
        "answer": "The principles of management for Perthes disease focus on maintaining femoral head sphericity and preventing deformity while managing symptoms. The approach varies based on disease severity, age, and stage:\n\n**Core Management Principles:**\n\n1. **Symptom Management:**\n   - Appropriate analgesia (NSAIDs, paracetamol)\n   - Activity modification to reduce pain\n   - Physiotherapy for maintaining range of motion\n   - Traction during acute painful phase if needed\n\n2. **Containment Strategy:**\n   - Goal: Keep the femoral head contained within the acetabulum during healing\n   - Allows the acetabulum to act as a mold for the remodeling femoral head\n   - Most effective during fragmentation stage before significant deformity\n\n3. **Age-Appropriate Approach:**\n   - <6 years: Better remodeling potential, often observation or non-surgical containment\n   - 6-8 years: Intermediate, decisions based on classification and risk factors\n   - >8-9 years: Poorer prognosis, lower threshold for surgical intervention\n\n**Conservative Management Options:**\n\n1. **Observation:**\n   - Suitable for Herring A, some Herring B in younger children\n   - Regular clinical and radiological follow-up\n   - Activity modification as needed\n\n2. **Range of Motion Exercises:**\n   - Physiotherapy to maintain hip abduction and internal rotation\n   - Home exercise programs\n   - Swimming and non-weight bearing exercises\n\n3. **Bracing/Orthoses:**\n   - Scottish Rite orthosis (abduction brace)\n   - Petrie cast (abduction-internal rotation cast)\n   - Goal: Maintain femoral head containment during weight-bearing\n\n**Surgical Management Options:**\n\n1. **Femoral Osteotomy:**\n   - Varus derotation osteotomy most common\n   - Redirects femoral head into acetabulum\n   - Indicated for lateral subluxation and older children with more severe disease\n\n2. **Pelvic Osteotomy:**\n   - Salter innominate osteotomy\n   - Triple innominate osteotomy\n   - Goal: Improve acetabular coverage of femoral head\n\n3. **Shelf Procedure:**\n   - Augments acetabular coverage laterally\n   - Can be combined with other procedures\n\n4. **Arthrodiastasis:**\n   - Joint distraction with external fixator\n   - Less commonly used but may help in certain cases\n\n**Follow-up and Monitoring:**\n\n1. **Regular Clinical Assessment:**\n   - Pain levels\n   - Range of motion\n   - Gait pattern\n   - Limb length\n\n2. **Radiological Surveillance:**\n   - Every 3-6 months during active disease\n   - Assess progression through Waldenström stages\n   - Monitor for complications (hinge abduction, joint incongruity)\n\n3. **Long-term Follow-up:**\n   - Continue into adolescence\n   - Monitor for early degenerative changes\n   - Transition to adult services if needed\n\n**Multidisciplinary Approach:**\n\n1. **Physiotherapy:**\n   - Maintain range of motion\n   - Strengthen supporting musculature\n   - Gait training\n\n2. **School and Activity Modifications:**\n   - Liaison with school regarding PE restrictions\n   - Adaptive equipment if needed\n\n3. **Psychological Support:**\n   - Address impact of chronic condition\n   - Support for child and family\n\nThe ultimate goal is to achieve a spherical, congruent hip joint at skeletal maturity to minimize the risk of early osteoarthritis in adulthood.",
