        "answer": "The radiograph shows a subtrochanteric femur fracture, which is defined as a fracture occurring within 5cm distal to the lesser trochanter. \n\nSpecific findings include:\n- Fracture line extending from the lateral cortex below the greater trochanter to the medial cortex below the lesser trochanter\n- Comminution of the medial cortex (characteristic of subtrochanteric fractures)\n- Displacement of the fracture fragments\n- Shortening of the femur\n- External rotation deformity\n\nThe typical deformity pattern shows the proximal fragment in flexion, abduction, and external rotation due to the pull of the iliopsoas and abductor muscles, while the distal fragment is displaced medially and proximally due to the pull of the adductors and hamstrings.\n",
