        "answer": "Subtrochanteric femur fractures present several significant biomechanical challenges:\n\n**1. High Stress Zone:**\n- The subtrochanteric region experiences some of the highest stresses in the body, with forces up to 1200 N/cm² in the medial cortex during single-leg stance\n- This area has primarily cortical bone with relatively poor vascularity\n\n**2. Deforming Muscle Forces:**\n- The proximal fragment is typically pulled into flexion by the iliopsoas\n- Abductors pull the proximal fragment into abduction and external rotation\n- Adductors and hamstrings pull the distal fragment medially and proximally\n- These deforming forces make reduction difficult and increase stress on fixation constructs\n\n**3. Medial Buttress Disruption:**\n- Loss of the medial cortical buttress is common\n- This creates a significant mechanical disadvantage for healing and implant stability\n- Restoration of the medial buttress is critical for successful treatment\n\n**4. Implant Stress:**\n- High cantilever bending forces on implants\n- Increased risk of implant failure (breakage, cut-out, or varus collapse)\n- Requires stronger implants with better fatigue resistance\n\n**5. Bone Quality Issues:**\n- Often occurs in elderly patients with osteoporotic bone (bisphosphonate fracture)\n- High association with primary or metastatic cancer\n- Reduced screw purchase and holding power\n- Increased risk of implant cut-out or failure\n\nThese biomechanical challenges explain the historically high complication rates (up to 20-30%) including non-union, malunion, and implant failure.",
