        "answer": "The evidence base for subtrochanteric femur fracture management includes:\n\n**1. Implant Selection:**\n- **Cephalomedullary Nails vs. Plate Fixation:**\n  - Systematic review by Kuzyk et al. (2009) showed lower failure rates with nails (0-20%) compared to plates (12-56%)\n  - Meta-analysis by Liu et al. (2017) demonstrated significantly lower reoperation rates and better functional outcomes with nails\n  - Cochrane review by Parker et al. (2013) found reduced operative time and blood loss with nailing\n\n- **Long vs. Short Nails:**\n  - Prospective study by Kleweno et al. (2014) showed no significant difference in outcomes\n  - However, biomechanical studies demonstrate better stress distribution with long nails\n  - Current consensus favors long nails, especially in osteoporotic bone\n\n**2. Reduction Techniques:**\n- **Importance of Medial Buttress:**\n  - Mehrpour et al. (2012) demonstrated 3x higher failure rates with >5mm medial gap\n  - Anatomic reduction of medial cortex significantly reduces non-union rates\n\n- **Cerclage Wiring:**\n  - Tomas et al. (2013) showed improved reduction quality and union rates\n  - Concerns about periosteal devascularization not supported by clinical outcomes\n\n**3. Timing of Surgery:**\n- BOAST guidelines recommend surgery within 24-48 hours\n- Delayed surgery (>48 hours) associated with increased mortality and complications\n- Simunovic et al. (2010) meta-analysis showed 41% reduced mortality with early surgery\n\n**4. Weight-bearing Protocols:**\n- Historically, protected weight-bearing was recommended\n- Recent evidence supports early weight-bearing as tolerated:\n  - Kammerlander et al. (2018) showed no increase in complications with immediate weight-bearing\n  - Early mobilization reduces complications like pneumonia, DVT, and pressure sores\n\n**5. Biological Augmentation:**\n- Limited evidence for routine use of bone grafting in primary surgery\n- BMP and other biologics show promise in high-risk cases and non-unions\n- Systematic review by Garrison et al. (2010) showed improved union rates with BMP in complex fractures\n\n**6. Osteoporosis Management:**\n- Bisphosphonates improve implant stability and reduce subsequent fractures\n- HORIZON Recurrent Fracture Trial showed 35% reduction in clinical fractures\n- Delay of 2-12 weeks post-fracture recommended before starting bisphosphonates\n\n**7. Multidisciplinary Approach:**\n- Orthogeriatric co-management reduces mortality by 20-40%\n- Comprehensive rehabilitation programs improve functional outcomes\n- Grigoryan et al. (2014) showed improved return to pre-injury status with structured rehabilitation\n\n",
