        "answer": "Management modifications for a subtrochanteric femur fracture patient on anticoagulation therapy:\n\n**1. Assessment and Risk Stratification:**\n- Identify specific anticoagulant (warfarin, DOAC, antiplatelet)\n- Determine indication (AF, mechanical valve, recent VTE, etc.)\n- Assess bleeding risk vs. thrombotic risk\n- Consult hematology/cardiology for high-risk patients\n\n**2. Warfarin Management:**\n- Check INR on admission\n- For urgent surgery (within 24-48 hours):\n  - If INR >1.5: Vitamin K 5-10mg IV\n  - For rapid reversal: Prothrombin Complex Concentrate (PCC) 25-50 IU/kg\n  - Fresh Frozen Plasma if PCC unavailable (less effective)\n- Target INR <1.5 for surgery\n- Monitor INR every 6-12 hours until normalized\n\n**3. Direct Oral Anticoagulant (DOAC) Management:**\n- Determine time of last dose\n- For urgent surgery:\n  - Apixaban/Rivaroxaban: Delay surgery 24-48 hours from last dose if possible\n  - Dabigatran: Consider idarucizumab (Praxbind) for reversal\n  - Factor Xa inhibitors: Consider andexanet alfa if available for life-threatening bleeding\n- Check drug-specific anti-Xa levels if available\n- No routine reversal agent needed for most cases with appropriate timing\n\n**4. Antiplatelet Management:**\n- Aspirin: Can usually proceed with surgery without interruption\n- Clopidogrel/Ticagrelor:\n  - If possible, delay surgery 3-5 days\n  - For urgent cases, proceed with surgery accepting increased bleeding risk\n  - Consider platelet transfusion only for excessive bleeding\n  - Consult cardiology for patients with recent coronary stents (<6 months)\n\n**5. Bridging Therapy:**\n- Consider prophylactic dose LMWH when anticoagulation is interrupted\n- For high thrombotic risk (mechanical valve, recent VTE):\n  - Therapeutic dose LMWH bridging\n  - IVC filter consideration in extreme cases\n\n**6. Intraoperative Considerations:**\n- Meticulous hemostasis\n- Consider tranexamic acid (15mg/kg IV) if not contraindicated\n- Cell salvage techniques\n- Topical hemostatic agents\n- Drain placement consideration\n\n**7. Post-operative Management:**\n- Restart anticoagulation when hemostasis is secure:\n  - Warfarin: Resume on evening of surgery or post-op day 1\n  - DOACs: Resume 48-72 hours post-operatively\n  - Bridge with prophylactic LMWH until therapeutic\n- Close monitoring for bleeding complications\n- Regular hemoglobin checks\n- Clear documentation of anticoagulation plan\n\n**8. Multidisciplinary Approach:**\n- Involve hematology/cardiology\n- Anesthesia consultation for regional techniques\n- Clear communication with all team members about anticoagulation status",
