      {
        "answer": "**Duty of candour** is both a professional and statutory obligation to be open and honest with patients when something goes wrong with their treatment that causes, or has the potential to cause, harm or distress.\n\nIn this scenario, duty of candour applies because:\n\n1. A **notifiable safety incident** has occurred - damage to the anterior tibial artery during surgery is an unintended incident that could result in harm\n\n2. The statutory duty of candour requires:\n   - **Notification** - informing Sophie's parents as soon as reasonably practicable that the incident has occurred\n   - **Providing a truthful account** of what happened and what is known about the potential consequences\n   - **Offering an appropriate apology** - expressing genuine regret about what happened\n   - **Providing reasonable support** - ensuring Sophie receives appropriate care following the incident\n   - **Documentation** - recording the notification and apology in Sophie's medical records\n\n3. The professional duty (GMC) additionally requires:\n   - Being open and honest with patients when things go wrong\n   - Explaining fully to patients when complications or errors occur\n   - Reporting errors and taking steps to prevent harm\n\nFollowing duty of candour demonstrates professional integrity, maintains patient trust, and supports a culture of transparency and learning.",
