      {
        "answer": "**Key principles of effective communication when disclosing complications:**\n\n**Timeliness:**\n- Communicate promptly after the event\n- Don't delay disclosure once facts are established\n- Provide updates as new information becomes available\n\n**Honesty and transparency:**\n- Provide a truthful account of what happened\n- Avoid defensive language or minimizing the event\n- Acknowledge uncertainty where it exists\n\n**Clarity:**\n- Use plain, jargon-free language\n- Check understanding frequently\n- Provide written information when appropriate\n\n**Empathy:**\n- Acknowledge the emotional impact\n- Express genuine regret (\"I'm sorry this happened\")\n- Demonstrate that you understand their concerns\n\n**Responsibility:**\n- Acknowledge when errors have occurred\n- Avoid blaming others or the system\n- Focus on what is being done to address the situation\n\n**Support:**\n- Offer practical and emotional support\n- Provide contact details for further questions\n- Arrange appropriate follow-up\n\n**Structured approach:**\n- Use frameworks like SPIKES to ensure comprehensive communication\n- Prepare key points before the conversation\n- Document the discussion afterward\n\n**Patient-centered:**\n- Tailor information to the individual's needs and understanding\n- Involve the patient/family in decision-making about next steps\n- Respect cultural and personal preferences\n\nThese principles align with GMC guidance on being open and honest with patients when things go wrong (duty of candour) and help maintain trust in the doctor-patient relationship even when complications occur.",
